(window.KG_PARTS = window.KG_PARTS || []).push({"group":"pattupattu","chapters":{"pp:1":{"book":"pattupattu","num":1,"title":"திருமுருகாற்றுப்படை","section":"திருமுருகாற்றுப்படை","theme":"Thirumurugatruppadai is a guide poem in which one who has received the god's grace directs another seeker to Murugan: after a 66-line opening sentence that ends in the promise that the seeker will have what he wants, it describes the god at six places (Parankunram, Alaivay, Avinankudi, Eragam, every hill, and Pazhamudircholai with all the places of his worship), each with its own worshippers, and closes by scripting the seeker's praise, the god's appearance and his gift, and the waterfall of his hill."},"pp:2":{"book":"pattupattu","num":2,"title":"பொருநராற்றுப்படை","section":"பொருநராற்றுப்படை","theme":"Porunaratruppadai: a bard who has been rewarded by Karikal Valavan guides another bard to him, describing the lute and its singer, his own reception, feast and parting gifts, the king's birth and victory at Venni, and the Chola land where the five landscapes mingle and the Kaveri does not fail."},"pp:3":{"book":"pattupattu","num":3,"title":"சிறுபாணாற்றுப்படை","section":"சிறுபாணாற்றுப்படை","theme":"Sirupanatruppadai: a bard who has been enriched by Nalliyakkodan, chief of Oymanadu, directs a hungry fellow bard and his troupe to him, first measuring the patron against the three crowned cities and the seven famous givers, then promising the food of each land on the road to his town and the gifts he gives before he is asked."},"pp:4":{"book":"pattupattu","num":4,"title":"பெரும்பாணாற்றுப்படை (1/2)","section":"பெரும்பாணாற்றுப்படை","theme":"The first half of Perumpanatruppadai: a bard rewarded by Thondaiman Ilanthiraiyan tells a hungry bard of the patron's lineage, just rule and safe roads, and then describes the way to him land by land (salt caravans and armed traders, the hunters of the dry tract, cattle-raiding warriors, herders, dry-land farmers, the wet paddy country with its rich town, sugar presses and fishers' huts), promising at each settlement the meal its people will give."},"pp:5":{"book":"pattupattu","num":5,"title":"பெரும்பாணாற்றுப்படை (2/2)","section":"பெரும்பாணாற்றுப்படை","theme":"The second half of Perumpanatruppadai finishes the road to Kanchi, through the fisherfolk's settlement, the Brahmin village, the port of Neerpeyarru, the toddy-sellers' houses, the lighthouse, the grove farmers and the shrine of the god on the serpent bed, then praises Thondaiman Ilanthiraiyan's city, victory and court, scripts the bard's praise, and lists the gifts the king gives the same day before closing on his peaceful hill."},"pp:6":{"book":"pattupattu","num":6,"title":"முல்லைப்பாட்டு","section":"முல்லைப்பாட்டு","theme":"Mullaippattu is the waiting of mullai told in three places at one rainy night: a wife at home who will not be comforted by the old women's omen, a king in his war camp kept awake by what the battle cost, and the road on which his chariot returns through the flowering forest; the poem joins the two vigils only through sound, ending when his horses fill her ears."},"pp:7":{"book":"pattupattu","num":7,"title":"மதுரைக்காஞ்சி (1/2)","section":"மதுரைக்காஞ்சி","theme":"The first half of Maduraikkanchi, Mangudi Maruthanar's counsel to the Pandya who won at Thalaiyalanganam: a long address that praises the king's lineage, battles, sieges, port wealth and giving, ravages his enemies' land, blesses him, and then sets against all this the countless kings who ruled as he does and passed away; with அதனால், 'therefore', it turns to a description of his realm in its five lands (marutham, mullai, kurinji, paalai, neythal) and approaches Madurai through the Vaiyai's banks, the refugees and defeated kings at its edge, its walls, its festival flags and its streets."},"pp:8":{"book":"pattupattu","num":8,"title":"மதுரைக்காஞ்சி (2/2)","section":"மதுரைக்காஞ்சி","theme":"The second half of Maduraikkanchi walks through one day and night of Madurai, from the day market, the shrines, the sages, the court and the crafts to the evening market, the lovers and courtesans, the festivals, the guarded watches and the dawn, then turns to the king: the spoils that feed the city, his waking among women, his honouring of warriors by merit and his open-gated giving, and ends by bidding him rejoice daily in the good time he has been given within its bound."},"pp:9":{"book":"pattupattu","num":9,"title":"நெடுநல்வாடை","section":"நெடுநல்வாடை","theme":"Nedunalvadai describes the long cold season, first in the world and the town, then inside a king's palace, where the queen lies unadorned and unconsoled on a bed made for two; it ends with the poet's wish that the war end now, and with the king at midnight walking his winter camp to see his wounded, so that a poem the colophon files as vaakai spends most of its lines on a wife's waiting."},"pp:10":{"book":"pattupattu","num":10,"title":"குறிஞ்சிப்பாட்டு","section":"குறிஞ்சிப்பாட்டு","theme":"Kurinjippattu entire: the friend discloses the secret love to the mother (அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்), telling how on the day the mother sent the girls to guard the millet they bathed, gathered the flowers of every land, were met by a hill hunter, were saved by him from a wild elephant, and how he embraced the girl, swore to marry her and has come every night since, through dangers the girl weeps over."},"pp:11":{"book":"pattupattu","num":11,"title":"பட்டினப்பாலை","section":"பட்டினப்பாலை","theme":"Pattinappalai is one long sentence: 217 lines on Puhar and the Chola land (the unfailing Kaveri, the port, its trade, its festivals, the virtues of its ploughmen and merchants), the hero's three-line refusal to leave her even for that city, and eighty lines on Karikalan's youth, wars, ruin of enemy lands and founding works, all set up for the closing comparison: the forest is hotter than his spear, her shoulders cooler than his sceptre."},"pp:12":{"book":"pattupattu","num":12,"title":"மலைபடுகடாம் (1/2)","section":"மலைபடுகடாம்","theme":"The first half of Malaipadukadam: a bard who has been rewarded by Nannan addresses another troupe through its drums, pipes and great lute, praises Nannan son of Nannan and promises to tell of his land, lists the out-of-season harvest of his hills, promises the hunters' feasts and welcome, and then guides the troupe stage by stage along the dangerous hill road, with rules for sacred places, rivers, forests and night, until it rests and hears the many sounds of the hill."},"pp:13":{"book":"pattupattu","num":13,"title":"மலைபடுகடாம் (2/2)","section":"மலைபடுகடாம்","theme":"The second half of Malaipadukadam takes the bards from the patron's mountain, whose countless sounds make the 'resonance of the mountain', down its dangerous roads and through the hospitality of herders, bowmen, farmers and fishers to Nannan's town and court, where his welcome, his reflection that only fame outlives death, and his gifts close the poem on his title."}},"verses":{"pp:1:1-11":{"book":"pattupattu","num":1,"t":["உலகம் உவப்ப வலன்ஏர்பு திரிதரு","பலர்புகழ் ஞாயிறு கடற்கண் டாஅங்கு","ஓவற இமைக்கும் சேண்விளங்கு அவிரொளி","உறுநர்த் தாங்கிய மதனுடை நோன்றாள்","செறுநர்த் தேய்த்த செல்லுறழ் தடக்கை","மறுவில் கற்பின் வாணுதற் கணவன்","கார்கோள் முகந்த கமஞ்சூல் மாமழை","வாள்போழ் விசும்பில் வள்ளுறை சிதறித்","தலைப்பெயல் தலை இய தண்ணறுங் கானத்து","இருள்படப் பொதுளிய பராரை மராஅத்து","உருள்பூந் தண்டார் புரளும் மார்பினன், -"],"p":"","r":"The god's far-reaching brightness is first likened to the sun that the world praises, rising out of the sea; then he is named by his works, feet that bore up those who came to him and a great hand that wore down his foes, as the husband of a bright-browed woman of spotless chastity, wearing on his chest a cool rolling garland from the kadamba of a forest freshly soaked by the first rains.","n":"Lines 4 and 5 are built as a pair (உறுநர்த் தாங்கிய ... நோன்றாள், செறுநர்த் தேய்த்த ... தடக்கை): the same god is shelter to those who come and ruin to those who oppose, which is the whole promise the guide will make. The consort of line 6 is not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","adaikkalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 1-11","lb":"PP1:1-11","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["கடற்கண்","கடல்",2,"pira","simile","sea"],["மாமழை","மா மழை",7,"pira","scene","great rain cloud"],["மராஅத்து","மராஅம்",10,"maram","ornament","kadamba"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:12-37":{"book":"pattupattu","num":2,"t":["மால்வரை நிவந்த சேணுயர் வெற்பிற்","கிண்கிணி கவைஇய ஒண்செஞ் சீறடி,","கணைக்கால் வாங்கிய நுசுப்பின், பணைத்தோள்,","கோபத் தன்ன தோயாப் பூந்துகில்,","பல்காசு நிரைத்த சில்காழ் அல்குல்,","கைபுனைந்து இயற்றாக் கவின்பெறு வனப்பின்,","நாவலொடு பெயரிய பொலம்புனை அவிரிழை,","சேணிகந்து விளங்கும் செயிர்தீர் மேனி,","துணையோர் ஆய்ந்த இணையீர் ஓதிச்","செங்கால் வெட்சிச் சீறிதழ் இடையிடுபு","பைந்தாட் குவளைத் தூவிதழ் கிள்ளித்","தெய்வ வுத்தியொடு வலம்புரி வயின்வைத்துத்","திலகம் தைஇய தேங்கமழ் திருநுதல்","மகரப் பகுவாய் தாழமண் ணுறுத்துத்","துவர முடித்த துகளறு முச்சிப்","பெருந்தண் சண்பகஞ் செரீஇக் கருந்தகட்டு","உளைப்பூ மருதின் ஒள்ளிணர் அட்டிக்","கிளைக்கவின்று எழுதரு கீழ்நீர்ச் செவ்வரும்பு","இணைப்புறு பிணையல் வளைஇத் துணைத்தக","வண்காது நிறைந்த பிண்டி ஒண்டளிர்","நுண்பூண் ஆகம் திளைப்பத் திண்காழ்","நறுங்குறடு உரிஞ்சிய பூங்கேழ்த் தேய்வை","தேங்கமழ் மருதிணர் கடுப்பக் கோங்கின்","குவிமுகிழ் இளமுலைக் கொட்டி விரிமலர்","வேங்கை நுண்டாது அப்பிக் காண்வர","வெள்ளிற் குறுமுறி கிள்ளுபு தெறியாக்"],"p":"","r":"On the god's high hill, women with anklets on small red feet, cloth as red as the cochineal insect and girdles strung with coins dress their parted hair with vetchi and kuvalai petals, set a tilakam on the brow, tuck in champak and marutham clusters, hang pindi shoots in their ears, smear their young breasts with sandal paste and vengai pollen, and pluck and toss the tender leaves of the vila tree.","n":"The women are not named until line 41 (சூரர மகளிர், the fearsome maidens of the hill), so this long toilet scene is a description of goddesses of the slope, not of mortal girls; the flowers they wear are of every land, and the poem does not comment on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 12-37","lb":"PP1:12-37","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["கோபத்","கோபம்",15,"pira","simile","cochineal insect"],["நாவலொடு","நாவல்",18,"maram","ornament","jambu gold"],["வெட்சி","வெட்சி",21,"maram","ornament",""],["குவளை","குவளை",22,"maram","ornament",""],["சண்பகஞ்","சண்பகம்",27,"maram","ornament",""],["மருதின்","மருது",28,"maram","ornament",""],["பிண்டி","பிண்டி",31,"maram","ornament","asoka"],["தேய்வை","தேய்வை",33,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["மருதிணர்","மருது இணர்",34,"maram","simile",""],["கோங்கின்","கோங்கு",34,"maram","simile",""],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",36,"maram","ornament",""],["வெள்ளிற்","வெள்ளில்",37,"maram","scene","wood-apple"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:38-66":{"book":"pattupattu","num":3,"t":["'கோழி ஓங்கிய வென்றடு விறற்கொடி","வாழிய பெரிதென்று ஏத்திப் பலருடன்","சீர்திகழ் சிலம்பகம் சிலம்பப் பாடி","சூரர மகளிர் ஆடும் சோலை","மந்தியும் அறியா மரன்பயில் அடுக்கத்துச்","சுரும்பும் மூசாச் சுடர்ப்பூங் காந்தட்","பெருந்தண் கண்ணி மிலைந்த சென்னியன், -","பார்முதிர் பனிக்கடல் கலங்கவுள் புக்குச்","சூர்முதல் தடிந்த சுடரிலை நெடுவேல்","உலறிய கதுப்பின் பிறழ்பற் பேழ்வாய்ச்","சுழல்விழிப் பசுங்கண் சூர்த்த நோக்கின்","கழல்கட் கூகையொடு கடும்பாம்பு தூங்கப்","பெருமுலை அலைக்கும் காதின் பிணர்மோட்டு","உருகெழு செலவின் அஞ்சுவரு பேய்மகள்","குருதி ஆடிய கூருகிர்க் கொடுவிரற்","கண்தொட் டுண்ட கழிமுடைக் கருந்தலை","ஒண்டொடித் தடக்கையின் ஏந்தி வெருவர","வென்றடு விறற்களம் பாடித்தோள் பெயரா","நிணம்தின் வாயள் துணங்கை தூங்க","இருபே ருருவின் ஒருபே ரியாக்கை","அறுவேறு வகையின் அஞ்சுவர மண்டி","அவுணர் நல்வலம் அடங்கக் கவிழிணர்","மாமுதல் தடிந்த மறுவில் கொற்றத்து","எய்யா நல்லிசைச் செவ்வேற் சேஎய்,-","சேவடி படரும் செம்மல் உள்ளமொடு","நலம்புரி கொள்கைப் புலம்புரிந் துறையும்","செலவுநீ நயந்தனை யாயின், பலவுடன்","நன்னர் நெஞ்சத்து இன்னசை வாய்ப்ப","இன்னே பெறுதிநீ முன்னிய வினையே:"],"p":"","r":"The women sing 'long live the victorious cock banner' and dance in a grove on a slope so dense even the female monkey does not know it; the god wears on his head a cool garland of kaanthal that bees do not swarm, and with his long bright spear entered the cold old sea and cut down Sur at the root, while a dread demoness with dry hair and rolling eyes, owl and snake hanging from her ears, held up a skull and danced the thunangai of the battlefield; this red-speared god of flawless victory is the one, the guide says, that you will reach at once if your heart desires to go to his red feet.","n":"The poem's first sentence runs 66 lines before its main verb: the sunrise, the hill, the women and the war on Sur all lead to the promise இன்னே பெறுதி நீ முன்னிய வினையே (66), so the whole description serves as the guide's grounds for that promise. Lines 57 and 58 (இருபே ருருவின் ஒருபே ரியாக்கை / அறுவேறு வகையின்) are hard: whether the great body of two forms is Sur's or the god's, and what the six ways are, I cannot settle from the words alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","thiruvadi","aatruppadai","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 38-66","lb":"PP1:38-66","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கோழி","கோழி",38,"pul","scene","cock (banner)"],["மந்தியும்","மந்தி",42,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["சுரும்பும்","சுரும்பு",43,"pira","scene","bee"],["காந்தட்","காந்தள்",43,"maram","ornament",""],["பனிக்கடல்","கடல்",45,"pira","scene",""],["கூகையொடு","கூகை",49,"pul","ornament","owl"],["கடும்பாம்பு","பாம்பு",49,"pira","ornament","snake"],["மாமுதல்","மா முதல்",60,"maram","scene","mango tree"]],"en":[["patron","சேஎய்",61]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:67-77":{"book":"pattupattu","num":4,"t":["செருப்புகன்று எடுத்த சேணுயர் நெடுங்கொடி","வரிப்புனை பந்தொடு பாவை தூங்கப்","பொருநர்த் தேய்த்த போரரு வாயில்","திருவீற் றிருந்த தீதுதீர் நியமத்து","மாடமலி மறுகின் கூடற் குடவயின்","இருஞ்சேற்று அகல்வயல் விரிந்துவாய் அவிழ்ந்த","முட்டாள் தாமரைத் துஞ்சி வைகறைக்","கட்கமழ் நெய்தல் ஊதி எற்படக்","கண்போல் மலர்ந்த காமர் சுனைமலர்","அஞ்சிறை வண்டின் அரிக்கணம் ஒலிக்கும்","குன்றமர்ந்து உறைதலும் உரியன் ; அதாஅன்று,"],"p":"","r":"He also has a right to dwell on the hill west of Koodal, the city of crowded mansion streets whose gate, hung with tall war banners, ball and doll, has worn down its enemies; there bees sleep in the thorny-stemmed lotus of the wide muddy fields, hum on the honeyed neythal at dawn, and when the sun is up swarm humming round the pool flowers that open like eyes on the hill.","n":"The bees' day, from field lotus to dawn neythal to the hill pool, joins the city's farmland to the god's hill: the shrine is placed by walking the eye from the walled city out to the slope, and the war-scarred gate of Koodal is the first thing the seeker passes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thalam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 67-77","lb":"PP1:67-77","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["தாமரைத்","தாமரை",73,"maram","scene",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",74,"maram","scene","neythal flower"],["சுனைமலர்","சுனை மலர்",75,"maram","simile",""],["வண்டின்","வண்டு",76,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[["town","கூடற்",71]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:78-102":{"book":"pattupattu","num":5,"t":["வைந்நுதி பொருத வடுவாழ் வரிநுதல்","வாடா மாலை ஓடையொடு துயல்வரப்","படுமணி இரட்டும் மருங்கின் கடுநடைக்","கூற்றத்து அன்ன மாற்றரு மொய்ம்பின்","கால்கிளர்ந் தன்ன வேழ மேல்கொண்டு","ஐவேறு உருவின் செய்வினை முற்றிய","முடியொடு விளங்கிய முரண்மிகு திருமணி","மின்உறழ் இமைப்பின் சென்னிப் பொற்ப","நகைதாழ்பு துயல்வரூஉம் வகையமை பொலங்குழை","சேண்விளங்கு இயற்கை வாண்மதி கவைஇ","அகலா மீனின் அவிர்வன இமைப்பத்","தாவில் கொள்கைத் தந்தொழில் முடிமார்","மனனேர்பு எழுதரு வாள்நிற முகனே","மாயிருள் ஞாலம் மறுவின்றி விளங்கப்","பல்கதிர் விரிந்தன்று ஒருமுகம்; ஒருமுகம்,","ஆர்வலர் ஏத்த அமர்ந்தினி தொழுகிக்","காதலின் உவந்து வரங்கொடுத் தன்றே; ஒருமுகம்,","மந்திர விதியின் மரபுளி வழாஅ","அந்தணர் வேள்விஓர்க் கும்மே; ஒருமுகம்,","எஞ்சிய பொருள்களை ஏமுற நாடித்","திங்கள் போலத் திசைவிளக் கும்மே; ஒருமுகம்,","செறுநர்த் தேய்த்துச் செல்சமம் முருக்கிக்","கறுவுகொள் நெஞ்சமொடு களம்வேட் டன்றே; ஒருமுகம்","குறவர் மடமகள் கொடிபோல் நுசுப்பின்","மடவரல் வள்ளியொடு நகையமர்ந் தன்றே."],"p":"","r":"At Alaivay he rides an elephant fast as the wind and irresistible as Death, its scarred, striped brow carrying an unfading garland and frontlet and bells ringing at its sides; gems of five kinds flash like lightning in his crown, gold earrings shine like stars clasping the moon, and each of his six faces does a work: one spreads rays so the dark world shines, one gladly grants boons to those who praise, one watches over the sacrifices of brahmins who keep the mantra rule, one lights the directions like the moon as it searches out the meanings left over, one crushes enemies in battle with a wrathful heart, and one smiles with Valli, the young kuravar girl with a waist like a creeper.","n":"The six faces are a list of offices (light, boon, sacrifice, knowledge, war, love) rather than a portrait; only the last face is turned toward one person, Valli, and she is placed among the kuravar of the hill, not among the gods.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","valli","andhanar","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 78-102","lb":"PP1:78-102","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["வேழ","வேழம்",82,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மின்","மின்",85,"pira","simile","lightning"],["மீனின்","மீன்",88,"pira","simile","star"],["திங்கள்","திங்கள்",98,"pira","simile","moon"],["குறவர்","குறவர்",101,"seythi","scene",""],["கொடி","கொடி",101,"maram","simile","creeper"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:103-125":{"book":"pattupattu","num":6,"t":["ஆங்குஅம் மூவிருமுகனும் முறைநவின்று ஒழுகலின்","ஆரம் தாழ்ந்த அம்பகட்டு மார்பின்","செம்பொறி வாங்கிய மொய்ம்பின் சுடர்விடுபு","வண்புகழ் நிறைந்து வசிந்துவாங்கு நிமிர்தோள்","விண்செலல் மரபின் ஐயர்க்கு ஏந்தியது","ஒருகை; உக்கம் சேர்த்தியது ஒருகை;","நலம்பெறு கலிங்கத்துக் குறங்கின்மிசை அசைஇயதொருகை;","அங்குசங் கடாவ ஒருகை, இருகை","ஐயிரு வட்டமொடு எஃகுவலந் திரிப்ப,","ஒருகை மார்பொடு விளங்க ஒருகை","ஒருகை தாரொடு பொலிய, ஒருகை","கீழ்வீழ் தொடியொடு மீமிசைக்கொட்ப, ஒருகை","பாடின் படுமணி இரட்ட, ஒருகை","நீனிற விசும்பின் மலிதுளி பொழிய, ஒருகை","வானர மகளிர்க்கு வதுவை சூட்ட,","ஆங்குஅப் பன்னிரு கையும் பாற்படஇயற்ற,","அந்தரப் பல்லியம் கறங்கத் திண்காழ்","வயிர்எழுந் திசைப்ப, வால்வளை ஞரல,","உரந்தலைக் கொண்ட உருமிடி முரசமொடு","பல்பொறி மஞ்ஞை வெல்கொடி அகவ,","விசும்பஆ றாக விரைசெலல் முன்னி,","உலகம் புகழ்ந்த ஓங்குயர் விழுச்சீர்","அலைவாய்ச் சேறலும் நிலைஇய பண்பேஅதான்று,"],"p":"","r":"As the six faces keep their order, each of the twelve hands does its part: one raised for the sages who travel the sky, one at the waist, one on the thigh, one driving the goad, two whirling shield and spear, one at the chest, one with the garland, one swinging the bangle aloft, one ringing the bell, one pouring rain from the blue sky, one crowning the heavenly women with the wedding garland; and with the sky's instruments, horns, white conches and thunder-voiced drums sounding and the peacock crying on his victory banner, he goes swiftly by the sky road to Alaivay, which the world praises: dwelling there is his settled nature.","n":"Where the faces were given offices in the world, the hands are given gestures, and two of them (rain, line 116; the wedding garland, line 117) reach out to the sky and its women. The god reaches his sea shrine by the sky road (விசும்பு ஆறாக), not over land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 103-125","lb":"PP1:103-125","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["மலிதுளி","துளி",116,"pira","scene","raindrop"],["பல்லியம்","பல் இயம்",119,"yaazh","scene",""],["வயிர்","வயிர்",120,"yaazh","scene","horn"],["வால்வளை","வளை",120,"yaazh","scene","conch"],["முரசமொடு","முரசம்",121,"parai","scene",""],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",122,"pul","scene","peacock"]],"en":[["town","அலைவாய்",125]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:126-147":{"book":"pattupattu","num":7,"t":["சீரை தைஇய உடுக்கையர் சீரொடு","வலம்புரி புரையும் வால்நரை முடியினர்","மாசுஅற இமைக்கும் உருவினர் மானின்","உரிவை தைஇய ஊன்கெடு மார்பின்","என்புஎழுந்து இயங்கும் யாக்கையர் நன்பகல்","பலவுடன் கழிந்த உண்டியர் இகலொடு","செற்றம் நீக்கிய மனத்தினர் யாவதும்","கற்றோர் அறியா அறிவினர் கற்றோர்க்குத்","தாம்வரம்பு ஆகிய தலைமையர் காமமொடு","கடுஞ்சினம் கடிந்த காட்சியர் இடும்பை","யாவதும் அறியா இயல்பினர் மேவரத்","துனியில் காட்சி முனிவர் முற்புக,","புகைமுகந் தன்ன மாசில் தூவுடை","முகைவாய் அவிழ்ந்த தகைசூழ் ஆகத்துச்","செவிநேர்பு வைத்த செய்வுறு திவவின்","நல்லியாழ் நவின்ற நயனுடை நெஞ்சின்","மென்மொழி மேவலர் இன்னரம்பு உளர,","நோயின்று இயன்ற யாக்கையர் மாவின்","அவிர்தளிர் புரையும் மேனியர் அவிர்தொறும்","பொன்னுரை கடுக்கும் திதலையர் இன்னகைப்","பருமம் தாங்கிய பணிந்தேந்து அல்குல்","மாசில் மகளிரொடு மறுவின்றி விளங்க,"],"p":"","r":"At Avinankudi the sages go first: clothed in bark, hair white as the valampuri conch, bodies shining, deerskin on chests where the flesh has wasted, bones showing, fasting for many days, minds rid of hostility and hatred, knowing what the learned do not and themselves the limit of the learned, having cast out lust and fierce anger and knowing no distress; after them come musicians in spotless clothes like smoke, their gentle hearts trained to the lute, plucking its sweet strings, with flawless women whose bodies glow like mango shoots and whose skin is spotted like gold on a touchstone.","n":"The sages are described almost wholly by what they have removed (flesh, food, hostility, lust, anger, distress), and that list is what gives them the right to walk in first, ahead of the gods of the next lines.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thuravu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 126-147","lb":"PP1:126-147","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["சீரை","சீரை",126,"maram","ornament","bark cloth"],["வலம்புரி","வலம்புரி",127,"pira","simile","right-whorled conch"],["மானின்","மான்",128,"maa","ornament","deer (skin)"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",141,"yaazh","scene",""],["மாவின்","மா",143,"maram","simile","mango"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:148-159":{"book":"pattupattu","num":8,"t":["கடுவொடு ஒடுங்கிய தூம்படை வால்எயிற்று","அழலென உயிர்க்கும் அஞ்சுவரு கடுந்திறல்","பாம்புபடப் புடைக்கும் பல்வரிக் கொடுஞ்சிறைப்","புள்ளணி நீள்கொடிச் செல்வனும், வெள்ளேறு","வலைவயின் உயரிய பலர்புகழ் திணிதோள்","உமையமர்ந்து விளங்கும் இமையா முக்கண்","மூவெயில் முருக்கிய முரண்மிகு செல்வனும்,","நூற்றுப்பத்து அடுக்கிய நாட்டத்து நூறுபல்","வேள்வி முற்றிய வென்றடு கொற்றத்து","ஈரிரண்டு ஏந்திய மருப்பின் எழில்நடைத்","தாழ்பெருந் தடக்கை உயர்த்த யானை","எருத்தம் ஏறிய திருக்கிளர் செல்வனும்,"],"p":"","r":"Then come three lords, each known by banner, mount and deed: the rich one whose long banner bears the many-striped bird that strikes down the snake with venomous fangs and fire-hot breath; the one with the white bull raised on his banner, Uma beside him, three unwinking eyes, who destroyed the three walled cities; and the one with a thousand eyes, who completed a hundred sacrifices, mounted on the neck of the four-tusked elephant.","n":"None of the three gods is named; they are identified by emblem and deed, and all three come as visitors to the younger god's shrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","sivam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 148-159","lb":"PP1:148-159","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["பாம்புபடப்","பாம்பு",150,"pira","scene","snake"],["புள்ளணி","புள்",151,"pul","ornament","bird on the banner"],["வெள்ளேறு","வெள் ஏறு",151,"maa","ornament","white bull"],["யானை","யானை",158,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:160-176":{"book":"pattupattu","num":9,"t":["நாற்பெருந் தெய்வத்து நன்னகர் நிலைஇய","உலகம் காக்கும் ஒன்றுபுரி கொள்கைப்","பலர்புகழ் மூவரும் தலைவ ராக","ஏமுறு ஞாலந் தன்னில் தோன்றித்","தாமரை பயந்த தாவில் ஊழி","நான்முக ஒருவற் சுட்டிக் காண்வர","பகலிற் றோன்றும் இகலில் காட்சி","நால்வேறு இயற்கைப் பதினொரு மூவரொடு","ஒன்பதிற்று இரட்டி உயர்நிலை பெறீஇயர்","மீன்பூத் தன்ன தோன்றலர் மீன்சேர்பு","வளிகிளர்ந் தன்ன செலவினர் வளியிடைத்","தீயெழுந் தன்ன திறலினர் தீப்பட","உரும்இடித் தன்ன குரலினர் விழுமிய","உறுகுறை மருங்கிற்றம் பெறுமுறை கொண்மார்","அந்தரக் கொட்பினர் வந்துடன் காணத்","தாவில் கொள்கை மடந்தையொடு சின்னாள்","ஆவினன்குடி அசைதலும் உரியன்: அதான்று,"],"p":"","r":"With those three famed lords, who guard the world from their good cities, at their head, and with the four-faced one born of the lotus in mind, the thirty-three gods of four kinds and the eighteen companies, bright as stars in bloom, swift as rising wind, strong as fire in wind and loud as thunder, whirl through the sky and come together to see him and win what they need; and with his flawless consort he rests some days at Avinankudi.","n":"Lines 160 to 168 are hard: why the four-faced one is 'pointed to' (நான்முக ஒருவற் சுட்டி) and what high state the gods seek (உயர்நிலை பெறீஇயர்) are not said. One later reading takes it as the gods coming to ask for Brahma's release; the words here say only that they come with a need (உறுகுறை).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thalam","iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 160-176","lb":"PP1:160-176","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",164,"maram","scene","lotus"],["மீன்பூத்","மீன்",169,"pira","simile","star"]],"en":[["town","ஆவினன்குடி",176]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:177-189":{"book":"pattupattu","num":10,"t":["இருமூன்று எய்திய இயல்பினின் வழாஅது","இருவர்ச் சுட்டிய பல்வேறு தொல்குடி","அறுநான்கு இரட்டி இளமை நல்லியாண்டு","ஆறினிற் கழிப்பிய அறன்நவில் கொள்கை","மூன்றுவகைக் குறித்த முத்தீச் செல்வத்து","இருபிறப் பாளர் பொழுதறிந்து நுவல,","ஒன்பது கொண்ட மூன்றுபுரி நுண்ஞாண்","புலராக் காழகம் புலர உடீஇ,","உச்சிக் கூப்பிய கையினர் தற்புகழ்ந்து","ஆறெழுத்து அடக்கிய அருமறைக் கேள்வி","நாஇயல் மருங்கில் நவிலப் பாடி","விரையுறு நறுமலர் ஏந்திப் பெரிதுஉவந்து","ஏரகத்து உறைதலும் உரியன்: அதான்று,"],"p":"","r":"At Eragam, the twice-born of many ancient families, never failing in the six duties, who spent forty-eight years of youth in study, devoted to dharma and rich in the three fires, chant at the proper hours; wearing the fine thread of three strands of nine, their wet cloth drying on them, hands joined above their heads, they praise him, recite with their tongues the rare hidden mantra of six letters, and holding fragrant flowers rejoice; and there he dwells.","n":"Every number in the passage (six duties, 'the two', forty-eight years, three fires, nine threads, six letters) marks the brahmins' order, and the six-letter mantra (ஆறெழுத்து, line 186) is the only place the idyll names a mantra. இருவர்ச் சுட்டிய (178) is obscure to me: it may mean families traced from both parents.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","mandhiram","vazhipaadu","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 177-189","lb":"PP1:177-189","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["முத்தீச்","முத்தீ",181,"pira","scene","three fires"],["இருபிறப்","இருபிறப்பாளர்",182,"seythi","scene","brahmins"],["நறுமலர்","நறு மலர்",188,"maram","gift_food",""]],"en":[["town","ஏரகத்து",189]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:190-205":{"book":"pattupattu","num":11,"t":["பைங்கொடி நறைக்காய் இடையிடுபு வேலன்","அம்பொதிப் புட்டில் விரைஇக் குளவியொடு","வெண்கூ தாளம் தொடுத்த கண்ணியன்","நறுஞ்சாந்து அணிந்த கேழ்கிளர் மார்பின்","கொடுந்தொழில் வல்விற் கொலைஇய கானவர்","நீடமை விளைந்த தேக்கள் தேறல்","குன்றகச் சிறுகுடிக் கிளையுடன் மகிழ்ந்து","தொண்டகச் சிறுபறைக் குரவை அயர","விரலுளர்ப்பு அவிழ்ந்த வேறுபடு நறுங்கால்","குண்டுசுனை பூத்த வண்டுபடு கண்ணி","இணைத்த கோதை அணைத்த கூந்தல்","முடித்த குல்லை இலையுடை நறும்பூச்","செங்கால் மராஅத்த வாலிணர் இடையிடுபு","சுரும்புணத் தொடுத்த பெருந்தண் மாத்தழை","திருந்துகாழ் அல்குல் திளைப்ப உடீஇ","மயில்கண் டன்ன மடநடை மகளிரொடு"],"p":"","r":"On the hills the velan wears a garland of green naRai pods with wild jasmine and white koothaalam, his chest smeared with fragrant sandal; the hunters with their cruel bows, drinking with their kin in the small hill village the toddy matured in long bamboo, dance the kuravai to the small thondagam drum; and the women, their hair bound with flowers from deep pools, kulai leaves and the white clusters of red-stemmed kadamba, wearing skirts of cool leaves strung for the bees, walk with the soft gait of peacocks.","n":"This is the only section of the idyll where the worshippers are the people of the land itself, the velan, the hunters and their women, drinking and dancing, rather than sages, gods or brahmins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","kal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 190-205","lb":"PP1:190-205","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["நறைக்காய்","நறைக் காய்",190,"maram","ornament",""],["வேலன்","வேலன்",190,"seythi","scene","priest of Murugan"],["குளவி","குளவி",191,"maram","ornament","wild jasmine"],["கூதாளம்","கூதாளம்",192,"maram","ornament",""],["நறுஞ்சாந்து","சாந்து",193,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",194,"seythi","scene","hill hunters"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",195,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["தொண்டகச்","தொண்டகப் பறை",197,"parai","scene",""],["குரவை","குரவை",197,"yaazh","scene","round dance"],["குல்லை","குல்லை",201,"maram","ornament",""],["மராஅத்த","மராஅம்",202,"maram","ornament","kadamba"],["மாத்தழை","தழை",203,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["மயில்","மயில்",205,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:206-217":{"book":"pattupattu","num":12,"t":["செய்யன் சிவந்த ஆடையன் செவ்வரைச்","செயலைத் தண்டளிர் துயல்வருங் காதினன்","கச்சினன் கழலினன் செச்சைக் கண்ணியன்","குழலன் கோட்டன் குறும்பல் லியத்தன்","தகரன் மஞ்ஞையன் புகரில் சேவலங்","கொடியன் நெடியன் தொடியணி தோளன்","நரம்பார்த் தன்ன இன்குரல் தொகுதியொடு","குறும்பொறிக் கொண்ட நறுந்தண் சாயல்","மருங்கிற் கட்டிய நிலன்நேர்பு துகிலினன்","முழவுறழ் தடக்கையின் இயல ஏந்தி","மென்றோட் பல்பிணை தழீஇத் தலைத்தந்து","குன்றுதொ றாடலும் நின்றதன்பண்பே; அதான்று,"],"p":"","r":"The god himself is red, in red clothes, with tender seyalai shoots swaying at his ears, wearing waistband, anklets and a garland of red ixora, with flute, horn and many small instruments, the ram and the peacock, and the flawless cock on his banner; tall, with bangled shoulders, among women whose voices are sweet as plucked strings, wearing a fine cloth that reaches the ground, he takes the many soft-shouldered does in his drum-like arms and leads them: dancing on every hill is his settled nature.","n":"The god is described in the same list form as the hill people of the previous lines, with the same red garland, instruments and women: on the hills he is one of the dancers, leading the kuravai, not a figure carried through the sky as at Alaivay.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 206-217","lb":"PP1:206-217","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["செயலை","செயலை",207,"maram","ornament","asoka"],["செச்சை","செச்சை",208,"maram","ornament","ixora"],["குழலன்","குழல்",209,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["கோட்டன்","கோடு",209,"yaazh","scene","horn"],["தகரன்","தகர்",210,"maa","scene","ram"],["மஞ்ஞையன்","மஞ்ஞை",210,"pul","scene","peacock"],["சேவலங்","சேவல்",210,"pul","scene","cock (banner)"],["முழவுறழ்","முழவு",215,"parai","simile",""],["பல்பிணை","பிணை",216,"maa","simile","doe"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:218-226":{"book":"pattupattu","num":13,"t":["சிறுதினை மலரொடு விரைஇ மறியறுத்து","வாரணக் கொடியொடு வயிற்பட நிறீஇ","ஊரூர் கொண்ட சீர்கெழு விழவினும்,","ஆர்வலர் ஏத்த மேவரும் நிலையினும்,","வேலன் தைஇய வெறியயர் களனும்,","காடும் காவும் கவின்பெறு துருத்தியும்,","யாறும் குளனும் வேறுபல் வைப்பும்,","சதுக்கமும் சந்தியும் புதுப்பூங் கடம்பும்,","மன்றமும் பொதியிலும் கந்துடை நிலையினும்"],"p":"","r":"He is found wherever he is worshipped: at the festivals each town holds, where small millet is mixed with flowers, a kid is cut and the cock banner is set up; wherever devotees praise him; at the veri ground the velan prepares; in forests, groves and lovely river islets, at rivers and tanks and many other places, at crossroads and junctions and under fresh-flowered kadamba, in the common ground, the hall and at the sacred post.","n":"The list moves from the town's festival to the open country, the crossroads and a single kadamba tree: the god's places are not only the named shrines but any spot where he is called, which is why the guide will say 'whatever the place may be' (ஆண்டாண்டு ஆயினும் ஆக, line 250).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","veri","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 218-226","lb":"PP1:218-226","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["சிறுதினை","தினை",218,"unavu","gift_food","millet"],["மறியறுத்து","மறி",218,"maa","gift_food","kid (goat)"],["வாரணக்","வாரணம்",219,"pul","scene","cock (banner)"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",222,"seythi","scene","priest of Murugan"],["கடம்பும்","கடம்பு",225,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:227-249":{"book":"pattupattu","num":14,"t":["மாண்டலைக் கொடியொடு மண்ணி அமைவர","நெய்யொடு ஐயவி அப்பி ஐதுரைத்துக்","குடந்தம் பட்டுக் கொழுமலர் சிதறி","முரண்கொள் உருவின் இரண்டுடன் உடீஇச்","செந்நூல் யாத்து வெண்பொரி சிதறி","மதவலி நிலைஇய மாத்தாட் கொழுவிடைக்","குருதியொடு விரைஇய தூவெள் ளரிசி","சில்பலிச் செய்து பல்பிரப்பு இரீஇச்","சிறுபசு மஞ்சளொடு நறுவிரை தெளித்துப்","பெருந்தண் கணவீர நறுந்தண் மாலை","துணையற அறுத்துத் தூங்க நாற்றி","நளிமலைச் சிலம்பின் நன்னகர் வாழ்த்தி","நறும்புகை எடுத்துக் குறிஞ்சி பாடி","இமிழிசை அருவியொடு இன்னியம் கறங்க","உருவப் பல்பூதத் தூஉய் வெருவரக்","குருதிச் செந்தினை பரப்பிக் குறமகள்","முருகியம் நிறுத்து முரணினர் உட்க","முருகாற்றுப் படுத்த உருகெழு வியல்நகர்","ஆடுகளம் சிலம்பப் பாடிப் பலவுடன்","கோடுவாய் வைத்துக் கொடுமணி இயக்கி","ஓடாப் பூட்கைப் பிணிமுகம் வாழ்த்தி","வேண்டுநர் வேண்டியாங்கு எய்தனர் வழிபட","ஆண்டாண்டு உறைதலும் அறிந்த வாறே"],"p":"","r":"There the rite is performed: the place is washed with a fine banner raised, smeared with ghee and white mustard, words murmured and bows made, rich flowers scattered, two clothes of contrasting colour worn, a red thread tied, white puffed rice strewn, pure white rice mixed with the blood of a strong fat ram set out in small offerings in many baskets, turmeric and fragrant water sprinkled, cool oleander garlands cut in pairs and hung, the hill town praised, incense raised and the kurinji tune sung; with waterfall and instruments sounding, flowers scattered so that all fear, and red millet spread with blood, the kuramagal sets up Murugu's music in the dread great house where the god is brought, horns are blown and curved bells rung, the unyielding elephant is praised, and those who seek obtain what they sought; so it is known that he dwells in each place.","n":"முருகாற்றுப் படுத்த (244), 'where Murugu is guided in', uses the same verb as the poem's own title: the priestess guides the god to the worshippers just as the poem guides the worshipper to the god. The rite is presented as effective without qualification (வேண்டுநர் வேண்டியாங்கு எய்தினர், 248).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","veri","aaguthi","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 227-249","lb":"PP1:227-249","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["ஐயவி","ஐயவி",228,"maram","gift_food","white mustard"],["கொழுமலர்","மலர்",229,"maram","gift_food",""],["வெண்பொரி","பொரி",231,"unavu","gift_food","puffed rice"],["கொழுவிடைக்","விடை",232,"maa","gift_food","ram"],["தூவெள் ளரிசி","அரிசி",233,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["மஞ்சளொடு","மஞ்சள்",235,"maram","gift_food","turmeric"],["கணவீர","கணவீரம்",236,"maram","gift_food","oleander"],["அருவியொடு","அருவி",240,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["இன்னியம்","இன் இயம்",240,"yaazh","scene",""],["செந்தினை","தினை",242,"unavu","gift_food","millet"],["குறமகள்","குறமகள்",242,"seythi","scene","priestess of the hill tribe"],["கோடுவாய்","கோடு",246,"yaazh","scene","horn"],["கொடுமணி","மணி",246,"yaazh","scene","bell"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:250-265":{"book":"pattupattu","num":15,"t":["ஆண்டாண்டு ஆயினும் ஆக; காண்தக","முந்துநீ கண்டுழி முகனமர்ந்து ஏத்திக்","கைதொழூஉப் பரவிக் காலுற வணங்கி,","‘நெடும்பெருஞ் சிமையத்து நீலப் பைஞ்சுனை","ஐவருள் ஒருவன் அங்கை ஏற்ப","அறுவர் பயந்த ஆறமர் செல்வ!","ஆல்கெழு கடவுட் புதல்வ! மால்வரை","மலைமகள் மகனே! மாற்றோர் கூற்றே!","வெற்றி வெல்போர்க் கொற்றவை சிறுவ!","இழையணி சிறப்பின் பழையோள் குழவி!","வானோர் வணங்குவில் தானைத் தலைவ!","மாலை மார்ப! நூலறி புலவி!","செருவில் ஒருவ! பொருவிறல் மள்ள!","அந்தணர் வெறுக்கை! அறிந்தோர் சொல்மலை!","மங்கையர் கணவ! மைந்தர் ஏறே!","வேல்கெழு தடக்கைச் சால்பெருஞ் செல்வ!"],"p":"","r":"Whatever the place, when you first see him, praise him with a glad face, worship with joined hands, bow to touch his feet, and say: lord of six dwellings, born of the six, whom one of the five took into his palm at the blue pool on the high peak; son of the god of the banyan; son of the mountain's daughter; Death to enemies; child of victorious Kotravai; infant of the jewelled ancient one; leader of the army of the gods; garlanded chest; poet who knows the texts; unmatched in war; warrior of peerless strength; wealth of the brahmins; mountain of the words of the wise; husband of the women; bull among men; great lord with the spear in your hand.","n":"The guide gives the seeker the very words to say: the poem turns from description to a script of address. The epithets of birth name three mothers in a row (the mountain's daughter, Kotravai, the ancient one), and the six of his birth and the six of his dwelling are set side by side (அறுவர் பயந்த ஆறமர் செல்வ).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirunaamam","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 250-265","lb":"PP1:250-265","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["பைஞ்சுனை","சுனை",253,"pira","scene","mountain pool"],["ஆல்கெழு","ஆல்",256,"maram","scene","banyan"],["கொற்றவை","கொற்றவை",258,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:266-276":{"book":"pattupattu","num":16,"t":["குன்றம் கொன்ற குன்றாக் கொற்றத்து","விண்பொரு நெடுவரைக் குறிஞ்சிக் கிழவ!","பலர்புகழ் நன்மொழிப் புலவர் ஏறே!","அரும்பெறல் மரபின் பெரும்பெயர் முருக!","நசையுநர்க்கு ஆர்த்தும் இசைபே ராள!","அலந்தோர்க்கு அளிக்கும் பொலம்பூண் சேஎய்!","மண்டமர் கடந்தநின் வென்றாடு அகலத்துப்","பரிசிலர்த் தாங்கும் உருகெழு நெடுவேஎள்!","பெரியோர் ஏத்தும் பெரும்பெயர் இயவுள்!","சூர்மருங்கு அறுத்த மொய்ம்பின் மதவலி!","போர்மிகு பொருந! குரிசில்!’ எனப்பல"],"p":"","r":"...of undiminished victory, who killed the mountain; lord of kurinji with its sky-touching peaks; bull among poets of praised good speech; Muruga, of great name and rare lineage; famed one who satisfies those who long; red one of gold ornament who gives to the afflicted; tall Vel who shelters suppliants on your fearsome chest that won great wars; god of great name whom the great praise; mighty one whose strength cut down Sur; warrior great in battle; chief!","n":"The god is praised in the words a bard uses of a patron: one who satisfies those who long (நசையுநர்க்கு ஆர்த்தும்), gives to the afflicted, and shelters suppliants (பரிசிலர்த் தாங்கும்). The puram patron's praise is carried over whole to a god, and he is also made a poet himself (புலவர் ஏறே).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirunaamam","thamizh","parisil","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 266-276","lb":"PP1:266-276","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["முருக","முருகன்",269,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[["patron","முருக",269],["patron","நெடுவேஎள்",273]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:277-295":{"book":"pattupattu","num":17,"t":["யானறி அளவையின் ஏத்தி ஆனாது,","‘நின்னளந் தறிதல் மன்னுயிர்க் கருமையின்","நின்னடி உள்ளி வந்தனென்; நின்னொடு","புரையுநர் இல்லாப் புலமை யோய்!’எனக்","குறித்தது மொழியா அளவையிற் குறித்துடன்","வேறுபல் உருவின் குறும்பல் கூளியர்","சாறுஅயர் களத்து வீறுபெறத் தோன்றி,","‘அளியன் றானே முதுவாய் இரவலன்","வந்தோன் பெரும! நின் வண்புகழ் நயந்தென","இனியவும் நல்லவும் நனிபல ஏத்தித்","தெய்வம் சான்ற திறல்விளங்கு உருவின்","வான்தோய் நிவப்பின் தான்வந் தெய்தி","அணங்குசால் உயர்நிலை தழீஇப் பண்டைத்தன்","மணங்கமழ் தெய்வத்து இளநலங் காட்டி,","‘அஞ்சல் ஓம்புமதி; அறிவல்நின் வரவென","அன்புடன் நன்மொழி அளைஇ விளிவின்று","இருள்நிற முந்நீர் வளைஇய உலகத்து","ஒருநீ யாகித் தோன்ற விழுமிய","பெறலரும் பரிசில் நல்குமதி பலவுடன்"],"p":"","r":"Praising him tirelessly as far as you know, say: 'Since it is hard for living beings to know you to your measure, I have come thinking of your feet, wise one who has no equal'; and before you finish what you meant, his many small attendants of many forms will appear at the festival ground and say, 'Great one, a poor old-mouthed suppliant has come, longing for your generous fame,' praising many sweet and good things; and he, coming in a sky-high form full of divine power, will take his awesome high place, show the young beauty of his fragrant ancient godhead, say 'Fear not; I knew of your coming,' speak kind words lovingly, and give you the rare gift that makes you stand alone in the dark-sea-girt world.","n":"The scene follows the bard's arrival at a king's court step by step: the suppliant (இரவலன்) is announced by attendants, praised to the patron, reassured, and given a gift; only the gift is different, since it makes him unique in the world rather than rich. நின்னளந்து அறிதல் மன்னுயிர்க்கு அருமையின் (278) gives the reason for coming to the feet at all: the god cannot be known, only approached.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","parisil","iravu","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 277-295","lb":"PP1:277-295","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"],["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["கூளியர்","கூளியர்",282,"deyvam","scene","attendant spirits"],["முந்நீர்","முந்நீர்",293,"pira","scene","sea"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:1:296-317":{"book":"pattupattu","num":18,"t":["வேறுபல் துகிலின் நுடங்கி அகில்சுமந்து","ஆர முழுமுதல் உருட்டி வேரற்","பூவுடை அலங்குசினை புலம்பவேர் கீண்டு","விண்பொரு நெடுவரைப் பரிதியிற் றொடுத்த","தண்கமழ் அலரிறால் சிதைய நன்பல","ஆசினி முதுசுளை கலாவ மீமிசை","நாக நறுமலர் உதிர ஊகமொடு","மாமுக முசுக்கலை பனிப்பப் பூநுதல்","இரும்பிடி குளிர்ப்ப வீசிப் பெருங்களிற்று","முத்துடை வான்கோடு தழீஇத் தத்துற்று","நன்பொன் மணிநிறம் கிளரப்பொன் கொழியா","வாழை முழுமுதல் துமியத் தாழை","இளநீர் விழுக்குலை உதிரத் தாக்கிக்","கறிக்கொடிக் கருந்துணர் சாயப் பொறிப்புற","மடநடை மஞ்ஞை பலவுடன் வெரீஇக்","கோழி வயப்பெடை இரியக் கேழலொடு","இரும்பனை வெளிற்றின் புன்சாய் அன்ன","குரூஉமயிர் யாக்கைக் குடாவடி உளியம்","பெருங்கல் விடரளைச் செறியக் கருங்கோட்டு","ஆமா நல்லேறு சிலைப்பச் சேணின்று","இழுமென இழிதரும் அருவிப்","பழமுதிர் சோலை மலைகிழ வோனே"],"p":"","r":"The poem closes on the god's waterfall: swaying like many different cloths, carrying agil, rolling whole sandal trunks, tearing up bamboo with its flowered branches, breaking the cool fragrant honeycombs that hang like the sun's disc from the sky-touching peak, mixing in old jackfruit segments, shedding fragrant naga flowers, chilling langur and monkey, drenching the great she-elephant, sweeping along the pearl-bearing tusk of the great tusker so that gold and gems gleam, felling banana trunks and coconut clusters, bending pepper vines, scattering peacocks and the jungle hen, driving the boar and the bear with fur like palmyra fibre into the rock clefts and making the wild bull bellow, it roars down from afar: he is the lord of the hill of Pazhamudircholai.","n":"The last sentence (296 to 317) is a single torrent through every native thing of the hill, trees, fruit, beasts and birds, and only its last line names the god as lord of the hill: the poem ends by showing the seeker the god's land in motion rather than the god.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thalam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Thirumurugatruppadai 296-317","lb":"PP1:296-317","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"முருகப்பெருமான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["அகில்சுமந்து","அகில்",296,"maram","scene",""],["ஆர","ஆரம்",297,"maram","scene","sandal"],["வேரற்","வேரல்",297,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["அலரிறால்","இறால்",300,"pira","scene","honeycomb"],["ஆசினி","ஆசினி",301,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["நாக","நாகம்",302,"maram","scene","naga tree"],["ஊகமொடு","ஊகம்",302,"maa","scene","monkey"],["முசுக்கலை","முசு",303,"maa","scene","langur"],["இரும்பிடி","பிடி",304,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["பெருங்களிற்று","களிறு",304,"maa","scene","tusker"],["வாழை","வாழை",307,"maram","scene",""],["தாழை","தாழை",307,"maram","scene","coconut palm"],["கறிக்கொடிக்","கறிக் கொடி",309,"maram","scene","pepper vine"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",310,"pul","scene","peacock"],["கோழி","கோழி",311,"pul","scene","jungle fowl"],["கேழலொடு","கேழல்",311,"maa","scene","boar"],["இரும்பனை","பனை",312,"maram","simile","palmyra"],["உளியம்","உளியம்",313,"maa","scene","bear"],["ஆமா","ஆமா",315,"maa","scene","wild cattle"],["அருவிப்","அருவி",316,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[["hill","பழமுதிர் சோலை",317]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:1"},"pp:2:1-22":{"book":"pattupattu","num":19,"t":["அறாஅ யாணரகன் றலைப் பேரூர்ச்","சாறுகழி வழிநாட் சோறுநசை யுறாது","வேறுபுல முன்னிய விரகறி பொருந","குளப்புவழி யன்ன கவடுபடு பத்தல்","விளக்கழ லுருவின் விசியுறு பச்சை","எய்யா விளஞ்சூற் செய்யோ ளவ்வயிற்","றைதுமயி ரொழுகிய தோற்றம் போலப்","பொல்லம் பொத்திய பொதியுறு போர்வை","அளைவா ழலவன் கண்கண் டன்ன","துளைவாய் தூர்ந்த துரப்பமை யாணி","எண்ணாட் டிங்கள் வடிவிற் றாகி","அண்ணா வில்லா அமைவரு வறுவாய்ப்","பாம்பணந் தன்ன வோங்கிரு மருப்பின்","மாயோள் முன்கை ஆய்தொடி கடுக்கும்","கண்கூ டிருக்கைத் திண்பிணித் திவவின்","ஆய்தினை யரிசி யவைய லன்ன","வேய்வை போகிய விரலுளர் நரம்பின்","கேள்வி போகிய நீள்விசித் தொடையல்","மணங்கமழ் மாதரை மண்ணி யன்ன","அணங்குமெய்ந் நின்ற அமைவரு காட்சி","ஆறலை கள்வர் படைவிட அருளின்","மாறுதலை பெயர்க்கு மருவுஇன் பாலை"],"p":"","r":"The guide hails a skilled porunan who, once the festival of a great town of unfailing plenty is over, cares nothing for its leftover food and sets out for another land, and describes his lute part by part: a bowl forked like a hoofprint, a covering skin the colour of lamp flame stitched with a seam like the line of down on a young pregnant woman's belly, pegs like the eyes of a crab in its hole, a mouth shaped like the eighth-day moon, a stem rising like a snake's raised hood, bands like the bangles on a dark woman's forearm, strings fine as pounded millet, the whole like a bathed and fragrant bride, a paalai lute so sweet that it makes the robbers who waylay travellers drop their weapons and turn back.","n":"Almost every part of the lute is likened to a woman's body or ornament (the down on a pregnant woman's belly, a dark woman's bangles, a bathed bride), so the instrument is already a body before the singing woman of the next unit is described. The power of the paalai tune over robbers (lines 21 and 22) is the first proof that this bard's art protects him on the road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","azhagu","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 1-22","lb":"PP2:1-22","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["பொருந","பொருநன்",3,"seythi","scene","bard"],["ழலவன்","அலவன்",9,"pira","simile","crab"],["டிங்கள்","திங்கள்",11,"pira","simile","moon"],["பாம்பணந்","பாம்பு",13,"pira","simile","snake"],["தினை","தினை",16,"unavu","simile","millet"],["கள்வர்","கள்வர்",21,"seythi","scene","highway robbers"],["பாலை","பாலை யாழ்",22,"yaazh","scene","paalai lute"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:2:23-47":{"book":"pattupattu","num":20,"t":["வாரியும் வடித்தும் உந்தியு முறழ்ந்தும்","சீருடை நன்மொழி நீரொடு சிதறி","அறல்போற் கூந்தல் பிறைபோல் திருநுதற்","கொலைவிற் புருவத்துக் கொழுங்கடை மழைக்கண்","இலவிதழ் புரையும் இன்மொழித் துவர்வாய்ப்","பலஉறு முத்திற் பழிதீர் வெண்பல்","மயிர்குறை கருவி மாண்கடை யன்ன","பூங்குழை ஊசற் பொறைசால் காதின்","நாண்அடச் சாய்ந்த நலங்கிள ரெருத்தின்","ஆடமைப் பணைத்தோ ளரிமயிர் முன்கை","நெடுவரை மிசைஇய காந்தள் மெல்விரற்","கிளிவா யப்பி னொளிவிடு வள்ளுகிர்","அணங்கென உருத்த சுணங்கணி யாகத்","தீர்க்கிடை போகா ஏரிள வனமுலை","நீர்ப்பெயற் சுழியி னிறைந்த கொப்பூழ்","உண்டென வுணரா உயவும் நடுவின்","வண்டிருப் பன்ன பல்காழ் அல்குல்","இரும்பிடித் தடக்கையிற் செறிந்துதிரள் குறங்கின்","பொருந்துமயி ரொழுகிய திருந்துதாட் கொப்ப","வருந்துநாய் நாவிற் பெருந்தகு சீறடி","அரக்குருக் கன்ன செந்நில னொதுங்கலிற்","பரற்பகை யுழந்த நோயொடு சிவணி","மரற்பழுத் தன்ன மறுகுநீர் மொக்குள்","நன்பக லந்தி நடையிடை விலங்கலிற்","பெடைமயி லுருவிற் பெருந்தகு பாடினி"],"p":"","r":"Playing the lute in all its ways and scattering fine words with the music, the bard is accompanied by his paadini, described from head to foot: hair like rippled sand, brow like the crescent, brows like a killing bow, mouth red as the silk-cotton petal, teeth like pearls, ears hung with swinging earrings, neck bent with modesty, shoulders like swaying bamboo, fingers like the kaanthal of the high hills, nails like a parrot's beak, breasts so close a split rib cannot pass between, navel like an eddy, a waist one hardly knows is there, a girdle of strands like rows of bees, thighs like a she-elephant's trunk, and small feet like a panting dog's tongue, blistered like ripe marul fruit from walking on ground red as melted lac, so that at midday she stops on the road, like a peahen.","n":"The head-to-foot description ends not on beauty but on injury: the feet like a panting dog's tongue are soft because they are hurt, and the blisters are the road's mark on her. The ideal body and the poverty of the journey are drawn in one sentence, which is the guide poem's whole case for going to the patron.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","aatruppadai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 23-47","lb":"PP2:23-47","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["அறல்","அறல்",25,"pira","simile","rippled sand"],["இலவிதழ்","இலவு",27,"maram","simile","silk-cotton"],["முத்திற்","முத்து",28,"pira","simile","pearl"],["ஆடமைப்","அமை",32,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",33,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["கிளிவா","கிளி",34,"pul","simile","parrot"],["வண்டிருப்","வண்டு",39,"pira","simile","bee"],["இரும்பிடித்","பிடி",40,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["நாய்","நாய்",42,"maa","simile","dog"],["மரற்","மரல்",45,"maram","simile","marul"],["பெடைமயி","பெடை மயில்",47,"pul","simile","peahen"],["பாடினி","பாடினி",47,"seythi","scene","singing woman"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:2:48-59":{"book":"pattupattu","num":21,"t":["பாடின பாணிக் கேற்ப நாடொறும்","களிறு வழங்கதர்க் கானத் தல்கி","இலைஇல் மராஅத்த எவ்வந் தாங்கி","வலைவலந் தன்ன மென்னிழன் மருங்கிற்","காடுறை கடவுட்கடன் கழிப்பிய பின்றைப்","பீடுகெழு திருவிற் பெரும்பெயர் நோன்றாள்","முரசுமுழங்கு தானை மூவருங் கூடி","அரசவை யிருந்த தோற்றம் போலப்","பாடல் பற்றிய பயனுடை எழாஅற்","கோடியர் தலைவ கொண்ட தறிந","அறியா மையி னெறிதிரிந் தொராஅ","தாற்றெதிர்ப் படுதலு நோற்றதன் பயனே"],"p":"","r":"Singing to the beat day after day, halting in forest tracks where tuskers roam, bearing the hardship of thin, net-like shade under the leafless maraa, and making the due offering to the god who dwells in the forest, the leader of the performers with his lute, which looks like the three drum-thundering kings seated together in council, is told that having strayed from the way he did not know, meeting the guide on the road is the fruit of his past penance.","n":"What the simile of the three kings in council (lines 53 to 55) describes is not fixed by the words: it may be the lute, held up as a court in itself, or the gathered company of performers. 'நோற்றதன் பயனே' (line 59) makes the chance meeting a reward for past austerity, the first of the poem's comparisons between the bard's luck and the fruit of tapas (see lines 91 and 92).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","thavam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 48-59","lb":"PP2:48-59","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",49,"maa","scene","tusker"],["மராஅத்த","மராஅம்",50,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["கடவுட்கடன்","கடவுள்",52,"deyvam","scene","forest god"],["முரசுமுழங்கு","முரசு",54,"parai","simile","war drum"],["எழாஅற்","எழால்",56,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",57,"seythi","scene","performers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:2:60-72":{"book":"pattupattu","num":22,"t":["போற்றிக் கேண்மதி புகழ்மேம் படுந","ஆடுபசி யுழந்தநின் இரும்பே ரொக்கலொடு","நீடுபசி யொராஅல் வேண்டி னீடின்","றெழுமதி வாழி ஏழின் கிழவ","பழுமர முள்ளிய பறவையின் யானுமவன்","இழுமென் சும்மை யிடனுடை வரைப்பின்","நசையுநர்த் தடையா நன்பெரு வாயில்","இசையேன் புக்கென் இடும்பை தீர","எய்த்த மெய்யே னெய்யே னாகிப்","பைத்த பாம்பின் துத்தி யேய்ப்பக்","கைக்கச டிருந்தவென் கண்ணகன் தடாரி","இருசீர்ப் பாணிக் கேற்ப விரிகதிர்","வெள்ளி முளைத்த நள்ளிருள் விடியல்"],"p":"","r":"Listen well, the guide says, you who excel in fame: if you want to end the long hunger of your large kin, worn by wasting hunger, rise without delay, master of the seven strings; for I too, like a bird that thinks of a fruiting tree, entered unannounced the wide humming palace whose great gate stops no one who comes in need, weary in body, to end my distress, and beat my wide drum, its face stained by the hand like the spots on a cobra's spread hood, to a double beat in the depth of night as Venus rose.","n":"The open gate 'that does not bar those who come in need' (line 66) is what makes the fruit-tree simile work: a fruiting tree in the open is free to every bird. The guide's argument is his own story, told in the first person, so the promise rests on testimony rather than on the patron's reputation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","aatruppadai","inmai","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 60-72","lb":"PP2:60-72","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["பறவையின்","பறவை",64,"pul","simile","bird"],["பழுமர","பழு மரம்",64,"maram","simile","fruiting tree"],["பாம்பின்","பாம்பு",69,"pira","simile","cobra"],["தடாரி","தடாரி",70,"parai","scene","small drum"],["வெள்ளி","வெள்ளி",72,"pira","scene","Venus"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:2:73-95":{"book":"pattupattu","num":23,"t":["ஓன்றியான் பெட்டா அளவையி னொன்றிய","கேளிர் போலக் கேள்கொளல் வேண்டி","வேளாண் வாயில் வேட்பக் கூறிக்","கண்ணிற் காண நண்ணுவழி இரீஇப்","பருகு அன்ன அருகா நோக்கமொடு","உருகு பவைபோ லென்பு குளிர்கொளீஇ","ஈரும் பேனும் இருந்திறை கூடி","வேரொடு நனைந்து வேற்றிழை நுழைந்த","துன்னற் சிதாஅர் துவர நீக்கி","நோக்குநுழை கல்லா நுண்மைய பூக்கனிந்து","அரவுரி யன்ன அறுவை நல்கி","மழையென மருளும் மகிழ்செய் மாடத்து","இழையணி வனப்பி னின்னகை மகளிர்","போக்கில் பொலங்கல நிறையப் பல்கால்","வாக்குபு தரத்தர வருத்தம் வீட","ஆர வுண்டு பேரஞர் போக்கிச்","செருக்கொடு நின்ற காலை மற்றவன்","திருக்கிளர் கோயி லொருசிறைத் தங்கித்","தவஞ்செய் மாக்கள் தம்முடம் பிடாஅ","ததன்பய மெய்திய வளவை மான","ஆறுசெல் வருத்தம் அகல நீக்கி","அனந்தர் நடுக்க மல்ல தியாவதும்","மனங்கவல் பின்றி மாழாந் தெழுந்து"],"p":"","r":"Before I could say what I wanted, the patron, wishing to take me as kin, spoke words of welcome, seated me close where his eyes could reach me, looked at me without tiring as if drinking me in, so that my bones cooled as if melting, had my rags, sweat-soaked, patched with other threads and full of lice and nits, stripped off and gave me cloth fine as a snake's slough that the eye cannot see through, and had jewelled, smiling women pour drink again and again into golden cups in his cloud-high mansion until my weariness ended; so I stayed in a corner of his palace like ascetics who gain the fruit of their penance without leaving the body, and rose free of every care except the drowsy tremor of drink.","n":"The patron gives before he is asked (line 73), and the comparison in lines 91 and 92 makes the bard's ease the reward that ascetics get only after death, here received in the living body. The removal of the patched rags is the first act, before food: the bard is re-dressed as a guest of rank.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","eegai","parisil","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 73-95","lb":"PP2:73-95","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["ஈரும்","ஈர்",79,"pira","scene","nits"],["பேனும்","பேன்",79,"pira","scene","lice"],["அரவுரி","அரவு",83,"pira","simile","snake"],["மழையென","மழை",84,"pira","simile","rain cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:2:96-118":{"book":"pattupattu","num":24,"t":["மாலை யன்னதோர் புன்மையுங் காலைக்","கண்டோட் மருளும் வண்டுசூழ் நிலையும்","கனவென மருண்டவென் னெஞ்சே மாப்ப","வல்லஞர் பொத்திய மனம்மகிழ் சிறப்பக்","கல்லா இளைஞர் சொல்லிக் காட்டக்","கதுமெனக் கரைந்து வம்மெனக் கூஉய்","அதன்முறை கழிப்பிய பின்றைப் பதனறிந்து","துராஅய் துற்றிய துருவையம் புழுக்கின்","பராஅரை வேவை பருகெனத் தண்டிக்","காழிற் சுட்ட கோழூன் கொழுங்குறை","ஊழின் ஊழின் வாய்வெய் தொற்றி","அவையவை முனிகுவ மெனினே சுவைய","வேறுபல் லுருவின் விரகுதந் திரீஇ","மண்ணமை முழவின் பண்ணமை சீறியாழ்","ஒண்ணுதல் விறலியர் பாணி தூங்க","மகிழ்ப்பதம் பன்னாட் கழிப்பி யொருநாள்","அவிழ்ப்பதங் கொள்கென் றிரப்ப முகிழ்த்தகை","முரவை போகிய முரியா அரிசி","விரலென நிமிர்ந்த நிரலமை புழுக்கல்","பரல்வறைக் கருனை காடியின் மிதப்ப","அயின்ற காலைப் பயின்றினி திருந்து","கொல்லை உழுகொழு ஏய்ப்பப் பல்லே","எல்லையு மிரவும் ஊன்றின்று மழுங்கி"],"p":"","r":"Waking to find his former misery gone like dusk and his state now one of bee-ringed garlands that amazes all who see it, the bard wonders whether it is a dream, until untaught young servants tell him; the patron calls him at once, and after the rites serves boiled meat of sheep fed on grass and fat roasted on spits, urging him to eat, then many kinds of sweets when he tires of meat, with drum and small lute and bright-browed dancing women keeping time; after many days of drinking he begs him to take rice, unbroken, husked, the grains straight as fingers, with fried relish floating in sour gruel, until the guests' teeth, blunted by meat day and night, are like the ploughshare that has ploughed a dry field.","n":"The comedy of lines 117 and 118 answers the hunger of line 61: the poor bard's teeth wear down from too much meat, like a share on dry ground. Rice is begged on him (இரப்ப, line 112), so the host now does the asking that the bard did before. Line 96's 'misery like dusk' is uncertain in its construction, and line 97 prints கண்டோட், which reads best as கண்டோர்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","undi","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 96-118","lb":"PP2:96-118","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["வண்டுசூழ்","வண்டு",97,"pira","scene","bee"],["துராஅய்","துராய்",103,"maram","scene","grass"],["துருவையம்","துருவை",103,"maa","gift_food","sheep"],["கோழூன்","கொழு ஊன்",105,"unavu","gift_food","fat meat"],["முழவின்","முழவு",109,"parai","scene","drum"],["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",109,"yaazh","scene","small lute"],["விறலியர்","விறலி",110,"seythi","scene","dancing women"],["மகிழ்ப்பதம்","மகிழ்",111,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["அரிசி","அரிசி",113,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["புழுக்கல்","புழுக்கல்",114,"unavu","gift_food","boiled rice"],["கருனை","கருனை",115,"unavu","gift_food","fried relish"],["காடியின்","காடி",115,"unavu","gift_food","sour gruel"],["ஊன்றின்று","ஊன்",118,"unavu","gift_food","meat"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:2:119-129":{"book":"pattupattu","num":25,"t":["உயிர்ப்பிடம் பெறாஅ தூண்முனிந் தொருநாள்","செயிர்த்தெழு தெவ்வர் திறைதுறை போகிய","செல்வ சேறுமெந் தொல்பதிர் பெயர்ந்தென","மெல்லெனக் கிளந்தன மாக வல்லே","அகறி ரொவெம் ஆயம் விட்டென","சிரறிய வன்போற் செயிர்த்த நோக்கமொடு","துடியடி யன்ன தூங்குநடைக் குழவியொடு","பிடிபுணர் வேழம் பெட்டவை கொள்கெனத்","தன்னறி யளவையின் தரத்தர யானும்","என்னறி யளவையின் வேண்டுவ முகந்துகொண்டு","இன்மை தீர வந்தனென் வென்வேல்"],"p":"","r":"Sated with meat and with no room left to breathe, one day the bard softly tells the lord to whom angry enemies bring tribute that he will go back to his old town; the lord, looking at him angrily like one wronged, asks 'will you leave our company?', and then, saying 'take what you like', gives elephants with their mates and calves whose slow gait is like the beat of the thudi drum, giving and giving by the measure he knows, while the bard takes what he needs by the measure he knows and comes away with his poverty ended.","n":"The king's anger at being left is the form his affection takes. Lines 127 and 128 set two measures side by side, தன் அறி அளவையின் and என் அறி அளவையின்: the giver gives to the limit of what he knows to give, and the taker takes to the limit of what he knows to need, so that the gift is bounded by the receiver's restraint as much as by the giver's wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","inmai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 119-129","lb":"PP2:119-129","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["துடியடி","துடி",125,"parai","simile","thudi drum"],["குழவியொடு","குழவி",125,"maa","gift_food","elephant calf"],["பிடிபுணர்","பிடி",126,"maa","gift_food","she-elephant"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",126,"maa","gift_food","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:2:130-148":{"book":"pattupattu","num":26,"t":["உருவப் பஃறேர் இளையோன் சிறுவன்","முருகற் சீற்றத் துருகெழு குருசில்","தாய்வயிற் றிருந்து தாய மெய்தி","எய்யாத் தெவ்வர் ஏவல் கேட்பச்","செய்யார் தேஎம் தெருமரல் கலிப்பப்","பவ்வ மீமிசைப் பகற்கதிர் பரப்பி","வெல்வெஞ் செல்வன் விசும்புபடர்ந் தாங்குப்","பிறந்துதவழ் கற்றதற் றொட்டுச் சிறந்தநன்","னாடுசெகிற் கொண்டு நாடொறும் வளர்ப்ப","ஆளி நன்மான் அணங்குடைக் குருளை","மீளி மொய்ம்பின் மிகுவலி செருக்கி","முலைக்கோள் விடாஅ மாத்திரை ஞெரேரெனத்","தலைக்கோள் வேட்டங் களிறட் டாஅங்கு","இரும்பனம் போந்தைத் தோடுங் கருஞ்சினை","அரவாய் வேம்பின் அங்குழைத் தெரியலும்","ஒங்கிருஞ் சென்னி மேம்பட மிலைந்த","இருபெரு வேந்தரு மொருகளத் தவிய","வெண்ணித் தாக்கிய வெருவரு நோன்றாட்","கண்ணார் கண்ணிக் கரிகால் வளவன்"],"p":"","r":"The patron is the son of Ilaiyon of many fine chariots, a lord fierce with Murugan's anger, who received his inheritance while still in the womb, made unknowing foes obey, threw enemy lands into turmoil, and grew from the day he learned to crawl, bearing his land on his shoulder, like the sun that spreads its rays over the sea and climbs the sky; like the fierce cub of the yaali, which before it leaves the breast kills a tusker in its first hunt, Karikal Valavan with his eye-filling chaplet struck at Venni so that the two great kings, who wore the palmyra frond and the neem's tender leaf on their heads, fell together on one field.","n":"The whole stretch answers one charge, youth: the king inherited in the womb, ruled from the crawling age, and won at Venni like a cub still at the breast. The two defeated kings are named only by their flowers, the Chera palmyra and the Pandya neem, so the victory is told as heraldry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","ilamai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 130-148","lb":"PP2:130-148","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["முருகற்","முருகன்",131,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"],["ஆளி","ஆளி",139,"maa","simile","yaali"],["களிறட்","களிறு",142,"maa","simile","tusker"],["போந்தைத்","போந்தை",143,"maram","ornament","palmyra"],["வேம்பின்","வேம்பு",144,"maram","ornament","neem"]],"en":[["king","இளையோன்",130],["battle","வெண்ணி",147],["king","கரிகால் வளவன்",148]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:2:149-162":{"book":"pattupattu","num":27,"t":["தாணிழல் மருங்கி னணுகுபு குறுகித்","தொழுதுமுன் னிற்குவி ராயிற் பழுதின்","றீற்றா விருப்பிற் போற்றுபு நோக்கிநும்","கையது கேளா அளவை ஒய்யெனப்","பாசி வேரின் மாசொடு குறைந்த","துன்னற் சிதாஅர் நீக்கித் தூய","கொட்டைக் கரைய பட்டுடை நல்கிப்","பெறலருங் கலத்திற் பெட்டாங் குண்கெனப்","பூக்கமழ் தேறல் வாக்குபு தரத்தர","வைகல் வைகல் கைகவி பருகி","எரியகைந் தன்ன வேடில் தாமரை","சுரியிரும் பித்தை பொலியச் சூட்டி","நூலின் வலவா நுணங்கரில் மாலை","வாலொளி முத்தமொடு பாடினி யணியக்"],"p":"","r":"If you go near the shade of his feet and stand before him in worship, he will look on you with the unfailing love of a cow that has calved and, before you even ask, strip off your stitched rags, worn and dirty like the roots of moss, and give you silk cloth with knotted borders, pour flower-scented toddy into precious cups day after day until you cover the cup with your hand, set on your curly hair a lotus of gold, petalless, bright as flame, and adorn your paadini with a fine chain that no thread strings and with bright pearls.","n":"The promised gifts repeat in order what the guide himself received (lines 79 to 88: the rags removed, cloth given, drink poured), so the guide poem works by turning one man's experience into a forecast. The lotus is 'without petals' (ஏடில்) because it is gold: a flower that will not fade.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","eegai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 149-162","lb":"PP2:149-162","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["றீற்றா","ஈற்று ஆ",151,"maa","scene","cow that has calved"],["பாசி","பாசி",153,"pira","simile","moss"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",157,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",159,"maram","ornament","golden lotus"],["முத்தமொடு","முத்தம்",162,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["பாடினி","பாடினி",162,"seythi","scene","singing woman"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:2:163-177":{"book":"pattupattu","num":28,"t":["கோட்டிற் செய்த கொடுஞ்சி நெடுந்தேர்","ஊட்டுளை துயல்வர வோரி நுடங்கப்","பால்புரை புரவி நால்குடன் பூட்டிக்","காலி னேழடிப் பின்சென்று கோலின்","தாறுகளைந் தேறென் றேற்றி வீறுபெறு","பேரியாழ் முறையுழிக் கழிப்பி நீர்வாய்த்","தண்பணை தழீஇய தளரா விருக்கை","நன்பல் லூர நாட்டொடு நன்பல்","வெரூஉப்பறை நுவலும் பரூஉப்பெருந் தடக்கை","வெருவரு செலவின் வெகுளி வேழம்","தரவிடைத் தங்கலோ விலனே வரவிடைப்","பெற்றவை பிறர்பிறர்க் கார்த்தித் தெற்றெனச்","செலவுகடைக் கூட்டுதி ராயிற் பலபுலந்து","நில்லா வுலகத்து நிலைமை தூக்கிச்","செல்கென விடுக்குவ னல்ல நொல்லெனத்"],"p":"","r":"He will yoke four milk-white horses with swaying dyed plumes to a tall chariot whose rail is of ivory, walk seven steps behind it on foot, take away the goad and bid you mount, send you off in due order with your great lute, and give you a land of many good villages in cool fields and many fierce elephants at whom the warning drum is beaten; he does not stop giving, and if, feeding others with what you received on the way, you hurry to leave, he will grieve, and weighing the nature of this unstable world, will not be the one to send you off saying 'go'.","n":"Walking seven steps behind the departing guest's chariot is the king's humility in giving. The syntax of lines 173 to 177 is not certain: the participle ஆர்த்தி most naturally has the bard as subject (you feed others with what you got), and பல புலந்து and நிலைமை தூக்கி may belong to the king, who will not dismiss a guest because he knows how little in the world stays.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["eegai","parisil","nilaiyaamai","anbu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 163-177","lb":"PP2:163-177","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["புரவி","புரவி",165,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["பேரியாழ்","பேரியாழ்",168,"yaazh","scene","great lute"],["தண்பணை","பணை",169,"pira","scene","fields"],["வெரூஉப்பறை","பறை",171,"parai","scene","warning drum"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",172,"maa","gift_food","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:2:178-188":{"book":"pattupattu","num":29,"t":["திரை பிறழிய விரும் பெளவத்துக்","கரை சூழ்ந்த அகன் கிடக்கை","மா மாவின் வயின் வயினெற்","றாழ் தாழைத் தண் டண்டலைக்","கூடு கெழீஇய குடி வயினாற்","செஞ் சோற்ற பலி மாந்திய","கருங் காக்கை கவவு முனையின்","மனை நொச்சி நிழலாங் கண்","ஈற்றி யாமைதன் பார்ப்பு ஓம்பவும்","இளையோர் வண்ட லயரவும் முதியோர்","அவைபுகு பொழுதிற்றம் பகைமுரண் சொலவும்"],"p":"","r":"His land lies wide within the shore of the great wave-tossed sea, with dark mango trees, paddy here and there, low-hanging coconut palms and cool groves; in homes with granaries the black crow, when it tires of the red rice offerings it has eaten, leaves the tortoise that has laid its eggs to guard its young in the shade of the house's nochchi, the young play at building sand houses, and the old, entering the assembly, speak their quarrels there.","n":"The crow is sated by the offerings (கவவு முனையின்), so the nesting tortoise is safe: in this land even the predator is fed. The quarrels of the old are carried to the assembly, not settled by force, which is the only sign of conflict in the picture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","kudi","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 178-188","lb":"PP2:178-188","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["பெளவத்துக்","பௌவம்",178,"pira","scene","sea"],["மாவின்","மா",180,"maram","scene","mango"],["வயினெற்","நெல்",180,"unavu","scene","paddy"],["தாழைத்","தாழை",181,"maram","scene","coconut palm"],["சோற்ற","சோறு",183,"unavu","gift_food","rice offering"],["காக்கை","காக்கை",184,"pul","scene","crow"],["நொச்சி","நொச்சி",185,"maram","scene","nochchi"],["யாமை","யாமை",186,"pira","scene","tortoise"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:2:189-207":{"book":"pattupattu","num":30,"t":["முடக் காஞ்சிச் செம் மருதின்","மடக் கண்ண மயில் ஆலப்","பைம் பாகற் பழந் துணரிய","செஞ் சுளைய கனி மாந்தி","அறைக் கரும்பி னரி நெல்லின்","இனக் களமர் இசை பெருக","வற ளடும்பி னிவர் பகன்றைத்","தளிர்ப் புன்கின் றாழ் காவின்","நனை ஞாழலொடு மரங் குழீஇய","அவண் முனையி னகன்று மாறி","அவிழ் தளவி னகன் தோன்றி","நகு முல்லை யுகுதேறு வீப்","பொற் கொன்றை மணிக் காயா","நற் புறவி னடை முனையிற்","சுற வழங்கும் இரும் பெளவத்","திற வருந்திய இன நாரை","பூம் புன்னைச் சினைச் சேப்பின்","ஓங்கு திரை யொலிவெரீஇத்","தீம் பெண்ணை மடற் சேப்பவும்"],"p":"","r":"Gentle-eyed peacocks dance on crooked kaanji and red marutham trees, feed on the clustered fruit of the green bitter gourd and red-segmented fruit amid the noise of labourers at the cut sugarcane and the reaped paddy, and, when they tire of that place with its beach creepers, pakanrai, budding punku, low groves and njaazhal, move to the good woodland with its thalavam, thondri, smiling mullai, golden konrai and sapphire kaayaa; and when that walk too wearies them, the herons that have fed on prawns of the shark-haunted sea, frightened from the flowering punnai by the roar of high waves, rest on the fronds of the sweet palmyra.","n":"The creatures move between the landscapes not from want but from surfeit: the repeated முனையின் (198, 202, and 184 before it) makes boredom with plenty the motive for crossing from field to woodland to shore, the reverse of the hungry bard's crossing that opened the poem. Who moves in lines 198 to 202, the peacocks or the field folk, is not fixed by the syntax.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 189-207","lb":"PP2:189-207","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["காஞ்சிச்","காஞ்சி",189,"maram","scene","kaanji tree"],["மருதின்","மருது",189,"maram","scene","marutham tree"],["மயில்","மயில்",190,"pul","scene","peacock"],["பாகற்","பாகல்",191,"maram","scene","bitter gourd"],["கரும்பி","கரும்பு",193,"maram","scene","sugarcane"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",193,"unavu","scene","paddy"],["களமர்","களமர்",194,"seythi","scene","field labourers"],["ளடும்பி","அடும்பு",195,"maram","scene","beach creeper"],["பகன்றைத்","பகன்றை",195,"maram","scene","pakanrai"],["புன்கின்","புன்கு",196,"maram","scene","punku"],["ஞாழலொடு","ஞாழல்",197,"maram","scene","njaazhal"],["தளவி","தளவு",199,"maram","scene","thalavam jasmine"],["தோன்றி","தோன்றி",199,"maram","scene","thondri"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",200,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",201,"maram","scene","konrai"],["காயா","காயா",201,"maram","scene","kaayaa"],["சுற","சுறவு",203,"pira","scene","shark"],["நாரை","நாரை",204,"pul","scene","heron"],["புன்னைச்","புன்னை",205,"maram","scene","punnai"],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",207,"maram","scene","palmyra"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:2:208-217":{"book":"pattupattu","num":31,"t":["கோட் டெங்கின் குலை வாழைக்","கொழுங் காந்தண் மலர் நாகத்துத்","துடிக் குடிஞைக் குடிப் பாக்கத்துக்","யாழ் வண்டின் கொளைக் கேற்பக்","கலவம் விரித்த மட மஞ்ஞை","நில வெக்கர்ப் பல பெயரத்","தேனெய் யொடு கிழங்கு மாறியோர்","மீனெய் யொடு நறவு மறுகவும்","தீங் கரும்போ டவல் வகுத்தோர்","மான் குறையொடு மது மறுகவும்"],"p":"","r":"Where tall coconuts, banana bunches, lush kaanthal and flowering naagam trees grow, in hamlets where the owl calls like a thudi drum, the gentle peacock spreads its plumes to the tune of bees humming like a lute and wanders over sand dunes white as moonlight; those who bartered honey and tubers go home with fish oil and toddy, and those who gave sweet sugarcane and flattened rice go home with venison and liquor.","n":"The barter of lines 214 to 217 pairs the hill's honey and tubers with the shore's fish oil, and the field's cane and flattened rice with the forest's venison: the five lands are joined by trade as well as by the wandering creatures of the previous unit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 208-217","lb":"PP2:208-217","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["டெங்கின்","தெங்கு",208,"maram","scene","coconut"],["வாழைக்","வாழை",208,"maram","scene","banana"],["காந்தண்","காந்தள்",209,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["நாகத்துத்","நாகம்",209,"maram","scene","naagam tree"],["குடிஞைக்","குடிஞை",210,"pul","scene","owl"],["துடிக்","துடி",210,"parai","scene","thudi drum"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",211,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["வண்டின்","வண்டு",211,"pira","scene","bee"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",212,"pul","scene","peacock"],["தேனெய்","தேன்",214,"unavu","gift_food","honey"],["கிழங்கு","கிழங்கு",214,"unavu","gift_food","tubers"],["மீனெய்","மீன் நெய்",215,"unavu","gift_food","fish oil"],["நறவு","நறவு",215,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["கரும்போ","கரும்பு",216,"unavu","gift_food","sugarcane"],["டவல்","அவல்",216,"unavu","gift_food","flattened rice"],["மான்","மான் குறை",217,"unavu","gift_food","venison"],["மது","மது",217,"unavu","gift_food","liquor"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:2:218-231":{"book":"pattupattu","num":32,"t":["குறிஞ்சி பரதவர் பாட நெய்தல்","நறும்பூங் கண்ணி குறவர் சூடக்","கானவர் மருதம் பாட அகவர்","நீனிற முல்லைப் பஃறிணை நுவலக்","கானக் கோழி கதிர் குத்த","மனைக் கோழி தினைக் கவர","வரை மந்தி கழி மூழ்க","கழி நாரை வரை யிறுப்பத்","தண் வைப்பினா னாடு குழீஇ","மண் மருங்கினான் மறு வின்றி","ஒரு குடையா னென்று கூறப்","பெரி தாண்ட பெருங் கேண்மை","அறனொடு புணர்ந்த திறனறி செங்கோல்","அன்னோன் வாழி வென்வேற் குருசில்"],"p":"","r":"There the fishermen sing the kurinji tune and the hill people wear chaplets of fragrant neythal flowers, the forest people sing the marutham tune and the bards tell of the mullai and the other landscapes, the jungle fowl pecks the ears of paddy and the house fowl takes the millet, hill monkeys dive in the backwaters and backwater herons perch on the hills: in the cool settlements the lands gather without blemish, and the people say there is one umbrella; such is the lord who has ruled greatly with great kinship and a just sceptre joined to virtue and knowing the right ways; long live that lord of the victorious spear.","n":"Each people sings another land's tune, wears another land's flower, and each land's creature eats or rests in another's place: the poet takes the poetics of karu, in which each landscape has its own people, birds, food and music, and inverts it point by point to show a realm where the borders of the five lands no longer hold under one umbrella.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","naadu","arasu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 218-231","lb":"PP2:218-231","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["குறிஞ்சி","குறிஞ்சி",218,"yaazh","scene","kurinji tune"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",218,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",218,"maram","ornament","water lily"],["குறவர்","குறவர்",219,"seythi","scene","hill people"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",220,"seythi","scene","forest people"],["மருதம்","மருதம்",220,"yaazh","scene","marutham tune"],["அகவர்","அகவர்",220,"seythi","scene","bards"],["முல்லைப்","முல்லை",221,"yaazh","scene","mullai tune"],["கானக் கோழி","கானக் கோழி",222,"pul","scene","jungle fowl"],["கதிர்","கதிர்",222,"unavu","gift_food","paddy ears"],["மனைக் கோழி","மனைக் கோழி",223,"pul","scene","house fowl"],["தினைக்","தினை",223,"unavu","gift_food","millet"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",224,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["நாரை","நாரை",225,"pul","scene","heron"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:2:232-248":{"book":"pattupattu","num":33,"t":["மன்னர் நடுங்கத் தோன்றிப் பன்மாண்","எல்லை தருநன் பல்கதிர் பரப்பிக்","குல்லை கரியவுங் கோடெரி நைப்பவும்","அருவி மாமலை நிழத்தவு மற்றக்","கருவி வானங் கடற்கோள் மறப்பவும்","பெருவற னாகிய பண்பில் காலையும்","நறையும் நரந்தமு மகிலு மாரமும்","துறைதுறை தோறும் பொறையுயிர்த் தொழுகி","நுரைத்தலைக் குரைப்புனல் வரைப்பகம் புகுதொறும்","புனலாடு மகளிர் கதுமெனக் குடையக்","கூனிக் குயத்தின் வாய்நெல் லரிந்து","சூடுகோ டாகப் பிறக்கி நாடொறும்","குன்றெனக் குவைஇய குன்றாக் குப்பை","கடுந்தெற்று மூடையின் இடங்கெடக் கிடக்கும்","சாலி நெல்லின் சிறைகொள் வேலி","ஆயிரம் விளையுட் டாகக்","காவிரி புரக்கு நாடுகிழ வோனே"],"p":"","r":"Even in a bad season of great drought, when the sun appears so that kings tremble and spreads its many rays, scorching the kullai and setting branches aflame, when the falls of the great hills dwindle and the clustered clouds forget to draw water from the sea, the Kaveri, carrying nari, naratham, agil and sandal, sets down its load at every ford and flows on, its foaming, roaring flood entering every enclosure where bathing women plunge in, and the paddy cut with bent sickles is piled in sheaves day after day in unfailing heaps like hills, packed into sacks that crowd the space, in hedged fields of saali paddy that yield a thousandfold: he is the lord of the land the Kaveri nourishes.","n":"The drought is granted in full, sun, dried falls and clouds that forget the sea, and the river is set against it: the land's wealth is made independent of the rain, and so of the cosmic signs that might be read against a king. The sun that makes kings tremble here is the same image the poem used for the king himself (lines 135 and 136), now turned against the land and answered by the Kaveri.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","naadu","selvam","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Porunaratruppadai 232-248","lb":"PP2:232-248","poet":"முடத்தாமக் கண்ணியார்","pat":"சோழன் கரிகால் பெருவளத்தான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","ஆக்கம்"]],"k":[["குல்லை","குல்லை",234,"maram","absent","kullai"],["அருவி","அருவி",235,"pira","absent","waterfall"],["வானங்","வானம்",236,"pira","absent","rain clouds"],["நறையும்","நறை",238,"maram","scene","fragrant nari"],["நரந்தமு","நரந்தம்",238,"maram","scene","naratham"],["மகிலு","அகில்",238,"maram","scene","agil"],["மாரமும்","ஆரம்",238,"maram","scene","sandal"],["குரைப்புனல்","புனல்",240,"pira","scene","flood water"],["வாய்நெல்","நெல்",242,"unavu","scene","paddy"],["சாலி","சாலி நெல்",246,"unavu","scene","saali paddy"]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",248]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:2"},"pp:3:1-12":{"book":"pattupattu","num":34,"t":["மணிமலைப் பணைத்தோள் மாநில மடந்தை","யணிமுலைத் துயல்வரூஉ மாரம் போலச்","செல்புன லுழந்த சேய்வரற் கான்யாற்றுக்","கொல்கரை நறும்பொழிற் குயில்குடைந் துதிர்த்த","புதுப்பூஞ் செம்மல் சூடிப் புடைநெறித்துக்","கதுப்புவிரித் தன்ன காழக நுணங்கற","லயிலுருப் பனைய வாகி யைதுநடந்து","வெயிலுருப் புற்ற வெம்பரல் கிழிப்ப","வேனி னின்ற வெம்பத வழிநாட்","காலைஞா யிற்றுக் கதிர்கடா வுறுப்பப்","பாலை நின்ற பாலை நெடுவழிச்","சுரன்முதன் மராஅத்த வரிநிழ லசைஇ"],"p":"","r":"The poem opens on the summer road the suppliants are walking: a forest river that once wound like the necklace swaying on the breast of the earth, a woman whose broad shoulders are gem-bearing hills, has left on its bank flowers the koels shook down and fine dark sand rippled like spread hair, and that sand has grown hot as heated iron, so that the travellers, stepping lightly over pebbles that tear their feet in the late days of summer under the driving morning sun, rest in the striped shade of a maraa tree on the long paalai road.","n":"Line 11 says பாலை நின்ற பாலை: the paalai tree standing on the paalai road, or the paalai season standing over it; the pun is the poem's own. The bard will play the paalai mode on this same road at line 36, so land, season and music are tuned to one another. The river is dry here only by implication: its sand is described, not its water.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 1-12","lb":"PP3:1-12","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["குயில்","குயில்",4,"pul","scene",""],["கான்யாற்றுக்","கான் யாறு",3,"pira","simile","forest river"],["பாலை","பாலை",11,"maram","scene","paalai tree"],["மராஅத்த","மராஅம்",12,"maram","scene","kadamba"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:13-31":{"book":"pattupattu","num":35,"t":["யைதுவீ ழிகுபெய லழகுகொண் டருளி","நெய்கனிந் திருளிய கதுப்பிற் கதுப்பென","மணிவயின் கலாபம் பரப்பிப் பலவுடன்","மயின்மயிற் குளிக்குஞ் சாயற் சாஅ","யுயங்குநாய் நாவி னல்லெழி லசைஇ","வயங்கிழை யுலறிய அடியி னடிதொடர்ந்","தீர்ந்துநிலந் தோயு மிரும்பிடித் தடக்கையிற்","சேர்ந்துடன் செறிந்த குறங்கிற் குறங்கென","மால்வரை யொழுகிய வாழை வாழைப்","பூவெனப் பொலிந்த ஓதி ஓதி","நளிச்சினை வேங்கை நாண்மலர் நச்சிக்","களிச்சுரும் பரற்றுஞ் சுணங்கிற் சுணங்குபிதிர்ந்","தியாணர்க் கோங்கி னவிர்முகை யெள்ளிப்","பூணகத் தொடுங்கிய வெம்முலை முலையென","வண்கோட் பெண்ணை வளர்த்த நுங்கி","னின்சே றிகுதரு மெயிற்றி னெயிறெனக்","குல்லையம் புறவிற் குவிமுகை யவிழ்ந்த","முல்லை சான்ற கற்பின் மெல்லியல்","மடமா னோக்கின் வாணுதல் விறலியர்"],"p":"","r":"The women dancers of the troupe are described feature by feature, each surpassing a thing of the land: their oiled dark hair outdoes the train that peacocks spread in the thin rain, so that the peacocks hide before their grace; their small feet are lovely as a tired dog's tongue; their thighs are closer and smoother than a she-elephant's trunk; the knot of their hair shines like the plantain's flower; bees drawn to the vengai's fresh blossom buzz at the spots on their skin; their breasts mock the kongu bud; their teeth drip sweetness like the palmyra's young fruit; and they are gentle, deer-eyed and bright-browed, with a chastity full as the mullai that opens in the kullai woodland.","n":"The figure repeated through the passage (கதுப்பிற் கதுப்பு, குறங்கிற் குறங்கு, எயிற்றின் எயிறு, முலை முலை) sets each feature against a thing of the land that it surpasses, so the women are praised by making the forest yield to them; the frame returns at lines 263 and 264, where the peacocks of the patron's hill spread their trains like women's hair. The comparison of the feet to a tired dog's tongue is one of the poem's boldest: softness and redness, taken from the road's own weariness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 13-31","lb":"PP3:13-31","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["மயின்மயிற்","மயில்",16,"pul","simile",""],["நாய்","நாய்",17,"maa","scene",""],["மிரும்பிடித்","பிடி",19,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["வாழை","வாழை",21,"maram","simile","plantain"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",23,"maram","scene",""],["களிச்சுரும்","சுரும்பு",24,"pira","scene","bee"],["கோங்கி","கோங்கு",25,"maram","simile",""],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",27,"maram","simile","palmyra"],["நுங்கி","நுங்கு",27,"unavu","simile","palmyra fruit"],["குல்லையம்","குல்லை",29,"maram","scene",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",30,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["மடமா","மான்",31,"maa","scene","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:32-40":{"book":"pattupattu","num":36,"t":["நடைமெலிந் தசைஇய நன்மென் சீறடி","கல்லா விளையர் மெல்லத் தைவரப்","பொன்வார்ந் தன்ன புரியடங்கு நரம்பி","னின்குரற் சீறியா ழிடவயிற் றழீஇ","நைவளம் பழுநிய நயந்தெரி பாலை","கைவல் பாண்மகன் கடனறிந் தியக்க","வியங்கா வையத்து வள்ளியோர் நசைஇத்","துனிகூ ரெவ்வமொடு துயராற்றுப் படுப்ப","முனிவிகந் திருந்த முதுவா யிரவல"],"p":"","r":"While untaught young men gently stroke the small soft feet of the women, worn out by walking, the skilled bard holds on his left side the small sweet-voiced lute with strings tight-twisted like drawn gold and plays the paalai mode as its rule requires; and the guide turns to the addressee: old-mouthed suppliant, who has put weariness behind you though grief and longing for the generous in an unmoving world drive you along the road.","n":"Line 38 இயங்கா வையத்து is hard: either a world that does not move to help, or a world in which the generous do not go about; both make the suppliant's search the cause of his grief. The address முதுவாய் இரவல, the suppliant of seasoned speech, is the same phrase the god's attendants use of the devotee in Thirumurugatruppadai line 284.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","iravu","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 32-40","lb":"PP3:32-40","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["சீறியா","சீறியாழ்",35,"yaazh","scene","small lute"],["பாலை","பாலை",36,"yaazh","scene","paalai mode"],["பாண்மகன்","பாணன்",37,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:41-50":{"book":"pattupattu","num":37,"t":["கொழுமீன் குறைய வொதுங்கி வள்ளிதழ்க்","கழுநீர் மேய்ந்த கயவா யெருமை","பைங்கறி நிவந்த பலவி னீழல்","மஞ்சண் மெல்லிலை மயிர்ப்புறந் தைவர","விளையா விளங்க ணாற மெல்குபு பெயராக்","குளவிப் பள்ளிப் பாயல் கொள்ளுங்","குடபுலங் காவலர் மருமா னொன்னார்","வடபுல விமயத்து வாங்குவிற் பொறித்த","எழுவுறழ் திணிதோ ளியறேர்க் குட்டுவண்","வருபுனல்வாயில் வஞ்சியும் வறிதே யதாஅன்று"],"p":"","r":"In the western land, a wide-mouthed buffalo that has grazed the broad-petalled blue lilies, making the fat fish scatter, lies chewing on a bed of wild jasmine in the shade of jackfruit trees climbed by green pepper vines, with soft turmeric leaves stroking its hairy back and the scent of young toddy about it; yet even Vanji, with flood-water at its gate, the city of Kuttuvan of the well-made chariot, the descendant of the western guardians whose bar-like shoulders carved the curved bow on the northern Himalaya, is set down as empty.","n":"வறிதே can mean that Vanji is small beside the patron the poem will name, or that it was barren for suppliants like these; the three cities are dismissed in the same words (lines 50, 67, 83) before the poem says what they are measured against, so the comparison is held back for more than seventy lines. The Chera's mark on the Himalaya is the boast that the Chera bow was carved there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vendhan","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 41-50","lb":"PP3:41-50","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["கொழுமீன்","மீன்",41,"pira","scene","fish"],["கழுநீர்","கழுநீர்",42,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["யெருமை","எருமை",42,"maa","scene",""],["பைங்கறி","கறி",43,"maram","scene","pepper"],["பலவி","பலா",43,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["மஞ்சண்","மஞ்சள்",44,"maram","scene","turmeric"],["குளவிப்","குளவி",46,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"]],"en":[["king","குட்டுவண்",49],["town","வஞ்சி",50],["hill","விமயத்து",48]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:51-67":{"book":"pattupattu","num":38,"t":["நறவுவா யுறைக்கும் நாகுமுதிர் நுணவத்","தறைவாய்த் குறுந்துணி யயிலுளி பொருத","கைபுனை செப்பங் கடைந்த மார்பிற்","செய்பூங் கண்ணி செவிமுத றிருத்தி","நோன்பகட் டுமண ரொழுகையொடு வந்த","மகாஅ ரன்ன மந்தி மடவோர்","நகாஅ ரன்ன நளிநீர் முத்தம்","வாள்வா யெருந்தின் வயிற்றகத் தடக்கித்","தோள்புற மறைக்கும் நல்கூர் நுசுப்பி","னுளரிய லைம்பா லுமட்டிய ரீன்ற","கிளர்பூட் புதல்வரொடு கிலுகிலி யாடுந்","தத்துநீர் வரைப்பிற் கொற்கைக் கோமான்","தென்புலங் காவலர் மருமா னொன்னார்","மண்மாறு கொண்ட மாலை வெண்குடைக்","கண்ணார் கண்ணிக் கடுந்தேர்ச் செழியன்","தமிழ்நிலை பெற்ற தாங்கரு மரபின்","மகிழ்நனைமறுகின் மதுரையும் வறிதே யுதாஅன்று"],"p":"","r":"Young female monkeys, decked like children with carved chest-pieces of old honey-dripping nunavu wood and made flower chaplets set at the ear, come with the strong-oxen carts of the salt merchants and play rattle, with pearls from the sea like teeth taken from the bellies of sharp-edged shells, together with the ornamented sons born to the salt women whose loose five-part hair hides their shoulders: such is the wave-bounded Korkai of the Pandya Seliyan, descendant of the southern guardians, of the swift chariot, eye-filling chaplet and garlanded white umbrella that took the enemies' land; yet even Madurai, of his unbearable lineage in which Tamil found its standing, its streets soaked in revelry, is set down as empty.","n":"Monkeys playing with pearls beside the salt carts makes the pearl a toy: the wealth of Korkai is shown by how carelessly it is handled. தமிழ்நிலை பெற்ற in line 66 ties the standing of Tamil to the Pandya lineage and its city, though the poem says it only in passing, as an epithet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vendhan","thamizh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 51-67","lb":"PP3:51-67","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["நுணவத்","நுணா",51,"maram","scene",""],["நோன்பகட்","பகடு",55,"maa","scene","ox"],["டுமண","உமணர்",55,"seythi","scene","salt merchants"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",56,"maa","simile","female monkey"],["முத்தம்","முத்து",57,"pira","simile","pearl"],["யெருந்தின்","எருந்து",58,"pira","scene","bivalve shell"],["லுமட்டிய","உமட்டியர்",60,"seythi","scene","salt women"]],"en":[["town","கொற்கை",62],["king","செழியன்",65],["town","மதுரை",67]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:68-83":{"book":"pattupattu","num":39,"t":["நறுநீர்ப் பொய்கை யடைகரை நிவந்த","துறுநீர்க் கடம்பின் றுணையார் கோதை","ஓவத் தன்ன வுண்டுறை மருங்கிற்","கோவத் தன்ன கொங்குசேர் புறைத்தலின்","வருமுலை யன்ன வண்முகை யுடைந்து","திருமுக மவிழ்ந்த தெய்வத் தாமரை","யாசி லங்கை யரக்குத்தோய்ந் தன்ன","சேயிதழ் பொதிந்த செம்பொற் கொட்டை","யேம வின்றுணை தழீஇ யிறகுளர்ந்து","காமரு தும்பி காமரஞ் செப்புந்","தண்பணை தழீஇய தளரா இருக்கைக்","குணபுலங் காவலர் மருமா னொன்னா","ரோங்கெயிற் கதவ முருமுச்சுவல் சொறியுந்","தூங்கெயி லெறிந்த தொடிவிளங்கு தடக்கை","நாடா நல்லிசை நற்றேர்ச் செம்பிய","னோடாப் பூட்கை யுறந்தையும் வறிதே யதாஅன்று"],"p":"","r":"On the bank of a fragrant pond beside a ford lovely as a painting, where kadamba garlands rise from the thick water, the divine lotus opens its face, its rich bud like a young breast, its pollen-covered outer petals red as the cochineal insect, and its golden seed-head wrapped in red petals like a flawless palm dipped in lac, while a bee, ruffling its wings and clasping its sweet mate, sings the kaamaram tune: such is the cool field country of the Chola Sembiyan, descendant of the eastern guardians, of the good chariot and fame that need not be sought, whose bangled hand destroyed the hanging fort whose high gates the thunder scrapes; yet even Uranthai, of his unretreating resolve, is set down as empty.","n":"Each of the three cities is entered through one small scene of its land (the buffalo of the west, the monkeys of the south, the lotus and bee of the east) and each king through his ancestors and one deed; the pattern makes the three crowned dynasties a single formula that the patron, a minor chief, is then said to exceed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vendhan","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 68-83","lb":"PP3:68-83","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["கடம்பின்","கடம்பு",69,"maram","scene",""],["கோவத்","கோவம்",71,"pira","simile","cochineal insect"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",73,"maram","simile","lotus"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",77,"pira","scene","bee"],["காமரஞ்","காமரம்",77,"yaazh","scene","kaamaram tune"]],"en":[["king","செம்பிய",82],["battle","தூங்கெயி லெறிந்த",81],["town","யுறந்தையும்",83]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:84-113":{"book":"pattupattu","num":40,"t":["வானம் வாய்த்த வளமலைக் கவாஅற்","கான மஞ்ஞைக்குக் கலிங்க நல்கிய","அருந்திற லணங்கி னாவியர் பெருமகன்","பெருங்க னாடன் பேகனுஞ் சுரும்புண","நறுவீ யுறைக்கு நாக நெடுவழிச்","சிறுவீ முல்லைக்குப் பெருந்தேர் நல்கிய","பிறங்குவெள் ளருவி வீழுஞ் சாரல்","பறம்பிற் கோமான் பாரியுங் கறங்குமணி","வாலுளைப் புரவியொடு வையக மருள","வீர நன்மொழி யிரவலர்க் கீந்த","வழறிகழ்ந் திமைக்கு மஞ்சுவரு நெடுவேற்","கழறொடித் தடக்கைக் காரியு நிழறிகழ்","நீல நாக நல்கிய கலிங்க","ஆலமர் செல்வற் கமர்ந்தனன் கொடுத்த","சாவந் தாங்கிய சாந்துபுலர் திணிதோ","ளார்வ நன்மொழி யாயு மால்வரைக்","கமழ்பூஞ் சாரற் கவினிய நெல்லி","யமிழ்துவிளை தீங்கனி யெளவைக் கீந்த","வுரவுச்சினங் கனலுமொளிதிகழ் நெடுவே","லரவக்கடற் றானை யதிகனுங் கரவாது","நட்டோ ருவப்ப நடைப்பரி கார","முட்டாது கொடுத்த முனைவிளங்கு தடக்கைத்","துளிமழை பொழியும் வளிதுஞ்சு நெடுங்கோட்டு","நளிமலை நாட னள்ளியு நளிசினை","நறும்போது கஞலிய நாகுமுதிர் நாகத்துக்","குறும்பொறை நன்னாடு கோடியர்க் கீந்த","காரிக் குதிரைக் காரியொடு மலைந்த","வோரிக் குதிரை யோரியு மெனவாங்","கெழுசமங் கடந்த வெழுவுறழ் திணிதோ","ளெழுவர் பூண்ட வீகைச் செந்நுகம்"],"p":"","r":"The seven givers of old are named by their gifts: Pekan, chief of the Aviyar of the hill land guarded by a fearsome goddess, who gave a cloak to the forest peacock; Pari of Parambu, where white falls drop, who gave his great chariot to the small-flowered mullai on the long road; Kaari of the flashing spear, who gave suppliants horses with ringing bells and white manes and kind words that amazed the world; Aay, of loving speech and bow-bearing shoulders, who gave the god under the banyan the cloth the shining blue serpent had given him; Athikan of the roaring army, who gave Avvai the sweet nectar-yielding nelli fruit of the fragrant slope; Nalli of the dense hill, who gave his friends without stint the means to live; and Ori of the horse Ori, who fought Kaari of the horse Kaari and gave the performers a good land of hillocks: these seven together wore the red yoke of giving.","n":"Several of the gifts are useless to the receiver (a cloak to a peacock, a chariot to a creeper, a god's cloth to a god): the list praises giving that does not weigh the receiver's need. Two of the seven, Kaari and Ori, are named as having fought each other (line 110), yet they share one yoke; the rivalry of chiefs is set aside in the canon of givers. The next unit says the patron bore alone the yoke the seven bore together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 84-113","lb":"PP3:84-113","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["மஞ்ஞைக்குக்","மஞ்ஞை",85,"pul","scene","peacock"],["லணங்கி","அணங்கு",86,"deyvam","scene",""],["சுரும்புண","சுரும்பு",87,"pira","scene","bee"],["நாக","நாகம்",88,"maram","scene","naagam tree"],["முல்லைக்குப்","முல்லை",89,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["புரவியொடு","புரவி",92,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["நாக","நாகம்",96,"pira","scene","serpent"],["ஆலமர் செல்வற்","ஆலமர் செல்வன்",97,"deyvam","scene",""],["நெல்லி","நெல்லி",100,"maram","scene",""],["தீங்கனி","நெல்லிக்கனி",101,"unavu","gift_food",""],["குதிரைக்","குதிரை",110,"maa","scene",""],["கோடியர்க்","கோடியர்",109,"seythi","scene","performers"]],"en":[["patron","பேகனுஞ்",87],["hill","பறம்பிற்",91],["patron","பாரியுங்",91],["patron","காரியு",95],["patron","யாயு",99],["patron","யதிகனுங்",103],["patron","னள்ளியு",107],["patron","யோரியு",111]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:114-129":{"book":"pattupattu","num":41,"t":["விரிகடல் வேலி வியலகம் விளங்க","வொருதான் றாங்கிய வுரனுடைய நோன்றா","ணறுவீ நாககு மகிலு மாரமுந்","துறையாடு மகளிர்க்குத் தோட்புணை யாகிய","பொருபுன றரூஉம் போக்கரு மரபிற்","றொன்மா விலங்கைக் கருவொடு பெயரிய","நன்மா விலங்கை மன்ன ருள்ளும்","மறுவின்றி விளங்கிய வடுவில் வாய்வா","ளுறுபுலித் துப்பி னோவியர் பெருமகன்","களிற்றுத்தழும் பிருந்த கழறயங்கு திருந்தடிப்","பிடிக்கணஞ் சிதறும் பெயல்மழைத் தடக்கைப்","பல்லியக் கோடியர் புரவலன் பேரிசை","நல்லியக் கோடனை நயந்த கொள்கையொடு","தாங்கரு மரபிற் றன்னுந் தந்தை","வான்பொரு நெடுவரை வளனும் பாடி","முன்நாள் சென்றன மாக விந்நா"],"p":"","r":"He who alone, with strong legs and strength, bore that yoke so that the sea-fenced wide world shone is the great chief of the Oviyar, best among the kings of good Mavilangai (named after ancient Ilangai, where the dashing river brings fragrant naagam, agil and sandal that serve the bathing women as floats), with a true sword without blemish, the strength of a pouncing tiger, anklets on feet scarred by the elephant, and a hand that pours like rain and scatters herds of she-elephants as gifts: Nalliyakkodan, the famed patron of the many-instrumented performers; desiring him, we sang him, his father of unbearable lineage and the wealth of his sky-touching hill, and went to him the other day.","n":"Here the comparison held back since line 50 is completed: the yoke seven wore together, this one bore alone (ஒருதான் தாங்கிய), and the three crowned cities were 'empty' beside it. The town is said to carry the name of ancient Ilangai, a claim about the name and not about the place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","kudi","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 114-129","lb":"PP3:114-129","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["நாககு","நாகம்",116,"maram","scene","naagam tree"],["மகிலு","அகில்",116,"maram","scene","agil"],["மாரமுந்","ஆரம்",116,"maram","scene","sandal"],["களிற்றுத்","களிறு",123,"maa","scene","elephant"],["பிடிக்கணஞ்","பிடி",124,"maa","gift_food","she-elephant"],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",125,"seythi","scene","performers"]],"en":[["patron","நல்லியக் கோடனை",126],["town","நன்மா விலங்கை",120]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:130-145":{"book":"pattupattu","num":42,"t":["டிறவாக் கண்ண சாய்செவிக் குருளை","கறவாக் பால்முலை கவர்த னோனாது","புனிற்றுநாய் குரைக்கும் புல்லெ னட்டில்","காழ்சோர் முதுசுவர்க் கணச்சித லரித்த","பூழி பூத்த புழற்கா ளாம்பி","யல்குபசி யுழந்த வொடுங்குநுண் மருங்குல்","வளைக்கைக் கிணைமகள் வள்ளுகிர்க் குறைத்த","குப்பை வேளை யுப்பிலி வெந்ததை","மடவோர் காட்சி நாணிக் கடையடைத்","திருக்பே ரொக்கலொ டொருங்குடன் மிசையு","மழிபசி வருத்தம் வீடப் பொழிகவுட்","டறுகட் பூட்கைத் தயங்குமணி மருங்கிற்","சிறுகண் யானையொடு பெருந்தே ரெய்தி","யாமவ ணின்றும் வருதும் நீயிரு","மிவணயந் திருந்த விரும்பே ரொக்கற்","செம்ம லூள்ளமொடு செல்குவி ராயி"],"p":"","r":"In our dreary kitchen a new-whelped bitch, unable to bear her blind, droop-eared puppies tugging at her milkless teats, barked; under old walls with rotting rafters eaten by swarms of termites a hollow-stemmed mushroom grew in the dust; and the kinai-drummer's wife, her waist shrunk by settled hunger, nipped with her nails rubbish-heap velai greens, boiled them without salt and, ashamed before the gaze of the ignorant, shut the door to eat them with all her large family; so that this ruinous hunger might end, we got small-eyed elephants with streaming temples and bells at their sides and great chariots, and are coming from there; you too, with the large family that waits here in hope, if you go with a lifted heart . . .","n":"The poverty is shown by what is missing in the kitchen (milk, salt, fire) and by the shame that makes the woman shut the door: hunger is also a public disgrace. The gift (elephants and chariots) is out of all proportion to the need for food, which is the point of the comparison with the seven givers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","pasi","parisil","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 130-145","lb":"PP3:130-145","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["குருளை","குருளை",130,"maa","scene","puppy"],["புனிற்றுநாய்","நாய்",132,"maa","scene",""],["கணச்சித","சிதல்",133,"pira","scene","termite"],["ளாம்பி","ஆம்பி",134,"maram","scene","mushroom"],["கிணைமகள்","கிணைமகள்",136,"seythi","scene",""],["வேளை","வேளை",137,"maram","gift_food","velai greens"],["யுப்பிலி","உப்பு",137,"pira","absent","salt"],["யானையொடு","யானை",142,"maa","gift_food",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:146-163":{"book":"pattupattu","num":43,"t":["னலைநீர்த் தாழை யன்னம் பூப்பவுந்","தலைநாட் செருந்தி தமனியம் மருட்டவுங்","கடுஞ்சூன் முண்டகங் கதிர்மணி கழாஅலவும்","நெடுங்காற் புன்னை நித்திலம் வைப்பவும்","கானல் வெண்மணல் கடலுலாய் நிமிர்தரப்","பாடல் சான்ற நெய்த னெடுவழி","மணிநீர்ப் வைப்பு மதிலொடு பெயரிய","பனிநீர்ப் படுவின் பட்டினம் படரி","னோங்குநிலை யொட்டகந் துயின்மடிந் தன்ன","வீங்குதிரை கொணர்ந்த விரைமர விறகிற்","கரும்புகைச் செந்தீ மாட்டிப் பெருந்தோண்","மதியேக் கறூஉம் மாசறு திருமுகத்து","நுதிவே னோக்கி னுளைமக ளரித்த","பழம்படு தேறல் பரதவர் மடுப்பக்","கிளைமலர்ப் படப்பைக் கிடங்கிற் கோமான்","தளையவிழ் தெரியற் றகையோற் பாடி","யறற்குழற் பாணி தூங்கி யவரொடு","வறற்குழற் சூட்டின் வயின்வயிற் பெறுகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"On the long neythal road worthy of song, where the screwpine on the waves blooms white as swans, the first-day serunthi mimics gold, the full-swollen mundagam sheds bright gems, the tall punnai sets out pearls and the sea roams over the white sand of the shore grove, if you go to the port town of cool pools, named for its wall, the fishermen will light a red fire with black smoke from fragrant driftwood the waves brought in, lying like tall camels asleep, and pour you old clear toddy strained by a fisher-girl with a faultless face that makes the moon pine and spear-tip eyes; singing the noble lord of Kidangil with his bud-opened garland, and dancing to the beat with your women of sand-dark hair, you will get roast dried fish at every halt.","n":"The town is named only by a riddle (named with the word for wall, மதிலொடு பெயரிய), which the reader is expected to solve as Eyil-pattinam. The camel, a beast of no Tamil landscape, is used as the image for logs on the beach. The patron is first named here as lord of Kidangil, the town the three roads lead to.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","aatruppadai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 146-163","lb":"PP3:146-163","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["தாழை","தாழை",146,"maram","scene","screwpine"],["யன்னம்","அன்னம்",146,"pul","scene","swan"],["செருந்தி","செருந்தி",147,"maram","simile",""],["முண்டகங்","முண்டகம்",148,"maram","scene",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",149,"maram","scene",""],["யொட்டகந்","ஒட்டகம்",154,"maa","simile","camel"],["விரைமர","விரைமரம்",155,"maram","scene","fragrant wood"],["னுளைமக","நுளைமகள்",158,"seythi","scene","fisher-girl"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",159,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",159,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["வறற்குழற் சூட்டின்","குழல் மீன் சூடு",163,"unavu","gift_food","roast dried fish"]],"en":[["town","பட்டினம்",153],["town","கிடங்கிற்",160]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:164-177":{"book":"pattupattu","num":44,"t":["பைந்நனை யவரை பவழங் கோப்பவுங்","கருநனைக் காயாக் கணமயி லவிழவுங்","கொழுங்கொடி முசுண்டை கொட்டங் கொள்ளவுஞ்","செழுங்குலைக் காந்தள் கைவிரல் பூப்பவுங்","கொல்லை நெடுவழிக் கோப மூரவு","முல்லை சான்ற முல்லையம் புறவின்","விடர்கா லருவி வியன்மலை மூழ்கிச்","சுடர்கான் மாறிய செவ்வி நோக்கித்","திறல்வே னுதியிற் பூத்த கேணி","விறல்வேல் வென்றி வேலூ ரெய்தி","னுறுவெயிற் குலைஇய வுருப்பவிர் குரம்பை","யெயிற்றிய ரட்ட இன்புளி வெஞ்சோறு","தேமா மேனிச் சில்வளை யாயமொ","டாமான் சூட்டி னமைவரப் பெறுகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"In the mullai woodland full of mullai, where the green-budded avarai strings corals, the dark-budded kaayaa opens like a flock of peacocks, the rich musundai creeper spreads, the clustered kaanthal blooms like fingers and cochineal insects crawl on the long field road, if, watching for the hour when the hill, bathed by falls from its clefts, has lost the sun's heat, you reach victorious Velur with its well that flowered at a spear's tip, you will get, in huts bright and shrivelled by the fierce sun, hot rice soured with sweet tamarind cooked by the hunter women, with roast wild cow, shared with your company of few bangles and mango-coloured skin.","n":"Line 172, the well that 'flowered at the tip of a mighty spear', points to a story of a well opened with a spear, which the poem does not tell. The flowers listed (kaayaa, kaanthal, the cochineal after rain) are signs of the rains, so the road has passed from the summer of the opening to the wet season without saying so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","aatruppadai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 164-177","lb":"PP3:164-177","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["யவரை","அவரை",164,"maram","scene","bean"],["காயாக்","காயா",165,"maram","scene",""],["கணமயி","மயில்",165,"pul","scene",""],["முசுண்டை","முசுண்டை",166,"maram","scene",""],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",167,"maram","scene",""],["கோப","கோபம்",168,"pira","scene","cochineal insect"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",169,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["யெயிற்றிய","எயிற்றியர்",175,"seythi","scene","hunter women"],["இன்புளி வெஞ்சோறு","புளிச்சோறு",175,"unavu","gift_food","tamarind rice"],["டாமான்","ஆமான்",177,"maa","gift_food","wild cow"]],"en":[["town","வேலூ",173]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:178-195":{"book":"pattupattu","num":45,"t":["நறும்பூங் கோதை தொடுத்த நாட்சினைக்","குறுங்காற் காஞ்சிக் கொம்ப ரேறி","நிலையருங் குட்ட நோக்கி நெடிதிருந்து","புலவுக்கய லெடுத்த பொன்வாய் மணிச்சிரல்","வள்ளுகிர் கிழித்த வடுவாழ் பாசடை","முள்ளரைத் தாமரை முகிழ்விரி நாட்போது","கொங்குகவர் நீலச் செங்கட் சேவல்","மதிசே ரரவின் மானத் தோன்று","மருதஞ் சான்ற மருதத் தண்பணை","யந்தண ரருகா வருங்கடி வியனக","ரந்தண் கிடங்கினவ னாமூ ரெய்தின்","வலம்பட நடக்கும் வலிபுண ரெருத்தி","னுரன்கெழு நோன்பகட் டுழவர் தங்கை","பிடிக்கை யன்ன பின்னுவீழ் சிறுபுறத்துத்","தொடிக்கை மகடூஉ மகமுறை தடுப்ப","விருங்கா ழுலக்கை யிரும்புமுகந் தேய்த்த","அவைப்புமா ணரிசி யமலைவெண் சோறு","கவைத்தா ளலவன் கலவையொடு பெறுகுவி"],"p":"","r":"In the cool marutham fields full of marutham, where a blue kingfisher with a golden beak, having sat long on a bough of the short-stemmed kaanji whose fresh branch looks like a strung garland, watching the deep pool, snatches a stinking kayal fish, its sharp claws scarring the green lotus leaf, and a dark red-eyed male bee taking pollen on the opening lotus looks like the serpent seizing the moon, if you reach his Amur, the great guarded town of the brahmins with its lovely cool moat, the ploughmen's sister, her braid hanging on her small back like a she-elephant's trunk, will stop you as kin and give you heaps of white rice, well pounded by a black pestle with a worn iron mouth, with a mixture of forked-legged crab.","n":"The three roads go through neythal, mullai and marutham in turn, each called 'full of' its own name (நெய்தல் 151, முல்லை சான்ற 169, மருதஞ் சான்ற 186), and each ends in a meal of its land; the patron's hill (kurinji) is reached last, at line 267, so his country holds all the lands. Which creature is the red-eyed male of line 184 is not certain; a bee fits the image of a dark thing on a bright flower.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","uzhavu","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 178-195","lb":"PP3:178-195","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["காஞ்சிக்","காஞ்சி",179,"maram","scene",""],["புலவுக்கய","கயல்",181,"pira","scene","kayal fish"],["மணிச்சிரல்","சிரல்",181,"pul","scene","kingfisher"],["பாசடை","அடை",182,"maram","scene","lotus leaf"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",183,"maram","scene","lotus"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",184,"pira","simile","male bee"],["மருதத்","மருதம்",186,"maram","scene",""],["ரெருத்தி","எருத்து",189,"maa","scene","ox"],["டுழவர்","உழவர்",190,"seythi","scene","ploughmen"],["பிடிக்கை","பிடி",191,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["வெண்சோறு","சோறு",194,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["ளலவன்","அலவன்",195,"pira","gift_food","crab"]],"en":[["town","னாமூ",188]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:196-202":{"book":"pattupattu","num":46,"t":["ரெரிமறிந் தன்ன நாவி னிலங்கெயிற்றுக்","கருமறிக் காதிற் கவையடிப் பேய்மக","ணிணனுண்டு சிரித்த தோற்றம் போலப்","பிணனுகைத்துச் சிவந்த பேருகிர்ப் பணைத்தா","ளண்ணல் யானை யருவிதுக ளவிப்ப","நீறடங்கு தெருவினவன் சாறயர் மூதூர்","சேய்த்து மன்று சிறிதுநணி யதுவே"],"p":"","r":"His festive old town, whose streets have their dust laid by the streams from his great war elephants, their broad feet with huge nails reddened by trampling corpses like the look of a ghoul-woman laughing after eating fat, with a tongue like overturned fire, gleaming teeth, ears like black kids' and forked feet, is not far: it is quite near.","n":"The ghoul feeding on the battlefield, a figure of the war poems, is used here to measure the redness of an elephant's feet in a town at festival: the patron's violence abroad is the guarantee of the peace in which suppliants are fed. The streams that lay the dust (அருவி) may be the elephants' ichor or water they spray; the line does not decide.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 196-202","lb":"PP3:196-202","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["பேய்மக","பேய்மகள்",197,"deyvam","simile","ghoul"],["கருமறிக்","மறி",197,"maa","scene","goat kid"],["யானை","யானை",200,"maa","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:203-220":{"book":"pattupattu","num":47,"t":["பொருநர்க் காயினும் புலவர்க் காயினு","மருமறை நாவி னந்தணர்க் காயினுங்","கடவுண் மால்வரை கண்விடுத் தன்ன","அடையா வாயிலவ னருங்கடை குறுகிச்","செய்ந்நன்றி யறிதலுஞ் சிற்றின மின்மையு","மின்முக முடையையு மினிய னாதலுஞ்","செறிந்துவிளங்கு சிறப்பி னறிந்தோ ரேத்த","அஞ்சினர்க் களித்தலும் வெஞ்சின மின்மையு","மாணணி புகுதலு மழிபடை தாங்கலும்","வாண்மீக் கூற்றத்து வயவ ரேத்தக்","கருதியது முடித்தலுங் காமுறப் படுதலு","மொருவழிப் படாமையு மோடிய துணர்தலு","மரியே ருண்க ணரிவைய ரேத்த","அறிவுமடம் படுதலு மறிவுநன் குடைமையும்","வரிசை யறிதலும் வரையாது கொடுத்தலும்","பரிசில் வாழ்க்கைப் பரிசில ரேத்தப்","பன்மீ னடுவட் பான்மதி போல","இன்னகை யாயமோ டிருந்தோற் குறுகிப்"],"p":"","r":"Approaching the hard threshold of his gate, which is never shut to war-bards, poets or brahmins with the rare Veda on their tongues, open like the eye of a god's great hill, you will find him seated among his smiling company like the full moon among many stars: the wise praise him for knowing a favour done, keeping no low company, a glad face and a sweet manner; warriors of sword-sharp speech praise him for shielding those in fear, freedom from fierce anger, entering the enemy's array and holding a breaking army; women with lovely kohled eyes praise him for finishing what he purposes, being loved, not leaning to one side and understanding what has passed; and suppliants who live by gifts praise him for seeming simple in his knowledge while knowing well, knowing each one's due and giving without limit.","n":"The virtues come in four sets of four, each set closed by the people who praise it (அறிந்தோர் ஏத்த, வயவர் ஏத்த, அரிவையர் ஏத்த, பரிசிலர் ஏத்த): the poem divides one man's character among the audiences who know it best. The suppliants praise last and praise two things about judgement (knowing each one's due) before the limitless giving that concerns them most. அறிவுமடம் படுதல் in line 216, knowledge that consents to seem simple, is a rare phrase.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","nanri","eegai","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 203-220","lb":"PP3:203-220","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["பொருநர்க்","பொருநர்",203,"seythi","scene","war-bards"],["புலவர்க்","புலவர்",203,"seythi","scene","poets"],["னந்தணர்க்","அந்தணர்",204,"seythi","scene","brahmins"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:221-234":{"book":"pattupattu","num":48,"t":["பைங்க ணூகம் பாம்புபிடித் தன்ன","வங்கோட்டுச் செறிந்த வவிழ்ந்துவீங்கு திவவின்","மணிநிரைத் தன்ன வனப்பின் வாயமைத்து","வயிறுசேர் பொழுகிய வகையமை யகளத்துக்","கானக் குமிழின் கனிநிறங் கடுப்பப்","புகழ்வினைப் பொலிந்த பச்சையொடு தேம்பெய்","தமிழ்துபொதிந் திலிற்று மடங்குபுரி நரம்பிற்","பாடுதுறை முற்றிய பயன்றெரி கேள்விக்","கூடுகொ ளின்னியங் குரல்குர லாக","நூனெறி மரபிற் பண்ணி யானாது","முதுவோர்க்கு முகிழ்த்த கையினை யெனவு","மிளையோர்க்கு மலர்ந்த மார்பினை யெனவு","மேரோர்க்கு நிழன்ற கோலினை யெனவுந்","தேரோர்க் கழன்ற வேலினை யெனவு"],"p":"","r":"Holding your lute, whose stem with its bands is tight like a green-eyed langur gripping a snake, its mouth set with beauty like a row of gems, its bowl smoothly joined and covered with famed leather the colour of the forest kumizh fruit, its strings tight-twisted and oozing nectar, tune it by the rule of the treatise, voice answering voice, with the learning of one who has mastered the modes, and sing without tiring: 'you have a hand folded before elders, a chest open to the young, a sceptre that shades the ploughmen and a spear that burns the chariot warriors.'","n":"The four praises are two pairs of opposites (folded and open; shade and fire), each pair setting a gentle gesture toward one's own people against a fierce one toward others; the poem gives the bard the words of his praise, so the guide also teaches what to say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalai","pugazh","sengol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 221-234","lb":"PP3:221-234","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["ணூகம்","ஊகம்",221,"maa","simile","langur"],["பாம்புபிடித்","பாம்பு",221,"maa","simile","snake"],["குமிழின்","குமிழ்",225,"maram","simile",""],["ளின்னியங்","இன்னியம்",229,"yaazh","scene","instrument"],["மேரோர்க்கு","ஏரோர்",233,"seythi","scene","ploughmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:235-260":{"book":"pattupattu","num":49,"t":["நீசில மொழியா வளவை மாசில்","காம்புசொலித் தன்ன வறுவை யுடீஇப்","பாம்புவெகுண் டன்ன தேற னல்கிக்","காவெரி யூட்டிய கவர்கணைத் தூணிப்","பூவிரி கச்சைப் புகழோன் றன்முன்","பனிவரை மார்பன் பயந்த நுண்பொருட்","பனுவலின் வழாஅப் பல்வே றடிசில்","வாணிற விசும்பிற் கோண்மீன் சூழ்த்த","விளங்கதிர் ஞாயி றெள்ளுந் தோற்றத்து","விளங்குபொற் கலத்தில் விரும்புவன பேணி","யானா விருப்பிற் றனின் றூட்டித்","திறல்சால் வென்றியொடு தெவ்வுப்புல மகற்றி","விறல்வேன் மன்னர் மன்னெயின் முருக்கி","நயவர் பாணர் புன்கண் டீர்த்தபின்","வயவர் தந்த வான்கேழ் நிதியமொடு","பருவ வானத்துப் பாற்கதிர் பரப்பி","யுருவ வான்மதி யூர்கொண் டாங்குக்","கூருளி பொருத வடுவாழ் நோன்குறட்","டாரஞ் சூழ்ந்த வயில்வாய் நேமியொடு","சிதர்நனை முருக்கின் சேணோங்கு நெடுஞ்சினைத்","ததர்பிணி யவிழ்ந்த தோற்றம் போல","வுள்ளரக் கெறிந்த வுருக்குறு போர்வைக்","கருந்தொழில் வினைஞர் கைவினை முற்றி","யூர்ந்துபெயர் பெற்ற வெழினடைப் பாகரொடு","மாசெல வொழிக்கு மதனுடை நோன்றாள்","வாண்முகப் பாண்டில் வலவனொடு தரீஇ"],"p":"","r":"Before you have said those few words, he will dress you in flawless cloth like the peeled skin of bamboo, give you toddy fierce as an angry snake, stand and feed you himself with endless care, from shining gold vessels set round like planets about the young sun and mocking it, the many foods of the subtle treatise written by the snow-hill-chested elder brother of the famed one with the flowered girdle whose forked arrows fed fire to the grove; and after ending the distress of connoisseurs and bards with the treasure his warriors brought from driving out enemies and razing kings' forts, he will give you a chariot whose wheel, its hub scarred by the sharp chisel and its spokes ringed by an iron rim, is like the full moon with its halo spreading milk-white rays in the seasonal sky, its covering of poured lac like the crowded buds of the tall murukku opening, finished by the iron-smiths' craft, with famed drivers of fine gait, draught beasts strong enough to outrun horses, and a charioteer.","n":"The gift comes before the request (நீ சில மொழியா அளவை): the patron does not wait to be asked, which is the virtue the suppliants praised (வரையாது கொடுத்தல்). The cook-book author of lines 238 to 241 is described, not named, as the elder brother of a famed archer whose arrows fed fire to a grove; read as Bhima and Arjuna, it is a Mahabharata allusion inside a Tamil guide poem, and the food is said to follow that treatise to the letter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 235-260","lb":"PP3:235-260","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["காம்புசொலித்","காம்பு",236,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["பாம்புவெகுண்","பாம்பு",237,"maa","simile","snake"],["தேற","தேறல்",237,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["றடிசில்","அடிசில்",241,"unavu","gift_food","food"],["முருக்கின்","முருக்கு",254,"maram","simile",""],["பாண்டில்","பாண்டில்",260,"maa","gift_food","draught beasts"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:3:261-269":{"book":"pattupattu","num":50,"t":["யன்றே விடுக்குமவன் பரிசின் மென்றேட்","டுகிலணி யல்குற் றுளங்கியன் மகளி","ரகிலுண விரித்த வம்மென் கூந்தலின்","மணிமயிற் கலாப மஞ்சிடைப் பரப்பித்","துணிமழை தவழுந் துயல்கழை நெடுங்கோட்","டெறிந்துரு மிறந்த வேற்றருஞ் சென்னிக்","குறிஞ்சிக் கோமான் கொய்தளிர்க் கண்ணிச்","செல்லிசை நிலைஇய பண்பி","நல்லியக் கோடனை நயந்தனிர் செலினே"],"p":"","r":"He sends you off that very day with his gift: go, if you desire Nalliyakkodan, lord of the kurinji with a chaplet of plucked shoots and a nature in which far-going fame has settled, whose peaks of swaying bamboo, where clear clouds creep and blue peacocks spread their trains in the mist like the soft lovely hair that women with swaying gait and fine-clothed hips spread to take agil smoke, rise to a summit hard to climb where the thunder strikes and passes.","n":"The poem ends where it began: in lines 13 to 16 the women's hair outdid the peacock's train, and here the peacocks spread their trains like the women's hair, so the comparison is turned round at the patron's hill. The last line closes the conditional opened at line 145 (செல்குவிராயின் . . . செலினே): the whole road and every gift hang on the hearer's going.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","pugazh","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Sirupanatruppadai 261-269","lb":"PP3:261-269","poet":"இடைக்கழி நாட்டு நல்லூர் நத்தத்தனார்","pat":"ஓய்மானாட்டு நல்லியக்கோடன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["ரகிலுண","அகில்",263,"maram","scene","agil smoke"],["மணிமயிற்","மயில்",264,"pul","scene","peacock"],["துயல்கழை","கழை",265,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["கொய்தளிர்க்","தளிர்",267,"maram","ornament","leaf shoots"]],"en":[["patron","நல்லியக் கோடனை",269]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:3"},"pp:4:1-22":{"book":"pattupattu","num":51,"t":["அகலிரு விசும்பிற் பாயிருள் பருகிப்","பகல்கான் றெழுதரு பல்கதிர்ப் பருதி","காய்சினந் திருகிய கடுந்திறல் வேனிற்","பாசிலை யொழித்த பராஅரைப் பாதிரி","வள்ளிதழ் மாமலர் வயிற்றிடை வகுத்தத","னுள்ளகம் புரையு மூட்டுறு பச்சைப்","பரியரைக் கமுகின் பாளையம் பசும்பூக்","கருவிருந் தன்ன கண்கூடு செறிதுளை","யுருக்கி யன்ன பொருத்துறு போர்வைச்","சுனைவறந் தன்ன விருடூங்கு வறுவாய்ப்","பிறைபிறந் தன்ன பின்னேந்து கவைக்கடை","நெடும்பணைத் திரடோண் மடந்தை முன்கைக்","குறுந்தொடி யேய்க்கும் மெலிந்துவீங்கு திவவின்","மணிவார்ந் தன்ன மாயிரு மருப்பிற்","பொன்வார்ந் தன்ன புரியடங்கு நரம்பின்","றொடையமை கேள்வி யிடவயிற் றழீஇ","வெந்தெறற் கனலியொடு மதிவலந் திரிதருந்","தண்கடல் வரைப்பிற் றாங்குநர்ப் பெறாது","பொழிமழை துறந்த புகைவேய் குன்றத்துப்","பழுமரந் தேரும் பறவை போலக்","கல்லென் சுற்றமொடு கால்கிளர்ந்து திரிதரும்","புல்லென் யாக்கைப் புலவுவாய்ப் பாண!"],"p":"","r":"As the many-rayed sun rises, having drunk the darkness of the wide sky, in a summer whose fierce heat has stripped the pathiri of its leaves, the poet describes the bard's lute part by part (a belly like the inside of the pathiri flower, a cover fitted like molten wax, an empty mouth dark as a dried spring, a forked end like the new crescent, frets like a girl's small bangles, a neck like drawn sapphire, strings like drawn gold) and hails the lean bard with the meat-smelling mouth who, finding no supporter anywhere in the sea-bounded world, wanders with his noisy kin like birds searching for fruiting trees on a smoky hill the rain has abandoned.","n":"Fourteen lines go to the lute and one to its owner (புல்லென் யாக்கை): the opening sets a finely made instrument against the poverty of the man who carries it. The bard finds no supporter in the whole world that sun and moon circle (lines 17, 18), so the patron's rarity is put on a cosmic scale before he is even named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","inmai","iravu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 1-22","lb":"PP4:1-22","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["azhugai","வறுமை"]],"k":[["பாதிரி","பாதிரி",4,"maram","simile","trumpet-flower tree"],["கமுகின்","கமுகு",7,"maram","simile","areca palm"],["சுனைவறந்","சுனை",10,"pira","simile","spring"],["கேள்வி","கேள்வி",16,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["பழுமரந்","பழுமரம்",20,"maram","simile","fruiting tree"],["பறவை","பறவை",20,"pul","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:23-45":{"book":"pattupattu","num":52,"t":["பெருவறங் கூர்ந்த கானங் கல்லெனக்","கருவி வானந் துளிசொரிந் தாங்குப்","பழம்பசி கூர்ந்தவெம் மிரும்பே ரொக்கலொடு","வழங்கத் தவாஅப் பெருவள னேய்தி","வாலுளைப் புரவியொடு வயக்களிறு முகந்துகொண்","டியாமவ ணின்றும் வருதும் நீயிரு","மிருநிலங் கடந்த திருமறு மார்பின்","முந்நீர் வண்ணன் பிறங்கடை யந்நீர்த்","திரைதரு மரபி னுரவோ னும்பல்","மலர்தலை யுலகத்து மன்னுயிர் காக்கும்","முரசுமுழங்கு தானை மூவ ருள்ளு","மிலங்குநீர்ப் பரப்பின் வளைமீக் கூறும்","வலம்புரி யன்ன வசைநீங்கு சிறப்பி","னல்லது கடிந்த வறம்புரி செங்கோற்","பல்வேற் றிரையற் படர்குவி ராயிற்","கேளவ னிலையே கெடுகநின் னவல","மத்தஞ் செல்வோ ரலறத் தாக்கிக்","கைப்பொருள் வௌவுங் களவேர் வாழ்க்கைக்","கொடியோ ரின்றவன் கடியுடை வியன்புல","முருமும் உரறா தரவுந் தப்பா","காட்டுமாவு முறுகண் செய்யா வேட்டாங்","கசைவுழி யசைஇ நசைவுழித் தங்கிச்","சென்மோ விரவல சிறக்கநின் னுள்ளங்"],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that, like a cloud pouring rain on a parched forest, the patron gave him and his large kin, long hungry, a wealth that spending does not exhaust, and that they come from there with white-maned horses and strong elephants; if the bard too goes to Thiraiyan of many spears, heir of the sea-coloured one whose chest bears the mark of Sri, scion of the mighty line the waves brought, who among the three drum-sounding kings is praised above them as the valampuri is above other conches, whose just sceptre has banished all that is not dharma, then let him hear how it is: may his sorrow end, for in that guarded wide land there are no cruel men who live by the plough of theft, robbing travellers on the road, thunder does not strike, snakes do not bite and wild beasts do no harm, so he may rest where he likes, stay where he wishes and go on, his heart glad.","n":"The patron's protection reaches beyond men: thunder, snakes and beasts also do no harm in his land (lines 42, 43), which is the just sceptre of line 36 made into a law of nature. The speaker's credential is the gift itself (horses and elephants), and the old hunger of line 25 (பழம்பசி) is the starting point that the whole road will answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","eegai","sengol","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 23-45","lb":"PP4:23-45","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["துளிசொரிந்","மழைத்துளி",24,"pira","simile","rain"],["புரவியொடு","புரவி",27,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["வயக்களிறு","களிறு",27,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["முந்நீர் வண்ணன்","முந்நீர் வண்ணன்",30,"deyvam","scene","Vishnu"],["முரசுமுழங்கு","முரசு",33,"parai","scene",""],["வளைமீக்","வளை",34,"pira","simile","conch"],["வலம்புரி","வலம்புரி",35,"pira","simile","right-whorled conch"],["கொடியோ","களவேர் கொடியோர்",41,"seythi","absent","highway robbers"],["முருமும்","உரும்",42,"pira","scene","thunder"],["தரவுந்","அரவு",42,"pira","scene","snake"],["காட்டுமாவு","காட்டு மா",43,"maa","scene","beast"]],"en":[["king","றிரையற்",37]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:46-65":{"book":"pattupattu","num":53,"t":["கொழுஞ்சூட் டருந்திய திருந்துநிலை யாரத்து","முழவி னன்ன முழுமர வுருளி","யெழூஉப்புணர்ந் தன்ன பரூஉக்கை நோன்பார்","மாரிக் குன்றம் மழைசுமந் தன்ன","வாரை வேய்ந்த வறைவாய்ச் சகடம்","வேழங் காவலர் குரம்பை யேய்ப்பக்","கோழி சேக்குங் கூடுடைப் புதவின்","முளையெயிற் றிரும்பிடி முழந்தா ளேய்க்குந்","துளையரைச் சீறுர றூங்கத் தூக்கி","நாடக மகளி ராடுகளத் தெடுத்த","விசிவீங் கின்னியங் கடுப்பல் கயிறுபிணித்துக்","காடி வைத்த கலனுடை மூக்கின்","மகவுடை மகடூஉப் பகடுபுறந் துரப்பக்","கோட்டிணர் வேம்பி னேட்டிலை மிடைந்த","படலைக் கண்ணிப் பரேரெறுழ்த் திணிதோண்","முடலை யாக்கை முழுவலி மாக்கள்","சிறுதுளைக் கொடுநுக நெறிபட நிரைத்த","பெருங்கயிற் றொழுகை மருங்கிற் காப்பச்","சில்பத வுணவின் கொள்ளை சாற்றிப்","பல்லெருத் துமணர் பதிபோகு நெடுநெறி"],"p":"","r":"On the long road from town to town go the salt merchants with their many oxen: their roofed carts have spokes set in a fat hub, wheels of whole timber like drums, axle-beams like joined bolts, a hood like a rain-laden hill, a hen-coop at the door like an elephant-watcher's hut, a small mortar hung up like a cow elephant's knee and ropes bound like the stretched drum on a dancers' stage; a woman with her child drives the oxen from behind while men with neem garlands and massive shoulders guard the long train of yoked carts, proclaiming the rate of their goods against grain.","n":"The cart is a moving house, with a coop for the hen and a mortar for the grain, and the whole family travels: a mother with her child drives from behind while armed men walk beside. The phrase கொள்ளை சாற்றி (line 64), proclaiming the exchange rate, is the same formula an Akananuru poet uses for the salt carts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 46-65","lb":"PP4:46-65","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["முழவி","முழவு",47,"parai","simile","drum"],["வேழங்","வேழம்",51,"maa","simile","elephant"],["கோழி","கோழி",52,"pul","scene","hen"],["றிரும்பிடி","பிடி",53,"maa","simile","cow elephant"],["கின்னியங்","இன்னியம்",56,"parai","simile","drum"],["காடி","காடி",57,"unavu","scene","sour gruel"],["பகடுபுறந்","பகடு",58,"maa","scene","ox"],["வேம்பி","வேம்பு",59,"maram","ornament","neem"],["வுணவின்","உணவு",64,"unavu","scene","grain"],["துமணர்","உமணர்",65,"seythi","scene","salt merchants"],["பல்லெருத்","எருது",65,"maa","scene","ox"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:66-82":{"book":"pattupattu","num":54,"t":["யெல்லிடைக் கழியுநர்க் கேம மாக","மலையவுங் கடலவு மாண்பயந் தரூஉ","மரும்பொரு ளருத்துந் திருந்துதொடை நோன்றா","ளடிபுதை யரண மெய்திப் படம்புக்குப்","பொருகணை தொலைச்சிய புண்டீர் மார்பின்","விரவுவரிக் கச்சின் வெண்கை யொள்வாள்","வரையூர் பாம்பிற் பூண்டுபுடை தூங்கச்","சுரிகை நுழைந்த சுற்றுவீங்கு செறிவுடைக்","கருவி லோச்சிய கண்ணக னெறுழ்த்தோட்","கடம்பமர் நெடுவே ளன்ன மீளி","யுடம்பிடித் தடக்கை யோடா வம்பலர்","தடவுநிலைப் பலவின் முழுமுதற் கொண்ட","சிறுசுளைப் பெரும்பழங் கடுப்ப மிரியற்","புணர்ப்பொறை தாங்கிய வடுவாழ் நோன்புறத்","தணர்ச்செவிக் கழுதைச் சாத்தொடு வழங்கு","முல்குடைப் பெருவழிக் கவலை காக்கும்","வில்லுடை வைப்பின் வியன்காட் டியவி"],"p":"","r":"As a safeguard for those who pass by day, armed travellers who carry the rare goods of hills and seas (feet shod, coats on, chests healed of arrow wounds, bright white-hilted swords hanging at their sides like snakes climbing a hill, daggers thrust in their belts, heroes like the tall Vel who loves the kadamba, spear in hand, never turning back) go along with caravans of long-eared donkeys whose scarred backs bear sacks of pepper like the great fruit of the jackfruit tree, along the great toll road whose forks the bowmen's posts guard in the wide forest.","n":"The safety promised in lines 39 to 43 is shown here as an institution, not a miracle: armed traders guard their own goods and bowmen's posts hold the road's junctions where the toll (உல்கு) is taken. Who is the subject of lines 67 and 68 (the traders who bring the goods of hill and sea, or the escort who makes it possible) is not fixed by the syntax, and the reading takes the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maram","naadu","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 66-82","lb":"PP4:66-82","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["பாம்பிற்","பாம்பு",72,"pira","simile","snake"],["கடம்பமர்","கடம்பு",75,"maram","simile",""],["நெடுவே","நெடுவேள்",75,"deyvam","simile","Murugan"],["பலவின்","பலா",77,"maram","simile","jackfruit"],["மிரியற்","மிரியல்",78,"pira","scene","pepper"],["கழுதைச்","கழுதை",80,"maa","scene","donkey"],["வில்லுடை","வில்லோர்",82,"seythi","scene","bowmen at the toll posts"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:83-105":{"book":"pattupattu","num":55,"t":["னீளரை யிலவத் தலங்குசினை பயந்த","பூளையம் பசுங்காய் புடைவிரிந் தன்ன","வரிப்புற வணிலொடு கருப்பை யாடா","தியாற்றறல் புரையும் வெரிநுடைக் கொழுமடல்","வேற்றலை யன்ன வைந்நுதி நெடுந்தக","ரீத்திலை வேய்ந்த வெய்ப்புறக் குரம்பை","மான்றோற் பள்ளி மகவொடு முடங்கி","யீன்பிண வொழியப் போகி நோன்கா","ழிரும்புதலை யாத்த திருந்துகணை விழுக்கோ","லுளிவாய்ச் சுரையின் மிளிர மிண்டி","யிருநிலக் கரம்பைப் படுநீ றாடி","நுண்பு லடக்கிய வெண்ப லெயிற்றியர்","பார்வை யாத்த பறைதாள் விளவி","னீழன் முன்றி னிலவுரற் பெய்து","குறுங்கா ழுலக்கை யோச்சி நெடுங்கிணற்று","வல்லூற் றுவரி தோண்டித் தொல்லை","முரவுவாய்க் குழிசி முரியடுப் பேற்றி","வாரா தட்ட வாடூன் புழுக்கல்","வாடாத் தும்பை வயவர் பெருமக","னோடாத் தானை யொண்டொழிற் கழற்காற்","செவ்வரை நாடன் சென்னிய மெனினே","தெய்வ மடையிற் றேக்கிலைக் குவைஇநும்","பைதீர் கடும்பொடு பதமிகப் பெறுகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"In the huts of the bowmen's forest, thatched with date-palm leaves whose spined fronds are like spear-heads and whose backs ripple like river sand, where the striped squirrel and the rat do not play, the mother lies curled with her newborn on a deerskin bed while the white-toothed hunting women go out with iron-tipped digging sticks, heave the hard ground until they are covered with dust, gather the fine grass seed, pound it in the ground-mortar in the shade of the vila tree where the decoy is tied, draw brackish water from a deep well and cook it with dried meat in an old broken-rimmed pot on a broken hearth; if the bard says he belongs to the lord of the red hills, chief of warriors with unfading thumbai, he and his kin will get that food heaped on teak leaves like a god's offering.","n":"This is the poorest meal of the road, grass seed dug from hard ground and boiled with dried meat in a broken pot, yet it is served like a god's offering, and the hosts give it because the bard names his patron: Thiraiyan's name works as a passport across his land. The sense of சென்னியம் (line 103) as a claim to belong to the lord's following is the reading taken here; the word is rare.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","aatruppadai","inmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 83-105","lb":"PP4:83-105","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["யிலவத்","இலவம்",83,"maram","simile","silk-cotton tree"],["வணிலொடு","அணில்",85,"maa","scene","squirrel"],["கருப்பை","கருப்பை",85,"maa","scene","rat"],["ரீத்திலை","ஈந்து இலை",88,"maram","scene","date-palm leaf"],["மான்றோற்","மான் தோல்",89,"maa","scene","deerskin"],["லெயிற்றியர்","எயிற்றியர்",94,"seythi","scene","hunting women"],["நுண்பு","நுண் புல்",94,"unavu","gift_food","grass seed"],["பார்வை","பார்வை",95,"maa","scene","decoy animal"],["விளவி","விளா",95,"maram","scene","wood-apple"],["வாடூன்","வாடு ஊன்",100,"unavu","gift_food","dried meat"],["தும்பை","தும்பை",101,"maram","ornament",""],["றேக்கிலை","தேக்கு இலை",104,"maram","scene","teak leaf"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:106-133":{"book":"pattupattu","num":56,"t":["மானடி பொறித்த மயங்கதர் மருங்கின்","வான்மடி பொழுதி னீர்நசைஇக் குழித்த","வகழ்சூழ் பயம்பி னகத்தொளித் தொடுங்கிப்","புகழா வாகைப் பூவி னன்ன","வளைமருப் பேனம் வரவுபார்த் திருக்கு","மரைநாள் வேட்ட மழுங்கிற் பகனாட்","பகுவாய் ஞமலியொடு பைம்புத லெருக்கித்","தொகுவாய் வேலித் தொடர்வலை மாட்டி","முள்ளரைத் தாமரைப் புல்லிதழ் புரையு","நெடுஞ்செவிக் குறுமுயல் போக்கற வளைஇக்","கடுங்கட் கானவர் கடறுகூட் டுண்ணு","மருஞ்சுர மிறந்த வம்பர்ப் பருந்துபட","வொன்னாத் தெவ்வர் நடுங்க வோச்சி","வைந்நுதி மழுங்கிய புலவுவா யெஃகம்","வடிமணிப் பலகையொடு நிரைஇ முடிநாட்","சாபஞ் சார்த்திய கணைதுஞ்சு வியனக","ரூகம் வேய்ந்த வுயர்நிலை வரைப்பின்","வரைத்தேன் புரையுங் கவைக்கடைப் புதையொடு","கடுந்துடி தூங்குங் கணைக்காற் பந்தர்த்","தொடர்நா யாத்த துன்னருங் கடிநகர்","வாழ்முள் வேலிச் சூழ்மிளைப் படப்பைக்","கொடுநுகந் தழீஇய புதவிற் செந்நிலை","நெடுநுதி வயக்கழு நிரைத்த வாயிற்","கொடுவி லெயினக் குறும்பிற் சேப்பிற்","களர்வள ரீந்தின் காழ்கண் டன்ன","சுவல்விளை நெல்லின் செவ்வவிழ்ச் சொன்றி","ஞமலி தந்த மனவுச்சூ லுடும்பின்","வறைகால் யாத்தது வயின்றொறும் பெருகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"Along paths confused with deer tracks, the fierce-eyed hunters hide in pits dug round dried water-holes at the time the sky withholds rain, waiting by night for the boar whose curved tusks are like the vaakai flower, and when that hunt fails, by day they beat the green bushes with wide-mouthed dogs, string chain-nets on a fence and ring in the long-eared hare whose ears are like the coarse petals of the thorny lotus, and eat together on the slope; in the hard wasteland the curved-bow eyinar keep fortified homes, spears blunted from striking foes and laid with shields, bows leaning, arrows asleep under a roof of ooga grass, quivers like honeycomb and the tudi drum hung on the pandal posts, chained dogs, thorn fences, a guarded wood and gates set with sharp stakes; there the bard will receive in every house red rice of upland paddy like the seeds of the eenthu palm, with fried iguana the dogs have caught.","n":"The hunters' settlement is a small fort (thorn fence, guarded gate, stakes, chained dogs) because it holds the frontier of the dry tract; the men whose spear-tips are blunt from killing foes are the bard's hosts, which is the guide poem's point: under this patron the violence of the frontier faces outward. The hill hunt for boar and hare is set in the waste (அருஞ்சுரம்), so the land is mixed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","aran","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 106-133","lb":"PP4:106-133","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["மானடி","மான்",106,"maa","scene","deer"],["வாகைப்","வாகை",109,"maram","simile",""],["பேனம்","ஏனம்",110,"maa","scene","boar"],["ஞமலியொடு","ஞமலி",112,"maa","scene","dog"],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",114,"maram","simile","lotus"],["குறுமுயல்","முயல்",115,"maa","scene","hare"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",116,"seythi","scene","hunters"],["பருந்துபட","பருந்து",117,"pul","scene","kite"],["ரூகம்","ஊகம்",122,"maram","scene","thatching grass"],["வரைத்தேன்","தேன்",123,"pira","simile","honeycomb"],["கடுந்துடி","துடி",124,"parai","scene",""],["லெயினக்","எயினர்",129,"seythi","scene","bowmen tribe"],["ரீந்தின்","ஈந்து",130,"maram","simile","date palm"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",131,"unavu","gift_food","upland paddy"],["லுடும்பின்","உடும்பு",132,"maa","gift_food","monitor lizard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:134-146":{"book":"pattupattu","num":57,"t":["யானை தாக்கினு மரவுமேற் செலினு","நீனிற விசும்பின் வல்லேறு சிலைப்பினுஞ்","சூன்மகள் மாறா மறம்பூண் வாழ்க்கை","வலிக்கூட் டுணவின் வாட்குடிப் பிறந்த","புலிப்போத் தன்ன புல்லணற் காளை","சென்னா யன்ன கருவிற் சுற்றமொடு","கேளா மன்னர் கடிபுலம் புக்கு","நாள்ஆ தந்து நறவுநொடை தொலைச்சி","யில்லடு கள்ளின் றோப்பி பருகி","மல்லல் மன்றத்து மதவிடை கெண்டி","மடிவாய்த் தண்ணுமை நடுவட் சிலைப்பச்","சிலைநவி லெறுழ்த்தோ ளோச்சி வலன்வளையூஉப்","பகல்மகிழ் தூங்குந் தூங்கா விருக்கை"],"p":"","r":"Born of a sword-wielding clan whose food is taken by force and whose life of valour even a pregnant woman will not give up though an elephant charges, a snake crawls over her or thunder roars in the blue sky, the thin-bearded youth like a tiger cub goes with kin whose bows are like wild dogs into the guarded land of hostile kings, drives cattle home by daylight, trades them for toddy, drinks the thick home-brewed liquor, slaughters a rutting bull in the rich common ground and, as the drum sounds in their midst, raises his bow-trained arm and dances in a ring: such is the never-sleeping settlement that revels by day.","n":"The raid, the sale of the stolen cattle for toddy and the day-long drinking are told as marks of a valour that even the pregnant women keep, and the poem passes no judgement: the warrior settlement is one stage of the bard's road. Its epithet துஞ்சா/தூங்கா இருக்கை recurs for each settlement (146, 242 தளரா, 253 துளங்கா), a refrain of the patron's land. The matter of the unit is vetchi, the colophon's thinai is that of the whole idyll.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kal","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 134-146","lb":"PP4:134-146","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"vetchi","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",134,"maa","scene",""],["மரவுமேற்","அரவு",134,"pira","scene","snake"],["வல்லேறு","ஏறு",135,"pira","scene","thunder"],["புலிப்போத்","புலிப் போத்து",138,"maa","simile","tiger cub"],["சென்னா","செந்நாய்",139,"maa","simile","wild dog"],["நாள்ஆ","ஆ",141,"maa","scene","cattle"],["நறவுநொடை","நறவு",141,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["றோப்பி","தோப்பி",142,"unavu","gift_food","home-brewed liquor"],["மதவிடை","விடை",143,"maa","gift_food","bull"],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",144,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:147-168":{"book":"pattupattu","num":58,"t":["முரண்டலை கழிந்த பின்றை மறிய","குளகுஅரை யாத்த குறுங்காற் குரம்பைச்","செற்றை வாயிற் செறிகழிக் கதவிற்","கற்றை வேய்ந்த கழித்தலைச் சாம்பி","னதளோன் றுஞ்சுங் காப்பி னுதள","நெடுந்தாம்பு தொடுத்த குறுந்தறி முன்றிற்","கொடுமுகத் துருவையொடு வெள்ளை சேக்கு","மிடுமுள் வேலி யெருப்படு வரைப்பி","னள்ளிருள் விடியற் புள்ளெழப் போகிப்","புலிக்குரன் மத்த மொலிப்ப வாங்கி","யாம்பி வான்முகை யன்ன கூம்புமுகி","ழுறையமை தீந்தயிர் கலக்கி நுரைதெரிந்து","புகர்வாய்க் குழிசி பூஞ்சுமட் டிரீஇ","நாண்மோர் மாறு நன்மா மேனிச்","சிறுகுழை துயல்வருங் காதிற் பணைத்தோட்","குறுநெறிக் கொண்ட கூந்த லாய்மக","ளளைவிலை யுணவிற் கிளையுட னருத்தி","நெய்விலைக் கட்டிப் பசும்பொன் கொள்ளா","ளெருமை நல்லான் கருநாகு பெறூஉ","மடிவாய்க் கோவலர் குடிவயிற் சேப்பி","னிருங்கிளை ஞெண்டின் சிறுபார்ப் பன்ன","பசுந்தினை மூரல் பாலொடும் பெறுகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"Beyond that hostile ground lie the herders' huts with short legs bound with fodder leaves, brushwood doorways and doors of close-set sticks, where lambs are tied to short stakes with long ropes and the curved-faced ram and the white goat sleep inside a dung-heaped thorn fence; at dawn, when the birds rise from the thick dark, the herd girl, dark and fine, small earrings swaying, hair gathered in short curls, churns the sweet set curd whose top is closed like the white bud of a mushroom with a churning rod that growls like a tiger, lifts out the butter, carries the pot on a flower-pad on her head and sells the day's buttermilk, feeds her kin with food bought by buttermilk and will not take gold nuggets for her ghee but takes good buffalo and black heifers; at those herders' homes the bard will get green millet rice, like the small young of the crab, with milk.","n":"The herd girl's trade is exact: buttermilk buys food for her kin, but ghee she exchanges only for livestock, never for gold, so the household turns its best product back into the herd. The idyll records a barter economy in which cattle, not metal, are wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","ilvaazhkkai","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 147-168","lb":"PP4:147-168","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["மறிய","மறி",147,"maa","scene","lamb"],["குளகு","குளகு",148,"maram","scene","fodder leaves"],["துருவையொடு","துருவை",153,"maa","scene","ram"],["வெள்ளை","வெள்ளை",153,"maa","scene","white goat"],["புள்ளெழப்","புள்",155,"pul","scene",""],["யாம்பி","ஆம்பி",157,"pira","simile","mushroom"],["தீந்தயிர்","தயிர்",158,"unavu","scene","curd"],["நாண்மோர்","மோர்",160,"unavu","scene","buttermilk"],["லாய்மக","ஆய்மகள்",162,"seythi","scene","herd girl"],["ளளைவிலை","அளை",163,"unavu","scene","buttermilk"],["நெய்விலைக்","நெய்",164,"unavu","scene","ghee"],["ளெருமை","எருமை",165,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["கருநாகு","நாகு",165,"maa","scene","heifer"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",166,"seythi","scene","herders"],["ஞெண்டின்","ஞெண்டு",167,"pira","simile","crab"],["பசுந்தினை","தினை",168,"unavu","gift_food","millet"],["பாலொடும்","பால்",168,"unavu","gift_food","milk"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:169-183":{"book":"pattupattu","num":59,"t":["தொடுதோன் மரீஇய வடுவாழ் நோனடி","விழுத்தண் டூன்றிய மழுத்தின் வன்கை","யுறிக்கா வூர்ந்த மறுப்படு மயிர்ச்சுவன்","மேம்பா லுரைத்த வோரி யோங்குமிசைக்","கோட்டவுங் கொடியவும் விரைஇக் காட்ட","பல்பூ மிடைந்த படலைக் கண்ணி","யன்றம ருடுக்கைக் கூழா ரிடையன்","கன்றமர் நிரையொடு கானத் தல்கி","யந்நு ணவிர்புகை கமழக் கைம்முயன்று","ஞெலிகோற் கொண்ட பெருவிறல் ஞெகிழிச்","செந்தீத் தோட்ட கருந்துளைக் குழலி","னின்றீம் பாலை முனையிற் குமிழின்","புழற்கோட்டுத் தொடுத்த மரற்புரி நரம்பின்","வில்யா ழிசைக்கும் விரலெறி குறிஞ்சிப்","பல்காற் பறவை கிளைசெத் தோர்க்கும்"],"p":"","r":"The herdsman, his feet scarred by his sandals, his hand hard from leaning on his staff, his shoulder worn bare by the sling of pots, wearing a garland of many forest flowers from trees and creepers and living on gruel, camps in the forest with his calf-loving herd, twirls a fire-drill till the smoke smells sweet, burns the holes of his black flute, and when he tires of the sweet paalai tune on it plays the kurinji mode on a bow-lute strung with twisted marul fibre on a hollow kumizh stick, so that the many-legged bees listen, taking it for the hum of their kin.","n":"The herdsman makes his own instruments (he burns the flute's holes with a fire-drill and strings a bow-lute with fibre) and plays the paalai and kurinji modes far from those lands: the bard on his way to a patron is shown his own art in its plainest form. Paalai and kurinji here name melodies, not landscapes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalai","inbam","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 169-183","lb":"PP4:169-183","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["கோட்டவுங்","கோடு",173,"maram","ornament","tree flowers"],["கொடியவும்","கொடி",173,"maram","ornament","creeper flowers"],["கூழா","கூழ்",175,"unavu","scene","gruel"],["ரிடையன்","இடையன்",175,"seythi","scene","herdsman"],["நிரையொடு","நிரை",176,"maa","scene","herd"],["குழலி","குழல்",179,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["பாலை","பாலை",180,"yaazh","scene","paalai melody"],["குமிழின்","குமிழ்",180,"maram","scene",""],["மரற்புரி","மரல்",181,"maram","scene","bowstring hemp"],["வில்யா","வில் யாழ்",182,"yaazh","scene","bow-lute"],["குறிஞ்சிப்","குறிஞ்சி",182,"yaazh","scene","kurinji melody"],["பல்காற் பறவை","பல்கால் பறவை",183,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:184-196":{"book":"pattupattu","num":60,"t":["புல்லார் வியன்புலம் போகி முள்ளுடுத்","தெழுகா டோங்கிய தொழுவுடை வரைப்பிற்","பிடிக்கணத் தன்ன குதிருடை முன்றிற்","களிற்றுத்தாள் புரையுந் திரிமரப் பந்தர்க்","குறுஞ்சாட் டுருளையொடு கலப்பை சார்த்தி","நெடுஞ்சுவர் பறைந்த புகைசூழ் கொட்டிற்","பருவ வானத்துப் பாமழை கடுப்பக்","கருவை வேய்ந்த கவின்குடிச் சீறூர்","நெடுங்குரற் பூளைப் பூவி னன்ன","குறுந்தாள் வரகின் குறளவிழ்ச் சொன்றிப்","புகரிணர் வேங்கை வீகண் டன்ன","வவரை வான்புழுக் கட்டிப் பயில்வுற்","றின்சுவை மூரற் பெறுவிர் ஞாங்கர்க்"],"p":"","r":"Past that grassy wide land, in the farmers' small village fenced by tall thorn forest, with cattle pens, granaries like a herd of cow elephants in the yards, pandals on posts like the legs of tuskers, small cart wheels and ploughs leaning against them, smoke-filled sheds with weathered long walls and thatch of karuvai grass like the spread clouds of the rainy sky, the bard will get sweet rice of short-stalked varagu like the flowers of the long-spiked poolai, mixed with white-boiled avarai beans like a spotted cluster of vengai blossom.","n":"The dry-land farmers' wealth is measured with elephants (granaries like a herd of cows, posts like tuskers' legs), and the food is not paddy but millet and beans: plenty in this land is the plenty of rain-fed fields.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","virundhu","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 184-196","lb":"PP4:184-196","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["புல்லார்","புல்",184,"maram","scene","grass"],["பிடிக்கணத்","பிடி",186,"maa","simile","cow elephant"],["களிற்றுத்தாள்","களிறு",187,"maa","simile","tusker"],["கலப்பை","கலப்பை",188,"seythi","scene","plough"],["கருவை","கருவை",191,"maram","scene","thatching grass"],["பூளைப்","பூளை",192,"maram","simile",""],["வரகின்","வரகு",193,"unavu","gift_food","kodo millet"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",194,"maram","simile",""],["வவரை","அவரை",195,"unavu","gift_food","beans"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:197-205":{"book":"pattupattu","num":61,"t":["குடிநிறை வல்சிச் செஞ்சா லுழவர்","நடைநவில் பெரும்பகடு புதவிற் பூட்டிப்","பிடிவா யன்ன மடிவாய் நாஞ்சி","லுடுப்புமுக முழுக்கொழு மூழ்க வூன்றித்","தொடுப்பெறிந் துழுத துளர்படு துடவை","யரிபுகு பொழுதி னிரியல் போகி","வண்ணக் கடம்பி னறுமல ரன்ன","வளரிளம் பிள்ளை தழீஇக் குறுங்காற்","கறையணற் குறும்பூழ் கட்சிச் சேக்கும்"],"p":"","r":"Nearby, ploughmen of the red furrow, whose grain fills their households, yoke their great trained oxen at the gate and drive the curved plough, its mouth like a cow elephant's, deep into the earth, ploughing the field again and again till it crumbles, and when the reapers enter at harvest the short-legged, dark-throated quail flees and settles in the brush, holding close its growing chick that looks like a fragrant kadamba flower.","n":"The quail that flees the reapers with its chick is the only creature of the chapter shown protecting its young; in a love poem it might carry a sense of its own, but here, in praise, it is part of the harvest scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 197-205","lb":"PP4:197-205","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["லுழவர்","உழவர்",197,"seythi","scene","ploughmen"],["பெரும்பகடு","பகடு",198,"maa","scene","ox"],["பிடிவா","பிடி",199,"maa","simile","cow elephant"],["நாஞ்சி","நாஞ்சில்",199,"seythi","scene","plough"],["கடம்பி","கடம்பு",203,"maram","simile",""],["குறும்பூழ்","குறும்பூழ்",205,"pul","scene","quail"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:206-228":{"book":"pattupattu","num":62,"t":["வன்புல மிறந்த பின்றை மென்றோன்","மிதியுலைக் கொல்லன் முறிகொடிற் றன்ன","கவைத்தா ளலவ னளற்றளை சிதையப்","பைஞ்சாய் கொன்ற மண்படு மருப்பிற்","காரேறு பொருத கண்ணகன் செறுவி","னுழாஅ நுண்டொளி நிரவிய வினைஞர்","முடிநா றழுத்திய நெடுநீர்ச் செறுவிற்","களைஞர் தந்த கணைக்கா னெய்தற்","கட்கமழ் புதுப்பூ முனையின் முட்சினை","முகைசூழ் தகட்ட பிறழ்வாய் முள்ளிக்","கொடுங்கான் மாமலர் கொய்துகொண் டவண","பஞ்சாய்க் கோரை பல்லிற் சவட்டிப்","புணர்நார்ப் பெய்த புனைவின் கண்ணி","யீருடை யிருந்தலை யாரச் சூடிப்","பொன்காண் கட்டளை கடுப்பக் கண்பின்","புன்காய்ச் சுண்ணம் புடைத்த மார்பி","னிரும்புவடித் தன்ன மடியா மென்றோற்","கருங்கை வினைஞர் காதலஞ் சிறாஅற்","பழஞ்சோற் றமலை முனைஇ வரம்பிற்","புதுவை வேய்ந்த கவிகுடின் முன்றி","லவலெறி யுலக்கைப் பாடுவிறந் தயல","கொடுவாய்க் கிள்ளை படுபகை வெரூஉம்","நீங்கா யாணர் வாங்குகதிர்க் கழனிக்"],"p":"","r":"After the dry land come the wide wet fields: there a black bull, its horns muddied from goring the green sedge, has fought and broken the mud-holes of the crab whose forked legs are like a smith's broken tongs, workers have levelled the soft mud and pressed in seedlings from the bundles, and the dark-handed workers, skin taut like hammered iron, chests smeared with sedge pollen as gold is rubbed on a touchstone, tire of the neythal flowers the weeders bring, pluck the mulli's curved-stalked flowers, chew sedge into fibre to tie garlands for their wet heads, while their dear children, bored with lumps of old rice, pound flattened rice outside the new-thatched huts on the ridges so loudly that the curved-beaked parrot fears it as an enemy, in fields of bending ears whose fresh yield never ends.","n":"The field labourers, dark-skinned like hammered iron, are given a toilet scene of their own (flowers chosen and rejected, garlands woven from sedge fibre, pollen on the chest), the kind of scene the court poems give to nobles; and their children are bored with old rice, the poem's quick sign of a land that has more than enough.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","naadu","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 206-228","lb":"PP4:206-228","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["கொல்லன்","கொல்லன்",207,"seythi","simile","smith"],["ளலவ","அலவன்",208,"pira","scene","crab"],["பைஞ்சாய்","சாய்",209,"maram","scene","sedge"],["காரேறு","ஏறு",210,"maa","scene","bull"],["வினைஞர்","வினைஞர்",211,"seythi","scene","field workers"],["களைஞர்","களைஞர்",213,"seythi","scene","weeders"],["னெய்தற்","நெய்தல்",213,"maram","ornament",""],["முள்ளிக்","முள்ளி",215,"maram","ornament",""],["பஞ்சாய்க்","பஞ்சாய்",217,"maram","ornament","sedge"],["கண்பின்","கண்பு",220,"maram","ornament","sedge pollen"],["பழஞ்சோற்","சோறு",224,"unavu","scene","rice"],["லவலெறி","அவல்",226,"unavu","scene","flattened rice"],["கிள்ளை","கிள்ளை",227,"pul","scene","parrot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:229-241":{"book":"pattupattu","num":63,"t":["கடுப்புடைப் பறவைச் சாதியன்ன","பைதற விளைந்த பெருஞ்செந் நெல்லின்","தூம்புடைத் திரடா டுமித்த வினைஞர்","பாம்புறை மருதி னோங்குசினை நீழற்","பலிபெறு வியன்கள மலிய வேற்றிக்","கணங்கொள் சுற்றமொடு கைபுணர்ந் தாடுந்","துணங்கையம் பூதந் துகிலுடுத் தவைபோற்","சிலம்பி வானூல் வலந்த மருங்கிற்","குழுமுநிலைப் போரின் முழுமுத றொலைச்சிப்","பகடூர் பிழிந்த பின்றைத் துகடப","வையுந் துரும்பு நீக்கிப் பைதறக்","குடகாற் றெறிந்த குப்பை வடபாற்","செம்பொன் மலையிற் சிறப்பத் தோன்றுந்"],"p":"","r":"The workers cut the round hollow stalks of the great red paddy, ripe like a swarm of stinging insects, heap them on the wide threshing floor that receives offerings in the shade of the tall-branched marutham where snakes live, the stacks wrapped in white spider-web like the spirits of the thunangai dance dressed in cloth, dancing hand in hand with their troop; then they pull down the stacks, drive oxen over them, sift out dust and straw, winnow in the west wind, and the heap of grain shines like the red-gold mountain of the north.","n":"The threshing floor receives offerings (பலிபெறு) and the stacks are seen as dancing spirits; the harvest is a sacred scene before it is a store of wealth, and the heap is measured against the golden mountain of the north.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","selvam","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 229-241","lb":"PP4:229-241","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["பறவைச்","பறவை",229,"pira","simile","wasp"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",230,"unavu","scene","paddy"],["மருதி","மருது",232,"maram","scene",""],["பாம்புறை","பாம்பு",232,"pira","scene","snake"],["பூதந்","பூதம்",235,"deyvam","simile","spirits"],["சிலம்பி","சிலம்பி",236,"pira","scene","spider"],["பகடூர்","பகடு",238,"maa","scene","ox"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:242-256":{"book":"pattupattu","num":64,"t":["தண்பணை தழீஇய தளரா விருக்கைப்","பகட்டுஆ ஈன்ற கொடுநடைக் குழவிக்","கவைத்தாம்பு தொடுத்த காழூன் றல்கு","லேணி யெய்தா நீணெடு மார்பின்","முகடுதுமித் தடுக்கிய பழம்பல் லுணவிற்","குமரி மூத்த கூடோங்கு நல்லிற்","றச்சச் சிறாஅர் நச்சப் புனைந்த","வூரா நற்றே ருருட்டிய புதல்வர்","தளர்நடை வருத்தம் வீட வலர்முலைச்","செவிலியம் பெண்டிர்த் தழீஇப் பாலார்ந்","தமளித் துஞ்சு மழகுடை நல்லிற்","றொல்பசி யறியாத் துளங்கா விருக்கை","மல்லற் பேரூர் மடியின் மடியா","வினைஞர் தந்த வெண்ணெல் வல்சி","மனைவா ழளகின் வாட்டொடும் பெறுகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"In the unfailing settlement set among cool fields, in good houses where the crooked-gaited calf of the buffalo cow is tied to a stake with a forked rope, whose tall granaries, beyond the reach of a ladder, have grown old unopened and are cut at the top to pour out old grain, where the boys, tired of toddling after pulling the toy chariot the carpenter's lads made for them, cling to their full-breasted nurses, drink milk and sleep in their beds, in the great rich town that has never known hunger, whose workers do not idle when others idle, the bard will get white rice that the workers bring with fried domestic fowl.","n":"The granaries have 'grown old as maidens' (குமரி மூத்த), never needing to be opened, and the town has never known hunger from of old (தொல்பசி அறியா, line 253): the phrase answers the bard's old hunger (பழம்பசி) of line 25, so the road ends its first stage where his want began.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","virundhu","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 242-256","lb":"PP4:242-256","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["பகட்டுஆ","பகட்டு ஆ",243,"maa","scene","buffalo cow"],["குழவிக்","குழவி",243,"maa","scene","calf"],["றச்சச்","தச்சர்",248,"seythi","scene","carpenters"],["செவிலியம்","செவிலி",251,"seythi","scene","nurse"],["வினைஞர்","வினைஞர்",255,"seythi","scene","field workers"],["வெண்ணெல்","வெண்ணெல்",255,"unavu","gift_food","white rice"],["ளகின்","அளகு",256,"pul","gift_food","hen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:257-262":{"book":"pattupattu","num":65,"t":["மழைவிளை யாடுங் கழைவள ரடுக்கத்","தணங்குடை யாளி தாக்கலிற் பலவுடன்","கணஞ்சால் வேழங் கதழ்வுற் றாஅங்","கெந்திரஞ் சிலைக்குந் துஞ்சாக் கம்பலை","விசய மடூஉம் புகைசூ ழாலைதொறுங்","கரும்பின் றீஞ்சாறு விரும்பினிர் மிசைமின்"],"p":"","r":"At every smoky shed where sugar is boiled, where the cane press roars with a never-sleeping din like a great herd of elephants stampeding together when a dread yali attacks on the bamboo slopes where the clouds play, the bard may drink the sweet juice of the cane as much as he likes.","n":"The terror of the hill (a herd of elephants fleeing the yali) is borrowed to measure the noise of the press, so a kurinji scene of danger becomes the sound of the wet land's plenty; the bard is not promised this but told to help himself (மிசைமின்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 257-262","lb":"PP4:257-262","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["மழைவிளை","மழை",257,"pira","simile","cloud"],["கழைவள","கழை",257,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["யாளி","யாளி",258,"maa","simile",""],["வேழங்","வேழம்",259,"maa","simile","elephant"],["விசய","விசயம்",261,"unavu","scene","sugar"],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",262,"unavu","gift_food","sugarcane"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:263-270":{"book":"pattupattu","num":66,"t":["வேழ நிரைத்து வெண்கோடு விரைஇத்","தாழை முடித்துத் தருப்பை வேய்ந்த","குறியிறைக் குரம்பைப் பறியுடை முன்றிற்","கொடுங்காற் புன்னைக் கோடுதுமித் தியற்றிய","பைங்காய் தூங்கும் பாய்மணற் பந்த","ரிளையரு முதியருங் கிளையுடன் துவன்றிப்","புலவுநுனைப் பகழியுஞ் சிலையு மானச்","செவ்வரிக் கயலொடு பச்சிறாப் பிறழும்"],"p":"","r":"In the courtyards of low-eaved huts built of rows of reed bound with white stems and screwpine and thatched with darbha grass, with fishing baskets lying about, under pandals of spread sand made from cut branches of the curved punnai where green fruit hangs, young and old crowd together with their kin, and the red-striped kayal and the green prawns leap like arrows with flesh-smelling tips and like bows.","n":"வேழம் here is the reed, not the elephant of line 259. The sentence does not end at line 270: the leaping fish belong to a scene that continues in the next chapter, which this unit cannot cross.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 263-270","lb":"PP4:263-270","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["வேழ","வேழம்",263,"maram","scene","reed"],["தாழை","தாழை",264,"maram","scene","screwpine"],["தருப்பை","தருப்பை",264,"maram","scene","darbha grass"],["பறியுடை","பறி",265,"pira","scene","fishing basket"],["புன்னைக்","புன்னை",266,"maram","scene",""],["கயலொடு","கயல்",270,"pira","simile","carp"],["பச்சிறா","இறா",270,"pira","simile","prawn"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:4"},"pp:4:271-282":{"book":"pattupattu","num":67,"t":["மையிருங் குட்டத்து மகவொடு வழங்கிக்","கோடை நீடினுங் குறைபட லறியாத்","தோடாழ் குளத்த கோடுகாத் திருக்குங்","கொடுமுடி வலைஞர் குடிவயிற் சேப்பி","னவையா வரிசி யங்களித் துழவை","மலர்வாய்ப் பிழாவிற் புலர வாற்றிப்","பாம்புறை புற்றிற் குரும்பி யேய்க்கும்","பூம்புற நல்லடை யளைஇத் தேம்பட","வெல்லையு மிரவு மிருமறை கழிப்பி","வல்வாய்ச் சாடியின் வழைச்சற விளைந்த","வெந்நீர ரரியல் விரலலை நறும்பிழி","தண்மீன் சூட்டொடு தளர்தலும் பெறுகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"At the settlement of the net-fishers who guard the banks of a deep tank that never dwindles even in a long summer, moving with their children through its dark pool, the guide promises the bards a toddy made from unpounded rice mash cooled in wide-mouthed baskets, mixed with malt whose flowered crust looks like the grubs in a snake's anthill, left two turns of day and night, matured in strong-mouthed jars, strained hot and squeezed by hand, served with roasted fish, until they stagger.","n":"The brew is given step by step (mash, cool, mix in malt, ferment, mature in jars, strain, squeeze), so the guide's knowledge of how the poorest folk of the road make their drink is itself part of the promise that the road will feed the bard. இருமறை கழிப்பி (279) I take as two turns of day and night; it could also mean two coverings of the jar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","kal","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 271-282","lb":"PP4:271-282","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["குளத்த","குளம்",273,"pira","scene",""],["வலைஞர்","வலைஞர்",274,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["வரிசி","அரிசி",275,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["குரும்பி","குரும்பி",277,"pira","simile","anthill grubs"],["நல்லடை","அடை",278,"unavu","gift_food","malt"],["ரரியல்","அரியல்",281,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["தண்மீன்","மீன்",282,"pira","gift_food","fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:5"},"pp:4:283-296":{"book":"pattupattu","num":68,"t":["பச்சூன் பெய்த கவல்பிணி பைந்தோற்","கோள்வல் பாண்மகன் றலைவலித் தியாத்த","நெடுங்கழைத் தூண்டி னடுங்கநாண் கொளீஇக்","கொடுவா யிரும்பின் மடிதலை புலம்பப்","பொதியிரை கதுவிய போழ்வாய் வாளை","நீர்நணிப் பிரம்பி னடுங்குநிழல் வெரூஉம்","நீத்துடை நெருங்கயந் தீப்பட மலர்ந்த","கடவு ளொண்பூ வடைத லோம்பி","யுறைகான் மாறிய வோங்குயர் நனந்தலை","யகலிரு வானத்துக் குறைவி லேய்ப்ப","வரக்கிதழ்க் குவளையொடு நீல நீடி","முரட்பூ மலிந்த முதுநீர்ப் பொய்கைக்","குறுந ரிட்ட கூம்புவிடு பன்மலர்","பெருநாள ளமையத்துப் பிணையினிர் கழிமின்"],"p":"","r":"A fisher-bard skilled at catching ties his line to a long bamboo rod with a green hide bag of raw-meat bait, and the split-mouthed vaalai that has seized the bait on the bent iron hook shies from the trembling shadow of the cane by the water; in the old deep pond thick with red-petalled kuvalai and blue lilies, where the god's bright flower that has bloomed like fire is left unplucked and the whole is like the wide sky at its fullness after the rain, the guide tells the bards to wear the many opened flowers the pluckers have thrown down, on the great festival day, and pass on.","n":"The lotus is called the god's bright flower (கடவுள் ஒண்பூ) and is not taken (அடைதல் ஓம்பி), while the bards are told to wear the flowers that others have dropped: even flowers on the road follow a rule of what may be taken. Lines 291 and 292 compare something to the wide sky 'without lack' after the rain has stopped; I read the pond at its fullest, but the tenor is not fixed by the words, and the angler's and the fish's parts in 283 to 288 may belong to one image of the pond rather than to an act of fishing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","vazhipaadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 283-296","lb":"PP4:283-296","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["பாண்மகன்","பாண்மகன்",284,"seythi","scene","fisher"],["நெடுங்கழைத்","கழை",285,"maram","scene","bamboo rod"],["வாளை","வாளை",287,"pira","scene","vaalai fish"],["பிரம்பி","பிரம்பு",288,"maram","scene","cane"],["ளொண்பூ","பூ",290,"maram","scene","lotus"],["குவளையொடு","குவளை",293,"maram","scene",""],["நீல","நீலம்",293,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["பொய்கைக்","பொய்கை",294,"pira","scene",""],["குறுந","குறுநர்",295,"seythi","scene","flower-pluckers"],["பன்மலர்","மலர்",295,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:5"},"pp:4:297-310":{"book":"pattupattu","num":69,"t":["செழுங்கன் றியாத்த சிறுதாட் பந்தர்ப்","பைஞ்சேறு மெழுகிய படிவ நன்னகர்","மனையுறை கோழியொடு ஞமலி துன்னாது","வளைவாய்க் கிள்ளை மறைவிளி பயிற்றும்","மறைகாப் பாள ருறைபதிச் சேப்பிற்","பெருநல் வானத்து வடவயின் விளங்குஞ்","சிறுமீன் புரையுங் கற்பி னறுநுதல்","வளைக்கை மகடூஉ வயினறிந் தட்ட","சுடர்க்கடைப் பறவைப் பெயர்ப்படு வத்தஞ்","சேதா நறுமோர் வெண்ணெயின் மாதுளத்","துருப்புறு பசுங்காய்ப் போழொடு கறிகலந்து","கஞ்சக நறுமுறி யளை இப் பைந்துணர்","நெடுமரக் கொக்கின் னறுவடி விதிர்த்த","தகைமாண் காடியின் வகைபடப் பெறுகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"In the good houses of those who keep the vows, with a low pandal where the fat calf is tied and floors smeared with fresh dung, which the house fowl and the dog do not approach, where the hook-beaked parrot is taught the secret recitation, if the bards stay in the village of the guardians of the Veda, the bangled wife of fragrant brow, chaste like the small star that shines in the north, will knowingly cook for them the rice named after a bird and serve it with the fragrant buttermilk and butter of the red cow, slices of young pomegranate fried with pepper and mixed with fragrant leaves, and pieces of tender mango from the tall tree in fine pickle.","n":"The street is marked by what keeps away as much as by what is there: the fowl and the dog do not come near, and even the parrot repeats the recitation (மறைவிளி), so the Veda is heard from the birds. 'The rice named after a bird' (பறவைப் பெயர்ப்படு வத்தம்) is given only as a riddle; I take it as the swan-named rice. The cook is the wife herself, praised for chastity in the same breath as for knowing what to cook (வயினறிந்து அட்ட).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","virundhu","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 297-310","lb":"PP4:297-310","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["செழுங்கன்","கன்று",297,"maa","scene","calf"],["பைஞ்சேறு","சேறு",298,"pira","scene","cow dung"],["கோழியொடு","கோழி",299,"pul","absent","fowl"],["ஞமலி","ஞமலி",299,"maa","absent","dog"],["கிள்ளை","கிள்ளை",300,"pul","scene","parrot"],["வத்தஞ்","வத்தம்",305,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["சேதா","சே ஆ",306,"maa","scene","red cow"],["நறுமோர்","மோர்",306,"unavu","gift_food","buttermilk"],["வெண்ணெயின்","வெண்ணெய்",306,"unavu","gift_food",""],["மாதுளத்","மாதுளம்",306,"maram","gift_food","pomegranate"],["கறிகலந்து","கறி",307,"unavu","gift_food","pepper"],["நறுமுறி","முறி",308,"maram","gift_food","leaves"],["கொக்கின்","கொக்கு",309,"maram","gift_food","mango"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:5"},"pp:4:311-335":{"book":"pattupattu","num":70,"t":["வண்ட லாயமொ டுண்டுறைத் தலைஇப்","புனலாடு மகளி ரிட்ட பொலங்குழை","யிரைதேர் மணிச்சிர லிரைசெத் தெறிந்தெனப்","புள்ளார் பெண்ணைப் புலம்புமடற் செல்லாது","கேள்வி யந்தண ரருங்கட னிறுத்த","வேள்வித் தூணத் தசைஇ யவன","ரோதிம விளக்கி னுயர்மிசைக் கொண்ட","வைகுறு மீனிற் பைபயத் தோன்றும்","நீர்ப்பெயற் றெல்லைப் போகிப் பாற்கேழ்","வாலுளைப் புரவியொடு வடவளந் தரூஉம்","நாவாய் சூழ்ந்த நளிநீர்ப் படப்பை","மாட மோங்கிய மணன்மலி மறுகிற்","பரதர் மலிந்த பல்வேறு தெருவிற்","சிலதர் காக்குஞ் சேணுயர் வரைப்பி","னெல்லுழு பகட்டொடு கறவை துன்னா","ஏழகத் தகரோ டெகினங் கொட்குங்","கூழுடை நல்லிற் கொடும்பூண் மகளிர்","கொன்றை மென்சினைப் பனிதவழ்பவை போற்","பைங்கா ழல்கு னுண்டுகி னுடங்க","மால்வரைச் சிலம்பின் மகிழ்சிறந் தாலும்","பீலி மஞ்ஞையி னியலிக் கால","தமனியப் பொற்சிலம் பொலிப்ப வுயர்நிலை","வான்றோய் மாடத்து வரிப்பந் தசைஇக்","கைபுனை குறுந்தொடி தத்தப் பைபய","முத்த வார்மணற் பொற்கழங் காடும்"],"p":"","r":"When girls bathing with their playmates at the drinking ford let fall a golden earring, the jewel-coloured kingfisher hunting prey strikes it for a fish, cannot carry it to the lonely frond of the bird-filled palmyra and rests on the sacrificial pillar set up by Brahmins of learning who paid their hard debt of sacrifice, showing slowly like the dawn star above the Yavanas' swan lamp; past the bounds of Neerpeyarru, by the watered gardens crowded with ships that bring milk-white horses and northern wealth, in sandy streets of high mansions filled with traders and guarded by servants, in rich houses where the plough-ox and the milch cow do not come but rams and swans wander, girls with curved jewels, their fine cloth swaying like dew creeping along the soft kondrai branch, move like peacocks dancing on a great hill, gold anklets ringing, and after the striped ball play golden kazhangu on the pearl sand of their sky-touching terraces.","n":"The port is marked by what does not belong to the farm: the plough-ox and the milch cow keep away from its houses while rams and swans walk there, and ships bring horses from the north. One sentence carries the earring from a bathing girl through a kingfisher and a palmyra to a Brahmin sacrificial post beside a Yavana lamp, so the poem's single image joins the port's peoples: women at play, priests at sacrifice, foreign craftsmen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","azhagu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 311-335","lb":"PP4:311-335","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["பொலங்குழை","குழை",312,"pira","ornament","earring"],["மணிச்சிர","சிரல்",313,"pul","scene","kingfisher"],["புள்ளார்","புள்",314,"pul","scene",""],["பெண்ணைப்","பெண்ணை",314,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["யந்தண","அந்தணர்",315,"seythi","scene",""],["புரவியொடு","புரவி",320,"maa","scene","horse"],["பரதர்","பரதர்",323,"seythi","scene","traders"],["பகட்டொடு","பகடு",325,"maa","absent","plough-ox"],["கறவை","கறவை",325,"maa","absent","milch cow"],["தகரோ","தகர்",326,"maa","scene","ram"],["டெகினங்","எகினம்",326,"pul","scene","swan"],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",328,"maram","simile",""],["மஞ்ஞையி","மஞ்ஞை",331,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[["town","நீர்ப்பெயற்",319]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:5"},"pp:4:336-345":{"book":"pattupattu","num":71,"t":["பட்டின மருங்கி னசையின் முட்டில்","பைங்கொடி நுடங்கும் பலர்புகு வாயிற்","செம்பூத் தூய செதுக்குடை முன்றில்","கள்ளடு மகளிர் வள்ள நுடக்கிய","வார்ந்துகு சின்னீர் வழிந்த குழம்பி","னீர்ஞ்சே றாடிய விரும்பல் குட்டிப்","பன்மயிர்ப் பிணவொடு பாயம் போகாது","நென்மா வல்சி தீற்றிப் பன்னாட்","குழிநிறுத் தோம்பிய குறுந்தா ளேற்றைக்","கொழுநிணத் தடியொடு கூர்நறாப் பெறுகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"Near the port, at the toddy-sellers' houses whose green flags sway over doorways many enter and whose swept courtyards are strewn with red flowers, the guide promises the bards strong toddy with chunks of fat from the short-legged boar that, once a dark piglet wallowing in the mud made by the little water the brewing women spilled washing their bowls, was kept many days in a pit, fed on rice flour and not let go to its hairy sow.","n":"The boar's whole life is told in three lines, from piglet in the brewers' runoff to a pit where it is fed rice flour and kept from its sow (பாயம் போகாது), so the guests' meat is shown as raised for them. கள்ளடு (339) is a genuine அடு, to brew, not the comitative.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","pulaal","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 336-345","lb":"PP4:336-345","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["செம்பூத்","பூ",338,"maram","scene",""],["கள்ளடு மகளிர்","கள் அடு மகளிர்",339,"seythi","scene","toddy brewers"],["குட்டிப்","குட்டி",341,"maa","scene","piglet"],["பிணவொடு","பிணவு",342,"maa","scene","sow"],["நென்மா","நெல் மா",343,"unavu","scene","rice flour"],["ளேற்றைக்","ஏற்றை",344,"maa","gift_food","boar"],["கொழுநிணத்","நிணம்",345,"unavu","gift_food","fat"],["கூர்நறாப்","நறவு",345,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:5"},"pp:4:346-362":{"book":"pattupattu","num":72,"t":["வான மூன்றிய மதலை போல","வேணி சாத்திய வேற்றருஞ் சென்னி","விண்பொர நிவந்த வேயா மாடத்","திரவின் மாட்டிய இலங்குசுடர் ஞெகிழி","யுரவுநீ ரழுவத் தோடுகலங் கரையுந்","துறைபிறக் கொழியப் போகிக் கறையடிக்","குன்றுறழ் யானை மருங்கு லேய்க்கும்","வண்டோட்டுத் தெங்கின் வாடுமடல் வேய்ந்த","மஞ்சண் முன்றின் மணநாறு படப்பைத்","தண்டலை யுழவர் தனிமனைச் சேப்பிற்","றாழ்கோட் பலவின் சூழ்சுளைப் பெரும்பழம்","வீழி றாழைக் குழவித் தீம்நீர்க்","கவைமுலை யிரும்பிடிக் கவுண்மருப் பேய்க்குங்","குலைமுதிர் வாழைக் கூனி வெண்பழந்","திரளரைப் பெண்ணை நுங்கொடு பிறவுந்","தீம்பஃ றார முனையிற் சேம்பின்","முளைப்புற முதிர்கிழங் கார்குவிர் பகற்பெயன்"],"p":"","r":"Passing the harbour where at night a bright torch hung on an unroofed tower, rising to fight the sky like a pillar propping it, its hard top reached by ladders, calls the ships sailing the rough sea, the bards come to the lone houses of the grove farmers, roofed with withering fronds of coconut whose trunk is like the flank of a hill-like elephant, with yellow courtyards and fragrant gardens; there they will eat the big jackfruit of clustered segments from the low branch, the sweet water of tender coconut, the curved white fruit of ripe banana bunches like the cheek-tusks of a dark she-elephant, palmyra nungu and other sweet things, and when tired of these, the full tubers of the chembu.","n":"The torch calls the ships (ஓடுகலம் கரையும்), with the verb used for a crow that calls guests, so the port too has a way of inviting strangers. The elephant is the measure twice, for the coconut trunk and for the curve of the banana: the farmers' grove is described in the terms of the hill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","virundhu","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 346-362","lb":"PP4:346-362","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",352,"maa","simile","elephant"],["தெங்கின்","தெங்கு",353,"maram","scene","coconut"],["மஞ்சண்","மஞ்சள்",354,"maram","scene","turmeric"],["யுழவர்","உழவர்",355,"seythi","scene",""],["பலவின்","பலா",356,"maram","gift_food","jackfruit"],["றாழைக்","தாழை",357,"maram","gift_food","coconut"],["யிரும்பிடிக்","பிடி",358,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["வாழைக்","வாழை",359,"maram","gift_food","banana"],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",360,"maram","gift_food","palmyra"],["சேம்பின்","சேம்பு",361,"maram","gift_food","colocasia"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:5"},"pp:4:363-392":{"book":"pattupattu","num":73,"t":["மழைவீழ்ந் தன்ன மாத்தாட் கமுகின்","புடைசூழ் தெங்கின் முப்புடைத் திரள்கா","யாறுசெல் வம்பலர் காய்பசி தீரச்","சோறடு குழிசி யிளக விழூஉம்","வீயா யாணர் வளங்கெழு பாக்கத்துப்","பன்மர நீளிடைப் போகி நன்னகர்","விண்டோய் மாடத்து விளங்குசுவ ருடுத்த","வாடா வள்ளியின் வளம்பல தரூஉம்","நாடுபல கழிந்த பின்றை நீடுகுலைக்","காந்தளஞ் சிலம்பிற் களிறுபடிந் தாங்குப்","பாம்பணைப் பள்ளி யமர்ந்தோ னாங்கண்","வெயினுழை பறியாக் குயினுழை பொதும்பர்க்","குறுங்காற் காஞ்சி சுற்றிய நெடுங்கொடிப்","பாசிலைக் குருகின் புன்புற வரிப்பூக்","காரகற் கூவியர் பாகொடு பிடித்த","விழைசூழ் வட்டம் பால்கலந் தவைபோ","னிழறாழ் வார்மண னீர்முகத் துறைப்பப்","புனல்கால் கழீஇய பொழிறொறுந் திரள்காற்","சோலைக் கமுகின் சூல்வயிற் றன்ன","நீலப் பைங்குடந் தொலைச்சி நாளும்","பெருமகி ழிருக்கை மரீஇச் சிறுகோட்டுக்","குழவித் திங்கட் கோணேர்ந் தாங்குச்","சுறவுவா யமைத்த சுரும்புசூழ் சுடர்நுத","னறவுபெயர்த் தமர்த்த நல்லெழின் மழைக்கண்","மடவரன் மகளிரொடு பகல்விளை யாடிப்","பெறற்கருந் தொல்சீர்த் துறக்க மேய்க்கும்","பொய்யா மரபிற் பூமலி பெருந்துறைச்","செவ்விகொள் பவரோ டசைஇ யவ்வயி","னருந்திறற் கடவுள் வாழ்த்திச் சிறிதுநுங்","கருங்கோட் டின்னிய மியக்கினிர் கழிமின்"],"p":"","r":"Through the long way of many trees in never-failing rich villages where tall areca palms stand like streaks of daytime rain among coconuts whose three-sided nuts fall and break the pots cooking rice for hungry travellers, and past many lands of good towns whose sky-touching walls are clothed with unfading painted creepers, the bards come to where the one on the serpent bed lies like a bull elephant lying on a kaanthal slope; there, in groves no sunlight enters but the cuckoo does, where kuruku creepers wound on kaanji trees drop striped flowers like the round sweets that cake-makers shape with treacle and dip in milk onto the shaded sand at the water's edge, they may empty blue pots of toddy shaped like the areca's swelling spathe, sit in great joy, play by day with young women whose foreheads carry the shark-mouth ornament like the young crescent and whose eyes are drowsy with drink, rest at the great flowered ford of unfailing custom, like heaven itself, with those who wait for the hour, then praise the god of great strength, play a little on their black-horned instruments, and pass on.","n":"The god's town is not named; the serpent bed and the bull elephant lying on the slope identify him, and the bards' worship is brief: praise him, play a little, go on. The groves' pleasures (toddy, play with women) and the shrine sit in one sentence, likened as a whole to heaven (துறக்கம் ஏய்க்கும், 388), a line the Pattinappalai repeats at its line 104. In line 378 போல் stands as போ before னிழல் by sandhi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thalam","vazhipaadu","kal","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 363-392","lb":"PP4:363-392","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["கமுகின்","கமுகு",363,"maram","scene","areca"],["தெங்கின்","தெங்கு",364,"maram","scene","coconut"],["வம்பலர்","வம்பலர்",365,"seythi","scene","travellers"],["காந்தளஞ்","காந்தள்",372,"maram","simile",""],["களிறுபடிந்","களிறு",372,"maa","simile","bull elephant"],["பாம்பணைப் பள்ளி","பாம்பணைப் பள்ளி அமர்ந்தோன்",373,"deyvam","scene","Vishnu"],["குயினுழை","குயில்",374,"pul","scene","cuckoo"],["காஞ்சி","காஞ்சி",375,"maram","scene",""],["குருகின்","குருகு",376,"maram","simile","kuruku creeper"],["கூவியர்","கூவியர்",377,"seythi","simile","cake-makers"],["கமுகின்","கமுகு",381,"maram","simile","areca"],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",385,"pira","scene","bee"],["னறவு","நறவு",386,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["கடவு","கடவுள்",391,"deyvam","scene",""],["கருங்கோட் டின்னிய","இன்னியம்",392,"yaazh","scene","instrument"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:5"},"pp:4:393-411":{"book":"pattupattu","num":74,"t":["காழோ ரிகழ்பதம் நோக்கிக் கீழ","நெடுங்கை யானை நெய்ம்மிதி கவளங்","கடுஞ்சூன் மந்தி கவருங் காவிற்","களிறுகத னடக்கிய வெளிறில் கந்திற்","றிண்டேர் குழித்த குண்டுநெடுந் தெருவிற்","படைதொலை பறியா மைந்துமலி பெரும்புகழ்க்","கடைகால் யாத்த பல்குடி கெழீஇக்","கொடையுங் கோளும் வழங்குநர்த் தடுத்த","வடையா வாயின் மிளைசூழ் படப்பை","நீனிற வுருவி னெடியோன் கொப்பூழ்","நான்முக வொருவற் பயந்த பல்லிதழ்த்","தாமரைப் பொகுட்டிற் காண்வரத் தோன்றிச்","சுடும ணோங்கிய நெடுநகர் வரைப்பி","னிழுமென் புள்ளி னீண்டுகிளைத் தொழுதிக்","கொழுமென் சினைய கோளி யுள்ளும்","பழமீக் கூறும் பலாஅப் போலப்","புலவுக் கடலுடுத்த வானஞ் சூடிய","மலர்தலை யுலகத் துள்ளும் பலர்தொழ","விழவுமேம் பட்ட பழவிறன் மூதூ"],"p":"","r":"Kanchi: groves where a big-bellied female monkey snatches the ghee-trodden rice ball of the long-trunked elephant while its keepers look away, posts where tuskers' rage is tamed, deep long streets cut by strong chariots, many families of great fame whose strength never knew defeat, gates never shut, crowded with those who give and take, gardens ringed with thorn, a city of tall brick mansions that rises beautiful like the pericarp of the many-petalled lotus that bore the four-faced one from the navel of the tall blue god; as the jackfruit is praised for its fruit among the rich-branched trees that bear without flowering where flocks of birds gather, so among the cities of this wide world, clothed in the smelling sea and crowned by the sky, this old town of ancient valour, worshipped by many, excels in festivals.","n":"The lotus from Vishnu's navel is a picture of the city's plan: the walled town is the flower and its great houses rise in it like the pericarp (பொகுட்டு); the Paripadal fragment on Madurai uses the same lotus, with petals for streets and the pericarp for the palace. The jackfruit 'praised for its fruit' among trees that fruit without flowering (கோளி) gives the town a rank among cities rather than a picture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","pugazh","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 393-411","lb":"PP4:393-411","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["காழோ","காழோர்",393,"seythi","scene","elephant keepers"],["யானை","யானை",394,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கவளங்","கவளம்",394,"unavu","scene","rice ball"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",395,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["களிறு","களிறு",396,"maa","scene",""],["னெடியோன்","நெடியோன்",402,"deyvam","simile","Vishnu"],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",404,"maram","simile","lotus"],["புள்ளி","புள்",406,"pul","simile",""],["கோளி","கோளி",407,"maram","simile","trees fruiting without flowers"],["பலாஅப்","பலா",408,"maram","simile","jackfruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:5"},"pp:4:412-435":{"book":"pattupattu","num":75,"t":["ரவ்வாய் வளர்பிறைச் சூடிச் செவ்வா","யந்தி வானத் தாடுமழை கடுப்ப","வெண்கோட் டிரும்பிணங் குருதி யீர்ப்ப","ஈரைம் பதின்மரும் பொருதுகளத் தவியப்","பேரமர்க் கடந்த கொடுஞ்சி நெடுந்தே","ராராச் செருவி னைவர் போல","வடங்காத் தானையோ டுடன்றுமேல் வந்த","வொன்னாத் தெவ்வ ருலைவிடத் தார்த்துக்","கச்சி யோனே கைவண் டோன்ற","னச்சிச் சென்றோர்க் கேம மாகிய","வளியுந் தெறலு மெளிய வாகலின்","மலைந்தோர் தேஎம் மன்றம் பாழ்பட","நயந்தோர் தேஎம் நன்பொன் பூப்ப","நட்புக்கொளல் வேண்டி நயந்திசி னோருந்","துப்புக்கொளல் வேண்டிய துணையி லோருங்","கல்வீ ழருவி கடற்படர்ந் தாங்குப்","பல்வேறு வகையிற் பணிந்த மன்ன","ரிமையவ ருறையுஞ் சிமையச் செவ்வரை","வெண்டிரை கிழித்த விளங்குசுடர் நெடுங்கோட்டுப்","பொன்கொழித் திழிதரும் போக்கருங் கங்கைப்","பெருநீர் போகு மிரியன் மாக்க","ளருமரப் பாணியிற் தூங்கி யாங்குத்","தொய்யா வெறுக்கையொடு துவன்றுபு குழீஇச்","செவ்வி பார்க்குஞ் செழுநகர் முற்றத்துப்"],"p":"","r":"Like the Five whose banner-crowned tall chariot crossed the great war that never ended, when all the hundred fell fighting on a field where the bodies of white-tusked elephants dragged blood like the dancing clouds of a red evening sky crowned with the waxing crescent, he roared at the place where the hostile foes who came against him with countless armies were destroyed: he is the lord of Kachchi, open-handed, a refuge to those who come in want; because his grace and his wrath are both ready, the lands of those who fought him become ruined commons and the lands of those who love him bloom with gold, and kings who want his friendship and kings without allies who want his help, bowing in many ways like waterfalls running to the sea, crowd with unfailing wealth, like people waiting at the hard-to-cross Ganges ford for the boat that cleaves its white waves, watching for their hour in the courtyard of his rich city.","n":"Lines 422 to 424 state the king's double face in one balanced pair, அளியும் தெறலும் எளிய: the same readiness that makes him a refuge makes him dangerous, and the poem shows the result in land, ruined commons for foes and gold-blooming country for friends. The Five (ஐவர்) and the hundred (ஈரைம் பதின்மர்) are the Bharata war, used as a measure of victory.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 412-435","lb":"PP4:412-435","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["டிரும்பிணங்","பிணம்",414,"maa","scene","elephant corpses"],["தாடுமழை","மழை",413,"pira","simile","cloud"],["ழருவி","அருவி",427,"pira","simile","waterfall"]],"en":[["town","கச்சி",420],["patron","கச்சி யோனே",420],["river","கங்கைப்",431],["hill","சிமையச் செவ்வரை",429]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:5"},"pp:4:436-447":{"book":"pattupattu","num":76,"t":["பெருங்கை யானைக் கொடுந்தொடி படுக்குங்","கருங்கைக் கொல்ல னிரும்புவிசைத் தெறிந்த","கூடத் திண்ணிசை வெரீஇ மாடத்","திறையுறை புறவின் செங்காற் சேவ","லின்றுயி லிரியும் பொன்றுஞ்சு வியனகர்க்","குணகடல் வரைப்பின் முந்நீர் நாப்பட்","பகல்செய் மண்டிலம் பாரித் தாங்கு","முறைவேண்டு நர்க்குங் குறைவேண்டு நர்க்கும்","வேண்டுப வேண்டுப வேண்டினர்க் கருளி","யிடைத்தெரிந் துணரு மிருடீர் காட்சிக்","கொடைக்கட னிறுத்த கூம்பா வுள்ளத்","துரும்பில் சுற்றமோ டிருந்தோற் குறுகிப்"],"p":"","r":"In his great city where the red-legged cock-dove nesting in the eaves of the mansion starts from its sweet sleep, afraid of the hard ringing of the smithy where the dark-handed smith hammers the curved rings for the great-trunked elephants' tusks, he sits among his flawless kin like the sun spreading its disc over the middle of the eastern sea, granting to those who seek justice and to those who seek their needs whatever they ask, with a clear sight that discerns between them and a heart that never closes in the debt of giving: approach him.","n":"முறைவேண்டுநர் and குறைவேண்டுநர் (443) are those who come for a judgment and those who come for a need; the king answers both, and the clear sight that discerns between them (இடைத்தெரிந்து உணரும் இருள்தீர் காட்சி) is what makes the giving just. The frightened dove in the eaves lets the smithy's war-work be heard inside the king's house.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","eegai","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 436-447","lb":"PP4:436-447","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["யானைக்","யானை",436,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கொல்ல","கொல்லன்",437,"seythi","scene","smith"],["புறவின்","புறவு",439,"pul","scene","dove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:5"},"pp:4:448-461":{"book":"pattupattu","num":77,"t":["பொறிவரிப் புகர்முகந் தாக்கிய வயமான்","கொடுவரிக் குருளை கொளவேட் டாங்குப்","புலவர் பூண்கட னாற்றிப் பகைவர்","கடிமதி லெறிந்து குடுமி கொள்ளும்","வென்றி யல்லது வினையுடம் படினு","மொன்றல் செல்லா வுரவுவாட் டடக்கைக்","கொண்டி யுண்டித் தொண்டையோர் மருக","மள்ளர் மள்ள மறவர் மறவ","செல்வர் செல்வ செருமேம் படுந","வெண்டிரைப் பரப்பிற் கடுஞ்சூர்க் கொன்ற","பைம்பூட் சேஎய் பயந்தமா மோட்டுத்","துணங்கையஞ் செல்விக் கணங்குநொடித் தாங்குத்","தண்டா வீகைநின் பெரும்பெய ரேத்தி","வந்தேன் பெரும வாழிய நெடிதென"],"p":"","r":"Like the striped tiger cub that longs to seize the spotted-faced elephant its mother has struck, you pay the debt owed to poets and break your enemies' guarded walls to take their crowns; your strong sword-hand will not agree to any work but victory; descendant of the Tondaiyar who live on plunder, warrior of warriors, hero of heroes, lord of the wealthy, foremost in battle: as spirits snap their fingers before the goddess of the thunangai dance, the big-bellied mother of the red one with green jewels who killed fierce Sur in the white-waved sea, so praising your great name of unceasing giving I have come, lord; may you live long: so the guide tells the bard to say.","n":"The guide gives the bard the very words of his praise (மள்ளர் மள்ள, மறவர் மறவ, செல்வர் செல்வ) and the formula of arrival (வந்தேன் பெரும வாழிய நெடிது). கொண்டி உண்டித் தொண்டையோர் (454) says the Tondaiyar live on plunder, so what the king gives the bard is drawn from what he takes in war. Lines 457 to 459 are hard: I read அணங்கு நொடித்தாங்கு as spirits snapping their fingers before Murugan's mother, the goddess of the thunangai, but who snaps and for whom is not fixed by the words, and the tiger simile may belong to the line before.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","maram","eegai","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 448-461","lb":"PP4:448-461","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["புகர்முகந்","புகர்முகம்",448,"maa","simile","elephant"],["வயமான்","வயமான்",448,"maa","simile","tiger"],["சேஎய்","சேய்",458,"deyvam","simile","Murugan"],["செல்விக்","செல்வி",459,"deyvam","simile","goddess"]],"en":[["patron","தொண்டையோர் மருக",454]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:5"},"pp:4:462-479":{"book":"pattupattu","num":78,"t":["விடனுடைப் பேரியாழ் முறையுளிக் கழிப்பிக்","கடனறி மரபிற் கைதொழூஉப் பழிச்சி","நின்னிலை தெரியா வளவை யந்நிலை","நாவலந் தண்பொழில் வீவின்று விளங்க","நில்லா வுலகத்து நிலைமை தூக்கி","யந்நிலை யணுகல் வேண்டி நின்னரைப்","பாசி யன்ன சிதர்வை நீக்கி","யாவி யன்ன அவிர்நூற் கலிங்க","மிரும்பே ரொக்கலொ டொருங்குட னுடீஇக்","கொடுவாள் கதுவிய வடுவாழ் நோன்கை","வல்லோ னட்ட பல்லூன் கொழுங்குறை","யரிசெத் துணங்கிய பெருஞ்செந் நெல்லின்","தெரிகொ ளரிசித் திரணெடும் புழுக்க","லருங்கடித் தீஞ்சுவை யமுதொடு பிறவும்","விருப்புடை மரபிற் கரப்புடை யடிசின்","மீன்பூத் தன்ன வான்கலம் பரப்பி","மகமுறை மகமுறை நோக்கி முகனமர்ந்","தானா விருப்பிற் றானின் றூட்டி"],"p":"","r":"When you have played the roomy great yaazh in due order and praised him with joined hands as custom knows, before you have finished telling your state he weighs the condition of this unlasting world and, wishing his own state to shine without end in the cool land of the naaval tree, strips from your waist the rag like moss and dresses you and your great family together in cloth bright as vapour, and with a glad face, looking on you again and again as on his children, stands and feeds you himself without tiring, spreading white bowls like flowering stars with rich pieces of many meats cooked by a skilled cook whose strong hand is scarred by the curved knife, long boiled grains of choice rice of the great red paddy, and sweet dishes guarded like nectar.","n":"The king acts before the bard has finished asking (நின்னிலை தெரியா அளவை), and the poem gives his reason as a reckoning: he weighs the unlasting world (நில்லா உலகத்து நிலைமை தூக்கி) and wants the state of undying fame. He feeds the bard himself, standing (தான் நின்று ஊட்டி), looking at him as at his own children (மகமுறை); the order of the gift (rags off, new cloth on, food) is the order of other gift poems.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","virundhu","nilaiyaamai","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 462-479","lb":"PP4:462-479","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["பேரியாழ்","யாழ்",462,"yaazh","scene",""],["பாசி","பாசி",468,"pira","simile","moss"],["பல்லூன்","ஊன்",472,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",473,"unavu","gift_food","red paddy"],["புழுக்க","புழுக்கல்",474,"unavu","gift_food","boiled rice"],["யமுதொடு","அமுது",475,"unavu","gift_food","sweet dishes"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:5"},"pp:4:480-500":{"book":"pattupattu","num":79,"t":["மங்குல் வானத்துத் திங்க ளேய்க்கு","மாடுவண் டிமிரா வழல்தவிர் தாமரை","நீடிரும் பித்தை பொலியச் சூட்டி","யுரவுக்கடல் முகந்த பருவ வானத்துப்","பகற்பெயற் றுளியின் மின்னுநிமிர்ந் தாங்குப்","புனையிருங் கதுப்பகம் பொலியப் பொன்னின்","றொடையமை மாலை விறலியர் மலைய","நூலோர் புகழ்ந்த மாட்சிய மால்கடல்","வளைகண் டன்ன வாலுளைப் புரவி","துணைபுணர் தொழில நால்குடன் பூட்டி","யரித்தேர் நல்கியு மமையான் செருத்தொலைத்","தொன்னாத் தெவ்வ ருலைவிடத் தொழித்த","விசும்புசெ லிவுளியொடு பசும்படை தரீஇ","யன்றே விடுக்குமவன் பரிசி லின்சீர்க்","கின்னர முரலு மணங்குடைச் சாரல்","மஞ்ஞை யாலு மரம்பயி லுறும்பிற்","கலைபாய்ந் துதிர்த்த மலர்வீழ் புறவின்","மந்தி சீக்கு மாதுஞ்சு முன்றிற்","செந்தீப் பேணிய முனிவர் வெண்கோட்டுக்","களிறுதரு விறகின் வேட்கு","மொளிறிலங் கருவிய மலைகிழ வோனே"],"p":"","r":"He crowns your thick hair with a golden lotus like the moon in the misty sky, round which no bee hums, and has the viraliyar wear golden garlands that shine in their dark hair like lightning darting through the daytime rain of a season sky that has drunk from the rough sea; not content with giving a striped chariot yoked with four mated horses whose white manes look like the conches of the great sea, praised by the horse books, he adds the sky-going steeds and green trappings he took from the foes he routed in war, and sends you off that very day with his gift: he is the lord of the hill of shining falls, where kinnaras hum on haunted slopes, peacocks dance in the thickets, flowers shaken down by leaping stags lie in the glades, the female monkey sweeps the yards where the beasts sleep, and the sages who tend the red fire sacrifice with firewood that white-tusked elephants bring.","n":"The poem ends naming the patron only as lord of the hill (மலைகிழவோனே), and the hill is described by its peace: beasts sleep in yards a monkey sweeps, and elephants carry firewood for the sages' fire, so the last picture of the war-king's land is of wild things serving a sacrifice. அன்றே விடுக்கும் (493) makes speed part of the gift. Line 481's அழல்தவிர் (our reading) against அழலவிர் (bright as flame) changes only the epithet of the golden lotus.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["eegai","parisil","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Perumpanatruppadai 480-500","lb":"PP4:480-500","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"தொண்டைமான் இளந்திரையன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",481,"maram","ornament","golden lotus"],["புரவி","புரவி",488,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["லிவுளியொடு","இவுளி",492,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["கின்னர","கின்னரம்",494,"pul","scene","kinnara"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",495,"pul","scene","peacock"],["கலைபாய்ந்","கலை",496,"maa","scene","stag"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",497,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["முனிவர்","முனிவர்",498,"seythi","scene","sages"],["களிறுதரு","களிறு",499,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["patron","மலைகிழ வோனே",500]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:5"},"pp:5:1-23":{"book":"pattupattu","num":80,"t":["நனந்தலை யுலகம் வளைஇ நேமியொடு","வலம்புரி பொறித்த மாதாங்கு தடக்கை","நீர்செல நிமிர்ந்த மாஅல் போலப்","பாடிமிழ் பனிக்கடல் பருகி வலனேர்பு","கோடுகொண் டெழுந்த கொடுஞ்செல வெழிலி","பெரும்பெயல் பொழிந்த சிறுபுன் மாலை","யருங்கடி மூதூர் மருங்கிற் போகி","யாழிசை யினவண் டார்ப்ப நெல்லொடு","நாழி கொண்ட நறுவீ முல்லை","யரும்பவி ழலரி தூஉய்க் கைதொழுது","பெருமுது பெண்டிர் விரிச்சி நிற்பச்","சிறுதாம்பு தொடுத்த பசலைக் கன்றி","னுறுதுய ரலமர னோக்கு யாய்மக","ணடுங்குசுவ லசைத்த கையள் கைய","கொடுங்கோற் கோவலர் பின்னின் றுய்த்தர","வின்னே வருகுவர் தாய ரென்போ","ணன்னர் நன்மொழி கேட்டன மதனா","னல்ல நல்லோர் வாய்ப்புட் டெவ்வர்","முனைகவர்ந்து கொண்ட திறையர் வினைமுடித்து","வருத றலைவர் வாய்வது நீநின்","பருவர லெவ்வங் களைமா யோயெனக்","காட்டவுங் காட்டவுங் காணாள்க லுழ்சிறந்து","பூப்போ லுண்கண் புலம்புமுத் துறைப்பக்"],"p":"","r":"As a cloud that has drunk the roaring cold sea rises to the right like Mal standing tall as the water was poured, with conch and discus in his hand, and rains heavily in the small, sad evening, old women go out to the edge of the guarded old town, scatter paddy and fresh mullai buds, join their hands and stand listening for a chance word; they hear a herd-girl, her hands on her shivering shoulders, tell a calf tied with a short rope and looking about in distress that its mothers will come soon, driven by the cowherds, and they tell the wife that the good word means her lord will surely return with tribute, his work done, so she should put away her grief; but however often they show her, she will not see it, and her flower-like eyes, overflowing, drop the pearls of loneliness.","n":"The calf is a near miss for ullurai and iraicchi: tied, distressed and promised its mothers' return, it matches the wife at several points, but the old women state the match themselves (அதனால் ... வருதல் தலைவர் வாய்வது), so condition 2 fails and the calf stays scene; the herd-girl's words matter as overheard speech, the omen, not as an image. The poem's first sentence is itself a sign, since the cloud rises on the right (வலனேர்பு), so the sky's omen comes before the street's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virichi","pirivu","maalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Mullaippattu 1-23","lb":"PP5:1-23","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம்பட்டினத்துப் பொன்வாணிகனார் மகனார் நப்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["pira","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மாஅல்","மால்",3,"deyvam","simile",""],["பனிக்கடல்","பனிக் கடல்",4,"pira","scene","sea"],["வெழிலி","எழிலி",5,"pira","simile","rain cloud"],["யாழிசை","யாழ் இசை",8,"yaazh","simile",""],["யினவண்","இன வண்டு",8,"pira","scene","bees"],["நெல்லொடு","நெல்",8,"unavu","gift_food",""],["முல்லை","முல்லை",9,"maram","gift_food",""],["கன்றி","கன்று",12,"maa","scene","calf"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",15,"seythi","scene","cowherds"],["பூப்போ","பூ",23,"maram","simile",""],["புலம்புமுத்","முத்து",23,"pira","simile","pearl"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","","grader_only",null,""]],"ch":"pp:6"},"pp:5:24-44":{"book":"pattupattu","num":81,"t":["கான்யாறு தழீஇய வகனெடும் புறவிற்","சேணாறு பிடவமொடு பைம்புத லெருக்கி","வேட்டுப்புழை யருப்ப மாட்டிக் காட்ட","விடுமுட் புரிசை யேமுற வளைஇப்","படுநீர்ப் புணரியிற் பரந்த பாடி","யுவலைக் கூரை யொழுகிய தெருவிற்","கவலை முற்றங் காவ னின்ற","தேம்படு கவுள சிறுகண் யானை","யோங்குநிலைக் கரும்பொடு கதிர்மிடைந் தியாத்த","வயல்விளை யின்குள குண்ணாது நுதறுடைத்","தயினுனை மருப்பிற்றங் கையிடைக் கொண்டெனக்","கவைமுட் கருவியின் வடமொழி பயிற்றிக்","கல்லா விளைஞர் கவளங் கைப்பக்","கற்றோய்த் துடுத்த படிவப் பார்ப்பான்","முக்கோ லசைநிலை கடுப்ப நற்போ","ரோடா வல்விற் றூணி நாற்றிக்","கூடங் குத்திக் கயிறுவாங் கிருக்கைப்","பூந்தலைக் குந்தங் குத்திக் கிடுகுநிரைத்து","வாங்குவி லரண மரண மாக","வேறுபல் பெரும்படை நாப்பண் வேறோர்","நெடுங்காழ்க் கண்டங் கோலி யகநேர்பு"],"p":"","r":"In the wide, long woodland that a forest river embraces, the soldiers clear the far-scented pidavam and green bushes, set up the hunters' huts, ring the camp with a fence of laid thorns, and spread it out like the sea; in its leaf-thatched streets, at the crossings, stands a small-eyed elephant with musth on its cheeks that will not eat the sweet fodder of sugarcane and paddy ears bound together, but wipes its brow and holds it on its tusks, while unlettered young men speak to it in the northern tongue with forked goads and press the balls of food on it; bows that never run from battle, with quivers hung on them like the triple staff of a vowed brahmin in ochre cloth, are planted, posts driven and ropes drawn, spears set up and shields ranged, bows made into a wall, and in the middle of the many armies a separate tall-poled tent of cloth is raised for the king.","n":"The elephant's keepers are கல்லா இளைஞர், young men without learning, who drive it in வடமொழி: the northern speech is the language of elephant handling, not of learning. The fodder the elephant refuses, sugarcane and paddy from the fields, comes from the watered country, so marutham things sit in the forest camp.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paasarai","padai","aran"],"pos":[],"nm":"Mullaippattu 24-44","lb":"PP5:24-44","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம்பட்டினத்துப் பொன்வாணிகனார் மகனார் நப்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கான்யாறு","கான் யாறு",24,"pira","scene","forest river"],["பிடவமொடு","பிடவம்",25,"maram","scene",""],["புணரியிற்","புணரி",28,"pira","simile","sea"],["யுவலைக்","உவலை",29,"maram","scene","leaf thatch"],["யானை","யானை",31,"maa","scene",""],["கரும்பொடு","கரும்பு",32,"maram","gift_food","sugarcane"],["வயல்விளை","வயல் விளை",33,"unavu","gift_food","field crop"],["விளைஞர்","இளைஞர்",36,"seythi","scene","elephant keepers"],["கவளங்","கவளம்",36,"unavu","gift_food","fodder balls"],["பார்ப்பான்","பார்ப்பான்",37,"seythi","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:6"},"pp:5:45-66":{"book":"pattupattu","num":82,"t":["குறுந்தொடி முன்கைக் கூந்தலஞ் சிறுபுறத்","திரவுபகற் செய்யுந் திண்பிடி யொள்வாள்","விரவுவரிக் கச்சிற் பூண்ட மங்கையர்","நெய்யுமிழ் சுரையர் நெடுந்திரிக் கொளீஇக்","கையமை விளக்க நந்துதொறு மாட்ட","நெடுநா வொண்மணி நிழத்திய நடுநா","ளதிரல் பூத்த வாடுகொடிப் படாஅர்","சிதர்வர லசைவலிக் கசைவந் தாங்குத்","துகின்முடித்துப் போர்த்த தூங்க லோங்குநடைப்","பெருமூ தாள ரேமஞ் சூழப்","பொழுதளந் தறியும் பொய்யா மாக்க","டொழுதுகாண் கையர் தோன்ற வாழ்த்தி","யெறிநீர் வையகம் வெலீஇய செல்வோய்நின்","குறுநீர்க் கன்ன லினைத்தென் றிசைப்ப","மத்திகை வளைஇய மறிந்துவீங்கு செறிவுடை","மெய்ப்பை புக்க வெருவருந் தோற்றத்து","வலிபுணர் யாக்கை வன்கண் யவனர்","புலித்தொடர் விட்ட புனைமா ணல்லிற்","றிருமணி விளக்கங் காட்டித் திண்ஞா","ணெழினி வாங்கிய வீரறைப் பள்ளியு","ளுடம்பி னுரைக்கு முரையா நாவிற்","படம்புகு மிலேச்ச ருழைய ராக"],"p":"","r":"Inside, women with short bangles and hair falling on small backs, who make night into day, wear bright swords in striped belts and, holding oil-filled lamps with long wicks, relight them whenever they dim; at midnight, when the long-tongued bells are silenced, old guards wrapped in cloth sway as they doze, like the flowering athiral creeper on a padar bush stirred by a drizzling wind; men who measure the hours without error stand with joined hands, hail the king who goes out to conquer the wave-girt world and tell him how much water the little clock has let through; strong-bodied Yavanas of fearsome look, in tight coats with whips at their waists, set up a gem lamp in the fine chamber hung with tiger chains and screened by curtains on strong ropes, and Mlecchas who speak only by signs stand close by in their mantles.","n":"The guard of the king's sleep is a list of strangers to the Tamil household: armed women, Yavanas and Mlecchas who cannot speak the language (உடம்பின் உரைக்கும் உரையா நாவின்). The hour-keepers' formula addresses the king as the one who goes to conquer the world, so even the telling of time is praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paasarai","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Mullaippattu 45-66","lb":"PP5:45-66","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம்பட்டினத்துப் பொன்வாணிகனார் மகனார் நப்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மங்கையர்","மங்கையர்",47,"seythi","scene","sword-bearing women"],["நெய்யுமிழ்","நெய்",48,"pira","scene","lamp oil"],["ளதிரல்","அதிரல்",51,"maram","simile",""],["படாஅர்","படர்",51,"maram","simile","bush"],["பெருமூ தாள","பெருமுது ஆளர்",54,"seythi","simile","old guards"],["மாக்க","மாக்கள்",55,"seythi","scene","hour-keepers"],["யவனர்","யவனர்",61,"seythi","scene",""],["மிலேச்ச","மிலேச்சர்",66,"seythi","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:6"},"pp:5:67-79":{"book":"pattupattu","num":83,"t":["மண்டமர் நசையொடு கண்படை பெறாஅ","தெடுத்தெறி யெஃகம் பாய்தலிற் புண்கூர்ந்து","பிடிக்கண மறந்த வேழம் வேழத்துப்","பாம்புபதைப் பன்ன பரூஉக்கை துமியத்","தேம்பாய் கண்ணி நல்வலந் திருத்திச்","சோறுவாய்த் தொழிந்தோ ருள்ளியுந் தோறுமிபு","வைந்நுனைப் பகழி மூழ்கலிற் செவிசாய்த்","துண்ணா துயங்கு மாசிந் தித்து","மொருகை பள்ளி யொற்றி யொருகை","முடியொடு கடகஞ் சேர்த்தி நெடிதுநினைந்து","பகைவர்ச் சுட்டிய படைகொ ணோன்விர","னகைதாழ் கண்ணி நல்வலந் திருத்தி","யரசிருந்து பனிக்கு முரசுமுழங்கு பாசறை"],"p":"","r":"The king, eager for close battle, cannot sleep: he thinks of the tusker that forgot its herd of cow elephants when thrown spears pierced it and its thick trunk, writhing like a snake, was cut off; he remembers the men in honey-dripping garlands who fought well and fell, having made good the rice they ate; he thinks of the horses that, deep-pierced by sharp arrows, droop their ears and stand suffering without eating; one hand pressed on the bed, the other, with its bracelet, raised to his crown, he thinks long, his strong finger that grips the weapon pointing at the enemy, in the camp where the drum roars and kings tremble.","n":"His sleeplessness begins as desire for battle (மண்டமர் நசையொடு) and turns, line by line, into memory of what the battle cost, the elephant, the men, the horses; the camp is then called the place of the drum before which kings shiver, so the reckoning of loss sits inside the boast. The next line calls him இன்துயில் வதியுநன், one who lies in sweet sleep, which may say he has slept at last or name him as the husband the wife cannot see.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","paasarai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Mullaippattu 67-79","lb":"PP5:67-79","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம்பட்டினத்துப் பொன்வாணிகனார் மகனார் நப்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பிடிக்கண","பிடிக் கணம்",69,"maa","scene","cow elephants"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",69,"maa","scene","tusker"],["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",70,"pira","simile","snake"],["கண்ணி","கண்ணி",71,"maram","ornament","garland"],["சோறு","சோறு",72,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["மா","மா",74,"maa","scene","horse"],["முரசு","முரசு",79,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:6"},"pp:5:80-103":{"book":"pattupattu","num":84,"t":["யின்றுயில் வதியுநற் காணா டுயருழந்து","நெஞ்சாற்றுப் படுத்த நிறைதபு புலம்பொடு","நீடுநினைந்து தேற்றியு மோடுவளை திருத்தியு","மையல் கொண்டு மொய்யென வுயிர்த்து","மேவுறு மஞ்ஞையி னடுங்கி யிழைநெகிழ்ந்து","பாவை விளக்கிற் பரூஉச்சுட ரழல","விடஞ்சிறந் துயரிய வெழுநிலை மாடத்து","முடங்கிறைச் சொரிதரு மாத்திர ளருவி","யின்ப லிமிழிசை யோர்ப்பனள் கிடந்தோ","ளஞ்செவி நிறைய வாலின வென்றுபிறர்","வேண்டுபுலங் கவர்ந்த வீண்டுபெருந் தானையொடு","விசயம் வெல்கொடி யுயரி வலனேர்பு","வயிறும் வளையு மார்ப்ப வயிர","செறியிலைக் காயா வஞ்சன மலர","முறியிணர்க் கொன்றை நன்பொன் காலக்","கோடற் குவிமுகை யங்கை யவிழத்","தோடர் தோன்றி குருதி பூப்பக்","கான நந் திய செந்நிலப் பெருவழி","வானம் வாய்த்த வாங்குகதிர் வரகிற்","றிரிமருப் பிரலையொடு மட மானுகள்","வெதிர்செல் வெண்மழை பொழியுந் திங்களின்","முதிர்காய் வள்ளியங் காடுபிறக் கொழியத்","துனைபரி துரக்குஞ் செலவினர்","வினைவிளங்கு நெடுந்தேர் பூண்ட மாவே."],"p":"","r":"Not seeing him, the wife suffers, her loneliness breaking the self-command with which she held her heart; she thinks long and steadies herself, straightens her slipping bangles, falls into a daze and sighs deeply, trembles like a peacock struck by an arrow, her ornaments loosening, and lies in the seven-storeyed mansion, the lamp in the statuette's hands burning with a thick flame, listening to the sweet many-voiced music of the streams falling from the curved eaves; then her ears are filled by the sound of his horses: with the great gathered army that has seized the lands others wanted, the victory banner raised, rising to the right while horns and conches blare, along the great red road that the forest has enriched, where the kaayaa flowers collyrium, the konrai spills good gold, the kodal's closed buds open like palms and the thondri blooms blood, past the rain-fed millet where the twisted-horned stag leaps with the young doe, leaving behind the forest of ripe fruit in the month of white rain, the horses yoked to the tall chariot famed for its deeds race, urged on.","n":"The return is told through her hearing (அஞ்செவி நிறைய ஆலின): she lies listening to the rain on the eaves, and the next sound to fill her ears is his horses, so the poem ends before anyone meets. The road home is lined with the mullai flowers of the season he would have promised, each set as a jewel or as blood, the colours of victory.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nirai","melivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Mullaippattu 80-103","lb":"PP5:80-103","poet":"காவிரிப்பூம்பட்டினத்துப் பொன்வாணிகனார் மகனார் நப்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"முல்லை","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மஞ்ஞையி","மஞ்ஞை",84,"pul","simile","peacock"],["ளருவி","அருவி",87,"pira","scene","falling eave water"],["வயிறும்","வயிர்",92,"parai","scene","horn trumpet"],["வளையு","வளை",92,"parai","scene","conch trumpet"],["காயா","காயா",93,"maram","simile",""],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",94,"maram","simile",""],["கோடற்","கோடல்",95,"maram","simile",""],["தோன்றி","தோன்றி",96,"maram","simile",""],["வரகிற்","வரகு",98,"unavu","scene","millet"],["பிரலையொடு","இரலை",99,"maa","iraicchi","stag"],["மானுகள","மான்",99,"maa","iraicchi","doe"],["வெண்மழை","வெண் மழை",100,"pira","scene","white cloud"],["வள்ளியங்","வள்ளி",101,"maram","scene",""],["மாவே","மா",103,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[["iraicchi","பிரலையொடு","confirmed","1/1","the beasts of the season are already together, as the husband and wife will be when the chariot that passes them arrives"]],"ch":"pp:6"},"pp:6:1-23":{"book":"pattupattu","num":85,"t":["ஓங்கு திரை வியன் பரப்பின்","ஒலி முந்நீர் வரம் பாகத்","தேன் தூங்கும் உயர் சிமைய","மலை நாறிய வியன் ஞாலத்து","வல மாதிரத்தான் வளி கொட்ப","விய னாண்மீ னெறி யழுகப்","பகற் செய்யும் செஞ் ஞாயிறும்","இரவுச் செய்யும் வெண் திங்களும்","மை தீர்ந்து கிளர்ந்து விளங்க","மழைதொழில் உதவ மாதிரங் கொழுக்கத்","தொடுப்பின் ஆயிரம் வித்தியது விளைய","நிலனு மரனும் பயன்எதிர்பு நந்த","நோ யிகந்து நோக்கு விளங்க","மே தக மிகப் பொலிந்த","ஓங்கு நிலை வயக் களிறு","கண்டு தண்டாக் கட்கின் பத்து","உண்டு தண்டா மிகுவளத் தான்","உயர் பூரிம விழுத் தெருவிற்","பொய் யறியா வாய்மொழி யாற்","புகழ் நிறைந்த நன்மாந்த ரொடு","நல் லூழி அடிப் படரப்","பல் வெள்ளம் மீக் கூற","உலகம் ஆண்ட உயர்ந்தோர் மருக"],"p":"","r":"The poem opens on an ordered world, bounded by the roaring sea and standing on honey-hung mountains, where the winds keep their direction, the stars their course, sun and moon shine clear, the rain does its work, one seed yields a thousand and men are free of disease, and it names the king as descendant of the exalted ones who ruled that world among truthful, famous men, so that a good age followed in their steps.","n":"The order of the cosmos is described before any king is named and is credited to the rule of the ancestors (lines 21 to 23); truthful speech (பொய் யறியா வாய்மொழி, line 19) is counted among the goods of that world alongside rain and harvest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","mazhai","naadu","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 1-23","lb":"PP6:1-23","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["முந்நீர்","முந்நீர்",2,"pira","scene","sea"],["தேன்","தேன்",3,"pira","scene","honeycomb"],["மழைதொழில்","மழை",10,"pira","scene","rain"],["மரனும்","மரம்",12,"maram","scene","trees"],["களிறு","களிறு",15,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:24-42":{"book":"pattupattu","num":86,"t":["பிணக் கோட்ட களிற்றுக் குழும்பின்","நிணம் வாய்ப்பெய்த பேய் மகளிர்","இணை யலியிமிழ் துணங்கைச் சீர்ப்","பிணை யூபம் எழுந் தாட","அஞ்சு வந்த போர்க்களத் தான்","ஆண் டலை அணங் கடுப்பின்","வய வேந்தர் ஒண் குருதி","சினத் தீயிற் பெயர்பு பொங்கத்","தெற லருங் கடுந் துப்பின்","விறல் விளங்கிய விழுச் சூர்ப்பின்","தொடித் தோட்கை துடுப் பாக","ஆ டுற்ற ஊன் சோறு","நெறி யறிந்த கடிவா லுவன்","அடி யதுங்கிப் பிற் பெயராப்","படை யோர்க்கு முரு கயர","அமர் கடக்கும் வியன் றானைத்","தென்னவற் பெயரிய துன்னருந் துப்பின்","தொல்முது கடவுட் பின்னர் மேய","வரைத்தாழ் அருவிப் பொருப்பிற் பொருந"],"p":"","r":"On a dreadful field where elephants carry corpses on their tusks and demon women with fat in their mouths dance the tunangai in pairs, the blood of strong kings boils in the fire of wrath and, with a bangled arm for a ladle, the cook who knows the rite serves the flesh-rice to the soldiers who never stepped back; the king is hailed as lord of the hill of falling streams where the ancient god named after the southern king dwells.","n":"The food of the rite goes to படை யோர் ... பிற் பெயரா, the soldiers who did not step back (lines 37, 38): the grisly sacrifice is also the army's reward. Lines 40 to 42 (தென்னவற் பெயரிய ... தொல்முது கடவுள்) are hard: the hill may be one where an ancient god bearing the Pandya's name dwells, or the king may be said to dwell after that god; the reading is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalavelvi","maram","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 24-42","lb":"PP6:24-42","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["களிற்றுக்","களிறு",24,"maa","scene","elephant"],["பேய் மகளிர்","பேய்",25,"deyvam","scene","demon women"],["துணங்கைச்","துணங்கை",26,"yaazh","scene","tunangai dance"],["ஊன் சோறு","ஊன் சோறு",35,"unavu","gift_food","flesh-rice"],["கடிவா லுவன்","வாலுவன்",36,"seythi","scene","cook"],["கடவுட்","கடவுள்",41,"deyvam","scene","god"],["அருவிப்","அருவி",42,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[["king","தென்னவற்",40]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:43-63":{"book":"pattupattu","num":87,"t":["விழுச் சூழிய விளங்கோ டைய","கடுஞ் சினத்த கமழ்கடா அத்து","அளறு பட்ட நறுஞ் சென்னிய","வரை மருளும் உயர் தோன்றல","வினை நவின்ற பேர் யானை","சினஞ் சிறந்து களனு ழக்கவும்","மா வெடுத்த மலிகுரூஉத் துகள்","அகல் வானத்து வெயில் கரப்பவும்","வாம் பரிய கடுந்திண் டேர்","காற் றென்னக் கடிது கொட்பவும்","வாள் மிகு மற மைந்தர்","தோள் முறையான் வீறு முற்றவும்","இருபெரு வேந்தரொடு வேளிர் சாயப்","பொரு தவரைச் செரு வென்றும்","இலங் கருவிய வரை நீந்திச்","சுரம் போழ்ந்த இக லாற்றல்","உயர்ந் தோங்கிய விழுச் சிறப்பின்","நிலந் தந்த பே ருதவிப்","பொலந்தார் மார்பி னெடியோன் உம்பல்","மரந் தின்னூஉ வரை யுதிர்க்கும்","நரை யுருமின் ஏற னையை"],"p":"","r":"While the great war-trained elephant, mountain-like, its head wet with fragrant musth, tramples the field in fury, the dust the horses raise hides the sun, the swift chariots whirl like wind and sword-bearing warriors fight shoulder to shoulder, the king defeats the two great kings together with the Velir, crosses falling hills and splits the wilderness, and, descendant of the golden-garlanded Nediyon who gave the land, is like the white thunderbolt that eats trees and shatters mountains.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","vendhan","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 43-63","lb":"PP6:43-63","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",47,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மா","மா",49,"maa","scene","horse"],["கருவிய","அருவி",57,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["மரந்","மரம்",62,"maram","scene","trees"]],"en":[["king","னெடியோன்",61]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:64-74":{"book":"pattupattu","num":88,"t":["அருங் குழுமிளைக் குண்டுக் கிடங்கின்","உயர்ந் தோங்கிய நிரைப் புதவின்","நெடு மதில் நிரை ஞாயில்","அம் புமிழ் அயி லருப்பந்","தண் டாது தலைச் சென்று","கொண்டு நீங்கிய விழுச் சிறப்பின்","தென் குமரி வட பெருங்கல்","குண குட கடலா வெல்லைத்","தொன்று மொழிந்து தொழில் கேட்ப","வெற்ற மொடு வெறுத் தொழுகிய","கொற்ற வர்தங் கோனா குவை"],"p":"","r":"Going on without pause he took, and left, forts with guard forests, deep moats, rows of tall gates, long walls with lines of battlements and arrow-spitting towers, so that from southern Kumari to the great northern mountain and between the eastern and western seas the kings who lived in victory speak their old allegiance and obey, and he will be king over them all.","n":"The bounds of rule in lines 70 and 71 (Kumari, the northern mountain, the two seas) are the formula Purananuru 6 and 17 also use; here they come as the result of a run of sieges, not as an inheritance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","vendhan","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 64-74","lb":"PP6:64-74","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"uzhinai","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[],"en":[["hill","பெருங்கல்",70]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:75-88":{"book":"pattupattu","num":89,"t":["வானி யைந்த இரு முந்நீர்ப்","பேஎம் நிலைஇய இரும் பெளவத்துக்","கொடும் புணரி விலங்கு போழக்","கடுங் காலொடு கரை சேர","நெடுங் கொடிமிசை இதை யெடுத்து","இன் னிசைய முரச முழங்கப்","பொன் மலிந்த விழுப் பண்டம்","நா டார நன் கிழிதரும்","ஆடி யற் பெரு நாவாய்","மழை முற்றிய மலை புரையத்","துறை முற்றிய துளங் கிருக்கைத்","தெண் கடற் குண் டகழிச்","சீர் சான்ற உயர் நெல்லின்","ஊர் கொண்ட உயர் கொற்றவ"],"p":"","r":"On the dread, sky-touching sea, ships with sails raised on tall masts cut the curling waves before a strong wind and come to shore to the sound of drums, unloading gold and precious goods that fill the land, great rocking ships like cloud-capped mountains crowding the harbour; the king is hailed as the lofty victor who took that town of tall paddy with the clear sea for its moat.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 75-88","lb":"PP6:75-88","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"uzhinai","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["முந்நீர்ப்","முந்நீர்",75,"pira","scene","sea"],["முரச","முரசம்",80,"parai","scene","drum"],["நாவாய்","நாவாய்",83,"pira","scene","ship"],["மழை","மழை",84,"pira","simile","cloud"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",87,"maram","scene","paddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:89-105":{"book":"pattupattu","num":90,"t":["நீர்த் தெவ்வும் நிரைத் தொழுவர்","பாடு சிலம்பு மிசை யேற்றத்","தோடு வழங்கும் அக லாம்பியிற்","கய னகைய வய னிறைக்கு","மென் றொடை வன் கிழாஅர்","அதரி கொள்பவர் பகடுபூண் தெண்மணி","இரும்புள் ஒப்பும் இசையே என்றும்","மணிப்பூ முண்டகத்து மணல்மலி கானற்","பரதவர் மகளிர் குரவைய டொலிப்ப","ஒருசார், விழவுநின்ற விய லாங்கண்","முழவுத் தோள் முரட் பொருநர்க்கு","உரு கெழு பெருஞ் சிறப்பின்","இரு பெயர்ப் பேரா யமொடு","இலங்கு மருப்பிற் களிறு கொடுத்தும்","பொலந் தாமரைப் பூச் சூட்டியும்","நலஞ் சான்ற கலஞ் சிதறும்","பல் குட்டுவர் வெல் கோவே!"],"p":"","r":"Everywhere sound the songs of the water-lifters at their rows of lifts, the clear bells of the threshers' oxen that scare off the great birds, and the kuravai of the fisherwomen on the sandy shore of blue-flowered mundakam, while on one side, in the wide festival ground, the king gives the drum-shouldered bards bright-tusked elephants, crowns them with golden lotuses and scatters fine ornaments: he is the victorious king of the many Kuttuvar.","n":"The giving is set among the sounds of field and shore (இசையே என்றும், line 95): the bards' gift belongs to the same abundance as the threshing and the fisherwomen's dance. Line 92 கய னகைய and line 101 இரு பெயர்ப் பேரா யம் are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 89-105","lb":"PP6:89-105","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["தொழுவர்","தொழுவர்",89,"seythi","scene","field labourers"],["பகடு","பகடு",94,"maa","scene","oxen"],["இரும்புள்","புள்",95,"pul","scene","large birds"],["முண்டகத்து","முண்டகம்",96,"maram","scene","mundakam"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",97,"seythi","scene","fisherfolk"],["குரவைய","குரவை",97,"yaazh","scene","kuravai dance"],["பொருநர்க்கு","பொருநர்",99,"seythi","scene","bards"],["களிறு","களிறு",102,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",103,"maram","ornament","golden lotus"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:106-130":{"book":"pattupattu","num":91,"t":["கல் காயுங் கடுவேனி லொடு","இரு வானம் பெயலொ ளிப்பினும்","வரும் வைகல் மீன் பிறழினும்","வெள்ளமா றாது விளையுள் பெருக","நெல்லி னோதை அரிநர் கம்பலை","புள்ளிமிழ்ந் தொலிக்கும் இசையே என்றும்","சலம் புகன்று கறவுக் கலித்த","புலவு நீர் வியன் பெளவத்து","நிலவுக் கானல் முழவுத் தாழைக்","குளிர்ப் பொதும்பர் நளித் தூவல்","நிரைதிமில் வேட்டுவர் கரைசேர் கம்பலை","இருங்கழிச் செறுவின் வெள்ளுப்புப் பகர்நரொடு","ஒலி யோவாக் கலி யாணர்","முது வெள்ளிலை மீக் கூறும்","வியன் மேவல் விழுச் செல்வத்து","இரு வகையான் இசை சான்ற","சிறு குடிப் பெருந் தொழுவர்","குடி கெழீஇய நானிலவ ரொடு","தொன்று மொழிந்து தொழில் கேட்பக்","கா லென்னக் கடிது ராஅய்","நாடு கெட எரி பரப்பி","ஆலங் கானத் தஞ்சுவர விறுத்து","அரசு பட அமரு ழக்கி","முரசு கொண்டு களம் வேட்ட","அடு திறலுயர் புகழ் வேந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Even when summer scorches the stones, the sky hides its rain and the stars of the coming days go astray, the king's land never lacks flood or harvest: the din of paddy and reapers and the cries of birds never cease, and on the shore the fishermen landing their boats and the sellers of white salt keep up the noise of Muthuvellil's wealth; with the farmers of the small hamlets and the people of the four lands obeying him, he swept on like wind, spread fire till the land was ruined, camped fearsomely at Alankanam, crushed the kings, took their drums and made the battlefield his sacrifice.","n":"Lines 106 to 109 claim that the land yields even when rain fails and the stars go wrong, the reverse of the rule that rain follows the king; the land's plenty is offered as given, and the counsel that comes later has to work on a king who has it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","maram","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 106-130","lb":"PP6:106-130","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["நெல்லி","நெல்",110,"maram","scene","paddy"],["அரிநர்","அரிநர்",110,"seythi","scene","reapers"],["புள்ளிமிழ்ந்","புள்",111,"pul","scene","birds"],["தாழைக்","தாழை",114,"maram","scene","screwpine"],["திமில் வேட்டுவர்","திமில் வேட்டுவர்",116,"seythi","scene","boat fishermen"],["வெள்ளுப்புப்","வெள் உப்பு",117,"pira","scene","salt"],["தொழுவர்","தொழுவர்",122,"seythi","scene","farmers"],["முரசு","முரசு",129,"parai","scene","war drum"]],"en":[["town","முது வெள்ளிலை",119],["battle","ஆலங் கானத்",127]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:131-151":{"book":"pattupattu","num":92,"t":["நட்டவர் குடி யுயர்க் குவை","செற்றவர் அரசு பெயர்க் குவை","பேரு லகத்து மேஎந் தோன்றிச்","சீரு டைய விழுச் சிறப்பின்","விளைந்து முதிர்ந்த விழு முத்தின்","இலங்கு வளை இருஞ் சேரிக்","கட் கொண்டிக் குடிப் பாக்கத்து","நற் கொற்கை யோர்நசைப் பொருந","செற்ற தெவ்வர் கலங்கத் தலைச்சென்று","அஞ்சுவரத் தட்கும் அணங்குடைத் துப்பிற்","கோழூ உன்குறைக் கொழு வல்சிப்","புலவு விற் பொலி கூவை","ஒன்று மொழி ஒலி யிருப்பில்","தென் பரதவர் போ ரேறே","அரிய வெல்லாம் எளிதினிற் கொண்டு","உரிய வெல்லாம் ஓம்பாது வீசி","நனிபுகன் றுறைது மென்னா தேற்றெழுந்து","பனிவார் சிமையக் கானம் போகி","அகநாடு புக்கவர் விருப்பம் வெளவி","யாண்டுபல கழிய வேண்டுபுலத் திறுத்து","மேம்பட மரீஇய வெல்போர்க் குருசில்"],"p":"","r":"He raises the houses of his friends and unseats the kingdoms of his foes; desired by the people of good Korkai with its ripe pearls and conch-bangled quarters, he is the war-bull of the southern fisherfolk who eat rich meat; taking everything hard with ease and throwing away all that is his without keeping it, he will not say 'let us stay' but rises, goes through the forests of the snowy peaks, enters the inner lands, seizes what they prize and camps for many years in the land he wants.","n":"Lines 131 and 132 (நட்டவர் குடி யுயர்க் குவை, செற்றவர் அரசு பெயர்க் குவை) set friend and foe in one balanced pair; lines 145 and 146 pair taking and giving away the same way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","maram","eegai","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 131-151","lb":"PP6:131-151","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["முத்தின்","முத்து",135,"pira","scene","pearl"],["வளை","வளை",136,"pira","ornament","conch bangle"],["வல்சிப்","வல்சி",141,"unavu","gift_food","food"],["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",144,"seythi","scene","fisherfolk"]],"en":[["town","கொற்கை",138]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:152-176":{"book":"pattupattu","num":93,"t":["உறு செறுநர் புலம் புக்கவர்","கடி காவி னிலை தொலைச்சி","இழி பறியாப் பெருந்தண் பணை","குரூஉக் கொடிய எரி மேய","நா டெனும் பேர் காடாக","ஆ சேந்த வழி மாசேப்ப","ஊரி ருந்த வழி பாழாக","இலங்கு வளை மட மங்கையர்","துணங்கை யஞ்சீர்த் தழூஉ மறப்ப","அவை யிருந்த பெரும் பொதியிற்","கவை யடிக் கடு நோக்கத்துப்","பேய் மகளிர் பெயர் பாட","அணங்கு வழங்கு மகலாங் கண்","நிலத் தாற்றுங் குழூஉப் புதவின்","அரந்தைப் பெண்டிர் இனைந்தனர் அகவக்","கொழும் பதிய குடி தேம்பச்","செழுங் கேளிர் நிழல் சேர","நெடுநகர் வீழ்ந்த கரிகுதிர்ப் பள்ளிக்","குடுமிக் கூகை குராலொடு முரலக்","கழுநீர் பொலிந்த கண்ணகன் பொய்கைக்","களிறுமாய் செருந்தியொடு கண்பமன் றூர்தர","நல்லேர் நடந்த நசைசால் விளைவயல்","பன்மயிர்ப் பிணவொடு கேழல் உகள","வாழா மையின் வழிதவக் கெட்டுப்","பாழா யினநின் பகைவர் தேஎம்"],"p":"","r":"In the land of the enemies he entered, the guarded groves are cut down, fire grazes the great fields that never knew want, what was called country becomes forest, wild beasts stand where cattle stood and waste where towns stood; the young women forget the tunangai, demon women sing in the halls where assemblies sat, grieving women cry at the gates, families scatter to others' shelter, the crested owl hoots with its mate in fallen mansions, sedge and reed choke the lily pond and the boar frisks with his sow in the ploughed fields: for lack of living, his enemies' land has become a ruin.","n":"Each human use of the land is replaced by a wild one in a paired line (cattle by beasts, town by waste, dance by demon song, the ploughed field by the boar and sow); the owl calling to its mate (குராலொடு, line 170) and the boar with its sow put animal couples where the families were.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kedu","pagai","naadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 152-176","lb":"PP6:152-176","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"],["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["ஆ","ஆ",157,"maa","absent","cattle"],["மாசேப்ப","மா",157,"maa","scene","wild beasts"],["வளை","வளை",159,"pira","ornament","conch bangle"],["துணங்கை","துணங்கை",160,"yaazh","absent","tunangai dance"],["பேய் மகளிர்","பேய்",163,"deyvam","scene","demon women"],["கூகை","கூகை",170,"pul","scene","owl"],["கழுநீர்","கழுநீர்",171,"maram","absent","blue water-lily"],["செருந்தியொடு","செருந்தி",172,"maram","scene","sedge"],["கண்ப","கண்பு",172,"maram","scene","reed"],["நல்லேர்","ஏர்",173,"seythi","absent","plough"],["பிணவொடு","பிணவு",174,"maa","scene","sow"],["கேழல்","கேழல்",174,"maa","scene","boar"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:177-198":{"book":"pattupattu","num":94,"t":["எழாஅத் தோள் இமிழ்மு ழக்கின்","மாஅத் தாள் உயர் மருப்பிற்","கடுஞ் சினத்த களிறு பரப்பி","விரி கடல் வியன் றானையொடு","முரு குறழப் பகைத்தலைச் சென்று","அகல் விசும்பின் ஆர்ப் பிமிழப்","பெய லுறழக் கணை சிதறிப்","பல புரவி நீ றுகைப்ப","வளை நரல வயி ரார்ப்பப்","பீ டழியக் கடந் தட்டவர்","நா டழியக் எயில் வெளவிச்","சுற்ற மொடு தூ வறுத்தலிற்","செற்ற தெவ்வர் நின்வழி நடப்ப","வியன்கண் முதுபொழில் மண்டில முற்றி","அரசியல் பிழையா தறநெறி காட்டிப்","பெரியோர் சென்ற அடிவழிப் பிழையாது","குடமுதல் தோன்றிய தொன்றுதொழு பிறையின்","வழிவழிச் சிறக்கநின் வலம்படு கொற்றம்","குணமுதல் தோன்றிய ஆரிருள் மதியின்","தேய்வன கெடுகநின் தெவ்வர் ஆக்கம்","உயர்நிலை உலகம் அமிழ்தொடு பெறினும்","பொய்சேண் நீங்கிய வாய்நட் பினையே"],"p":"","r":"Spreading fierce tall-tusked elephants and an army wide as the sea, going against his foes like Murugan, arrows falling like rain, horses raising dust and conches and horns sounding, he broke their pride, took their forts and rooted them out with their kin, so that his enemies walk his way; he has ringed the old wide world, shown the path of dharma without failing in statecraft or straying from the steps of the great, and the poet wishes his victory to grow like the waxing crescent seen in the west and his foes' fortune to wane like the moon rising in the east; even for heaven with its nectar he would not leave his true friendship for a lie.","n":"The two moon similes of lines 193 to 196 are exactly matched: the crescent first seen in the west waxes, the moon that rises in the east after full moon wanes, so the king's growth and his enemies' decline are one cycle seen from two sides. Line 198 turns praise into a standard: a friendship free of lies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","vaaimai","maram","natpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 177-198","lb":"PP6:177-198","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",179,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கடல்","கடல்",180,"pira","scene","sea"],["முரு","முருகு",181,"deyvam","simile","Murugan"],["பெய","பெயல்",183,"pira","simile","rain"],["புரவி","புரவி",184,"maa","scene","horse"],["வளை","வளை",185,"pira","scene","conch"],["வயி","வயிர்",185,"yaazh","scene","horn"],["அமிழ்தொடு","அமிழ்து",197,"unavu","scene","nectar"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:199-209":{"book":"pattupattu","num":95,"t":["முழங்குகட லேணி மலர்தலை யுலகமொடு","உயர்ந்த தேஎத்து விழுமியோர் வரினும்","பகைவர்க் கஞ்சிப் பணிந்தொழு கலையே","தென்புல மருங்கின் விண்டு நிறைய","வாணன் வைத்த விழுநிதி பெறினும்","பழிநமக் கெழுக என்னாய் விழுநிதி","ஈதல் உள்ளமொடு இசைவேட் குவையே","அன்னாய் நின்னொடு முன்னிலை எவனோ","கொன்னொன்று கிளக்குவல் அடுபோர் அண்ணல்","கேட்டிசின் வாழி கெடுகநின் அவலம்","கெடாது நிலைஇயர்நின் சேண்விளங்கு நல்லிசை"],"p":"","r":"Even if the great ones of the high world came offering the earth ringed by the roaring sea, he would not bow and walk in fear of his enemies; even if he could have the treasure Vanan buried to fill the southern land, he would not say 'let blame come to us', but seeks fame with a mind to give treasure away; and now the poet asks leave to say one thing: let the lord of slaying war listen, may his sorrow perish and his far-shining fame stand unperishing.","n":"With கொன்னொன்று கிளக்குவல் ... கேட்டிசின் (lines 207, 208) the poet stops praising and announces counsel: the kaanchi of the colophon begins here. The praise of lines 199 to 205 is phrased in the conditional (வரினும், பெறினும்), as tests the king is said to pass.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","pugazh","eegai","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 199-209","lb":"PP6:199-209","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["கட","கடல்",199,"pira","scene","sea"]],"en":[["king","வாணன்",203]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:210-237":{"book":"pattupattu","num":96,"t":["தவாப் பெருக்கத் தறா யாணர்","அழித் தானாக் கொழுந் திற்றி","இழித் தானாப் பல சொன்றி","உண் டானாக் கூர் நறவில்","தின் றானா இன வைக","னிலனெடுக் கல்லா வொண்பல் வெறுக்கைப்","பயனற வறியா வளங்கெழு திருநகர்","நரம்பின் முரலு நயம்வரு முரற்சி","விறலியர் வறுங்கைக் குறுந்தொடி செறிப்பப்","பாணர் உவப்ப களிறுபல தரீஇக்","கலந்தோ ருவப்ப வெயிற்பல கடைஇ","மறங் கலங்கத் தலைச் சென்று","வாளுழந் ததன் தாள் வாழ்த்தி","நா ளீண்டிய நல் லகவர்க்குத்","தே ரோடு மா சிதறிச்","சூ டுற்ற சுடர்ப் பூவின்","பாடு புலர்ந்த நறுஞ் சாந்தின்","விழுமிய பெரியோர் சுற்ற மாகக்","கள்ளின் இரும்பைக் கலஞ்செல வுண்டு","பணிந்தோர் தேஎந் தம்வழி நடப்பப்","பணியார் தேஎம் பணித்துத்திறை கொண்மார்","பருந்துபறக் கல்லாப் பார்வற் பாசறைப்","படுகண் முரசங் காலை யியம்ப","வெடிபடக் கடந்து வேண்டுபுலத் திறுத்த","பணைகெழு பெருந்திறற் பல்வேல் மன்னர்","கரைபொரு திரங்கும் சுனையிரு முந்நீர்த்","திரையிடு மணலினும் பலரே உரைசெல","மலர்தலை யுலகம் ஆண்டுகழிந் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"The kings of many spears who had sacred cities of unfailing plenty, meat and rice and sharp liquor that never ran short however much was taken, treasure the ground could not hold; who filled the viralis' empty hands with bangles, gave the bards elephants and the panegyrists chariots and horses, drank toddy among great men, made the bowed lands follow them and set up camp over which no kite could fly to take tribute from the unbowed: those kings, more than the sand the waves throw on the shore, ruled the wide world with their fame spreading, and passed away.","n":"Every mark of greatness the poem has praised in its own king so far (plenty, giving to bards, conquest, tribute) is here given to the dead kings before him; the sentence is one long relative clause that ends only on கழிந்தோரே, so the reader learns that these were all past kings at the last word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pugazh","eegai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 210-237","lb":"PP6:210-237","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["திற்றி","திற்றி",211,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["சொன்றி","சொன்றி",212,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["நறவில்","நறவு",213,"unavu","gift_food","liquor"],["நரம்பின்","நரம்பு",217,"yaazh","scene","lute strings"],["விறலியர்","விறலி",218,"seythi","scene","women dancers"],["பாணர்","பாணர்",219,"seythi","scene","bards"],["களிறுபல","களிறு",219,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["மா","மா",224,"maa","gift_food","horse"],["பூவின்","பூ",225,"maram","ornament","flowers"],["சாந்தின்","சாந்து",226,"pira","ornament","sandal paste"],["கள்ளின்","கள்",228,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["பருந்துபறக்","பருந்து",231,"pul","scene","kite"],["முரசங்","முரசம்",232,"parai","scene","war drum"],["மணலினும்","மணல்",236,"pira","simile","sand"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:238-260":{"book":"pattupattu","num":97,"t":["அதனால், குணகடல் கொண்டு குடகடல்முற்றி","இரவு மெல்லையும் விளிவிட னறியாது","அவலு மிசையு நீர்த்திரள் பீண்டிக்","கவலையங் குழும்பின் அருவி ஒலிப்பக்","கழைவளர் சாரற் களிற்றின நடுங்க","வரைமுத லிரங்கும் ஏறொடு வான்ஞெமிர்ந்து","சிதரற் பெரும்பெயல் சிறத்தலிற் றாங்காது","குணகடற் கிவர்தருங் குரூஉப்புன லுந்தி","நிவந்துசெ னீத்தங் குளங்கொளச் சாற்றிக்","களிறு மாய்க்குங் கதிர்க் கழனி","ஒளி றிலஞ்சி அடை நிவந்த","முட் டாள சுடர்த் தாமரை","கட் கமழு நறு நெய்தல்","வள் ளிதழ் அவிழ் நீலம்","மெல் லிலை யரி யாம்பலொடு","வண்டிறை கொண்ட கமழ்பூம் பொய்கைக்","கம்புட் சேவல் இன்றுயில் இரிய","வள்ளை நீக்கி வயமீன் முகந்து","கொள்ளை சாற்றிய கொடுமுடி வலைஞர்","வேழப் பழனத்து நூழி லாட்டுக்","கரும்பி னெந்திரங் கட்பி னோதை","அள்ளற் றங்கிய பகடுறு விழுமங்","கள்ளார் களமர் பெயர்க்கும் ஆர்ப்பே"],"p":"","r":"Therefore: the clouds, drawing from the eastern sea and closing on the western, pour day and night without pause till falls roar in the rocky clefts and the elephant herds tremble on the bamboo slopes, and the flood that the land cannot hold runs back toward the eastern sea and fills the tanks; in fields where the grain hides elephants and in pools of lotus, neythal, blue lily and aambal, the fishermen disturb the waterhen's sleep scooping fish from the vallai, and the reed fields ring with the cane press, the weeders and the shouts of toddy-drunk field hands hauling oxen out of the mire.","n":"அதனால், 'therefore', at line 238 ties the long description of the five lands to the counsel just given about the kings who passed: the description is the ground of an argument whose conclusion lies beyond this chapter. The rain starts on the hills (kurinji things) and ends in the fields, so the marutham section is built as the journey of the water.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","uzhavu","naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 238-260","lb":"PP6:238-260","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",241,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["கழைவளர்","கழை",242,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["களிற்றின","களிறு",242,"maa","scene","elephant"],["ஏறொடு","ஏறு",243,"pira","scene","thunder"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",249,"maram","scene","lotus"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",250,"maram","scene","neythal flower"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",251,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["யாம்பலொடு","ஆம்பல்",252,"maram","scene","water-lily"],["வண்டிறை","வண்டு",253,"pira","scene","bees"],["கம்புட் சேவல்","கம்புள்",254,"pul","scene","waterhen"],["வள்ளை","வள்ளை",255,"maram","scene","vallai creeper"],["வயமீன்","மீன்",255,"pira","scene","fish"],["வலைஞர்","வலைஞர்",256,"seythi","scene","net fishermen"],["வேழப்","வேழம்",257,"maram","scene","reed"],["கரும்பி","கரும்பு",258,"maram","scene","sugarcane"],["பகடுறு","பகடு",259,"maa","scene","oxen"],["களமர்","களமர்",260,"seythi","scene","field hands"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:261-270":{"book":"pattupattu","num":98,"t":["ஒலிந்த பகன்றை விளைந்த கழனி","வன்கை வினைஞர் அரிபறை யின்குரல்","தளிமழை பொழியுந் தண்பரங் குன்றிற்","கலிகொள் சும்மை யொலிகொ ளாயந்","ததைந்த கோதை தாரொடு பொலியப்","புணர்ந்துட னாடும் இசையே யனைத்தும்","அகலிரு வானத் திமிழ்ந்தினி திசைப்பக்","குருகு நரல மனை மரத்தான்","மீன் சீவும் பாண் சேரியொடு","மருதஞ் சான்ற தண்பணை சுற்றிஒருசார்ச்"],"p":"","r":"In fields where pakanrai spreads and the grain is ripe, the reapers' drum sounds sweetly, and from cool Parankunram, where soft rain falls, comes the noise of the crowd of men and women who play together in dense garlands and wreaths; all these sounds ring sweetly in the wide sky, with the herons' cries and the bards' quarter where fish are scaled by the house trees, around the cool fields of the marutham land.","n":"The hill festival at Parankunram is counted as one more sound of the marutham countryside, between the reaping drum and the herons; the bards here (பாண் சேரி) scale fish, a reminder that the Paanar were also a fishing people.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","vizhavu","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 261-270","lb":"PP6:261-270","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["பகன்றை","பகன்றை",261,"maram","scene","pakanrai"],["வினைஞர்","வினைஞர்",262,"seythi","scene","reapers"],["அரிபறை","அரிபறை",262,"parai","scene","reaping drum"],["கோதை","கோதை",265,"maram","ornament","garland"],["குருகு","குருகு",268,"pul","scene","heron"],["மீன்","மீன்",269,"pira","scene","fish"],["பாண்","பாணர்",269,"seythi","scene","fisher bards"]],"en":[["hill","பரங் குன்றிற்",263]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:271-285":{"book":"pattupattu","num":99,"t":["சிறுதினை கொய்யக் கவ்வை கறுப்பக்","கருங்கால் வரகின் இருங்குரல் புலர","ஆழ்ந்த குழும்பில் திருமணி கிளர","எழுந்த கடற்றி னன்பொன் கொழிப்பப்","பெருங்கவின் பெற்ற சிறுதலை நெளவி","மடக்கட் பிணையொடு மறுகுவன உகளச்","சுடர்ப்பூங் கொன்றை தாஅய நீழற்","பாஅ யன்ன பாறை யணிந்து","நீலத் தன்ன பைம்பயிர் மிசைதொறும்","வெள்ளி யன்ன வொள்வி யுதிர்ந்து","சுரிமுகிழ் முசுண்டையொடு முல்லை தாஅய்","மணிமரு ணெய்தல் உறழக் காமர்","துணிநீர் மெல்லவற் றொய்யிலொடு மலர","வல்லோன் தைஇய வெறிக்களங் கடுப்ப","முல்லை சான்ற புறவணிந் தொருசார்"],"p":"","r":"On one side, as the small millet is cut, the pulse darkens and the dark-stalked varagu's ears dry, gems glitter in deep pits and gold is sifted in the woods, small-headed stags frisk with their soft-eyed does, kondrai petals strew the shade over rocks spread like mats, silver-white blossoms fall on crops green as sapphire, musundai and mullai spread, and flowers rivalling the gem-blue neythal open in the clear hollows with thoyyil: like a veri ground laid out by a skilled hand, the forest country full of mullai is adorned.","n":"The closing simile (line 284) likens the whole flowering forest to a veri ground laid out for Murugan's rite, so the mullai section ends on a kurinji rite; the stag with his doe (lines 275, 276) is scene here, in a puram description with no lovers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","azhagu","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 271-285","lb":"PP6:271-285","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["சிறுதினை","தினை",271,"maram","scene","millet"],["கவ்வை","கவ்வை",271,"maram","scene","pulse"],["வரகின்","வரகு",272,"maram","scene","varagu millet"],["திருமணி","மணி",273,"pira","scene","gems"],["பொன்","பொன்",274,"pira","scene","gold"],["நெளவி","நவ்வி",275,"maa","scene","stag"],["பிணையொடு","பிணை",276,"maa","scene","doe"],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",277,"maram","scene","kondrai"],["நீலத்","நீலம்",279,"pira","simile","sapphire"],["வெள்ளி","வெள்ளி",280,"pira","simile","silver"],["முசுண்டையொடு","முசுண்டை",281,"maram","scene","musundai"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",281,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["ணெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",282,"maram","simile","neythal flower"],["றொய்யிலொடு","தொய்யில்",283,"maram","scene","thoyyil"],["வல்லோன்","வல்லோன்",284,"seythi","simile","skilled ritualist"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:286-301":{"book":"pattupattu","num":100,"t":["நறுங்காழ் கொன்று கோட்டின் வித்திய","குறுங்கதிர்த் தோரை நெடுங்கால் ஐயவி","ஐவன வெண்ணெலொ டரில்கொள்பு நீடி","இஞ்சி மஞ்சட் பைங்கறி பிறவும்","பல்வேறு தாரமொடு கல்லகத் தீண்டித்","தினைவிளை சாரற் கிளிகடி பூசல்","மணிப்பூ அவரைக் குரூஉத்தளிர் மேயும்","ஆமா கடியுங் கானவர் பூசல்","சேணோன் அகழ்ந்த மடிவாய்ப் பயம்பின்","வீழ்முகக் கேழல் அட்ட பூசல்","கருங்கால் வேங்கை இருஞ்சினைப் பொங்கர்","நறும்பூக் கொய்யும் பூசல் இருங்கேழ்","ஏறடு வயப்புலிப் பூசலொ டனைத்தும்","இலங்குவெள் ளருவியொடு சிலம்பகத் திரட்டக்","கருங்காற் குறிஞ்சி சான்ற வெற்பணிந்து","அருங்கடி மாமலை தழீஇ ஒருசார்"],"p":"","r":"On one side, where fragrant hardwood has been felled and sown with digging sticks, short-eared thorai paddy, tall mustard and white aivanam rice grow thick with ginger, turmeric, green pepper and many other goods gathered among the rocks, and the shouts of the parrot-scarers on the millet slopes, of the hunters driving wild cattle off the avarai shoots, at the boar fallen into the watchman's pit, of those plucking vengai flowers and at the tiger that killed a bull all gather with the white falls on the slopes: the hills full of kurinji are adorned, embracing the great guarded mountain.","n":"The section is built from five பூசல், 'shouts' (lines 291 to 298), each the noise of one hill occupation, so the kurinji land, like the marutham, is given by its sounds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","uzhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 286-301","lb":"PP6:286-301","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தோரை","தோரை",287,"maram","scene","hill paddy"],["ஐயவி","ஐயவி",287,"maram","scene","mustard"],["வெண்ணெலொ","வெண்ணெல்",288,"maram","scene","white rice"],["இஞ்சி","இஞ்சி",289,"maram","scene","ginger"],["மஞ்சட்","மஞ்சள்",289,"maram","scene","turmeric"],["பைங்கறி","கறி",289,"maram","scene","pepper"],["தினைவிளை","தினை",291,"maram","scene","millet"],["கிளிகடி","கிளி",291,"pul","scene","parrot"],["அவரைக்","அவரை",292,"maram","scene","avarai bean"],["ஆமா","ஆமா",293,"maa","scene","wild cattle"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",293,"seythi","scene","hunters"],["சேணோன்","சேணோன்",294,"seythi","scene","watchman"],["கேழல்","கேழல்",295,"maa","scene","boar"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",296,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["ஏறடு","ஏறு",298,"maa","scene","bull"],["புலிப்","புலி",298,"maa","scene","tiger"],["ளருவியொடு","அருவி",299,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["குறிஞ்சி","குறிஞ்சி",300,"maram","scene","kurinji plant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:302-314":{"book":"pattupattu","num":101,"t":["இருவெதிர்ப் பைந்தூறு கூரெரி நைப்ப","நிழத்த யானை மேய்புலம் படரக்","கலித்த இயவர் இயந்தொட் டன்ன","கண்விடு புடையூஉத் தட்டை கவினழிந்து","அருவி யான்ற அணியில் மாமலை","வைகண் டன்ன புன்முளி யங்காட்டுக்","கமழ்சூழ் கோடை விடரக முகந்து","காலுறு கடலின் ஒலிக்குஞ் சும்மை","இலைவேய் குரம்பை உழையதட் பள்ளி","உவலைக் கண்ணி வன்சொல் இளைஞர்","சிலையுடைக் கையர் கவலை காப்ப","நிழலுரு விழந்த வேனிற்குன் றத்துப்","பாலை சான்ற சுரஞ்சேர்ந் தொருசார்"],"p":"","r":"On one side, where sharp fire wastes the green clumps of great bamboo, lean elephants go off to graze, the bamboo bursts at its nodes with a crackle like musicians striking their drums and loses its beauty, the great hill stands bare with its falls dried up, the dry grass is like straw and the west wind scoops the clefts with a roar like the wind-driven sea, harsh-voiced young men in garlands of withered leaves, bows in hand, guard the forking paths from their leaf huts with deer-skin beds: the wilderness full of paalai lies among the summer hills that have lost their shade.","n":"The paalai has no land of its own: it is the hill country gone dry (அருவி யான்ற, நிழலுரு விழந்த), which is how porul 1.11 describes it, and the poem says so by placing it in 'summer hills'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naadu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 302-314","lb":"PP6:302-314","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"]],"k":[["இருவெதிர்ப்","வெதிர்",302,"maram","absent","bamboo"],["யானை","யானை",303,"maa","scene","elephant"],["இயவர்","இயவர்",304,"seythi","simile","musicians"],["தட்டை","தட்டை",305,"maram","absent","bamboo"],["அருவி","அருவி",306,"pira","absent","waterfall"],["வைகண்","வை",307,"pira","simile","straw"],["கடலின்","கடல்",309,"pira","simile","sea"],["உழைய","உழை",310,"maa","scene","deer"],["உவலைக்","உவலை",311,"maram","ornament","withered leaves"],["இளைஞர்","இளைஞர்",311,"seythi","scene","young bowmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:315-326":{"book":"pattupattu","num":102,"t":["முழங்குகடல் தந்த விளங்குகதிர் முத்தம்","அரம்போழ்ந் தறுத்த கண்ணேர் இலங்குவளை","பரதர் தந்த பல்வேறு கூலம்","இருங்கழிச் செறுவிற் றீம்புளி வெள்ளுப்புப்","பரந்தோங்கு வரைப்பின் வன்கைத் திமிலர்","கொழுமீன் குறைஇய துடிக்கண் துணியல்","விழுமிய நாவாய் பெருநீ ரோச்சுநர்","நனந்தலை தேஎத்து நன்கலன் உய்ம்மார்","புணர்ந்துடன் கொணர்ந்த புரவிய டனைத்தும்","வைகல் தோறும் வழிவழிச் சிறப்ப","நெய்தல் சான்ற வளம்பல பயின்றாங்கு","ஐம்பால் திணையுங் கவினி யமைவர"],"p":"","r":"On one side, bright pearls the roaring sea yields, shining bangles sawn from conch, the many grains the traders bring, sweet tamarind and white salt from the pans of the great backwater, slices of fat fish cut by the strong-handed boatmen, and the horses brought together by those who sail great ships to fetch fine goods from distant lands, all grow day by day as the many riches of the neythal gather, and so the lands of all five kinds flourish and are settled.","n":"Line 326 names the scheme the description has followed: ஐம்பால் திணை, the land of five kinds, each introduced by '... சான்ற' and 'ஒருசார்'; the neythal is given not by its sounds, as the others are, but by its goods, most of them traded.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 315-326","lb":"PP6:315-326","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["முத்தம்","முத்து",315,"pira","scene","pearl"],["இலங்குவளை","வளை",316,"pira","scene","conch bangle"],["பரதர்","பரதர்",317,"seythi","scene","traders"],["கூலம்","கூலம்",317,"unavu","scene","grain"],["றீம்புளி","புளி",318,"unavu","scene","tamarind"],["வெள்ளுப்புப்","வெள் உப்பு",318,"pira","scene","salt"],["திமிலர்","திமிலர்",319,"seythi","scene","boatmen"],["கொழுமீன்","மீன்",320,"pira","scene","fish"],["நாவாய்","நாவாய்",321,"pira","scene","ship"],["புரவிய","புரவி",323,"maa","scene","horses"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:327-342":{"book":"pattupattu","num":103,"t":["முழ வீமிழும் அக லாங்கண்","விழவு நின்ற வியன் மறுகில்","துணங்கையந் தழூஉவின் மணங்கமழ் சேரி","இன்கலி யாணர்க் குழூஉப்பல பயின்றாங்குப்","பாடல் சான்ற நன்னாட்டு நடுவண்","கலை தாய உயர் சிமையத்து","மயி லகவு மலி பொங்கர்","மந்தி யாட மாவிசும் புகந்து","முழங்குகால் பொருத மரம்பயில் காவின்","இயங்குபுனல் கொழித்த வெண்டலைக் குவவுமணற்","கான்பொழில் தழீஇய அடைகரை தோறுந்","தாதுசூழ் கோங்கின் பூமலர் தாஅய்க்","கோதையி னொழுகும் விரிநீர் நல்வரல்","அவிரறல் வையைத் துறைதுறை தோறும்","பல்வேறு பூத்திரள் தண்டலை சுற்றி","அழுந்துபட் டிருந்த பெரும்பாண் இருக்கையும்"],"p":"","r":"In the middle of that land rich in song, with its wide drum-beating grounds, its festival streets and its quarters fragrant with weddings where the tunangai is danced and joyful crowds gather, along every bank of the Vaiyai, where stags roam the high peaks, peacocks call in the groves, monkeys play, wind-struck trees rise to the sky and the flowing water heaps white sand, the pollen-laden kongu strews its flowers and the river flows like a garland; and at every ford, surrounded by gardens of many flowers, are the long-settled quarters of the great bards.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 327-342","lb":"PP6:327-342","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["முழ","முழவு",327,"parai","scene","drum"],["துணங்கை","துணங்கை",329,"yaazh","scene","tunangai dance"],["கலை","கலை",332,"maa","scene","stag"],["மயி","மயில்",333,"pul","scene","peacock"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",334,"maa","scene","monkey"],["கோங்கின்","கோங்கு",338,"maram","scene","kongu tree"],["கோதையி","கோதை",339,"maram","simile","garland"],["பூத்திரள்","பூ",341,"maram","scene","flowers"],["பெரும்பாண்","பாணர்",342,"seythi","scene","bards"]],"en":[["river","வையைத்",340]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:343-361":{"book":"pattupattu","num":104,"t":["நிலனும் வளனுங் கண்டமை கல்லா","விளங்குபெருந் திருவின் மான விறல்வேள்","அழும்பில் அன்ன நாடிழந் தனருங்","கொழும்பல் புதிய குடியிழந் தனரும்","தொன்றுகறுத் துறையுந் துப்புத்தர வந்த","அண்ணல் யானை அடுபோர் வேந்தர்","இன்னிசை முரச மிடைப்புலத் தொழியப்","பன்மா றோட்டிப் பெயர்புறம் பெற்று","மண்ணுற ஆழ்ந்த மணிநீர்க் கிடங்கின்","விண்ணுற வோங்கிய பல்படைப் புரிசைத்","தொல்வலி நிலைஇய அணங்குடை நெடுநிலை","நெய்படக் கரிந்த திண்போர்க் கதவின்","மழையாடு மலையி னிவந்த மாடமொடு","வையை யன்ன வழக்குடை வாயில்","வகைபெற எழுந்து வான மூழ்கி","சில்காற் றிசைக்கும் பல்புழை நல்லில்","யாறுகிடந் தன்ன அகனெடுந் தெருவிற்","பல்வேறு குழாஅத் திசையெழுந் தொலிப்ப","மாகா லெடுத்த முந்நீர் போல"],"p":"","r":"There too are those who lost lands as rich as Azhumbil of the proud victorious Vel, whose land and wealth no eye could take in, and those who lost their rich new homes, and the kings of lordly elephants who came in old enmity and fled, leaving their drums in the middle ground; then the city: a moat of clear water sunk deep, a many-layered rampart rising to the sky, a tall gateway with its guardian goddess and doors blackened with poured ghee, mansions like cloud-capped mountains, a gate as busy as the Vaiyai, many-windowed houses singing in the breeze, and long wide streets lying like a river, where the voices of many crowds rise like the sea lifted by a great wind.","n":"The city is approached through its refugees: those who lost lands and homes, and the defeated kings, stand at its edge before the moat and walls are described, so the king's capital is shown to be fed by his wars. The sense of கண்டமை கல்லா (line 343) is not certain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","pagai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 343-361","lb":"PP6:343-361","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",348,"maa","scene","elephant"],["முரச","முரசம்",349,"parai","absent","war drum"],["மழையாடு","மழை",355,"pira","scene","cloud"],["யாறு","யாறு",359,"pira","simile","river"],["முந்நீர்","முந்நீர்",361,"pira","simile","sea"]],"en":[["town","அழும்பில்",345],["river","வையை",356]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:362-379":{"book":"pattupattu","num":105,"t":["முழங்கிசை நன்பணை அறைவனர் நுவலக்","கயங்குடைந் தன்ன இயந்தொட் டிமிழிசை","மகிழ்ந்தோ ராடுங் கலிகொள் சும்மை","ஓவுக்கண் டன்ன இருபெரு நியமத்துச்","சாறயர்ந் தெடுத்த உருவப் பல்கொடி","வேறுபல் பெயர ஆரெயில் கொளக்கொள","நாடோ றெடுத்த நலம்பெறு புனைகொடி","நீர்ஒலித் தன்ன நிலவுவேற் றானையொடு","புலவுப்படக் கொன்று மிடைதோ லோட்டிப்","புகழ்செய் தெடுத்த விறல்சா னன்கொடி","கள்ளின் களிநவில் கொடியொடு நன்பல","பல்வேறு குழூஉக்கொடி பதாகை நிலைஇப்","பெருவரை மருங்கி னருவியி னுடங்கப்","பனைமீன் வழங்கும் வளைமேய் பரப்பின்","வீங்குபிணி நோன்கயி றரீஇ யிதைபுடையூக்","கூம்புமுதல் முருங்க எற்றிக் காய்ந்துடன்","கடுங்காற் றெடுப்பக் கல்பொரு துரைஇ","நெடுஞ்சுழிப் பட்ட நாவாய் போல"],"p":"","r":"Drummers proclaim the news on the loud drum, instruments ring like hands splashing a pool, the joyful dance makes a din, and over the two great markets, like a painting, stand the many figured flags of the festival, the flags raised each time a strong fort was taken, the flags of fame raised by those who routed the shield-ranks with an army of spears that roars like water, the flags of the toddy shops and many crowd banners and pennants, waving like falls on a great mountain side; like a ship in a deep whirlpool of the wide sea, its ropes snapped, sail torn, mast shattered by the fierce wind and dashed on the rocks...","n":"The flags are sorted by what they record (festival, each fort taken, a victory, the toddy shop), so the skyline of the market is a record of the king's wars; the ship simile that ends the unit is completed only by the elephant of line 383.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","pugazh","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 362-379","lb":"PP6:362-379","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["பணை","பணை",362,"parai","scene","drum"],["நீர்ஒலித்","நீர்",369,"pira","simile","water"],["கள்ளின்","கள்",372,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["னருவியி","அருவி",374,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["பனைமீன்","பனைமீன்",375,"pira","scene","great fish"],["வளைமேய்","வளை",375,"pira","scene","conch"],["நாவாய்","நாவாய்",379,"pira","simile","ship"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:380-393":{"book":"pattupattu","num":106,"t":["இருதலைப் பணில மார்ப்பச் சினஞ்சிறந்து","கோலோர்க் கொன்று மேலோர் வீசி","மென்பிணி வன்றொடர் பேணாது காழ்சாய்த்துக்","கந்துநீத் துழிதருங் கடாஅ யானையும்","அங்கண்மால் விசும்பு புதைய வளிபோழ்ந்து","ஒண்கதிர் ஞாயிற் றூறளவாத் திரிதரும்","செங்கால் அன்னத்துச் சேவல் அன்ன","குரூஉமயிர்ப் புரவி யுராலிற் பரிநிமிர்ந்து","காலெனக் கடுங்குங் கவின்பெறு தேருங்","கொண்ட கோலன் கொள்கை நவிற்றலின்","அடிபடு மண்டிலத் தாதி போகிய","கொடிபடு சுவல விடுமயிர்ப் புரவியும்","வேழத் தன்ன வெருவரு செலவிற்","கள்ளார் களமர் இருஞ்செரு மயக்கமும்"],"p":"","r":"With the two-headed conch sounding, the musth elephant in rage kills its goad-men, throws off its riders, ignores soft bonds and strong chains, bends its post and leaves its pillar to roam; the beautiful chariot runs fast as wind with horses of bright manes like the red-legged swan cock that cleaves the wind toward the sun; horses with plumed crests, trained in circling paces by the whip-holding rider, go by; and toddy-drunk field hands, fearsome in their going like elephants, brawl in a great tangle.","n":"Elephant, chariot and horse are the limbs of an army, but here they move through festival streets, and the fourth limb, the foot soldiers, is replaced by drunken field hands; களமர் may also be read as warriors, which would make the last line a mock battle. In line 386 அன்னத்து is the swan, not the particle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","kal","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 380-393","lb":"PP6:380-393","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"],["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["பணில","பணிலம்",380,"pira","scene","conch"],["யானையும்","யானை",383,"maa","scene","elephant"],["அன்னத்துச்","அன்னம்",386,"pul","simile","swan"],["புரவி","புரவி",387,"maa","scene","horse"],["வேழத்","வேழம்",392,"maa","simile","elephant"],["கள்ளார்","கள்",393,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["களமர்","களமர்",393,"seythi","scene","field hands"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:7"},"pp:6:394-409":{"book":"pattupattu","num":107,"t":["அரியவும் பெரியவும் வருவன பெயர்தலிற்","தீம்புழல் வல்சிக் கழற்கால் மழவர்","பூந்தலை முழவின் நோன்றலை கடுப்பப்","பிடகைப் பெய்த கமழ்நறும் பூவினர்","பலவகை விரித்த வெதிர்பூங் கோதையர்","பலர்தொகுபு இடித்த தாதுகு சுண்ணத்தர்","தகைசெய் தீஞ்சேற் றின்னீர்ப் பசுங்காய்","நீடுகொடி யிலையினர் கோடுசுடு நூற்றினர்","இருதலை வந்த பகைமுனை கடுப்ப","இன்னுயிர் அஞ்சி இன்னா வெய்துயிர்த்து","ஏங்குவன ரிருந்தவை நீங்கிய பின்றைப்","பல்வேறு பண்ணியந் தழீஇத்திரி விலைஞர்","மலைபுரை மாடத்துக் கொழுநிழல் இருத்தர","இருங்கடல் வான்கோடு புரைய வாருற்றுப்","பெரும்பின் னிட்ட வானரைக் கூந்தலர்","நன்னர் நலத்தர் தொன்முது பெண்டிர்"],"p":"","r":"After the rare and great things have passed and the fear of the crowd has lifted, as at a battlefront hemmed in from both sides, the hawkers carrying many wares sit in the cool shade of mountain-like mansions: sellers of fragrant flowers in baskets, of garlands, of powder pounded by many hands, of sweet areca, long betel leaves and lime burnt from shell, and old women with white hair braided long like the white conch of the dark sea.","n":"The unit opens inside a sentence that began in chapter 7 (the war crowd of elephants and horses in the streets); lines 402 to 404 seem to say that the townspeople who had stood in fear, as if between two enemy fronts, are released once that procession has passed, and only then does the market settle. What the drum-head simile of lines 395 and 396 is compared to is not fixed by the text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["angaadi","nagar","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 394-409","lb":"PP6:394-409","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","ஆக்கம்"]],"k":[["வல்சிக்","வல்சி",395,"unavu","scene","food"],["முழவின்","முழவு",396,"parai","simile","drum"],["பூவினர்","பூ",397,"maram","scene","flowers"],["சுண்ணத்தர்","சுண்ணம்",399,"pira","scene","fragrant powder"],["பசுங்காய்","பசுங் காய்",400,"maram","scene","areca nut"],["யிலையினர்","இலை",401,"maram","scene","betel leaf"],["நூற்றினர்","நூறு",401,"pira","scene","lime"],["இருங்கடல்","இரும் கடல்",407,"pira","simile","sea"],["வான்கோடு","வான் கோடு",407,"pira","simile","white conch"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:410-430":{"book":"pattupattu","num":108,"t":["செந்நீர்ப் பசும்பொன் புனைந்த பாவை","செல்சுடர்ப் பசுவெயிற் றோன்றி யன்ன","செய்யர் செயிர்த்த நோக்கினர் மடக்கண்","ஐஇய கலுழு மாமையர் வையெயிற்று","வார்ந்த வாயர் வணங்கிறைப் பணைத்தோட்","சோர்ந்துகு வன்ன வயக்குறு வந்திகைத்","தொய்யில் பொறித்த சுணங்கெதி ரிளமுலை","மையுக் கன்ன மொய்யிருங் கூந்தல்","மயிலிய லோரும் மடமொழி யோரும்","கைஇ மெல்லிதின் ஒதுங்கிக் கையெறிந்து","கல்லா மாந்தரொடு நகுவனர் திளைப்பப்","புடையமை பொலிந்த வகையமை செப்பிற்","காம ருருவிற் றாம்வேண்டு பண்ணியம்","கமழ்நறும் பூவொடு மனைமனை மறுக","மழைகொளக் குறையாது புனல்புக மிகாது","கரைபொரு திரங்கு முந்நீர் போலக்","கொளக்கொளக் குறையாது தரத்தர மிகாது","கழுநீர் கொண்ட எழுநாள் அந்தி","ஆடுதுவன்று விழவி னாடார்த் தன்றே","மாடம் பிறங்கிய மலிபுகழ்க் கூடல்","நாளங் காடி நனந்தலைக் கம்பலை"],"p":"","r":"Young women glowing like a golden doll in the tender light of the setting sun, with wounding glances, dark skin, slipping armlets, painted young breasts and hair thick as spilled kohl, some with a peacock's gait and some with soft speech, walk gently, clap and laugh and play with unlettered men, and carry their wares in decorated caskets with fragrant flowers from house to house; and the din of the wide day market of Koodal, never lessened by what is taken nor swollen by what is brought, like the sea that clouds drink from and rivers pour into, rose like a land in festival on the evening of the seventh day.","n":"The sea simile (lines 424 to 426) is built as a double balance: the sea keeps its level because what the clouds take and what the rivers bring cancel out, and the market keeps its plenty because taking and bringing go on together. The sentence's subject, the din of the day market, is held back until after its verb ஆர்த்தன்றே, the same pattern the poet uses for the evening market at line 544.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["angaadi","nagar","selvam","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 410-430","lb":"PP6:410-430","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","ஆக்கம்"]],"k":[["வந்திகைத்","வந்திகை",415,"pira","ornament","armlet"],["தொய்யில்","தொய்யில்",416,"pira","ornament","painted design"],["மயிலிய","மயில்",418,"pul","simile","peacock"],["பூவொடு","பூ",423,"maram","scene","flowers"],["மழைகொளக்","மழை",424,"pira","simile","rain cloud"],["முந்நீர்","முந்நீர்",425,"pira","simile","sea"],["கழுநீர்","கழுநீர்",427,"maram","scene","blue water-lily"]],"en":[["town","கூடல்",429]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:431-452":{"book":"pattupattu","num":109,"t":["வெயிற்கதிர் மழுங்கிய படர்கூர் ஞாயிற்றுச்","செக்கர் அன்ன சிவந்துணங் குருவிற்","கண்பொரு புகூஉம் ஒண்பூங் கலிங்கம்","பொன்புனை வாளொடு பொலியக் கட்டித்","திண்டேர்ப் பிரம்பிற் புரளுந் தானைக்","கச்சந் தின்ற கழறயங்கு திருந்தடி","மொய்ம்பிறந்து திரிதரும் ஒருபெருந் தெரியல்","மணிதொடர்ந் தன்ன வொண்பூங் கோதை","அணிகிளர் மார்பி னாரமொ டளைஇக்","காலியக் கன்ன கதழ்பரி கடைஇக்","காலோர் காப்பக் காலெனக் கழியும்","வான வண்கை வளங்கெழு செல்வர்","நாள்மகிழ் இருக்கை காண்மார் பூணொடு","தெள்ளரிப் பொற்சிலம் பொலிப்ப வொள்ளழல்","தாவற விளங்கிய வாய்பொன் னவிரிழை","அணங்குவீழ் வன்ன பூந்தொடி மகளிர்","மணங்கமழ் நாற்றந் தெருவுடன் கமழ","ஒண்குழை திகழும் ஒளிகெழு திருமுகந்","திண்காழ் ஏற்ற வியலிரு விலோதந்","தெண்கடற் றிரையின் அசைவளி புடைப்ப","நிரைநிலை மாடத் தரமியந் தோறும்","மழைமாய் மதியிற் றோன்றுபு மறைய"],"p":"","r":"Rich lords of cloud-like generous hands, in bright cloth red as the evening glow tied with gold-hilted swords, garlands like strung gems on their adorned chests, drive horses swift as the wind with runners guarding them; and to watch their daily pleasure, women with gold anklets and bright bangles, fair as goddesses come down, fill the street with fragrance, their bright faces appearing and vanishing like the moon among clouds on the terraces of tiered mansions, between banners that wave like the waves of the clear sea.","n":"The rich men are seen through the eyes of the women who come to look at them (காண்மார், line 443), so the street scene is doubled: display below, watching above. The moon among clouds (line 452) catches the women's faces as they show and hide behind the moving banners.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nagar","selvam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 431-452","lb":"PP6:431-452","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","ஆக்கம்"]],"k":[["ஞாயிற்றுச்","ஞாயிறு",431,"pira","simile","sun"],["தெரியல்","தெரியல்",437,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கோதை","கோதை",438,"maram","ornament","flower garland"],["கதழ்பரி","பரி",440,"maa","scene","horse"],["பொற்சிலம்","சிலம்பு",444,"pira","ornament","anklet"],["பூந்தொடி","தொடி",446,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["தெண்கடற்","தெண் கடல்",450,"pira","simile","sea"],["மழைமாய்","மழை",452,"pira","simile","cloud"],["மதியிற்","மதி",452,"pira","simile","moon"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:453-467":{"book":"pattupattu","num":110,"t":["நீரு நிலனுந் தீயும் வளியும்","மாக விசும்போ டைந்துட னியற்றிய","மழுவா ணெடியோன் றலைவ னாக","மாசற விளங்கிய யாக்கையர் சூழ்சுடர்","வாடாப் பூவின் இமையா நாட்டத்து","நாற்ற உணவின் உருகெழு பெரியோர்க்கு","மாற்றரு மரபி னுயர்பலி கொடுமார்","அந்தி விழவிற் றூரியங் கறங்கத்","திண்கதிர் மதாணி யொண்குறு மாக்களை","ஓம்பினர்த் தழீஇத் தாம்புணர்ந்து முயங்கித்","தாதணி தாமரைப் போதுபிடித் தாங்குத்","தாமு மவரும் ஓராங்கு விளங்கக்","காமர் கவினிய பேரிளம் பெண்டிர்","பூவினர் புகையினர் தொழுவனர் பழிச்சிச்","சிறந்து புறங்காக்குங் கடவுட் பள்ளியுஞ்"],"p":"","r":"With the axe-bearing tall one, who made water, earth, fire, wind and the great sky together, at their head, the gods of spotless shining bodies, unfading flowers, unblinking eyes and food of fragrance receive high offerings of unchanging custom at the evening festival with drums; and mature women holding little boys in bright pendants, as one holds a pollen-filled lotus bud, shining together with them, worship with flowers and incense at the gods' shrine that guards the city from outside.","n":"The god who made the five elements is named only by his axe (மழு) and his height (நெடியோன்); the text does not settle which god is meant. The gods' marks in lines 456 to 458 (no blinking, flowers that do not fade, food that is only fragrance) are the standard signs that distinguish gods from men.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 453-467","lb":"PP6:453-467","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["ணெடியோன்","நெடியோன்",455,"deyvam","scene","the tall god"],["பூவின்","பூ",457,"maram","scene","flower"],["றூரியங்","தூரியம்",460,"parai","scene","drums"],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",463,"maram","simile","lotus"],["பூவினர்","பூ",466,"maram","scene","flowers"],["புகையினர்","புகை",466,"pira","scene","incense"],["கடவுட்","கடவுள்",467,"deyvam","scene","god"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:468-488":{"book":"pattupattu","num":111,"t":["சிறந்த வேதம் விளங்கப் பாடி","விழுச்சீர் எய்திய ஒழுக்கமொடு புணர்ந்து","நிலமமர் வையத் தொருதா மாகி","உயர்நிலை யுலக மிவணின் றெய்தும்","அறநெறி பிழையா அன்புடை நெஞ்சிற்","பெரியோர் மேஎ யினிதி னுறையுங்","குன்றுகுயின் றன்ன அந்தணர் பள்ளியும்","வண்டுபடப் பழுநிய தேனார் தோற்றத்துப்","பூவும் புகையுஞ் சாவகர் பழிச்சச்","சென்ற காலமும் வரூஉ மமயமும்","இன்றிவண் தோன்றிய ஒழுக்கமொடு நன்குணர்ந்து","வானமு நிலனுந் தாமுழு துணருஞ்","சான்ற கொள்கைச் சாயா யாக்கை","ஆன்றடங் கறிஞர் செறிந்தனர் நோன்மார்","கல்பொளிந் தன்ன விட்டுவாய்க் கரண்டைப்","பல்புரிச் சிமிலி நாற்றி நல்குவரக்","கயங்கண் டன்ன வயங்குடை நகரத்துச்","செம்பியன் றன்ன செஞ்சுவர் புனைந்து","நோக்குவிசை தவிர்ப்ப மேக்குயர்ந் தோங்கி","இறும்பூது சான்ற நறும்பூஞ் சேக்கையுங்","குன்றுபல குழீஇப் பொலிவன தோன்ற"],"p":"","r":"Next the Brahmins' dwelling, like a hill carved out, where great ones of loving heart live at ease, singing the Veda, keeping a conduct of high merit, unique in this world and reaching the higher world from here without straying from the path of right; and the fragrant flowered dwelling of the sages, praised with flowers and incense by the lay followers (சாவகர்), where knowers who understand past and coming time and the present and know sky and earth whole, of unbending body and restrained, crowd in to keep their fasts, with water pots hung in slings of twisted cord, high red walls like copper rising beyond sight, the place shining like many hills gathered together.","n":"Two religious houses are set side by side: the Brahmins, marked by Veda and rite but also by a loving heart (அன்புடை நெஞ்சு, line 472), and a community of ascetics whose lay followers are called சாவகர் and who know past, present and future. The text names their practices (fasting, water pots in slings) but not their sect; that they are Jain or Buddhist is an inference.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","vedham","thuravu","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 468-488","lb":"PP6:468-488","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["குன்று","குன்று",474,"pira","simile","hill"],["வண்டுபடப்","வண்டு",475,"pira","scene","bee"],["தேனார்","தேன்",475,"unavu","scene","honey"],["பூவும்","பூ",476,"maram","scene","flowers"],["புகையுஞ்","புகை",476,"pira","scene","incense"],["கயங்கண்","கயம்",484,"pira","simile","pool"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:489-506":{"book":"pattupattu","num":112,"t":["அச்சமும் அவலமும் ஆர்வமு நீக்கிச்","செற்றமும் உவகையுஞ் செய்யாது காத்து","ஞெமன்கோ லன்ன செம்மைத் தாகிச்","சிறந்த கொள்கை அறங்கூ றவையமும்","நறுஞ்சாந்து நீவிய கேழ்கிளர் அகலத்து","ஆவுதி மண்ணி அவிர்துகில் முடித்து","மாவிசும்பு வழங்கும் பெரியோர் போல","நன்றுந் தீதுங் கண்டாய்ந் தடக்கி","அன்பும் அறனும் ஒழியாது காத்துப்","பழியொரீஇ யுயர்ந்து பாய்புகழ் நிறைந்த","செம்மை சான்ற காவிதி மாக்களும்","அறநெறி பிழையா தாற்றி னொழுகி","குறும்பல் குழுவிற் குன்றுகண் டன்ன","பருந்திருந் துகக்கும் பன்மா ணல்லிற்","பல்வேறு பண்டமொ டூண்மலிந்து கவினி","மலையவு நிலத்தவு நீரவும் பிறவும்","பல்வேறு திருமணி முத்தமொடு பொன்கொண்டு","சிறந்த தேஎத்துப் பண்ணியம் பகர்நரும்"],"p":"","r":"Then the court that speaks right, which puts away fear, grief and desire, and keeps from both hatred and delight, being straight like the beam of a balance; the kaviti officers with sandal on their chests, who like the great ones who move in the sky examine good and evil and restrain them, keep love and right, shun blame and are full of fame; and the merchants who never stray from right, in many-roomed houses like a cluster of hills where kites perch and fly, rich with goods and food, selling things of hill, land and water and gems, pearls and gold from excellent countries.","n":"Lines 489 and 490 list exactly what the judges must set aside: three feelings that bend a judgment by weakness (fear, grief, desire) and two that bend it by partisanship (hatred, delight). The balance word ஞெமன் is the one Purananuru 6 uses for the same image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","naduvu","aram","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 489-506","lb":"PP6:489-506","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["நறுஞ்சாந்து","சாந்து",493,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["பருந்திருந்","பருந்து",502,"pul","scene","kite"],["திருமணி","மணி",505,"pira","scene","gem"],["முத்தமொ","முத்தம்",505,"pira","scene","pearl"],["பொன்கொண்டு","பொன்",505,"pira","scene","gold"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:507-526":{"book":"pattupattu","num":113,"t":["மழையொழுக் கறாஅப் பிழையா விளையுட்","பழையன் மோகூர் அவையகம் விளங்க","நான்மொழிக் கோசர் தோன்றி யன்ன","தாமேஎந் தோன்றிய நாற்பெருங் குழுவும்","கோடுபோழ் கடைநருந் திருமணி குயினரும்","சூடுறு நன்பொன் சுடரிழை புனைநரும்","பொன்னுரை காண்மருங் கலிங்கம் பகர்நரும்","செம்புநிறை கொண்மரும் வம்புநிறை முடிநரும்","பூவும் புகையும் ஆயு மாக்களும்","எவ்வகைச் செய்தியும் உவமங் காட்டி","நுண்ணிதின் உணர்ந்த நுழைந்த நோக்கிற்","கண்ணுள் வினைஞரும் பிறரும் கூடித்","தெண்டிரை யவிரறல் கடுப்ப வொண்பகல்","குறியவு நெடியவு மடிதரூஉ விரித்துச்","சிறியரும் பெரியருங் கம்மியர் குழீஇ","நால்வேறு தெருவினுங் காலுற நிற்றரக்","கொடும்பறைக் கோடியர் கடும்புடன் வாழ்த்துந்","தண்கட னாடன் ஒண்பூங் கோதை","பெருநா ளிருக்கை விழுமியோர் குழீஇ","விழைவுகொள் கம்பலை கடுப்பப் பலவுடன்"],"p":"","r":"Like the four-tongued Kosar appearing at the assembly of Pazhaiyan's Mohur, a town of rain that never fails and harvests that never fail, the four great groups appear; and shell cutters, gem borers, goldsmiths, gold assayers, cloth sellers, weighers of copper, bundle tiers, those who choose flowers and incense, and painters of keen sight who can show any work in images, small and great craftsmen together, spread their goods short and long like the ripples of a clear wave and stand in all four streets, with a clamour like the eager din of the great day's assembly of the cool-sea lord, whom drum-beating kodiyar bless with their families.","n":"The poet measures Madurai's assembly against another lord's: the four-tongued Kosar at Pazhaiyan's Mohur, a vassal court. Which the four groups are is not said. The trades of lines 511 to 518 are a catalogue of the city's crafts, ending with the painter, whose work is to 'show every work by likeness' (எவ்வகைச் செய்தியும் உவமங் காட்டி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nagar","selvam","angaadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 507-526","lb":"PP6:507-526","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","ஆக்கம்"]],"k":[["மழையொழுக்","மழை",507,"pira","scene","rain"],["கடைநருந்","கடைநர்",511,"seythi","scene","shell cutters"],["புனைநரும்","புனைநர்",512,"seythi","scene","goldsmiths"],["வினைஞரும்","கண்ணுள் வினைஞர்",518,"seythi","scene","painters"],["தெண்டிரை","தெண் திரை",519,"pira","simile","clear wave"],["கம்மியர்","கம்மியர்",521,"seythi","scene","craftsmen"],["கொடும்பறைக்","பறை",523,"parai","scene","drum"],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",523,"seythi","scene","drummer-dancers"],["கோதை","கோதை",524,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[["king","பழையன்",508],["town","மோகூர்",508]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:527-544":{"book":"pattupattu","num":114,"t":["சேறு நாற்றமும் பலவின் சுளையும்","வேறுபடக் கவினிய தேமாங் கனியும்","பல்வே றுருவிற் காயும் பழனும்","கொண்டல் வளர்ப்பக் கொடிவிடுபு கவினி","மென்பிணி யவிழ்ந்த குறுமுறி யடகும்","அமிர்தியன் றன்ன தீஞ்சேற்றுக் கடிகையும்","புகழ்படப் பண்ணிய பேரூன் சோறும்","கீழ்செல வீழ்ந்த கிழங்கொடு பிறவும்","இன்சோறு தருநர் பல்வயி னுகர","வாலிதை எடுத்த வளிதரு வங்கம்","பல்வேறு பண்ட மிழிதரும் பட்டினத்","தொல்லென் இமிழிசை மானக் கல்லென","நனந்தலை வினைஞர் கலங்கொண்டு மறுகப்","பெருங்கடற் குட்டத்துப் புலவுத்திரை யோதம்","இருங்கழி மருவிப் பாயப் பெரிதெழுந்து","உருகெழு பானாள் வருவன பெயர்தலிற்","பல்வேறு புள்ளின் இசையெழுந் தற்றே","அல்லங் காடி அழிதரு கம்பலை"],"p":"","r":"Fragrant sap, jackfruit segments, sweet mangoes of rare beauty, vegetables and fruits of many shapes, tender greens with their buds loosed, grown by the east wind's rain, pieces of sweet cane like nectar, famous rice with meat, tubers that grow downward and the rest are eaten everywhere from those who serve sweet rice; and, like the roar of the port where wind-driven ships with white sails unload many goods, or the cries of many birds that rise at midnight when the flood of the great sea pours into the backwaters, there spills the clamour of the evening market.","n":"As at line 430, the subject (the din of the evening market) comes after the predicate, so the two markets of Madurai, the day market (நாளங்காடி) and the evening market (அல்லங்காடி), each end a long period on the word கம்பலை. The noise is twice compared to the sea: the port, and the tide flooding the backwaters at midnight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["angaadi","undi","nagar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 527-544","lb":"PP6:527-544","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["பலவின்","பலா",527,"maram","gift_food","jackfruit"],["தேமாங்","தேமா",528,"maram","gift_food","mango"],["பழனும்","பழம்",529,"maram","gift_food","fruit"],["கொண்டல்","கொண்டல்",530,"pira","scene","east wind"],["யடகும்","அடகு",531,"maram","gift_food","greens"],["கடிகையும்","கடிகை",532,"maram","gift_food","sugarcane piece"],["பேரூன்","ஊன்",533,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["கிழங்கொடு","கிழங்கு",534,"maram","gift_food","tuber"],["வங்கம்","வங்கம்",536,"pira","simile","ship"],["இருங்கழி","கழி",541,"pira","simile","backwater"],["புள்ளின்","புள்",543,"pul","simile","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:545-561":{"book":"pattupattu","num":115,"t":["ஒண்சுடர் உருப்பொளி மழுங்கச் சினந்தணிந்து","சென்ற ஞாயிறு நன்பகற் கொண்டு","குடமுதற் குன்றஞ் சேரக் குணமுதல்","நாள்முதிர் மதியந் தோன்றி நிலாவிரிபு","பகலுரு வுற்ற இரவுவர நயந்தோர்","காதல் இன்றுணை புணர்மார் ஆயிதழ்த்","தண்ணறுங் கழுநீர் துணைப்ப இழைபுனையூஉ","நன்னெடுங் கூந்த னறுவிரை குடைய","நரந்த மரைப்ப நறுஞ்சாந்து மறுக","மென்னூற் கலிங்கங் கமழ்புகை மடுப்பப்","பெண்மகிழ் வுற்ற பிணைநோக்கு மகளிர்","நெடுஞ்சுடர் விளக்கம் கொளீஇ நெடுநகர்","எல்லை எல்லா நோயொடு புகுந்து","கல்லென் மாலை நீங்க நாணுக்கொள","ஏழ்புணர் சிறப்பின் இன்றொடைச் சீறியாழ்","தாழ்பயற் கனைகுரல் கடுப்பப் பண்ணுப்பெயர்த்து","வீழ்துணை தழீஇ வியல்விசும்பு கமழ"],"p":"","r":"The sun, its burning light dimmed and its anger cooled, takes the good day with it to the western hill; from the east the full moon rises and night comes wearing the look of day; women who long to join their dear partners string cool water-lilies, put on jewels, perfume their long hair, grind narantham, stir sandal and smoke their soft clothes; doe-eyed women glad in their womanhood light tall lamps; love-sickness enters every part of the great city as the noisy evening departs; and, tuning the seven-stringed small lute to a changed mode, they embrace the partners they desire, so that the wide sky is fragrant.","n":"This is akam matter (lovers making ready for union at nightfall) carried inside a puram poem's tour of the city, so the love-sickness (நோய், line 557) is not one woman's but the whole city's. Line 549 makes the moonlit night 'take on the form of day', which is why the city can keep moving. The vehicle of the simile in line 560 (தாழ்பயல்) is not clear to us.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","maalai","kaamam","nagar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 545-561","lb":"PP6:545-561","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"mixed","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["ஞாயிறு","ஞாயிறு",546,"pira","scene","sun"],["மதியந்","மதியம்",548,"pira","scene","moon"],["கழுநீர்","கழுநீர்",551,"maram","ornament","blue water-lily"],["நரந்த","நரந்தம்",553,"maram","ornament","narantham fragrance"],["நறுஞ்சாந்து","சாந்து",553,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["கமழ்புகை","புகை",554,"pira","scene","perfuming smoke"],["பிணைநோக்கு","பிணை",555,"maa","simile","doe"],["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",559,"yaazh","scene","small lute"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:562-589":{"book":"pattupattu","num":116,"t":["நீர்திரண் டன்ன கோதை பிறக்கிட்டு","ஆய்கோல் அவிர்தொடி விளங்க வீசிப்","போதவிழ் புதுமலர் தெருவுடன் கமழ","மேதகு தகைய மிகுநல மெய்திப்","பெரும்பல் குவளைச் சுரும்புபடு பன்மலர்","திறந்துமோந் தன்ன சிறந்துகமழ் நாற்றத்துக்","கொண்டல் மலர்ப்புதல் மானப்பூ வேய்ந்து","நுண்பூ ணாகம் வடுக்கொள முயங்கி","மாயப் பொய்பல கூட்டிக் கவவுக்கரந்து","சேயரு நணியரு நலனயந்து வந்த","இளம்பல் செல்வர் வளந்தப வாங்கி","நுண்தா துண்டு வறும்பூத் துறக்கும்","மென்சிறை வண்டின மானப் புணர்ந்தோர்","நெஞ்சே மாப்ப இன்றுயில் துறந்து","பழந்தேர் வாழ்க்கைப் பறவை போலக்","கொழுங்குடிச் செல்வரும் பிறரு மேஎய","மணம்புணர்ந் தோங்கிய அணங்குடை நல்லில்","ஆய்பொன் அவிர்தொடிப் பாசிழை மகளிர்","ஒண்சுடர் விளக்கத்துப் பலருடன் துவன்றி","நீனிற விசும்பில் அமர்ந்தனர் ஆடும்","வானவ மகளிர் மானக் கண்டோர்","நெஞ்சு நடுங்குறூஉக் கொண்டி மகளிர்","யாம நல்யாழ் நாப்ப ணின்ற","முழவின் மகிழ்ந்தனர் ஆடிக் குண்டுநீர்ப்","பனித்துறைக் குவவுமணல் முனைஇ மென்றளிர்க்","கொழுங்கொம்பு கொழுதி நீர்நனை மேவர","நெடுந்தொடர்க் குவளை வடிம்புற அடைச்சி","மணங்கமழ் மனைதொறும் பொய்தல் அயரக்"],"p":"","r":"The courtesans throw off garlands bright as gathered water, swing their bright bangles, fill the street with the scent of fresh flowers, deck themselves like a flowering bush in the rains, embrace until their ornaments scar the chest, weave many deceiving lies and hide their true embrace, and draw in the many rich young men who came from far and near for their beauty until their wealth is spent, then leave them as soft-winged bees drink the pollen and abandon the empty flower; and like fruit-seeking birds the wealthy householders and others come to their fine houses, where women in bright bangles crowd under lamps like celestial women dancing in the blue sky, kondi women who make onlookers' hearts tremble, dance to the drum amid the midnight lute, and, tired of the sand of the cold ford, pluck tender sprays, put on long-stalked water-lilies and play at poythal in every fragrant house.","n":"The poet names the deceit outright (மாயப் பொய்பல, line 570) and the draining of wealth (வளந்தப வாங்கி, line 572), and gives the men two images that are not flattering, the bee at an empty flower and the bird that lives by seeking fruit; but the scene stays inside the praise of the city. கொண்டி (line 583) elsewhere means plunder, so these may be women brought in from conquered lands.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","parathai","selvam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 562-589","lb":"PP6:562-589","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"mixed","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கோதை","கோதை",562,"maram","ornament","garland"],["புதுமலர்","மலர்",564,"maram","scene","fresh flowers"],["குவளைச்","குவளை",566,"maram","simile","water-lily"],["சுரும்புபடு","சுரும்பு",566,"pira","simile","bee"],["மலர்ப்புதல்","புதல்",568,"maram","simile","flowering bush"],["வண்டின","வண்டு",574,"pira","simile","bee"],["பறவை","பறவை",576,"pul","simile","bird"],["நல்யாழ்","யாழ்",584,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["முழவின்","முழவு",585,"parai","scene","drum"],["மணல்","மணல்",586,"pira","scene","sand"],["குவளை","குவளை",588,"maram","ornament","water-lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:590-603":{"book":"pattupattu","num":117,"t":["கணங்கொள் அவுணர்க் கடந்த பொலந்தார்","மாயோன் மேய ஓண நன்னாட்","கோணந் தின்ற வடுவாழ் முகத்த","சாணந் தின்ற சமந்தாங்கு தடக்கை","மறங்கொள் சேரி மாறுபொரு செருவில்","மாறா துற்ற வடுப்படு நெற்றிச்","சுரும்பார் கண்ணிப் பெரும்புகல் மறவர்","கடுங்களி றோட்டலிற் காணுநர் இட்ட","நெடுங்கரைக் காழக நிலம்பர லுறுப்பக்","கடுங்கள் தேறல் மகிழ்சிறந்து திரிதரக்","கணவ ருவப்பப் புதல்வர்ப் பயந்து","பணைத்தேந் திளமுலை அமுதம் ஊறப்","புலவுப்புனிறு தீர்ந்து பொலிந்த சுற்றமொடு","வளமனை மகளிர் குளநீர் அயரத்"],"p":"","r":"On the good day of Onam, the feast of Mayon of the golden garland who overcame the hosts of asuras, warriors of the fighting quarter, faces and foreheads scarred from unyielding battle, hands that bear the fight, wearing garlands full of bees, eager for combat, drive fierce elephants at each other in mock battle so that the cloths the watchers spread are strewn on the ground, and roam drunk on strong toddy; and women of rich houses who have borne sons to their husbands' joy, milk flowing from their young breasts, the smell of childbirth gone, bathe in the tank with their shining kin.","n":"Lines 592 to 598 are hard: what scarred the warriors' faces (கோணம், சாணம்) and what the watchers threw on the shore (காழகம்) we read only tentatively. The festival joins two kinds of strength the city honours, the scarred warrior and the mother newly risen from childbirth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vizhavu","maram","kal","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 590-603","lb":"PP6:590-603","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["மாயோன்","மாயோன்",591,"deyvam","scene","Mayon"],["கண்ணிப்","கண்ணி",596,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கடுங்களி","களிறு",597,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கடுங்கள்","கள்",599,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["குளநீர்","குளம்",603,"pira","scene","tank"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:604-619":{"book":"pattupattu","num":118,"t":["திவவுமெய்ந் நிறுத்துச் செவ்வழி பண்ணிக்","குரல்புணர் நல்யாழ் முழவோ டொன்றி","நுண்ணீ ராகுளி இரட்டப் பலவுடன்","ஒண்சுடர் விளக்க முந்துற மடையொடு","நன்மா மயிலின் மென்மெல இயலிக்","கடுஞ்சூன் மகளிர் பேணிக் கைதொழுது","பெருந்தோட் சாலினி மடுப்ப ஒருசார்","அருங்கடி வேலன் முருகொடு வளைஇ","அரிக்கூ டின்னியங் கறங்கநேர் நிறுத்துக்","கார்மலர்க் குறிஞ்சி சூடிக் கடம்பின்","சீர்மிகு நெடுவேட் பேணித் தழூஉப்பிணையூஉ","மன்றுதொறு நின்ற குரவை சேரிதொறும்","உரையும் பாட்டும் ஆட்டும் விரைஇ","வேறுவேறு கம்பலை வெறிகொள்பு மயங்கிப்","பேரிசை நன்னன் பெரும்பெயர் நன்னாட்","சேரி விழவின் ஆர்ப்பெழுந் தாங்கு"],"p":"","r":"Setting the frets and tuning the sevvazhi mode, the lute joined with the drum and the small aguli beating, lamps going ahead with the offerings, pregnant women moving gently like peacocks worship with joined hands while the broad-shouldered priestess gives them the offering; on one side the velan of strict rites surrounds them with Murugan's presence to the sound of sweet instruments, and wearing kurinji flowers of the rainy season and kadamba they honour the tall Vel and dance the kuravai hand in hand in every square; in every quarter speech, song and dance mingle, many separate clamours whirl in frenzy, like the roar of the street festival in the land of Nannan of great fame.","n":"The hill god's rite has come into the city: the kurinji flower (a thing of another land, line 613) is worn in Madurai's squares. The priestess (சாலினி) and the velan serve women at a life crisis, pregnancy, which suggests what the Murugan rite was used for in town.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velan","veri","vazhipaadu","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 604-619","lb":"PP6:604-619","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["நல்யாழ்","யாழ்",605,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["முழவோ","முழவு",605,"parai","scene","drum"],["ராகுளி","ஆகுளி",606,"parai","scene","small drum"],["மயிலின்","மயில்",608,"pul","simile","peacock"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",611,"seythi","scene","velan"],["முருகொடு","முருகு",611,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"],["குறிஞ்சி","குறிஞ்சி",613,"maram","ornament","kurinji flower"],["கடம்பின்","கடம்பு",613,"maram","ornament","kadamba"],["நெடுவேட்","நெடுவேள்",614,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"]],"en":[["king","நன்னன்",618]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:620-631":{"book":"pattupattu","num":119,"t":["முந்தை யாமம் சென்ற பின்றைப்","பணிலங் கலியவிந் தடங்கக் காழ்சாய்த்து","நொடைநவில் நெடுங்கடை அடைத்து மடமதர்","ஒள்ளிழை மகளிர் பள்ளி யயர","நல்வரி இறாஅல் புரையு மெல்லடை","அயிருருப் புற்ற ஆடமை விசயங்","கவவொடு பிடித்த வகையமை மோதகந்","தீஞ்சேற்றுக் கூவியர் தூங்குவனர் உறங்க","விழவி னாடும் வயிரியர் மடியப்","பாடான் றவிந்த பனிக்கடல் புரையப்","பாயல் வளர்வோர் கண்ணினிது மடுப்பப்","பானாட் கொண்ட கங்கு லிடையது"],"p":"","r":"When the first watch of night has gone, the conch's noise dies down, the traders lower their bars and shut their long shop doors, young women bright in jewels go to bed, the sweet-makers who sell soft cakes like fine-lined honeycomb, sugar shaped like fine sand and carefully folded modakam doze and sleep, the dancers of the festival lie still, and, like the cold sea when its roar has ceased, those on their beds sleep sweetly in the depth of midnight.","n":"The city that roared like the sea by day (lines 424 to 426, 536 to 543) is now the sea with its roar ceased; the poet keeps one image for Madurai across the whole day.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nagar","kaalam","vizhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 620-631","lb":"PP6:620-631","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பணிலங்","பணிலம்",621,"pira","scene","conch"],["இறாஅல்","இறால்",624,"pira","simile","honeycomb"],["மெல்லடை","அடை",624,"unavu","scene","soft cake"],["விசயங்","விசயம்",625,"unavu","scene","sugar"],["மோதகந்","மோதகம்",626,"unavu","scene","modakam"],["பனிக்கடல்","பனிக் கடல்",629,"pira","simile","sea"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:632-653":{"book":"pattupattu","num":120,"t":["பேயும் அணங்கும் உருவுகொண் டாய்கோற்","கூற்றக் கொஃறேர் கழுதொடு கொட்ப","இரும்பிடி மேஎந்தோ லன்ன இருள்சேர்பு","கல்லு மரனுந் துணிக்குங் கூர்மைத்","தொடலை வாளர் தொடுதோ லடியர்","குறங்கிடைப் பதித்த கூர்நுனைக் குறும்பிடிச்","சிறந்த கருமை நுண்வினை நுணங்கறல்","நிறங்கவர்பு புனைந்த நீலக் கச்சினர்","மென்னூல் ஏணிப் பன்மாண் சுற்றினர்","நிலனகழ் உளியர் கலனசைஇக் கொட்கும்","கண்மா றாடவர் ஒடுக்க மொற்றி","வயக்களிறு பார்க்கும் வயப்புலி போலத்","துஞ்சாக் கண்ணர் அஞ்சாக் கொள்கையர்","அறிந்தோர் புகழ்ந்த ஆண்மையர் செறிந்த","நூல்வழிப் பிழையா நுணங்குநுண் டேர்ச்சி","ஊர்காப் பாளர் ஊக்கருங் கணையினர்","தேர்வழங்கு தெருவி னீர்திரண் டொழுக","மழையமைந் துற்ற அரைநா ளமயமும்","அசைவிலர் எழுந்து நயம்வந்து வழங்கலிற்","கடவுள் வழங்குங் கையாறு கங்குலும்","அச்ச மறியா தேம மாகிய","மற்றை யாமம் பகலுறக் கழிப்பிப்"],"p":"","r":"In that midnight, ghosts and spirits take on forms and roam with Death of the killing chariot and the demons, and in darkness thick as the hide of a black she-elephant, thieves go about seeking jewels, with swords that cut stone and wood, leather on their feet, short daggers at the thigh, blue belts, rope ladders of soft thread and chisels that dig through earth; but the town's guards, who track the hiding places of these men, sleepless like a strong tiger watching a strong elephant, fearless, praised for their manhood, never straying from the treatise and keen in judgment, with arrows none can escape, rise tirelessly and walk the streets through the half night of rain, when water runs in the chariot streets and the gods themselves walk, so that the last watch passes safe and without fear until day.","n":"The thieves' kit is listed as closely as the craftsmen's tools by day (lines 511 to 518): night too has its trades. The guards are praised as men who keep to the treatise (நூல்வழிப் பிழையா, line 646), so watch-keeping is a learned craft, not only courage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","anangu","acham","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 632-653","lb":"PP6:632-653","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","கள்வர்"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["பேயும்","பேய்",632,"pira","scene","ghosts"],["அணங்கும்","அணங்கு",632,"deyvam","scene","spirits"],["கூற்றக்","கூற்றம்",633,"deyvam","scene","Death"],["கழுதொடு","கழுது",633,"pira","scene","demons"],["இரும்பிடி","பிடி",634,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["வயக்களிறு","களிறு",643,"maa","simile","elephant"],["வயப்புலி","புலி",643,"maa","simile","tiger"],["மழையமைந்","மழை",649,"pira","scene","rain"],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",651,"deyvam","scene","gods"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:654-667":{"book":"pattupattu","num":121,"t":["போதுபிணி விட்ட கமழ்நறும் பொய்கைத்","தாதுண் தும்பி போது முரன்றாங்","கோத லந்தணர் வேதம் பாடச்","சீரினிது கொண்டு நரம்பினி தியக்கி","யாழோர் மருதம் பண்ணக் காழோர்","கடுங்களிறு கவளங் கைப்ப நெடுந்தேர்ப்","பணைநிலைப் புரவி புல்லுணாத் தெவிட்டப்","பல்வேறு பண்ணியக் கடைமெழுக் குறுப்பக்","கள்ளோர் களிதொடை நுவல இல்லோர்","நயந்த காதலர் கவவுப்பிணித் துஞ்சிப்","புலர்ந்துவிரி விடிய லெய்த விரும்பிக்","கண்பொரா வெறிக்கு மின்னுக்கொடி புரைய","ஒண்பொ னவிரிழை தெழிப்ப இயலித்","திண்சுவர் நல்லிற் கதவங் கரைய"],"p":"","r":"At dawn, like the bee that drinks pollen humming at the bud in a fragrant pond whose flowers have loosed their bonds, the Brahmins who recite chant the Veda; lute players tune the strings sweetly and play the marutham mode; mahouts feed balls of food to the fierce elephants; the stabled horses of the tall chariots are sated with grass; the shops of many wares are washed with dung; the toddy sellers call out their drink; and wives who slept in their loving husbands' arms, wanting the spreading dawn, walk with their bright gold jewels ringing, like streaks of lightning that dazzle the eye, and the doors of the strong-walled houses creak open.","n":"The lute players' mode is marutham, the mode of the landscape whose hour, by porul 1.9, is dawn; the music keeps the time-scheme of akam even in a city poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vedham","andhanar","nagar","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 654-667","lb":"PP6:654-667","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பொய்கைத்","பொய்கை",654,"pira","simile","pond"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",655,"pira","simile","bee"],["யாழோர்","யாழ்",658,"yaazh","scene","lute players"],["மருதம்","மருதம்",658,"yaazh","scene","the marutham mode"],["கடுங்களிறு","களிறு",659,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கவளங்","கவளம்",659,"unavu","gift_food","food ball"],["புரவி","புரவி",660,"maa","scene","horse"],["புல்லுணாத்","புல்",660,"maram","gift_food","grass"],["கள்ளோர்","கள்ளோர்",662,"seythi","scene","toddy sellers"],["மின்னுக்கொடி","மின்னு",665,"pira","simile","lightning"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:668-686":{"book":"pattupattu","num":122,"t":["உண்டுமகிழ் தட்ட மழலை நாவிற்","பழஞ்செருக் காளர் தழங்குகுரல் தோன்றச்","சூதர் வாழ்ந்த மாகதர் நுவல","வேதா ளிகரொடு நாழிகை இசைப்ப","இமிழ்முர சிரங்க ஏறுமாறு சிலைப்பப்","பொறிமயிர் வாரணம் வைகறை இயம்ப","யானையங் குருகின் சேவலொடு காமர்","அன்னங் கரைய அணிமயில் அகவப்","பிடிபுணர் பெருங்களிறு முழங்க முழுவலிக்","கூட்டுறை வயமாப் புலியொடு குழும","வான நீங்கிய நீனிற விசும்பின்","மின்னுநிமிர்ந் தனைய ராகி நறவுமகிழ்ந்து","மாணிழை மகளிர் புலந்தனர் பரிந்த","பரூஉக்கா ழாரஞ் சொரிந்த முத்தமொடு","பொன்சுடு நெருப்பி னிலமுக் கென்ன","அம்மென் குரும்பைக் காய்படுபு பிறவுந்","தருமணன் முற்றத் தரிஞிமி றார்ப்ப","மென்பூஞ் செம்மலொடு நன்கலஞ் சீப்ப","இரவுத்தலைப் பெயரு மேம வைகறை"],"p":"","r":"Old warriors whose tongues lisp with drink raise their roaring voices; the sootar praise, the magadhar recite and the vaitalikar announce the hours; the sounding drum resounds and bulls bellow in answer; the spotted cock crows at daybreak; the heron with its mate and the lovely swan cry, the peacock calls, the great tusker with its cow trumpets, the caged beast roars with the tiger; and women of fine jewels, like lightning leaping in a sky cleared of cloud, who sulked over their wine and tore their thick necklaces, have the scattered pearls, the fallen young fruits and the soft faded flowers swept from the sanded courtyards where bees hum, at the safe dawn when night departs.","n":"The dawn is told entirely by sound: men (warriors, bards, heralds), then drum and bull, then birds, then beasts. The women's sulking over wine (புலந்தனர், line 680) is akam's oodal, glimpsed only by its litter. Lines 682 and 683 (the 'fire that melts gold' simile and the young fruits) we cannot fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nagar","kaalam","kal","oodal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 668-686","lb":"PP6:668-686","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முர","முரசு",672,"parai","scene","drum"],["ஏறுமாறு","ஏறு",672,"maa","scene","bull"],["வாரணம்","வாரணம்",673,"pul","scene","cock"],["குருகின்","குருகு",674,"pul","scene","heron"],["அன்னங்","அன்னம்",675,"pul","scene","swan"],["அணிமயில்","மயில்",675,"pul","scene","peacock"],["பெருங்களிறு","களிறு",676,"maa","scene","elephant"],["புலியொடு","புலி",677,"maa","scene","tiger"],["முத்தமொடு","முத்தம்",681,"pira","scene","pearl"],["குரும்பைக்","குரும்பை",683,"maram","scene","young fruit"],["ஞிமி","ஞிமிறு",684,"pira","scene","bee"],["செம்மலொடு","செம்மல்",685,"maram","scene","faded flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:687-699":{"book":"pattupattu","num":123,"t":["மைபடு பெருந்தோள் மழவ ரோட்டி","இடைப்புலத் தொழிந்த ஏந்துகோட் டியானை","பகைப்புலங் கவர்ந்த பாய்பரிப் புரவி","வேல்கோ லாக ஆள்செல நூறிக்","காய்சின முன்பிற் கடுங்கட் கூளியர்","ஊர்சுடு விளக்கிற் றந்த ஆயமும்","நாடுடை நல்லெயில் அணங்குடைத் தோட்டி","நாடொறும் விளங்கக் கைதொழூஉப் பழிச்சி","நாடர வந்த விழுக்கல மனைத்தும்","கங்கையம் பேரியாறு கடற்படர்ந் தாங்கு","அளந்துகடை யறியா வளங்கெழு தாரமொடு","புத்தே ளுலகம் கவினிக் காண்வர","மிக்குப்புகழ் எய்திய பெரும்பெயர் மதுரைச்"],"p":"","r":"Tuskers left behind in the middle ground when the dark-shouldered mazhavar were routed, leaping horses seized in enemy country, herds that the fierce kooliyar, cutting men down with the spear as their goad, brought back by the light of burning towns, and all the precious things that came from every land, worshipped daily at the guarded gate of the good fort, pour into Madurai of great name with immeasurable wealth, like the great Ganges spreading into the sea, so that it shines like the gods' world and has surpassing fame.","n":"The wealth of the markets has a source, and this unit names it: elephants left on the field, horses taken, herds driven home by the light of burning towns. The 'burning towns' (ஊர்சுடு) are what the Purananuru's raid poems celebrate; here they are the start of a flow that ends in the city's splendour. What the அணங்குடைத் தோட்டி of line 693 is (a guarded gate, a goad hung as an emblem) we read tentatively.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","selvam","maram","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 687-699","lb":"PP6:687-699","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["marutkai","ஆக்கம்"]],"k":[["டியானை","யானை",688,"maa","scene","elephant"],["புரவி","புரவி",689,"maa","scene","horse"],["வேல்கோ","வேல்",690,"pira","simile","spear"],["கடற்படர்ந்","கடல்",696,"pira","simile","sea"]],"en":[["river","கங்கை",696],["town","மதுரை",699]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:700-724":{"book":"pattupattu","num":124,"t":["சினைதலை மணந்த சுரும்புபடு செந்தீ","ஒண்பூம் பிண்டி அவிழ்ந்த காவிற்","சுடர்பொழிந் தேறிய விளங்குகதிர் ஞாயிற்று","இலங்குகதி ரிளவெயிற் றோன்றி யன்ன","தமனியம் வளைஇய தாவில் விளங்கிழை","நிலம்விளக் குறுப்ப மேதகப் பொலிந்து","மயிலோ ரன்ன சாயல் மாவின்","தளிரே ரன்ன மேனித் தளிர்ப்புறத்து","ஈர்க்கி னரும்பிய திதலையர் கூரெயிற்","றொண்குழை புணரிய வண்டாழ் காதிற்","கடவுட் கயத்தமன்ற சுடரிதழ்த் தாமரைத்","தாதுபடு பெரும்போது புரையும் வாண்முகத்","தாய்தொடி மகளிர் நறுந்தோள் புணர்ந்து","கோதையிற் பொலிந்த சேக்கைத் துஞ்சித்","திருந்துதுயில் எடுப்ப இனிதி னெழுந்து","திண்கா ழார நீவிக் கதிர்விடு","மொண்காழ் ஆரங் கவைஇய மார்பின்","வரிக்கடைப் பிரச மூசுவன மொய்ப்ப","எருத்தந் தாழ்ந்த விரவுப்பூந் தெரியற்","பொலஞ்செயப் பொலிந்த நலம்பெறு விளக்கம்","வலிகெழு தடக்கைத் தொடியொடு சுடர்வரச்","சோறமை வுற்ற நீருடைக் கலிங்கம்","உடையணி பொலியக் குறைவின்று கவைஇ","வல்லோன் தைஇய வரிப்புனை பாவை","முருகியன் றன்ன உருவினை யாகி"],"p":"","r":"In a grove where the ashoka's bright flowers, red as fire and humming with bees, have opened on every branch, you embraced the fragrant shoulders of women whose gold jewels shine like the young sunlight of the rising sun, with a peacock's gentle grace, skin like mango shoots, freckles on their backs, bright earrings in their ears and faces like the great pollen-laden lotus of the god's pond, and slept on a bed bright with garlands; then, rising sweetly when good sleep left you, you rubbed sandal on your chest hung with bright pearl strings, the bee-swarmed garland hanging to your neck, gold armlets shining on your strong hands, starched cloth wrapped as your dress, and stood in a form like Murugan's, like a painted figure made by a master.","n":"Here the poem turns from the city to the king and from the third person to 'you' (நின், line 727, and the second-person உருவினை யாகி, line 724). His waking repeats the city's: Madurai woke to sounds and opened doors, the king wakes from his women's arms and is dressed, so the ruler's day is told as one more part of the city's day.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","punarchi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 700-724","lb":"PP6:700-724","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"mixed","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["சுரும்புபடு","சுரும்பு",700,"pira","scene","bee"],["பிண்டி","பிண்டி",701,"maram","scene","ashoka"],["ஞாயிற்று","ஞாயிறு",702,"pira","simile","sun"],["மயிலோ","மயில்",706,"pul","simile","peacock"],["மாவின்","மா",706,"maram","simile","mango"],["தாமரைத்","தாமரை",710,"maram","simile","lotus"],["கோதையிற்","கோதை",713,"maram","ornament","garland"],["பிரச","பிரசம்",717,"pira","scene","bees"],["தெரியற்","தெரியல்",718,"maram","ornament","garland"],["முருகியன்","முருகு",724,"deyvam","simile","Murugan"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:725-746":{"book":"pattupattu","num":125,"t":["வருபுனற் கற்சிறை கடுப்ப விடையறுத்து","ஒன்னா ரோட்டிய செருப்புகல் மறவர்","வாள்வளம் புணர்ந்தநின் தாள்வலம் வாழ்த்த","வில்லைக் கவைஇக் கணைதாங்கு மார்பின்","மாதாங் கெறுழ்த்தோள் மறவர்த் தம்மின்","கல்லிடித் தியற்றிய இட்டுவாய்க் கிடங்கின்","நல்லெயி லுழந்த செல்வர்த் தம்மின்","கொல்லேற்றுப் பைந்தோல் சீவாது போர்த்த","மாக்கண் முரசம் ஓவில கறங்க","எரிநிமிர்ந் தன்ன தானை நாப்பண்","பெருநல் யானை போர்க்களத் தொழிய","விழுமிய வீழ்ந்த குரிசிலர்த் தம்மின்","புரையோர்க்குத் தொடுத்த பொலம்பூந் தும்பை","நீர்யார் என்னாது முறைகருதுபு சூட்டிக்","காழ்மண் டெஃகமொடு கணையலைக் கலங்கிப்","பிரிபிணை யரிந்த நிறஞ்சிதை கவயத்து","வானத் தன்ன வளநகர் பொற்ப","நோன்குறட் டன்ன ஊன்சாய் மார்பின்","உயர்ந்த உதவி ஊக்கலர்த் தம்மின்","நிவந்த யானைக் கணநிரை கவர்ந்த","புலர்ந்த சாந்தின் விரவுப்பூந் தெரியற்","பெருஞ்செய் ஆடவர்த் தம்மின் பிறரும்"],"p":"","r":"Warriors eager for battle, who cut through and routed the enemy like a stone dam that stops a flood, praise the victory of your feet joined with the sword's wealth; and the strong-armed warriors whose chests took arrows round the bow, the lords who strove at strong forts with stone-cut moats, the chiefs who fell nobly where great elephants fell in battle amid an army like rising flame and drums of unscraped bull hide sounded ceaselessly, and those of worn chests like a strong hub, armour split by spears and arrows, who did not slacken in high service, and the men of great deeds who captured herds of tall elephants: on the worthy you set the golden thumbai, not asking who they are but weighing their order of merit.","n":"Line 738 is the hinge: the king crowns the worthy 'without saying who are you' (நீர்யார் என்னாது) and 'considering order' (முறைகருதுபு). Honour goes by the order of deeds just listed (arrows taken, forts stormed, elephants captured), not by birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","padai","pugazh","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 725-746","lb":"PP6:725-746","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கொல்லேற்றுப்","ஏறு",732,"maa","scene","bull"],["முரசம்","முரசம்",733,"parai","scene","war drum"],["யானை","யானை",735,"maa","scene","elephant"],["தும்பை","தும்பை",737,"maram","ornament","thumbai flower"],["யானைக்","யானை",744,"maa","scene","elephant"],["சாந்தின்","சாந்து",745,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["தெரியற்","தெரியல்",745,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:747-763":{"book":"pattupattu","num":126,"t":["யாவரும் வருக ஏனோருந் தம்மென","வரையா வாயிற் செறாஅ திருந்து","பாணர் வருக பாட்டியர் வருக","யாணர்ப் புலவரொடு வயிரியர் வருகென","இருங்கிளை புரக்கும் இரவலர்க் கெல்லாம்","கொடுஞ்சி நெடுந்தேர் களிற்றொடும் வீசிக்","களந் தோறும் கள் ளரிப்ப","மரந் தோறு மை வீழ்ப்ப","நிண வூன்சுட் டுருக் கமைய","நெய் கனிந்து வறை யார்ப்பக்","குரூஉக் குய்ப்புகை மழை மங்குலிற்","பரந்து தோன்றா விய னகராற்","பல் சாலை முது குடுமியின்","நல் வேள்வித் துறை போகிய","தொல் லாணை நல் லாசிரியர்","புணர்கூட் டுண்ட புகழ்சால் சிறப்பின்","நிலந்தரு திருவி னெடியோன் போல"],"p":"","r":"'Let all come, and bring the others too,' you say, sitting without anger at a gate that is never barred, 'let the bards come, let the women singers come, let the new poets come with the dancers', and to all the suppliants who support large families you give tall chariots with kodinji and elephants; in every yard toddy is strained, at every tree post rams are felled, fat meat roasts and melts, ghee boils and frying sizzles, and the smoke of seasoning spreads like rain mist so the wide city cannot be seen; like the Tall One of land-giving fortune who, with the good teachers of ancient command trained in the sacrificial rites of Mudukudumi of many halls, ate the shared feast.","n":"The king is measured against the dynasty's past: the feast is like that of a Tall One (நெடியோன்) who ate with the teachers of Mudukudumi's sacrifices. Whether this நெடியோன் is an earlier Pandya or the god is not settled by the line. Line 761 (தொல்லாணை நல்லாசிரியர்) recurs in Pattinappalai 170, a shared formula for the Vedic teachers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","virundhu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 747-763","lb":"PP6:747-763","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["களிற்றொடும்","களிறு",752,"maa","gift_food","elephant"],["கள்","கள்",753,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["மை","மை",754,"maa","gift_food","ram"],["வூன்சுட்","ஊன்",755,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["நெய்","நெய்",756,"unavu","gift_food","ghee"],["மழை","மழை",757,"pira","simile","rain cloud"]],"en":[["king","முதுகுடுமியின்",759]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:6:764-782":{"book":"pattupattu","num":127,"t":["வியப்புஞ் சால்புஞ் செம்மை சான்றோர்","பலர்வாய்ப் புகரறு சிறப்பிற் றோன்றி","அரிய தந்து குடி யகற்றிப்","பெரிய கற் றிசை விளக்கி","முந்நீர் நாப்பண் ஞாயிறு போலவும்","பன்மீன் நடுவண் திங்கள் போலவும்","பூத்த சுற்றமொடு பொலிந்தினிது விளங்கிப்","பொய்யா நல்லிசை நிறுத்த புனைதார்ப்","பெரும்பெயர் மாறன் தலைவ னாகக்","கடந்தடு வாய்வாள் இளம்பல் கோசர்","இயனெறி மரபினின் வாய்மொழி கேட்பப்","பொலம்பூண் ஐவர் உட்படப் புகழ்ந்த","மறமிகு சிறப்பிற் குறுநில மன்ன","ரவரும் பிறகும் துவன்றிப்","பொற்புவிளங்கு புகழவை நிற்புகழ்ந் தேத்த","இலங்கிழை மகளிர் பொலங்கலத் தேந்திய","மணங்கமழ் தேறல் மடுப்ப நாளும்","மகிழ்ந்தினி துறைமதி பெரும்","வரைந்துநீ பெற்ற நல்லூ ழியையே."],"p":"","r":"Worthy in wonder, nobility and rectitude, flawless in the praise of many mouths, bringing rare things and widening your people, learning great things and spreading fame, shining sweetly with flourishing kin like the sun amid the sea and the moon amid many stars, while the young Kosar of victorious true swords, with Maran of great name and unfailing fame at their head, listen to your true word of ordered custom, and the chieftains of great valour, the five of golden ornaments among them, crowd in and praise you in your assembly of shining fame, and bright-jewelled women pour fragrant wine from golden vessels: rejoice and live sweetly every day, great one, in the good span of time you have been given within its bound.","n":"The poem's last word sets the whole description in its kaanchi frame: the good time is one 'you got within a bound' (வரைந்து நீ பெற்ற, line 782), so the closing 'rejoice every day' is counsel given in view of its end, not only a blessing. The formula 'with X at the head' (மாறன் தலைவனாக, line 772) also opens the list of praising chiefs, as in Purananuru 72.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","inbam","vendhan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Maduraikkanchi 764-782","lb":"PP6:764-782","poet":"மாங்குடி மருதனார்","pat":"தலையாலங்கானத்துச் செருவென்ற பாண்டியன்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"காஞ்சி","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["புனைதார்ப்","தார்",771,"maram","ornament","garland"],["முந்நீர்","முந்நீர்",768,"pira","simile","sea"],["ஞாயிறு","ஞாயிறு",768,"pira","simile","sun"],["பன்மீன்","மீன்",769,"pira","simile","stars"],["திங்கள்","திங்கள்",769,"pira","simile","moon"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",780,"unavu","gift_food","wine"]],"en":[["king","மாறன்",772]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:8"},"pp:7:1-20":{"book":"pattupattu","num":128,"t":["வையகம் பனிப்ப, வலனேர்பு வளைஇப்","பொய்யா வானம் புதுப்பெயல் பொழிந்தென","ஆர்கலி முனைஇய கொடுங்கோற் கோவலர்","ஏறுடை இனநிரை வேறுபுலம் பரப்பிப்","புலம்பெயர் புலம்பொடு கலங்கிக் கோடல்","நீடிதழ்க் கண்ணி நீரலைக் கலாவ","மெய்க்கொள் பெரும்பனி நலியப் பலருடன்","கைக்கொள் கொள்ளியர் கவுள்புடையூஉ நடுங்க","மாமேயல் மறப்ப மந்தி கூரப்","பறவை படிவன வீழக் கறவை","கன்றுகோ ளழியக் கடிய வீசிக்","குன்றுகுளிர்ப் பன்ன கூதிர்ப் பானாள்","புன்கொடி முசுண்டைப் பொறிப்புற வான்பூப்","பொன்போற் பீரமொடு புதற்புதல் மலரப்","பைங்காற் கொக்கின் மென்பறைத் தொழுதி","இருங்களி பரந்த ஈர வெண்மணற்","செவ்வரி நாரைய டெவ்வாயுங் கவரக்","கயலறல் எதிரக் கடும்புனற் சாஅய்ப்","பெயலுலந் தெழுந்த பொங்கல் வெண்மழை","அகலிரு விசும்பில் துவலை கற்ப"],"p":"","r":"When the unfailing clouds wheel to the right and pour new rain till the earth shivers, cowherds with crooked staffs drive their herds and bulls to other ground and, their kodal garlands sodden, shiver with firebrands held to their cheeks; beasts forget to graze, monkeys huddle, birds drop from their perches and cows kick away their calves; in the cold-season midnight that would chill a hill, musundai and gold-like peerkkai bloom on every bush, herons and storks pick fish on the wet white sand, and the emptied white clouds drift in drizzle across the sky.","n":"The cold is shown entirely through what it does to bodies, the herders' chattering jaws, the monkeys' huddle, the birds that fall: a reader of the whole idyll will later meet a queen who shivers for another reason, but this unit names no lover at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothir","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nedunalvadai 1-20","lb":"PP7:1-20","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"kurinji","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"கூதிர்ப்பாசறை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["கூதிர்ப் பாசறை"],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",3,"seythi","scene","cowherds"],["ஏறுடை","ஏறு",4,"maa","scene","bull"],["கோடல்","கோடல்",5,"maram","ornament","glory lily"],["மாமேயல்","மா",9,"maa","scene","beast"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",9,"maa","scene",""],["பறவை","பறவை",10,"pul","scene",""],["கறவை","கறவை",10,"maa","scene","milch cow"],["முசுண்டைப்","முசுண்டை",13,"maram","scene",""],["பீரமொடு","பீரம்",14,"maram","simile","ridge gourd"],["கொக்கின்","கொக்கு",15,"pul","scene",""],["நாரைய","நாரை",17,"pul","scene",""],["கயலறல்","கயல்",18,"pira","scene","carp"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:9"},"pp:7:21-35":{"book":"pattupattu","num":129,"t":["அங்கண் அகல்வயல் ஆர்பெயற் கலித்த","வண்தோட்டு நெல்லின் வருகதிர் வணங்க","முழுமுதற் கமுகின் மணியுறழ் எருத்திற்","கொழுமடல் அவிழ்ந்த குரூஉக்கொள் பெருங்குலை","நுண்ணீர் தெவிள வீங்கிப் புடைதிரண்டு","தெண்ணீர்ப் பசுங்காய் சேறுகொள முற்ற","நளிகொள் சிமைய விரவுமலர் வியன்காக்","குளிர்கொள் சினைய குரூஉத்துளி தூங்க","மாட மோங்கிய மல்லன் மூதூர்","ஆறுகிடந் தன்ன அகனெடுந் தெருவிற்","படலைக் கண்ணிப் பரேரெறுழ்த் திணிதோள்","முடலை யாக்கை முழுவலி மாக்கள்","வண்டுமூசு தேறல் மாந்தி மகிழ்சிறந்து","துவலைத் தண்துளி பேணார் பகலிறந்து","இருகோட்ட டறுவையர் வேண்டுவயின் திரிதர"],"p":"","r":"In the wide fields the paddy, grown thick in the rains, bows its coming ears; the clusters of the tall areca swell and their green nuts firm; the cold branches of the dense flowering groves drip; and in the rich old town of high mansions, along streets as broad as a river lying there, strong men in leaf garlands, drunk on bee-swarmed toddy, ignore the drizzle and wander past midday in cloth hanging on both sides as they please.","n":"The men who ignore the rain are the only people in the season's prelude who enjoy it, and they enjoy it in company and drink; the contrast with the shut chambers to come is the poet's arrangement, not stated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koothir","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nedunalvadai 21-35","lb":"PP7:21-35","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"கூதிர்ப்பாசறை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கூதிர்ப் பாசறை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["நெல்லின்","நெல்",22,"maram","scene","paddy"],["கமுகின்","கமுகு",23,"maram","scene","areca"],["படலைக்","படலை",31,"maram","ornament","leaf garland"],["வண்டுமூசு","வண்டு",33,"pira","scene","bee"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",33,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:9"},"pp:7:36-48":{"book":"pattupattu","num":130,"t":["வெள்ளி வள்ளி வீங்கிறைப் பணைத்தோள்","மெத்தென் சாயல் முத்துறழ் முறுவல்","பூங்குழைக் கமர்ந்த ஏந்தெழில் மழைக்கண்","மடவரல் மகளிர் பிடகைப் பெய்த","செவ்வி யரும்பின் பைங்காற் பித்திகத்து","அவ்வித ழவிழ்பதங் கமழப் பொழுதறிந்து","இரும்புசெய் விளக்கின் ஈர்ந்திரிக் கொளீஇ","நெல்லு மலருந் தூஉய்க் கைதொழுது","மல்லல் ஆவணம் மாலை யயர","மனையுறை புறவின் செங்காற் சேவல்","இன்புறு பெடையொடு மன்றுதேர்ந் துண்ணாது","இரவும் பகலும் மயங்கிக் கையற்று","மதலைப் பள்ளி மாறுவன இருப்பக்"],"p":"","r":"At evening young women with silver bangles on bamboo-smooth arms, soft gait, pearl smiles and cool eyes reaching to their flower earrings, knowing the hour by the scent of jasmine buds opening in their baskets, light the wet wicks of iron lamps, scatter paddy and flowers, join their hands and keep the evening rite in the rich market; while the red-legged male dove that lives in the house will not go out with his loving hen to feed in the common ground, but, unable to tell night from day, sits helpless on the rafter perch, shifting.","n":"The dove that stays on the rafter with his mate in the cold is the first pair in the poem kept together by the season; it passes iraicchi's conditions 1 and 2 (one pair, an act) but the unit itself states no love situation for its sense to bear on, so it is left as scene.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","koothir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nedunalvadai 36-48","lb":"PP7:36-48","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"mullai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"கூதிர்ப்பாசறை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கூதிர்ப் பாசறை"],"mp":[],"k":[["பித்திகத்து","பித்திகம்",40,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["நெல்லு","நெல்",43,"unavu","gift_food","paddy"],["மலருந்","மலர்",43,"maram","gift_food","flowers"],["புறவின்","புறா",45,"pul","scene","dove"],["பெடையொடு","பெடை",46,"pul","scene","hen dove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:9"},"pp:7:49-72":{"book":"pattupattu","num":131,"t":["கடியுடை வியனகர்ச் சிறுகுறுந் தொழுவர்","கொள்ளுறழ் நறுங்கல் பலகூட்டு மறுக","வடவர் தந்த வான்கேழ் வட்டம்","தென்புல மருங்கிற் சாந்தொடு துறப்பக்","கூந்தல் மகளிர் கோதை புனையார்","பல்லிருங் கூந்தல் சின்மலர் பெய்ம்மார்","தண்ணறுந் தகர முளரி நெருப்பமைத்து","இருங்காழ் அகிலொடு வெள்ளயிர் புகைப்பக்","கைவல் கம்மியன் கவின்பெறப் புனைந்த","செங்கேழ் வட்டஞ் சுருக்கிக் கொடுந்தறிச்","சிலம்பி வானூல் வலந்தன தூங்க","வானுற நிவந்த மேனிலை மருங்கின்","வேனிற் பள்ளித் தென்வளி தரூஉம்","நேர்வாய்க் கட்டளை திரியாது திண்ணிலைப்","போர்வாய்க் கதவம் தாழொடு துறப்பக்","கல்லென் துவலை தூவலின் யாவரும்","தொகுவாய்க் கன்னல் தண்ணீர் உண்ணார்","பகுவாய்த் தடவில் செந்நெருப் பார","ஆடல் மகளிர் பாடல்கொளப் புணர்மார்","தண்மையிற் றிரிந்த இன்குரல் தீந்தொடை","கொம்மை வருமுலை வெம்மையிற் றடைஇக்","கருங்கோட்டுச் சீறியாழ் பண்ணுமுறை நிறுப்பக்","காதலர்ப் பிரிந்தோர் புலம்பப் பெயல்கனைந்து","கூதிர்நின் றன்றாற் போதே மாதிரம்"],"p":"","r":"In the guarded mansion the servants grind fragrant stone for many blends, the round stone the northerners brought lies unused with the southern sandal, the women wear no garlands and put only a few flowers in their dense hair, dried over fires of tagaram stems with dark aloe and white resin; spider webs hang on the folded fans; the summer chambers of the high upper storey that let in the south wind are shut with bolted doors; for the spray, no one drinks cold water from the jars but all sit by the braziers; the dancing women warm against their breasts the lute strings gone slack in the cold before they tune the small lute; and so, while the rains thicken and those parted from their lovers lament, the cold season has set in.","n":"Twenty-two lines of a household's defences against the cold (fires, shut doors, no cold water, warmed strings) end in a single clause naming the people the season hurts, the parted lovers; the line's formula is shared with Akananuru 264, where பொழுதே stands for this போதே. The sense of கொள்ளுறழ் நறுங்கல் in line 50 is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["koothir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nedunalvadai 49-72","lb":"PP7:49-72","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"mullai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"கூதிர்ப்பாசறை","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["கூதிர்ப் பாசறை"],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["கொள்ளுறழ்","கொள்",50,"unavu","simile","horse gram"],["சாந்தொடு","சாந்து",52,"maram","absent","sandal"],["கோதை","கோதை",53,"maram","absent","garland"],["சின்மலர்","மலர்",54,"maram","ornament",""],["தகர","தகரம்",55,"maram","scene",""],["அகிலொடு","அகில்",56,"maram","scene","aloe wood"],["வெள்ளயிர்","வெள் அயிர்",56,"pira","scene","resin"],["சிலம்பி","சிலம்பி",59,"pira","scene","spider"],["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",70,"yaazh","scene","small lute"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:9"},"pp:7:73-100":{"book":"pattupattu","num":132,"t":["விரிகதிர் பரப்பிய வியல்வாய் மண்டிலம்","இருகோற் குறிநிலை வழுக்காது குடக்கேர்","பொருதிறஞ் சாரா அரைநாள் அமயத்து","நூலறி புலவர் நுண்ணிதிற் கயிறிட்டுத்","தேஎங் கொண்டு தெய்வம் நோக்கிப்","பெரும்பெயர் மன்னர்க் கொப்ப மனைவகுத்து","ஒருங்குடன் வளைஇ ஓங்குநிலை வரைப்பிற்","பருவிரும்பு பிணித்துச் செல்வரக் குரீஇத்","துணைமாண் கதவம் பொருத்தி இணைமாண்டு","நாளொடு பெயரிய கோளமை விழுமரத்துப்","போதவிழ் குவளைப் புதுப்பிடி காலமைத்துத்","தாளொடு குயின்ற போரமை புணர்ப்பிற்","கைவல் கம்மியன் முடுக்கலிற் புரைதீர்ந்து","ஐயவி யப்பிய நெய்யணி நெடுநிலை","வென்றெழு கொடியோடு வேழஞ் சென்றுபுகக்","குன்றுகுயின் றன்ன ஓங்குநிலை வாயில்","திருநிலை பெற்ற தீதுதீர் சிறப்பின்","தருமணல் ஞெமிரிய திருநகர் முற்றத்து","நெடுமயி ரெகினத் தூநிற ஏற்றை","குறுங்கால் அன்னமோ டுகளு முன்கடைப்","பணைநிலை முனைஇய பல்லுளைப் புரவி","புல்லுணாத் தெவிட்டும் புலம்புவிடு குரலொடு","நிலவுப்பயன் கொள்ளும் நெடுவெண் முற்றத்துக்","கிம்புரிப் பகுவாய் அம்பண நிறையக்","கலுழ்ந்துவீழ் அருவிப் பாடிறந் தயல","ஒலிநெடும் பீலி ஒல்க மெல்லியல்","கலிமயில் அகவும் வயிர்மருள் இன்னிசை","நளிமலைச் சிலம்பிற் சிலம்புங் கோயில்"],"p":"","r":"At midday, when the spreading sun throws no slant from the two marker poles, builders who know the treatise stretch their cord, take the directions, worship the gods and lay out a house fit for great kings; inside its high encircling wall stands a gateway tall as a cut hill, with paired doors bound in iron, posts of a fine tree named for a star, a lintel carved with a newly opened water-lily, smeared with white mustard and ghee, high enough for an elephant with its victory flag to pass; in the courtyard of spread sand male swans with long feathers play with short-legged hens, maned horses tired of the stall chew their grass with lonely cries, rain-water pours from the gaping makara spouts, and peacocks call like horns from a palace that rings like a mountain slope.","n":"The courtyard's swans play in pairs and its horse cries alone (புலம்புவிடு குரல்), so even the description of masonry carries the poem's two states, together and parted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nedunalvadai 73-100","lb":"PP7:73-100","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"கூதிர்ப்பாசறை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கூதிர்ப் பாசறை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["விழுமரத்துப்","மரம்",82,"maram","scene","timber"],["குவளைப்","குவளை",83,"maram","scene","water lily, carved"],["ஐயவி","ஐயவி",86,"unavu","scene","white mustard"],["வேழஞ்","வேழம்",87,"maa","scene","elephant"],["ரெகினத்","எகினம்",91,"pul","scene","swan"],["அன்னமோ","அன்னம்",92,"pul","scene","swan"],["புரவி","புரவி",93,"maa","scene","horse"],["புல்லுணாத்","புல்",94,"unavu","gift_food","grass"],["கலிமயில்","மயில்",99,"pul","scene","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:9"},"pp:7:101-114":{"book":"pattupattu","num":133,"t":["யவனர் இயற்றிய வினைமாண் பாவை","கையேந் தையகல் நிறையநெய் சொரிந்து","பரூஉத்திரி கொளீஇய குரூஉத்தலை நிமிரெரி","அறுஅறு காலைதோ றமைவரப் பண்ணிப்","பல்வேறு பள்ளிதொறும் பாயிருள் நீங்கப்","பீடுகெழு சிறப்பிற் பெருந்தகை யல்லது","ஆடவர் குறுகா அருங்கடி வரைப்பின்","வரைகண் டன்ன தோன்றல வரைசேர்பு","வில்கிடந் தன்ன கொடிய பல்வயின்","வெள்ளி யன்ன விளங்குஞ் கதையுரீஇ","மணிகண் டன்ன மாத்திரள் திண்காழ்ச்","செம்பியன் றன்ன செய்வுறு நெடுஞ்சுவர்","உருவப் பல்பூ ஒருகொடி வளைஇக்","கருவொடு பெயரிய காண்பி னல்லில்"],"p":"","r":"In the palace, lamp-figures made by Yavanas hold bowls brimming with ghee whose thick wicks raise upright flames, trimmed whenever they burn low, so that the spreading dark leaves every chamber; within the hard-guarded precinct that no man but the great lord may approach stand rooms like hills, creepers like bows, walls plastered bright as silver, pillars dark and thick as gems, walls red as wrought copper painted round with a single creeper of many flowers, and among them the fine chamber named for the womb.","n":"That only the great lord (பெருந்தகை) may enter the precinct makes the chamber's emptiness the king's absence; the unit is architecture, but it locks the door that the poem will not open again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","arasu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nedunalvadai 101-114","lb":"PP7:101-114","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"கூதிர்ப்பாசறை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கூதிர்ப் பாசறை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["நிறையநெய்","நெய்",102,"unavu","scene","ghee"],["பல்பூ","பூ",113,"maram","scene","painted flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:9"},"pp:7:115-135":{"book":"pattupattu","num":134,"t":["தசநான் கெய்திய பணைமருள் நோன்றாள்","இகன்மீக் கூறும் ஏந்தெழில் வரிநுதல்","பொருதொழி நாக மொழியெயி றருகெறிந்து","சீருஞ் செம்மையும் ஒப்ப வல்லோன்","கூருளிக் குயின்ற ஈரிலை யிடையிடுபு","தூங்கியல் மகளிர் வீங்குமுலை கடுப்பப்","புடைதிரண் டிருந்த குடத்த இடைதிரண்டு","உள்ளி நோன்முதல் பொருத்தி அடியமைத்துப்","பேரள வெய்திய பெரும்பெயர்ப் பாண்டில்","மடைமாண் நுண்ணிழை பொலியத் தொடைமாண்டு","முத்துடைச் சாலேகம் நாற்றிக் குத்துறுத்துப்","புலிப்பொறிக் கொண்ட பூங்கேழ்த் தட்டத்துத்","தகடுகண் புதையக் கொளீஇத் துகள்தீர்ந்து","ஊட்டுறு பன்மயிர் விரைஇ வயமான்","வேட்டம் பொறித்து வியன்கட் கானத்து","முல்லைப் பல்போ துறழப் பூநிரைத்து","மெல்லிதின் விரிந்த சேக்கை மேம்படத்","துணைபுணர் அன்னத் தூநிறத் தூவி","இணையணை மேம்படப் பாயணை யிட்டுக்","காடி கொண்ட கழுவுறு கலிங்கத்துத்","தோடமை தூமடி விரித்த சேக்கை"],"p":"","r":"In that chamber stands a full-sized couch on strong legs, swelling like drums, cut from the tusks of a forty-year elephant fallen in battle and shaped with a sharp chisel by a master, the legs rounded like the breasts of women heavy with child and narrowing at the middle, set on garlic-shaped feet; it is strung with fine work and hung with a lattice of pearls, its board covered with a tiger-spotted spread whose dyed cushions picture a hunt of strong beasts in a wide forest among flowers set like mullai buds; on it a soft bed, paired pillows filled with the white down of swans that had mated, and a washed, starched white sheet over all.","n":"The down is from swans that had mated (துணைபுணர்) and the pillows are a pair (இணையணை): the bed is made for two in every detail before the next unit lays one woman on it. Lines 115 to 117, on the elephant's age and how its tusk was taken, are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nedunalvadai 115-135","lb":"PP7:115-135","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"mullai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"கூதிர்ப்பாசறை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":["கூதிர்ப் பாசறை"],"mp":[],"k":[["நாக","நாகம்",117,"maa","scene","elephant"],["உள்ளி","உள்ளி",122,"unavu","simile","garlic"],["புலிப்பொறிக்","புலி",126,"maa","simile","tiger"],["வயமான்","வயமா",128,"maa","scene","lion"],["முல்லைப்","முல்லை",130,"maram","simile","jasmine"],["அன்னத்","அன்னம்",132,"pul","scene","swan"],["காடி","காடி",134,"unavu","scene","rice starch"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:9"},"pp:7:136-156":{"book":"pattupattu","num":135,"t":["ஆரந் தாங்கிய அலர்முலை யாகத்துப்","பின்னமை நெடுவீழ் தாழத் துணைதுறந்து","நன்னுதல் உலறிய சின்மெல் லோதி","நெடுநீர் வார்குழை களைந்தெனக் குறுங்கண்","வாயுறை யழுத்திய வறிதுவீழ் காதிற்","பொலந்தொடி தின்ற மயிர்வார் முன்கை","வலம்புரி வளையொடு கடிகைநூல் யாத்து","வாளைப் பகுவாய் கடுப்ப வணக்குறுத்துச்","செவ்விரற் கொளீஇய செங்கேழ் விளக்கத்துப்","பூந்துகில் மரீஇய ஏந்துகோட் டல்குல்","அம்மா சூர்ந்த அவிர்நூற் கலிங்கமொடு","புனையா ஓவியங் கடுப்பப் புனைவில்","தளிரேர் மேனித் தாய சுணங்கின்","அம்பணைத் தடைஇய மென்றோள் முகிழ்முலை","வம்புவிசித் தியாத்த வாங்குசாய் நுசுப்பின்","மெல்லியல் மகளிர் நல்லடி வருட","நரைவிரா வுற்ற நறுமென் கூந்தல்","செம்முகச் செவிலியர் கைம்மிகக் குழீஇக்","குறியவும் நெடியவும் உரைபல பயிற்றி","இன்னே வருகுவர் இன்துணை யோரென","உகத்தவை மொழியவும் ஒல்லாள் மிகக்கலுழ்ந்து"],"p":"","r":"On it lies the queen: on her breast, where the necklace lay, only a long plait hangs; her few soft locks lie dry over her fair brow; her ears, the long earrings removed, keep only small rings; her wrists, bared of gold bangles, carry only the right-whorled conch bangle and a protective thread, her finger a red ring bent like a vaalai fish's mouth, and her waist a soiled cloth, so that she looks like a picture left uncoloured; soft-limbed maids rub her feet, and grey-haired, red-faced foster mothers crowd round her, telling tales short and long and saying her dear one will come at once, all to please her; but she will not take comfort and weeps.","n":"Each ornament is named with the token kept in its place (the plait for the necklace, the small ring for the earring, the conch bangle and thread for the gold), so a wife's karpu is read off what she has given up. The nurses' இன்னே வருகுவர் is the same promise Mullaippattu's old women hear in an omen; here it is only talk, and it fails. The colophon gives no speaker; the nurses' words are the unit's own speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","karpu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nedunalvadai 136-156","lb":"PP7:136-156","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"கூதிர்ப்பாசறை","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[["sevili","thalaivi",null,"karpu","கூதிர்ப்பாசறை"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஆரந்","ஆரம்",136,"pira","absent","necklace"],["வலம்புரி","வலம்புரி",142,"pira","ornament","conch"],["வாளைப்","வாளை",143,"pira","simile","vaalai fish"],["தளிரேர்","தளிர்",148,"maram","simile","young leaf"],["அம்பணைத்","பணை",149,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:9"},"pp:7:157-166":{"book":"pattupattu","num":136,"t":["நுண்சேறு வழித்த நோனிலைத் திரள்கால்","ஊறா வறுமுலை கொளீஇய காறிருத்திப்","புதுவ தியன்ற மெழுகுசெய் படமிசைத்","திண்ணிலை மருப்பின் ஆடுதலை யாக","விண்ணூர்பு திரிதரும் வீங்குசெலல் மண்டிலத்து","முரண்மிகு சிறப்பிற் செல்வனொடு நிலைஇய","உரோகிணி நினைவனள் நோக்கி நெடிதுயிரா","மாயிதழ் ஏந்திய மலிந்துவீழ் அரிப்பனி","செவ்விரல் கடைக்கண் சேர்த்திச் சிலதெறியாப்","புலம்பொடு வதியு நலங்கிளர் அரிவைக்கு"],"p":"","r":"She looks up at the canopy newly painted in wax on cloth over strong round posts, where the ram heads the circle of signs travelling the sky and the moon, the glorious lord, is shown with Rohini who never leaves him; thinking of Rohini she sighs long, gathers with her red finger-tips the tears that fall from the corners of her dark-lidded eyes and flicks a few away, and lies alone with her grief, this woman of shining beauty.","n":"The poem says only that she thinks of Rohini and sighs; the contrast with the star that stays with her lord is left for the reader. It is not tagged ullurai or iraicchi, since the vehicle is a painted star and not a native thing of the land (porul 1.50). Lines 157 and 158, on how the canopy posts are made, are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nedunalvadai 157-166","lb":"PP7:157-166","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"mullai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"கூதிர்ப்பாசறை","thd":1,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["ஆடுதலை","ஆடு",160,"maa","scene","ram, Aries"],["உரோகிணி","உரோகிணி",163,"deyvam","scene","the star Rohini"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:9"},"pp:7:167-188":{"book":"pattupattu","num":137,"t":["இன்னா அரும்படர் தீர விறறந்து","இன்னே முடிகதில் அம்ம மின்னவிர்","ஓடையொடு பொலிந்த வினைநவில் யானை","நீள்திரள் தடக்கை நிலமிசைப் புரளக்","களிறுகளம் படுத்த பெருஞ்செய் யாடவர்","ஒளிறுவாள் விழுப்புண் காணிய புறம்போந்து","வடந்தைத் தண்வளி எறிதொறும் நுடங்கித்","தெற்கேர் பிறைஞ்சிய தலைய நற்பல்","பாண்டில் விளக்கிற் பரூஉச்சுட ரழல","வேம்புதலை யாத்த நோன்காழ் எஃகமொடு","முன்னோன் முறைமுறை காட்டப் பின்னர்","மணிபுறத் திட்ட மாத்தாட் பிடியொடு","பருமங் களையாப் பாய்பரிக் கலிமா","இருஞ்சேற்றுத் தெருவின் எறிதுளி விதிர்ப்பப்","புடைவீழ் அந்துகில் இடவயின் தழீஇ","வாள்தோள் கோத்த வன்கட் காளை","சுவல்மிசை யமைத்த கையன் முகனமர்ந்து","நூல்கால் யாத்த மாலை வெண்குடை","தவ்வென் றசைஇத் தாதுளி மறைப்ப","நள்ளென் யாமத்தும் பள்ளி கொள்ளான்","சிலரொடு திரிதரும் வேந்தன்","பலரொடு முரணிய பாசறைத் தொழிலே"],"p":"","r":"May the war end now with victory, the poet prays, so that this bright woman's hard grief may end; for at the deep midnight the king, not taking to his bed, walks with a few men about the camp that is at war with many, going out to see the bright sword wounds of the warriors who brought down elephants whose long trunks now roll on the ground; the flames of the many lamps lean south as the cold north wind strikes them, a leader shows the wounded one by one with a strong neem-tied spear, harnessed horses shake off the rain in the muddy lane, and with his left hand holding his slipping cloth and his other arm on the shoulder of a sword-bearing youth, he looks at each face kindly while a pearl-strung white umbrella keeps off the falling drops.","n":"The whole idyll is one sentence that ends on பாசறைத் தொழிலே, and the king is never named: the neem on the spear is the only sign that he is the Pandya, and the poet's wish in lines 167 and 168 is the only bridge between the queen's chamber and the camp, which never meet in the poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paasarai","vendhan","maram","pirivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Nedunalvadai 167-188","lb":"PP7:167-188","poet":"மதுரைக் கணக்காயனார் மகனார் நக்கீரர்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"vaakai","cth":"வாகை","ctu":"கூதிர்ப்பாசறை","thd":0,"uri":"irutthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["கூதிர்ப் பாசறை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",169,"maa","scene","elephant"],["வேம்புதலை","வேம்பு",176,"maram","ornament","neem"],["பிடியொடு","பிடி",178,"maa","scene","cow elephant"],["கலிமா","மா",179,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:9"},"pp:8:1-22":{"book":"pattupattu","num":138,"t":["அன்னாய் வாழிவேண் டன்னை யண்ணுத","லொலிமென் கூந்தலென் றோழி மேனி","விறலிழை நெகிழ்த்த வீவருங் கடுநோ","யகலு ளாங்க ணறியுநர் வினாயும்","பரவியுந் தொழுதும் விரவுமலர் தூயும்","வேறுபல் லுருவிற் கடவுட் பேணி","நறையும் விரயு மோச்சியு மலவுற்","றெய்யா மையலை நீயும் வருந்துதி","நற்கவின் தொலையவு நறுந்தோ ணெகிழவும்","புட்பிற ரறியவும் புலம்புவந் தலைப்பவு","முட்கரந் துறையு முய்யா வரும்படர்","செப்பல் வன்மையிற் செறித்தியான் கடவலின்","முத்தினு மணியினும் பொன்னினு மத்துணை","நேர்வருங் குரைய கலங்கெடிற் புணருஞ்","சால்பும் வியப்பு மியல்புங் குன்றின்","மாசறக் கழீஇ வயங்குபுகழ் நிறுத்த","லாசறு காட்சி யையர்க்கு மந்நிலை","யெளிய வென்னார் தொன்மருங் கறிஞர்","மாதரு மடனு மோராங்குத் தணப்ப","நெடுந்தே ரெந்தை யருங்கடி நீவி","யிருவே மாய்ந்த மன்ற லிதுவென","நாமறி வுறாலிற் பழியு முண்டோ"],"p":"","r":"The friend tells the mother that the girl's wasting sickness, which has loosened her bright ornaments and which the mother has tried to cure by asking diviners, praising, worshipping, strewing flowers and offering incense to gods of many forms, is a grief the girl hid until the friend forced her to speak; and since jewels of pearl, gem and gold can be mended when broken but lost worth and fame cannot be washed clean even by sages, she asks whether it is wrong to let the mother know that, passing their father's strict guard, the two chose this marriage themselves.","n":"The friend's argument for speaking rests on irreparability: a broken ornament can be rejoined, but lost good name cannot be washed back even by the wisest, so silence now would cost what cannot be repaired. The mother's rites are not mocked, only shown to miss the cause. இருவேம் in line 21 most naturally means the lovers; it could also take in the friend, who calls the act hers too in line 32.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arathodunirral","kaamanoy","vazhipaadu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 1-22","lb":"PP8:1-22","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu","அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["கடவுட்","கடவுள்",6,"deyvam","scene","gods"],["விரவுமலர்","விரவு மலர்",5,"maram","scene","mixed flowers"],["நறையும்","நறை",7,"pira","scene","incense"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:23-34":{"book":"pattupattu","num":139,"t":["வாற்றின் வாரா ராயினு மாற்ற","வேனையுல கத்து மியைவதா னமக்கென","மானமர் நோக்கங் கலக்கிக் கையற்","றானாச் சிறுமைய ளிவளுந் தேம்பு","மிகன்மீக் கடவு மிருபெரு வேந்தர்","வினையிடை நின்ற சான்றோர் போல","விருபே ரச்சமோ டியானு மாற்றலேன்","கொடுப்பினன் குடைமையுங் குடிநிர லுடைமையும்","வண்ணமுந் துணையும் பொரீஇ யெண்ணா","தெமியேந் துணிந்த வேமஞ்சா லருவினை","நிகழ்ந்த வண்ண நீநனி யுணரச்","செப்ப லான்றிசிற் சினவா தீமோ"],"p":"","r":"Saying that even if the union does not come by the proper path it will be theirs in the other world, the girl, her deer-like gaze troubled, wilts helpless; the friend, holding two great fears like the worthy who stand between two great kings driven to war, cannot bear it either, and asks the mother to hear without anger how the protecting deed the two of them dared alone, without weighing lineage, rank, looks or support, came about.","n":"The simile of the go-between standing between two kings bent on war brings puram diplomacy into akam: the friend's two fears are that the father will be shamed and that the girl will die, and like an envoy she can serve one side only by speaking to the other. Lines 30 to 32 admit the act was undertaken without the weighing of family and fortune that a marriage arranged by kin would have made.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arathodunirral","acham","kudi","varaivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 23-34","lb":"PP8:23-34","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu","அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மானமர்","மான்",25,"maa","simile","deer"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:35-53":{"book":"pattupattu","num":140,"t":["நெற்கொ ணெடுவெதிர்க் கணந்த யானை","முத்தார் மருப்பி னிறங்குகை கடுப்பத்","துய்த்தலை வாங்கிய புனிறுதீர் பெருங்குர","னற்கோட் சிறுதினைப் படுபு ளோப்பி","யெற்பட வருதிய ரென நீ விடுத்தலிற்","கலிகெழு மரமிசைச் சேணோ னிழைத்த","புலியஞ் சிதண மேறி யவண","சாரற் சூரற் றகைபெற வலத்த","தழலுந் தட்டையுங் குளிரும் பிறவுங்","கிளிகடி மரபின வூழூழ் வாங்கி","யுரவுக்கதிர் தெறூஉ முருப்பவி ரமயத்து","விசும்பாடு பறவை வீழ்பதிப் படர","நிறையிரும் பௌவங் குறைபட முகந்துகொண்","டகலிரு வானத்து வீசுவளி கலாவலின்","முரசதிர்ந் தன்ன வின்குர லேற்றொடு","நிரைசெல னிவப்பிற் கொண்மூ மயங்கி","யின்னிசை முரசிற் சுடர்ப்பூட் சேஎ","யன்னார்க் கேந்திய விலங்கிலை யெஃகின்","மின்மயங்கு கருவிய கன்மிசைப் பொழிந்தென"],"p":"","r":"The friend recounts the day: the mother sent them to drive birds from the small millet, whose great ripe heads bent like the drooping trunk of a pearl-tusked elephant that has fed on bamboo, and to come home at sunset; they climbed the tiger-safe platform the hill watchman had built in a tree, took up in turn the cane sling, clapper and rattle, and in the heat of the day, as birds made for their roosts, clouds drew up the sea, mingled with the wind, thundered like a drum and poured on the hill with lightning like the bright spear Murugan raises against his foes.","n":"The mother herself sent the girls to the field (நீ விடுத்தலின், line 39), so the friend's story makes the mother's own errand the occasion of the meeting. Murugan enters only as the vehicle of a simile for lightning, the same god the mother's rites in lines 5 and 6 had been addressing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ilamai","mazhai","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 35-53","lb":"PP8:35-53","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu","அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யானை","யானை",35,"maa","simile","elephant"],["ணெடுவெதிர்க்","வெதிர்",35,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["சிறுதினைப்","தினை",38,"unavu","scene","millet"],["படுபு","புள்",38,"pul","scene","birds"],["சேணோ","சேணோன்",40,"seythi","scene","watchman"],["புலியஞ்","புலி",41,"maa","scene","tiger"],["சூரற்","சூரல்",42,"maram","scene","cane"],["தட்டையுங்","தட்டை",43,"seythi","scene","clapper"],["கிளிகடி","கிளி",44,"pul","scene","parrot"],["பறவை","பறவை",46,"pul","scene",""],["பௌவங்","பௌவம்",47,"pira","scene","sea"],["முரசதிர்ந்","முரசு",49,"parai","simile","drum"],["கொண்மூ","கொண்மூ",50,"pira","scene","cloud"],["சேஎ","சேய்",51,"deyvam","simile","Murugan"],["மின்மயங்கு","மின்",53,"pira","simile","lightning"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:54-61":{"book":"pattupattu","num":141,"t":["வண்ண னெடுங்கோட் டிழிதரு தெண்ணீ","ரவிர்துகில் புரையு மவ்வெள் ளருவித்","தவிர்வில் வேட்கையேந் தண்டா தாடிப்","பளிங்குசொரி வன்ன பாய்சுனை குடைவுழி","நளிபடு சிலம்பிற் பாயம் பாடிப்","பொன்னெறி மணியிற் சிறுபுறந் தாழ்ந்தவெம்","பின்னிருங் கூந்தல் பிழிவனந் துவரி","யுள்ளகஞ் சிவந்த கண்ணேம் வள்ளித"],"p":"","r":"From the great peak the clear water came down in a white waterfall like shining cloth; with desire that would not stop they bathed in it, plunged into the spreading pool like poured crystal, sang on the echoing slope, wrung and dried their dark braided hair hanging down their backs, and their eyes reddened inside from the water.","n":"The reddened eyes (உள்ளகம் சிவந்த கண்ணேம்) are the effect of long play in the water, the first sign in the story of the girls' freedom on the hill before the man appears.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 54-61","lb":"PP8:54-61","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["வெள்ளருவித்","அருவி",55,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["சுனை","சுனை",57,"pira","simile","pool"],["பளிங்குசொரி","பளிங்கு",57,"pira","simile","crystal"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:62-82":{"book":"pattupattu","num":142,"t":["ழொண்செங் காந்த ளாம்ப லனிச்சந்","தண்கயக் குவளை குறிஞ்சி வெட்சி","செங்கொடு வேரி தேமா மணிச்சிகை","யுரிதுநா றவிழ்தொத் துந்தூழ் கூவிள","மெரிபுரை யெறுழஞ் சுள்ளி கூவிரம்","வடவனம் வாகை வான்பூங் குடச","மெருவை செருவிளை மணிப்பூங் கருவிளை","பயினி வானி பல்லிணர்க் குரவம்","பசும்பிடி வகுளம் பல்லிணர்க் காயா","விரிமல ராவிரை வேரல் சூரல்","குரீஇப் பூளை குறுநறுங் கண்ணி","குறுகிலை மருதம் விரிபூங் கோங்கம்","போங்கந் திலகந் தேங்கமழ் பாதிரி","செருந்தி யதிரல் பெருந்தண் சண்பகங்","கரந்தை குளவி கடிகமழ் கலிமாத்","தில்லை பாலை கல்லிவர் முல்லை","குல்லை பிடவஞ் சிறுமா ரோடம்","வாழை வள்ளி நீணறு நெய்த","றாழை தளவ முட்டாட் டாமரை","ஞாழன் மௌவ னறுந்தண் கொகுடி","சேடல் செம்மல் சிறுசெங் குரலி"],"p":"","r":"The friend begins the list of the flowers they gathered: bright red kaanthal with large petals, aambal, anichcham, kuvalai of the cool pond, kurinji, vetchi, red-stemmed veri, sweet mango, manichchigai, unthoozh, koovilam, fire-like erutham, sulli, kooviram, vadavanam, vaagai, kudasam, eruvai, seruvilai, karuvilai, payini, vaani, kuravam, pasumpidi, vakulam, kaayaa, aavirai, veral, sooral, poolai, kurunarungkanni, marutham, kongam, pongam, thilakam, paathiri, serunthi, athiral, sanbagam, karanthai, kulavi, mango, thillai, paalai, mullai, kullai, pidavam, sirumaaradam, vaazhai, valli, neythal, thaazhai, thalavam, thaamarai, gnaazhal, mauval, kogudi, sedal, semmal and kurali.","n":"The list (it runs to line 96) is the girls' play turned into an inventory of every landscape's flowers, sea-shore thaazhai and gnaazhal and field lotus and marutham beside hill kaanthal: the flowers say nothing about the lovers, but they show the girls free and at play just before the man comes. Many of the names cannot now be matched to a plant with certainty; the senses given are only for the common ones.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 62-82","lb":"PP8:62-82","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["காந்த","காந்தள்",62,"maram","ornament","glory lily"],["ளாம்ப","ஆம்பல்",62,"maram","ornament","water lily"],["லனிச்சந்","அனிச்சம்",62,"maram","ornament",""],["குவளை","குவளை",63,"maram","ornament","blue lily"],["குறிஞ்சி","குறிஞ்சி",63,"maram","ornament",""],["வெட்சி","வெட்சி",63,"maram","ornament",""],["செங்கொடு வேரி","செங்கொடுவேரி",64,"maram","ornament",""],["தேமா","தேமா",64,"maram","ornament","sweet mango"],["மணிச்சிகை","மணிச்சிகை",64,"maram","ornament",""],["துந்தூழ்","உந்தூழ்",65,"maram","ornament",""],["கூவிள","கூவிளம்",65,"maram","ornament","bael"],["யெறுழஞ்","எறுழம்",66,"maram","ornament",""],["சுள்ளி","சுள்ளி",66,"maram","ornament",""],["கூவிரம்","கூவிரம்",66,"maram","ornament",""],["வடவனம்","வடவனம்",67,"maram","ornament",""],["வாகை","வாகை",67,"maram","ornament",""],["குடச","குடசம்",67,"maram","ornament",""],["மெருவை","எருவை",68,"maram","ornament",""],["செருவிளை","செருவிளை",68,"maram","ornament",""],["கருவிளை","கருவிளை",68,"maram","ornament",""],["பயினி","பயினி",69,"maram","ornament",""],["வானி","வானி",69,"maram","ornament",""],["குரவம்","குரவம்",69,"maram","ornament",""],["பசும்பிடி","பசும்பிடி",70,"maram","ornament",""],["வகுளம்","வகுளம்",70,"maram","ornament",""],["காயா","காயா",70,"maram","ornament",""],["ராவிரை","ஆவிரை",71,"maram","ornament",""],["வேரல்","வேரல்",71,"maram","ornament","small bamboo"],["சூரல்","சூரல்",71,"maram","ornament","cane"],["பூளை","பூளை",72,"maram","ornament",""],["குறுநறுங் கண்ணி","குறுநறுங்கண்ணி",72,"maram","ornament",""],["மருதம்","மருதம்",73,"maram","ornament",""],["கோங்கம்","கோங்கம்",73,"maram","ornament",""],["போங்கந்","போங்கம்",74,"maram","ornament",""],["திலகந்","திலகம்",74,"maram","ornament",""],["பாதிரி","பாதிரி",74,"maram","ornament",""],["செருந்தி","செருந்தி",75,"maram","ornament",""],["யதிரல்","அதிரல்",75,"maram","ornament",""],["சண்பகங்","சண்பகம்",75,"maram","ornament",""],["கரந்தை","கரந்தை",76,"maram","ornament",""],["குளவி","குளவி",76,"maram","ornament",""],["கலிமாத்","மா",76,"maram","ornament","mango"],["தில்லை","தில்லை",77,"maram","ornament",""],["பாலை","பாலை",77,"maram","ornament","paalai tree"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",77,"maram","ornament","jasmine"],["குல்லை","குல்லை",78,"maram","ornament",""],["பிடவஞ்","பிடவம்",78,"maram","ornament",""],["சிறுமா ரோடம்","சிறுமாரோடம்",78,"maram","ornament",""],["வாழை","வாழை",79,"maram","ornament","plantain"],["வள்ளி","வள்ளி",79,"maram","ornament","yam creeper"],["நெய்த","நெய்தல்",79,"maram","ornament","white lily"],["றாழை","தாழை",80,"maram","ornament","screwpine"],["தளவ","தளவம்",80,"maram","ornament",""],["டாமரை","தாமரை",80,"maram","ornament","lotus"],["ஞாழன்","ஞாழல்",81,"maram","ornament",""],["மௌவ","மௌவல்",81,"maram","ornament",""],["கொகுடி","கொகுடி",81,"maram","ornament",""],["சேடல்","சேடல்",82,"maram","ornament",""],["செம்மல்","செம்மல்",82,"maram","ornament",""],["குரலி","குரலி",82,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:83-98":{"book":"pattupattu","num":143,"t":["கோடல் கைதை கொங்குமுதிர் நறுவழை","காஞ்சி மணிக்குலைக் கட்கமழ் நெய்தல்","பாங்கர் மராஅம் பல்பூந் தணக்க","மீங்கை யிலவந் தூங்கிணர்க் கொன்றை","யடும்பம ராத்தி நெடுங்கொடி யவரை","பகன்றை பலாசம் பல்பூம் பிண்டி","வஞ்சி பித்திகம் சிந் துவாரம்","தும்பை துழாஅய் சுடர்ப்பூந் தோன்றி","நந்தி நறவ நறும்புன் னாகம்","பாரம் பீரம் பைங்குருக் கத்தி","யாரங் காழ்வை கடியிரும் புன்னை","நரந்த நாக நள்ளிரு ணாறி","மாயிருங் குருந்தும் வேங்கையும் பிறவு","மரக்குவிரித் தன்ன பரேரம் புழகுடன்","மாலங் குடைய மலிவன மறுகி","வான்கண் கழீஇய வகலறைக் குவைஇப்"],"p":"","r":"The list goes on: kodal, kaithai, honey-rich vazhai, kaanji, neythal with clustered buds smelling of toddy, paangar, maraam, thanakkam, eengai, ilavam, hanging konrai, adumbu, aathi, long-stemmed avarai, pakanrai, palaasam, pindi, vanji, piththigam, sinthuvaaram, thumbai, thuzhaay, bright thonri, nanthi, naravam, punnaagam, paaram, peeram, kurukkaththi, aaram, kaazhvai, punnai, narantham, naagam, the flower that smells at midnight, kurunthu, vengai and others, with large puzhagu red as spread lac; roaming the rich forest they gathered them all and heaped them on the wide rock the rain had washed.","n":"The heap of flowers on the rain-washed rock (வான்கண் கழீஇய அகலறைக் குவைஇ) ends the list and gives it a place: the flowers of every land are gathered onto one hill rock, as the whole idyll gathers its world into one day's story. Several names in this half of the list (பாரம், பீரம், நந்தி, காழ்வை) cannot be identified with confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 83-98","lb":"PP8:83-98","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["கோடல்","கோடல்",83,"maram","ornament",""],["கைதை","கைதை",83,"maram","ornament","screwpine"],["நறுவழை","வழை",83,"maram","ornament",""],["காஞ்சி","காஞ்சி",84,"maram","ornament",""],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",84,"maram","ornament","white lily"],["பாங்கர்","பாங்கர்",85,"maram","ornament",""],["மராஅம்","மராஅம்",85,"maram","ornament",""],["தணக்க","தணக்கம்",85,"maram","ornament",""],["மீங்கை","ஈங்கை",86,"maram","ornament",""],["யிலவந்","இலவம்",86,"maram","ornament","silk-cotton"],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",86,"maram","ornament",""],["யடும்ப","அடும்பு",87,"maram","ornament",""],["ராத்தி","ஆத்தி",87,"maram","ornament",""],["யவரை","அவரை",87,"maram","ornament",""],["பகன்றை","பகன்றை",88,"maram","ornament",""],["பலாசம்","பலாசம்",88,"maram","ornament",""],["பிண்டி","பிண்டி",88,"maram","ornament","asoka"],["வஞ்சி","வஞ்சி",89,"maram","ornament",""],["பித்திகம்","பித்திகம்",89,"maram","ornament",""],["சிந் துவாரம்","சிந்துவாரம்",89,"maram","ornament",""],["தும்பை","தும்பை",90,"maram","ornament",""],["துழாஅய்","துழாய்",90,"maram","ornament","basil"],["தோன்றி","தோன்றி",90,"maram","ornament",""],["நந்தி","நந்தி",91,"maram","ornament",""],["நறவ","நறவம்",91,"maram","ornament",""],["புன் னாகம்","புன்னாகம்",91,"maram","ornament",""],["பாரம்","பாரம்",92,"maram","ornament",""],["பீரம்","பீரம்",92,"maram","ornament",""],["குருக் கத்தி","குருக்கத்தி",92,"maram","ornament",""],["யாரங்","ஆரம்",93,"maram","ornament","sandal"],["காழ்வை","காழ்வை",93,"maram","ornament",""],["புன்னை","புன்னை",93,"maram","ornament",""],["நரந்த","நரந்தம்",94,"maram","ornament",""],["நாக","நாகம்",94,"maram","ornament","naga tree"],["ணாறி","நள்ளிருள் நாறி",94,"maram","ornament",""],["குருந்தும்","குருந்து",95,"maram","ornament",""],["வேங்கையும்","வேங்கை",95,"maram","ornament","kino tree"],["புழகுடன்","புழகு",96,"maram","ornament",""],["மரக்கு","அரக்கு",96,"pira","simile","lac"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:99-106":{"book":"pattupattu","num":144,"t":["புள்ளா ரியத்த விலங்குமலைச் சிலம்பின்","வள்ளுயிர்த் தெள்விளி யிடையிடைப் பயிற்றிக்","கிள்ளை யோப்பியுங் கிளையிதழ் பறியாப்","பைவரி யல்குற் கொய்தழை தைஇப்","பல்வே றுருவின் வனப்பமை கோதையெம்","மெல்லிரு முச்சிக் கவின்பெறக் கட்டி","யெரியவி ருருவி னங்குழைச் செயலைத்","தாதுபடு தண்ணிழ லிருந்தன மாக"],"p":"","r":"On the slope of the cross-lying hill, where the birds' noise sounds like instruments, the girls now and then sent out clear strong-breathed calls to drive off the parrots, tied leaf skirts of plucked petals and leaves about their waists, bound garlands of many kinds on their soft dark hair, and sat in the cool shade of the asoka, whose tender shoots shine like fire and drop pollen.","n":"The leaf skirt (தழை) is the dress the hero of kurinji often brings as a love gift; here the girls make their own, so they are still unclaimed when he arrives in the next lines.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 99-106","lb":"PP8:99-106","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["புள்ளா","புள்",99,"pul","scene","birds"],["கிள்ளை","கிள்ளை",101,"pul","scene","parrot"],["கொய்தழை","தழை",102,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"],["கோதையெம்","கோதை",103,"maram","ornament","garland"],["செயலைத்","செயலை",105,"maram","scene","asoka"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:107-127":{"book":"pattupattu","num":145,"t":["வெண்ணெய் நீவிய சுரிவளர் நறுங்காழ்த்","தண்ணறுந் தகரங் கமழ மண்ணி","யீரம் புலர விரலுளர்ப் பவிழாக்","காழகி லம்புகை கொளீஇ யாழிசை","யணிமிகு வரிமிஞி றார்ப்பத் தேங்கலந்து","மணிநிறங் கொண்ட மாயிருங் குஞ்சியின்","மலையவு நிலத்தவுஞ் சினையவுஞ் சுனையவும்","வண்ண வண்ணத்த மலராய்பு விரைஇய","தண்ணறுத் தொடையல் வெண்போழ்க் கண்ணி","நலம்பெறு சென்னி நாமுற மிலைச்சிப்","பைங்காற் பித்தகத் தாயித ழலரி","யந்தொடை யருகாழ் வளைஇச் செந்தீ","யண்பூம் பிண்டி யருகாது செரீஇ","யந்தளிர்க் குவவுமொய்ம் பலைப்பச் சாந்தருந்தி","மைந்திறை கொண்ட மலர்ந்தேந் தகலத்துத்","தொன்றுபடு நறுந்தார் பூணொடு பொலியச்","செம்பொறிக் கேற்ற வீங்கிறைத் தடக்கையின்","வண்ண வரிவில் லேந்தி யம்புதெரிந்து","நுண்வினைக் கச்சைத் தயக்கறக் கட்டி","யியலணிப் பொலிந்த வீகை வான்கழ","றுயல்வருந் தோறுந் திருந்தடிக் கலாவ"],"p":"","r":"Then a man appeared: his dark sapphire-coloured hair washed with butter-smoothed fragrant tagaram, dried with the fingers, scented with agil smoke, and loud with striped bees humming like a lute; on his head, to inspire awe, a cool garland of chosen flowers of every colour from hill, field, branch and pool, with a chaplet of white palm strips; a pitthigam garland curved behind, red asoka shoots tucked in and tender leaves swaying on his strong shoulders; sandal on his broad chest, an old fragrant garland shining with his ornaments; a painted banded bow in his large hand, arrows chosen, a finely worked belt tied tight, and bright warrior's anklets rubbing his fine feet as he walked.","n":"Line 113 has his garland gathered from hill, field, branch and pool, the same reach across lands as the girls' list of flowers: he comes dressed in what they had just gathered. The warrior's anklet (கழல்) and the painted bow make him a hunter-chief rather than a plain hunter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalaviyal","aanmai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 107-127","lb":"PP8:107-127","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["வெண்ணெய்","வெண்ணெய்",107,"unavu","ornament","butter"],["தகரங்","தகரம்",108,"maram","ornament","tagara"],["காழகி","அகில்",110,"maram","ornament","agil wood"],["யாழிசை","யாழ்",110,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["வரிமிஞி","மிஞிறு",111,"pira","scene","bee"],["மலராய்பு","மலர்",114,"maram","ornament",""],["வெண்போழ்க்","போழ்",115,"maram","ornament","palm strip"],["பித்தகத்","பித்திகம்",117,"maram","ornament",""],["பிண்டி","பிண்டி",119,"maram","ornament","asoka"],["யந்தளிர்க்","தளிர்",120,"maram","ornament","shoots"],["சாந்தருந்தி","சாந்து",120,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:128-145":{"book":"pattupattu","num":146,"t":["முனைபாழ் படுக்குந் துன்னருந் துப்பிற்","பகைபுறங் கண்ட பல்வே லிளைஞரி","னுரவுச்சினஞ் செருக்கித் துன்னுதொறும் வெகுளு","முளைவா ளெயிற்ற வள்ளுகிர் ஞமலி","திளையாக் கண்ண வளைகுபு நெரிதர","நடுங்குவன மெழுந்து நல்லடி தளர்ந்தியா","மிடும்பைகூர் மனத்தே மருண்டுபுலம் படர","மாறுபொரு தோட்டிய புகல்வின் வேறுபுலத்","தாகாண் விடையி னணிபெற வந்தெ","மலமர லாயிடை வெரூஉத லஞ்சி","மெல்லிய வினிய மேவரக் கிளந்தெ","மைம்பா லாய்கவி னேத்தி யண்டொடி","யசைமென் சாய லவ்வாங் குந்தி","மடமதர் மழைக்க ணிளையீ ரிறந்த","கெடுதியு முடையே னென்றென னதனெதிர்","சொல்லே மாதலி னல்லாந்து கலங்கிக்","கெடுதியும் விடீஇ ராயி னெம்மொடு","சொல்லலும் பழியோ மெல்லிய லீரென"],"p":"","r":"His hounds, with teeth like sharp sprouts and strong claws, fierce like his many-speared young warriors who have seen the backs of enemies and laid waste the frontier, angry at every approach, came round the girls with unblinking eyes; the girls rose trembling, their feet faltering, bewildered and distressed; he came, handsome as a bull among cows that has fought off and driven away a rival, and fearing they would take fright, spoke soft sweet words, praised their hair and their looks and said that he had lost a beast that came this way; when they gave no answer he was troubled and asked whether even speaking with him was a blame, if they would not tell him of the beast.","n":"The question about a lost beast (கெடுதி) is a pretext and the girls' silence is a rule of modesty, not rudeness: the man's reply, that speaking with him is no blame, names exactly the fear of blame that keeps them silent. Lines 128 and 129 can also be taken as describing his companions, who come with him; the reading here takes இளைஞரின் as the comparison for the hounds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kalaviyal","acham","naanam","inchol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 128-145","lb":"PP8:128-145","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["லிளைஞரி","இளைஞர்",129,"seythi","simile","young warriors"],["ஞமலி","ஞமலி",131,"maa","scene","hound"],["விடையி","விடை",136,"maa","simile","bull"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:146-165":{"book":"pattupattu","num":147,"t":["நைவளம் பழுநிய பாலை வல்லோன்","கைகவர் நரம்பி னிம்மென விமிரு","மாதர் வண்டொடு சுரும்புநயந் திறுத்த","தாதவி ழலரித் தாசினை பிளந்து","தாறடு களிற்றின் வீறுபெற வோச்சிக்","கல்லென் சுற்றக் கடுங்குர லவித்தெஞ்","சொல்லற் பாணி நின்றன னாக","விருவி வேய்ந்த குறுங்காற் குரம்பைப்","பிணையேர் நோக்கின் மனையோண் மடுப்பத்","தேம்பிழி தேறன் மாந்தி மகிழ்சிறந்து","சேம மடிந்த பொழுதின் வாய்மடுத்","திரும்புன நிழத்தலிற் சிறுமை நோனா","தரவுற ழஞ்சிலை கொளீஇ நோய்மிக்","குரவுச்சின முன்பா லுடற்சினஞ் செருக்கிக்","கணைவிடு புடையூக் கானங் கல்லென","மடிவிடு வீளையர் வெடிபடுத் தெதிரக்","கார்ப்பெய லுருமிற் பிளிறிச் சீர்த்தக","விரும்பிணர்த் தடக்கை யிருநிலஞ் சேர்த்திச்","சினந்திகழ் கடாஅஞ் செருக்கி மரங்கொல்பு","மையல் வேழ மடங்கலி னெதிர்தர"],"p":"","r":"He broke a flowering branch where bees settled humming like the strings a master of the paalai mode plucks, swung it proudly as one swings at an elephant that defies the goad, silenced the loud cries of his band and stood waiting for their word; just then, in a short-posted hut thatched with millet stalks, the field watchman, served honey-pressed toddy by his doe-eyed wife, drank and grew merry, and when the watch slackened an elephant broke into the great field; the watchman, unable to bear the loss, strung his snake-like bow, the whistling men shot arrows and raised a din that rang through the forest, and the maddened elephant, trumpeting like thunder of the rains, striking the ground with its trunk, in fierce musth and breaking trees, came at the girls like Death.","n":"The watchman's drinking is what lets the elephant in, so the poem makes a small domestic lapse the cause of the meeting that the friend calls களிறுதரு புணர்ச்சி in line 212. மடங்கல் in line 165 may be the lion or Death; either way it is the vehicle of the simile. Lines 151 and 152 can also be read as the girls silencing their own loud companions; the reading here gives the act to the man.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","acham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 146-165","lb":"PP8:146-165","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["வண்டொடு","வண்டு",148,"pira","simile","bee"],["சுரும்புநயந்","சுரும்பு",148,"pira","simile","bee"],["பாலை","பாலை",146,"yaazh","simile","paalai mode"],["நரம்பி","நரம்பு",147,"yaazh","simile","lute string"],["தாசினை","சினை",149,"maram","scene","branch"],["களிற்றின்","களிறு",150,"maa","simile","elephant"],["விருவி","இருவி",153,"maram","scene","millet stalk"],["குரம்பைப்","குரம்பை",153,"seythi","scene","hut"],["பிணையேர்","பிணை",154,"maa","simile","doe"],["தேறன்","தேறல்",155,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["புன","புனம்",157,"seythi","scene","millet field"],["வீளையர்","வீளையர்",161,"seythi","scene","whistling men"],["லுருமிற்","உரும்",162,"pira","simile","thunder"],["வேழ","வேழம்",165,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மடங்கலி","மடங்கல்",165,"deyvam","simile","Death"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:166-186":{"book":"pattupattu","num":148,"t":["வுய்விட மறியே மாகி யொய்யெனத்","திருத்துகோ லெல்வளை தெழிப்ப நாணுமறந்து","விதுப்புறு மனத்தேம் விரைந்தவற் பொருத்திச்","சூருறு மஞ்ஞையி னடுங்க வார்கோ","லுடுவுறும் பகழி வாங்கிக் கடுவிசை","யண்ணல் யானை யணிமுகத் தழுத்தலிற்","புண்ணுமிழ் குருதி முகம்பாய்ந் திழிதரப்","புள்ளி வரிநுதல் சிதைய நில்லா","தயர்ந்துபுறங் கொடுத்த பின்னர் நெடுவே","ளணங்குறு மகளி ராடுகளங் கடுப்பத்","திணிநிலைக் கடம்பின் றிரளரை வளைஇய","துணையறை மாலையிற் கைபிணி விடேஎ","நுரையுடைக் கலுழி பாய்தலி னுரவுத்திரை","யடுங்கரை வாழையி னடுங்கப் பெருந்தகை","யஞ்சி லோதி யசையல் யாவது","மஞ்ச லோம்புநின் னணிநல நுகர்கென","மாசறு சுடர்நுத னீவி நீடுநினைந்","தென்முக நோக்கி நக்கன னந்நிலை","நாணு முட்கு நண்ணுவழி யடைதர","வொய்யெனப் பிரியவும் விடாஅன் கவைஇ","யாக மடைய முயங்கலி னவ்வழிப்"],"p":"","r":"Not knowing where to escape, the girls, bangles jangling, forgetting shame, ran and clung to him trembling like peacocks seized by a spirit; he drew a long feathered arrow and drove it with great force into the noble elephant's face, so that blood gushed down over it and its spotted brow was torn, and it gave way and turned its back; the girls, still holding hands as tightly as the circle of women possessed by Murugan dancing round the thick trunk of the kadamba, trembled like a plantain on a bank beaten by a foaming flood; the noble man told the girl not to fear, that he would enjoy her beauty, stroked her bright brow, thought long, looked at the friend's face and smiled, and though shame and awe made her pull away at once, he would not let her go but drew her close and embraced her.","n":"Kapilar sets the love union inside the moment of terror: the girls' clinging to the man for safety becomes the embrace. That he looks at the friend's face and smiles (என்முகம் நோக்கி நக்கனன், line 183) makes the friend a witness, which is why she can now speak to the mother. The comparison to Murugan's possessed women, seized by the god, echoes the rites of lines 5 and 6, where the mother thinks a god has seized the girl.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kalaviyal","acham","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 166-186","lb":"PP8:166-186","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மஞ்ஞையி","மஞ்ஞை",169,"pul","simile","peacock"],["யானை","யானை",171,"maa","scene","elephant"],["நெடுவே","நெடுவேள்",174,"deyvam","simile","Murugan"],["கடம்பின்","கடம்பு",176,"maram","simile","kadamba"],["கலுழி","கலுழி",178,"pira","simile","flood"],["வாழையி","வாழை",179,"maram","simile","plantain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:187-199":{"book":"pattupattu","num":149,"t":["பழுமிள குக்க பாறை நெடுஞ்சுனை","முழுமுதற் கொக்கின் றீங்கனி யுதிர்ந்தெனப்","புள்ளெறி பிரசமொ டீண்டிப் பலவி","னெகிழ்ந்துகு நறும்பழம் விளைந்த தேற","னீர்செத் தயின்ற தோகை வியலூர்ச்","சாறுகொ ளாங்கண் விழவுக்கள நந்தி","யரிக்கூட் டின்னியங் கறங்க வாடுமகள்","கயிறூர் பாணியிற் றளருஞ் சாரல்","வரையர மகளிரிற் சாஅய் விழைதக","விண்பொருஞ் சென்னிக் கிளைஇய காந்தட்","டண்கம ழலரி தாஅய் நன்பல","வம்புவிரி களத்திற் கவின்பெறப் பொலிந்த","குன்றுகெழு நாடனெம் விழைதரு பெருவிற"],"p":"","r":"There, in the long rock pool into which ripe pepper had fallen and sweet mangoes dropped, mixed with honey the birds had scattered, the fragrant jackfruit that burst and fell had ripened into toddy, and a peacock drank it thinking it water and staggered like a rope-dancer at a big town's festival keeping time to a well-matched band, drooping like the goddesses of the hill: such is the slope of the lord of the hill country, whose peaks touching the sky are spread with kaanthal flowers like a field spread with new cloth.","n":"A peacock drunk on toddy that fruit has made by itself in a rock pool is Kapilar's image of the hill's unasked plenty (Akananuru 2 uses the same rock pool and the same toddy for a monkey). Read as iraicchi it would point to the girl who took joy unplanned, but the peacock does nothing a person of the situation does, so it is scene; the description praises the land of the man the girl has chosen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 187-199","lb":"PP8:187-199","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பழுமிள","மிளகு",187,"maram","scene","pepper"],["சுனை","சுனை",187,"pira","scene","pool"],["கொக்கின்","கொக்கு",188,"maram","scene","mango"],["புள்ளெறி","புள்",189,"pul","scene","birds"],["பிரசமொ","பிரசம்",189,"pira","scene","honey"],["பலவி","பலா",189,"maram","scene","jackfruit"],["தேற","தேறல்",190,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["தோகை","தோகை",191,"pul","simile","peacock"],["டின்னியங்","இன்னியம்",193,"parai","simile","band"],["வாடுமகள்","ஆடுமகள்",193,"seythi","simile","rope-dancer"],["வரையர மகளிரிற்","வரையர மகளிர்",195,"deyvam","simile","hill goddesses"],["காந்தட்","காந்தள்",196,"maram","scene","glory lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:200-214":{"book":"pattupattu","num":150,"t":["லுள்ளத் தன்மை யுள்ளினன் கொண்டு","சாறயர்ந் தன்ன மிடாஅச் சொன்றி","வருநர்க்கு வரையா வளநகர் பொற்ப","மலரத் திறந்த வாயில் பலருணப்","பைந்நிண மொழுகிய நெய்ம்மலி யடிசில்","வசையில் வான்றிணைப் புரையோர் கடும்பொடு","விருந்துண் டெஞ்சிய மிச்சில் பெருந்தகை","நின்னோ டுண்டலும் புரைவ தென்றாங்","கறம்புணை யாகத் தேற்றிப் பிறங்குமலை","மீமிசைக் கடவுள் வாழ்த்திக் கைதொழு","தேமுறு வஞ்சினம் வாய்மையிற் றேற்றி","யந்தீந் தெண்ணீர் குடித்தலி னெஞ்சமர்ந்","தருவிட ரமைந்த களிறுதரு புணர்ச்சி","வானுரி யுறையுள் வயங்கியோ ரவாவும்","பூமலி சோலை யப்பகல் கழிப்பி"],"p":"","r":"Understanding the girl's heart, the man of great strength whom they desired assured her, making virtue the raft: 'In a rich house that does not stint those who come, with pots of rice as for a festival and doors opened wide, where many eat rice rich with ghee and fresh fat, it will be fitting for me to eat with you, noble one, what is left after we feed guests with our blameless kin'; then, praising the god on the high peak with joined hands, he swore a binding oath in truth and drank the sweet clear water, and their hearts were at rest; so they spent that day of the union the elephant brought, in the hard cleft, in a flowered grove that even the dwellers of heaven would desire.","n":"The oath is sworn to the hill god and sealed by drinking water, and its content is a household: he promises a house of open doors and guests, not a feeling. The word for the oath, வஞ்சினம், is the one puram uses for a warrior's vow before battle. களிறுதரு புணர்ச்சி (line 212) names the convention, the union an elephant brings about.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","virundhu","vanjinam","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 200-214","lb":"PP8:200-214","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["சொன்றி","சொன்றி",201,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["பைந்நிண","நிணம்",204,"unavu","gift_food","fat"],["யடிசில்","அடிசில்",204,"unavu","gift_food","cooked rice"],["கடவுள்","கடவுள்",209,"deyvam","scene","hill god"],["தெண்ணீர்","தெண்ணீர்",211,"pira","gift_food","clear water"],["களிறுதரு","களிறு",212,"maa","scene","elephant"],["சோலை","சோலை",214,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:215-233":{"book":"pattupattu","num":151,"t":["யெல்லை செல்ல வேழூர் பிறைஞ்சிப்","பல்கதிர் மண்டிலங் கல்சேர்பு மறைய","மான்கண மரமுதற் றெவிட்ட வான்கணங்","கன்றுபயிர் குரல மன்றுநிறை புகுதர","வேங்குவயி ரிசைய கொடுவா யன்றி","லோங்கிரும் பெண்ணை யகமட லகவப்","பாம்புமணி யுமிழப் பல்வயிற் கோவல","ராம்பலந் தீங்குழற் றெள்விளி பயிற்ற","வாம்ப லாயிதழ் கூம்புவிட வளமனைப்","பூந்தொடி மகளிர் சுடர்தலைக் கொளுவி","யந்தி யந்தண ரயரக் கானவர்","விண்டோய் பணவை மிசைஞெகிழி பொத்த","வானமாமலை வாய்சூழ்பு கறுப்பக் கானங்","கல்லென் றிரட்டப் புள்ளின மொலிப்பச்","சினைஇய வேந்தன் செல்சமங் கடுப்பத்","துனைஇய மாலை துன்னுதல் காணூஉ","நேரிறை முன்கை பற்றி நுமர்தர","நாடறி நன்மண மயர்கஞ் சின்னாட்","கலங்க லோம்புமி னிலங்கிழை யீரென"],"p":"","r":"As the day went, the many-rayed sun bowed and hid behind the hill; deer crowded at the foot of trees, cows calling their calves filled the common, the curve-beaked anril cried with a horn's moan in the fronds of the tall palmyra, snakes spat out their gems, cowherds in many places played clear notes on ambal flutes, water lilies opened, women in rich houses lit lamps, brahmins did the evening rite, forest men lit torches on their sky-high watch platforms, the sky darkened round the great hill, the forest roared and flocks of birds cried; seeing the evening hasten on like the battle of an angry king, the man took the girl's forearm and said, 'When your people give her, we will hold a good wedding the whole land will know; for these few days, bright-jewelled ones, do not be troubled.'","n":"The sunset is drawn with mullai's hour and things, cows to their calves and the cowherd's flute, alongside kurinji's forest men and their torches; the evening is the hour when the lovers must part, and the man answers it with a promise of public marriage (நாடறி நன்மணம்), the first word of வரைவு in the poem. The simile of a king's army advancing is the second puram image the friend uses, after the two kings of line 27.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivu","maalai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 215-233","lb":"PP8:215-233","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மான்கண","மான்",217,"maa","scene","deer"],["வான்கணங்","ஆன்",217,"maa","scene","cows"],["கன்றுபயிர்","கன்று",218,"maa","scene","calves"],["யன்றி","அன்றில்",219,"pul","scene","anril bird"],["பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",220,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["பாம்புமணி","பாம்பு",221,"pira","scene","snake"],["கோவல","கோவலர்",221,"seythi","scene","cowherds"],["தீங்குழற்","குழல்",222,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["வாம்ப","ஆம்பல்",223,"maram","scene","water lily"],["யந்தண","அந்தணர்",225,"seythi","scene","brahmins"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",225,"seythi","scene","forest men"],["ஞெகிழி","ஞெகிழி",226,"pira","scene","torch"],["புள்ளின","புள்",228,"pul","scene","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:234-246":{"book":"pattupattu","num":152,"t":["வீர நன்மொழி தீரக் கூறித்","துணைபுண ரேற்றி னெம்மொடு வந்து","துஞ்சா முழவின் மூதூர் வாயி","லுண்டுறை நிறுத்தப் பெயர்ந்தன னதற்கொண்","டன்றை யன்ன விருப்போ டென்று","மிரவரன் மாலைய னேவரு தோறுங்","காவலர் கடுகினுங் கதநாய் குரைப்பினு","நீதுயி லெழினு நிலவுவெளிப் படினும்","வேய்புரை மென்றோ ளின்றுயி லென்றும்","பெறாஅன் பெயரினு முனிய லுறாஅ","னிளமையி னிகந்தன்று மிலனே வளமையிற்","றன்னிலை தீர்ந்தன்று மிலனே கொன்னூர்","மாய வரவி னியல்புநினைஇத் தேற்றி"],"p":"","r":"Having spoken kind good words in full, he came with them like a bull with its mate, brought them to the drinking ford at the gate of the old town where the drums never sleep, and left; since then, with the same desire as that day, he has made night-coming his habit, and every time he comes, if the guards hurry about, if the fierce dog barks, if you wake, if the moon comes out, never getting sweet sleep on her soft bamboo-like shoulders, he goes back without ever being angry; he has not strayed in youth, nor left his station through wealth; and the girl, thinking of the nature of his secret coming in that dreaded town, consoles herself.","n":"The friend gives the mother a character witness: the man keeps coming, is turned back by every hindrance and is never angry, and has not lapsed through youth or wealth (lines 243 to 245). The list of hindrances is told to the girl's mother, who is herself one of them (நீ துயில் எழினும).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kuri","anbu","porai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 234-246","lb":"PP8:234-246","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu","அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ரேற்றி","ஏறு",235,"maa","simile","bull"],["முழவின்","முழவு",236,"parai","scene","drum"],["கதநாய்","நாய்",240,"maa","scene","dog"],["நிலவு","நிலவு",241,"pira","scene","moonlight"],["வேய்புரை","வேய்",242,"maram","simile","bamboo"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:8:247-261":{"book":"pattupattu","num":153,"t":["நீரெறி மலரிற் சாஅயிதழ் சோரா","வீரிய கலுழுமிவள் பெருமதர் மழைக்க","ணாகத் தரிப்பனி யுறைப்ப நாளும்","வலைப்படு மஞ்ஞையி னலஞ்செலச் சாஅய்","நினைத்தொறுங் கலுழுமா லிவளே கங்கு","லளைச்செறி யுழுவையு மாளியு முளியமும்","புழற்கோட் டாமான் புகல்வியுங் களிறும்","வலியிற் றப்பும் வன்கண் வெஞ்சினத்","துருமுஞ் சூரு மிரைதே ரரவமு","மொடுங்கிருங் குட்டத் தருஞ்சுழி வழங்குங்","கொடுந்தாண் முதலையு மிடங்கருங் கராமு","நூழிலு மிழுக்கு மூழடி முட்டமும்","பழுவும் பாந்தளு முளப்படப் பிறவும்","வழுவின் வழாஅ விழுமமவர்","குழுமலை விடரக முடையவா லெனவே"],"p":"","r":"Her large bold cloud-dark eyes, drooping like flowers beaten by water, weep and their tears drip on her breast; every day her beauty wastes like a peacock caught in a net, and she weeps whenever she thinks that at night his clustered hill's clefts hold inescapable sorrows: the cave-dwelling tiger, the yali and the bear, the hollow-horned wild cattle and the elephant, the cruel fierce thunder and the spirits, the prey-seeking snake, the crooked-legged crocodile, idangar and karaam that move in the deep whirlpools, the slippery places, the narrow broken paths, the ghouls, the pythons and the rest.","n":"The poem ends not on the union but on the danger of the night path, which is the friend's strongest argument: the mother cannot keep the girl from the man, only from a marriage, and every night without one sends him through the beasts of lines 252 to 259. The netted peacock of line 250 answers the peacock of line 169: the girl who clung to him for safety is now caught.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arathodunirral","kaamanoy","acham","kuri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Kurinjippattu 247-261","lb":"PP8:247-261","poet":"கபிலர்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"குறிஞ்சி","ctu":"அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","sevili",null,"kalavu","அறத்தொடு நிற்றல்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"],["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["மலரிற்","மலர்",247,"maram","simile","flower"],["மஞ்ஞையி","மஞ்ஞை",250,"pul","simile","peacock"],["யுழுவையு","உழுவை",252,"maa","scene","tiger"],["மாளியு","ஆளி",252,"maa","scene","yali"],["முளியமும்","உளியம்",252,"maa","scene","bear"],["டாமான்","ஆமான்",253,"maa","scene","wild cattle"],["களிறும்","களிறு",253,"maa","scene","elephant"],["துருமுஞ்","உரும்",255,"pira","scene","thunder"],["சூரு","சூர்",255,"deyvam","scene","spirits"],["ரவமு","அரவம்",255,"pira","scene","snake"],["முதலையு","முதலை",257,"pira","scene","crocodile"],["மிடங்கருங்","இடங்கர்",257,"pira","scene","crocodile"],["கராமு","கராம்",257,"pira","scene","crocodile"],["பாந்தளு","பாந்தள்",259,"pira","scene","python"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:10"},"pp:9:1-28":{"book":"pattupattu","num":154,"t":["வசையில்புகழ் வயங்குவெண்மீன்","திசைதிரிந்து தெற்கேகினும்","தற்பாடிய தளியுணவிற்","புட்டேம்பப் புயன்மாறி","வான்பொய்ப்பினும் தான்பொய்யா","மலைத்தலைய கடற்காவிரி","புனல்பரந்து பொன்கொழிக்கும்","விளைவறா வியன்கழனிக்","கார்க்கரும்பின் கமழாலைத்","தீத்தெறுவிற் கவின்வாடி","நீர்ச்செறுவி னீணெய்தற்","பூச்சாம்பும் புலத்தாங்கட்","காய்ச்செந்நெற் கதிரருந்து","மோட்டெருமை முழுக்குழவி","கூட்டுநிழல் துயில்வதியும்","கோட்டெங்கிற் குலைவாழைக்","காய்க்கமுகிற் கமழ்மஞ்சள்","இனமாவின் இணர்ப்பெண்ணை","முதற்சேம்பின் முளையிஞ்சி","அனகர் வியன்முற்றத்துச்","சுடர்நுதல் மடநோக்கின்","நேரிழை மகளிர் உணங்குணாக் கவரும்","கோழி யெறிந்த கொடுங்காற் கனங்குழை","பொற்காற் புதல்வர் புரவியின் றுருட்டும்","முக்காற் சிறுதேர் முன்வழி விலக்கும்","விலங்குபகை யல்லது கலங்குபகை யறியாக்","கொழும்பல்குடிச் செழும்பாக்கத்துக்","குறும்பல்லூர் நெடுஞ்சோணாட்டு"],"p":"","r":"Even if the bright white star of spotless fame strays from its course and goes south, and the sky fails, withholding rain until the bird that sings for raindrops pines, the Kaveri that comes from the hills does not fail: its flood spreads gold over wide fields that never stop yielding, where the fires of the fragrant cane-presses wither the long neythal of the wet plots, the buffalo calf that ate the ripe paddy sleeps in the joined shade of coconut, plantain, areca, turmeric, mango, palmyra, taro and ginger, and in the wide courtyards women throw their heavy earrings at the fowl pecking the drying grain and so block the path of the boys' little three-wheeled carts; these are the many small towns of the long Chola land, which know no quarrel but that obstruction.","n":"The praise rests on a guarantee: the river does not fail even when the sky does (வான்பொய்ப்பினும் தான்பொய்யா), though line 6 admits the river comes from the hills. The villages' only 'enmity' is a pun: விலங்கு is the earring obstructing a toy cart, set against கலங்கு பகை, the hostility that troubles a people.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","mazhai","uzhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 1-28","lb":"PP9:1-28","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புட்டேம்பப்","புள்",4,"pul","scene","rain-bird"],["கார்க்கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",9,"maram","scene","sugarcane"],["னீணெய்தற்","நெய்தல்",11,"maram","absent",""],["செந்நெற்","செந்நெல்",13,"unavu","gift_food",""],["மோட்டெருமை","எருமை",14,"maa","scene",""],["கோட்டெங்கிற்","தெங்கு",16,"maram","scene",""],["குலைவாழைக்","வாழை",16,"maram","scene",""],["காய்க்கமுகிற்","கமுகு",17,"maram","scene",""],["மஞ்சள்","மஞ்சள்",17,"maram","scene",""],["இனமாவின்","மா",18,"maram","scene","mango"],["இணர்ப்பெண்ணை","பெண்ணை",18,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["முதற்சேம்பின்","சேம்பு",19,"maram","scene",""],["முளையிஞ்சி","இஞ்சி",19,"maram","scene",""],["உணங்குணாக்","உணங்கு உணா",22,"unavu","scene","drying grain"],["கோழி","கோழி",23,"pul","scene",""]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:29-50":{"book":"pattupattu","num":155,"t":["வெள்ளை யுப்பின் கொள்ளை சாற்றி","நெல்லொடு வந்த வல்லாய்ப் பஃறி","பணைநிலைப் புரவியின் அணைமுதற் பிணிக்கும்","கழிசூழ் படப்பைக் கலியாணர்ப்","பொழிற் புறவிற் பூந்தண்டலை","மழைநீங்கிய மாவிசும்பின்","மதிசேர்ந்த மகவெண்மீன்","உருகெழுதிறல் உயர்கோட்டத்து","முருகமர்பூ முரண்கிடக்கை","வரியணிசுடர் வான்பொய்கை","இருகாமத் திணையேரிப்","புலிப்பொறிப் போர்க்கதவின்","திருத்துஞ்சுந் திண்காப்பிற்","புகழ்நிலைஇய மொழிவளர","அறநிலைஇய அகனட்டிற்","சோறுவாக்கிய கொழுங்கஞ்சி","யாறுபோலப் பரந்தொழுகி","ஏறுபொரச் சேறாகித்","தேரொடத் துகள் கெழுமி","நீறாடிய களிறுபோல","வேறுபட்ட வினையோவத்து","வெண்கோயில் மாசூட்டுந்"],"p":"","r":"Boats that sold white salt and came back with paddy are tied at their posts like horses in a stall; groves and cool gardens ring the backwaters; near the high temple of fearsome power lie twin lakes bright with flowers, like the moon joined with a white star in a rainless sky; the gates bear the tiger emblem and a strong guard where fortune sleeps; and in the wide alms-kitchen where fame is fixed and dharma is kept, the rich rice-water poured off the cooked rice flows like a river, is churned to mud by fighting bulls and dried to dust by running chariots, so that it soils the white painted temple like an elephant that has rolled in dust.","n":"The charity is measured by its dirt: the alms-house's surplus rice-water becomes the mud and dust that soil the temples. Lines 34 to 39 are hard to construe; the moon-and-star image may describe the lakes or the temple tank, so the comparison is offered as one reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["selvam","aram","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 29-50","lb":"PP9:29-50","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["யுப்பின்","உப்பு",29,"pira","scene","salt"],["நெல்லொடு","நெல்",30,"unavu","scene",""],["புரவியின்","புரவி",31,"maa","simile","horse"],["பொழிற்","பொழில்",33,"maram","scene","grove"],["முருகமர்பூ","பூ",37,"maram","scene",""],["சோறுவாக்கிய","சோறு",44,"unavu","gift_food",""],["கொழுங்கஞ்சி","கஞ்சி",44,"unavu","gift_food","rice-water"],["ஏறுபொரச்","ஏறு",46,"maa","scene","bull"],["களிறுபோல","களிறு",48,"maa","simile",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:51-58":{"book":"pattupattu","num":156,"t":["தண்கேணித் தகைமுற்றத்துப்","பகட்டெருத்தின் பலசாலைத்","தவப்பள்ளித் தாழ்காவின்","அவிர்சடை முனிவர் அங்கி வேட்கும்","ஆவுதி நறும்புகை முனைஇக் குயில்தம்","மாயிரும் பெடையோ டிரியல் போகிப்","பூதங் காக்கும் புகலருங் கடிநகர்த்","தூதுணம் புறவொடு துச்சிற் சேக்கும்"],"p":"","r":"Beyond courtyards with cool wells and many halls, in the low groves of the hermitages where sages with shining matted hair tend the sacred fire, the cuckoo, shunning the fragrant smoke of the oblations, flies off with its dark mate and goes to roost with the pebble-eating doves in the eaves of the hard-to-enter guarded shrine that spirits watch over.","n":"The birds keep the city's order too: the cuckoo driven off by the sacrificial smoke does not leave the city but lodges with the doves in the spirit-guarded shrine. பகட்டெருத்தின் பலசாலை (line 52) is unclear: halls for bulls, or halls of the bull-necked.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 51-58","lb":"PP9:51-58","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தாழ்காவின்","கா",53,"maram","scene","grove"],["முனிவர்","முனிவர்",54,"seythi","scene","sages"],["குயில்தம்","குயில்",55,"pul","scene",""],["பூதங்","பூதம்",57,"deyvam","scene","guardian spirits"],["புறவொடு","புறவு",58,"pul","scene","dove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:59-72":{"book":"pattupattu","num":157,"t":["முதுமரத்த முரண்களரி","வரிமணல் அகன்திட்டை","இருங்கிளை யினனொக்கற்","கருந்தொழிற் கலிமாக்கள்","கடலிறவின் சூடுதின்றும்","வயலாமைப் புழுக்குண்டும்","வறளடும்பின் மலர்மலைந்தும்","புனலாம்பற் பூச்சூடியும்","நீனிற விசும்பின் வலனேர்பு திரிதரும்","நாண்மீன் விராய கோண்மீன் போல","மலர்தலை மன்றத்துப் பலருடன் குழீஇக்","கையினுங் கலத்தினு மெய்யுறத் தீண்டிப்","பெருஞ்சினத்தாற் புறக்கொடாஅது","இருஞ்செருவின் இகல்மொய்ம்பினோர்"],"p":"","r":"On the sandy mounds of the wrestling ground under old trees, the fishermen of rough work and many kin eat roasted prawns and boiled field tortoise, wear the flowers of the dry-sand adumbu creeper and the water lily, gather in the open common like planets moving among the stars of the dark-blue sky, and grapple with hands and with weapons, the strong ones of fierce fight never turning their backs in their great anger.","n":"The fight is play, but the poet gives it the war-ethic of never turning the back (புறக்கொடாஅது), the phrase the poem gives Karikalan's warriors in line 290.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 59-72","lb":"PP9:59-72","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["முதுமரத்த","மரம்",59,"maram","scene",""],["கலிமாக்கள்","மாக்கள்",62,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["கடலிறவின்","இறவு",63,"pira","gift_food","prawn"],["வயலாமைப்","ஆமை",64,"pira","gift_food","tortoise"],["வறளடும்பின்","அடும்பு",65,"maram","ornament",""],["புனலாம்பற்","ஆம்பல்",66,"maram","ornament",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:73-93":{"book":"pattupattu","num":158,"t":["கல்லெறியும் கவண்வெரீஇப்","புள்ளிரியும் புகர்ப்போந்தைப்","பறழ்ப்பன்றிப் பல்கோழி","உறைக்கிணற்றுப் புறச்சேரி","மேழகத் தகரொடு சிவல்விளை யாடக்","கிடுகுநிரைத் தெஃகூன்றி","நடுகல்லின் அரண்போல","நெடுந்தூண்டிலிற் காழ்சேர்த்திய","குறுங்கூரைக் குடிநாப்பண்","நிலவடைந்த இருள்போல","வலையுணங்கு மணல்முன்றில்","வீழ்த்தாழைத் தாட்டாழ்ந்த","வெண்கூ தாளத்துத் தண்பூங் கோதையர்","சினைச்சுறவின் கோடுநட்டு","மனைச்சேர்த்திய வல்லணங்கினால்","மடற்றாழை மலர்மலைந்தும்","பிணர்ப்பெண்ணைப் பிழிமாந்தியும்","புன்றலை இரும்பரதவர்","பைந்தழைமா மகளிரொடு","பாயிரும் பனிக்கடல் வேட்டஞ் செல்லாது","உவவுமடிந் துண்டாடியும்"],"p":"","r":"Where birds scatter in fear of the stones flung from slings, by the spotted palmyras, in the outer quarter of ring wells with piglets and fowl, rams play with quails; among the short-roofed homes the long fishing rods stand ranged like the shields and planted spears that fence a hero-stone, and the nets dry on the sandy yards like darkness lodged in moonlight; women wearing cool garlands of white koothaalam from under the hanging thazhai honour the strong goddess they have set in the house by planting a pregnant shark's horn, and on the full-moon day the fishermen, with their women in green leaf skirts, wear thazhai flowers, drink pressed palm toddy and do not go hunting on the wide cold sea, but rest, eat and play.","n":"The hero-stone, the memorial of a fallen warrior with its shield and spear, appears here only as a picture of stacked fishing gear; that the tenor is the rods of line 80 is my construal of a sentence that runs across the line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","inbam","vazhipaadu","nadukal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 73-93","lb":"PP9:73-93","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["புள்ளிரியும்","புள்",74,"pul","scene",""],["புகர்ப்போந்தைப்","போந்தை",74,"maram","scene","palmyra"],["பறழ்ப்பன்றிப்","பன்றி",75,"maa","scene",""],["பல்கோழி","கோழி",75,"pul","scene",""],["தகரொடு","தகர்",77,"maa","scene","ram"],["சிவல்விளை","சிவல்",77,"pul","scene","quail"],["வீழ்த்தாழைத்","தாழை",84,"maram","scene",""],["தாளத்துத்","கூதாளம்",85,"maram","ornament",""],["சினைச்சுறவின்","சுறவு",86,"pira","scene","shark"],["வல்லணங்கினால்","அணங்கு",87,"deyvam","scene",""],["மடற்றாழை","தாழை",88,"maram","ornament",""],["பிணர்ப்பெண்ணைப்","பெண்ணை",89,"maram","gift_food","palm toddy"],["இரும்பரதவர்","பரதவர்",90,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["பைந்தழைமா","தழை",91,"maram","ornament","leaf skirt"]],"en":[["herostone","நடுகல்லின்",79]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:94-110":{"book":"pattupattu","num":159,"t":["புலவுமணற் பூங்கானல்","மாமலை யணைந்த கொண்மூப் போலவும்","தாய்முலை தழுவிய குழவி போலவும்","தேறுநீர்ப் புணரியோ டியாறுதலை மணக்கும்","மலியோத் தொலிகூடல்","தீதுநீங்கக் கடலாடியும்","மாசுபோகப் புனல்படிந்தும்","அலவ னாட்டியும் உரவுத்திரை உழக்கியும்","பாவை சூழ்ந்தும் பல்பொறி மருண்டும்","அகலாக் காதலொடு பகல்விளை யாடிப்","பெறற்கருந் தொல்சீர்த் துறக்க மேய்க்கும்","பொய்யா மரபிற் பூமலி பெருந்துறைத்","துணைப்புணர்ந்த மடமங்கையர்","பட்டுநீக்கித் துகிலுடுத்தும்","மட்டுநீக்கி மதுமகிழ்ந்தும்","மைந்தர்கண்ணி மகளிர் சூடவும்","மகளிர்கோதை மைந்தர் மலையவும்"],"p":"","r":"On the flowered shore whose sand smells of fish, at the loud meeting of the tides where the river joins the clear sea as a cloud rests on a great hill and as a child clasps its mother's breast, people bathe in the sea to put away sin and in the river to wash off dirt, chase crabs, tread the strong waves, play around sand dolls and marvel at many sights, playing all day in unwavering love; at the great flowered ford of unfailing custom, which is like the ancient heaven hard to reach, young women with their partners put off silk for cotton and wine for mead, the women wear the men's chaplets and the men the women's garlands.","n":"Lovers appear here only as part of the city's festival, with no speaker or situation; yet the exchange of garlands and the comparison of the ford to heaven prepare the hero's choice in line 218, where even such a city weighs less than her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","theerththam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 94-110","lb":"PP9:94-110","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["கொண்மூப்","கொண்மூ",95,"pira","simile","cloud"],["அலவ","அலவன்",101,"pira","scene","crab"],["மட்டுநீக்கி","மட்டு",108,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["மதுமகிழ்ந்தும்","மது",108,"unavu","gift_food","mead"],["மைந்தர்கண்ணி","கண்ணி",109,"maram","ornament","chaplet"],["மகளிர்கோதை","கோதை",110,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:111-117":{"book":"pattupattu","num":160,"t":["நெடுங்கால் மாடத் தொள்ளெரி நோக்கிக்","கொடுந்திமிற் பரதவர் குரூஉச்சுடர் எண்ணவும்","பாட லோர்த்தும் நாடக நயந்தும்","வெண்ணிலவின் பயன்றுய்த்தும்","கண்ணடைஇய கடைக்கங்குலான்","மாஅகாவிரி மணங்கூட்டும்","தூஉவெக்கர்த் துயில் மடிந்து"],"p":"","r":"At night they watch the bright fires on the tall-pillared mansions while fishermen in their curved boats count the coloured lights, listen to songs, enjoy plays and take the pleasure of the white moonlight, and at the end of the night, when eyes close, they sleep on the clean sand dunes where the great Kaveri mingles with the sea.","n":"The fishermen count the mansion lamps from the sea (குரூஉச்சுடர் எண்ணவும்): the city's lights serve as their landmark.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","kalai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 111-117","lb":"PP9:111-117","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["பரதவர்","பரதவர்",112,"seythi","scene","fishermen"]],"en":[["river","காவிரி",116]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:118-141":{"book":"pattupattu","num":161,"t":["வாலிணர் மடற்றாழை","வேலாழி வியன்தெருவின்","நல்லிறைவன் பொருள்காக்கும்","தொல்லிசைத் தொழில்மாக்கள்","காய்சினத்த கதிர்ச்செல்வன்","தேர்பூண்ட மாஅபோல","வைகல்தொறும் அசைவின்றி","உல்குசெயக் குறைபடாது","வான்முகந்தநீர் மலைப்பொழியவும்","மலைப்பொழிந்தநீர் கடற்பரப்பவும்","மாரிபெய்யும் பருவம்போல","நீரினின்று நிலத்தேற்றவும்","நிலத்தின்று நீர்ப்பரப்பவும்","அளந்தறியாப் பலபண்டம்","வரம்பறியாமை வந்தீண்டி","அருங்கடிப் பெருங்காப்பின்","வலியுடை வல்லணங்கினோன்","புலிபொறித்துப் புறம்போக்கி","மதிநிறைந்த மலிபண்டம்","பொதிமூடைப் போரேறி","மழையாடு சிமைய மால்வரைக் கவாஅன்","வரையாடு வருடைத் தோற்றம் போலக்","கூருகிர் ஞமலிக் கொடுந்தா ளேற்றை","ஏழகத் தகரோ டுகளு முன்றிற்"],"p":"","r":"In the wide street by the sea lined with white-clustered thazhai, the customs men of ancient fame who guard the good king's revenue work every day without tiring, like the horses yoked to the chariot of the hot-rayed sun, and levy duty without shortfall; as in the rainy season, when water the sky has drawn up pours on the hills and the hills pour it to the sea, goods are landed from the sea and shipped from the land; countless goods without limit pile up, the strong officer of the hard guard stamps them with the tiger and sends them out, and on the heaps of bales the sharp-clawed male dog leaps with the ram in the yards like mountain goats skipping on the cloud-washed slopes of a great hill.","n":"The water-cycle simile makes trade a natural circulation: goods go from sea to land and land to sea as rain goes from sky to hill to sea. The tiger stamp (புலிபொறித்து) is the Chola seal on dutiable goods.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 118-141","lb":"PP9:118-141","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","ஆக்கம்"]],"k":[["மடற்றாழை","தாழை",118,"maram","scene",""],["மாஅபோல","மா",123,"maa","simile","horse"],["மாரிபெய்யும்","மாரி",128,"pira","simile","rain"],["வருடைத்","வருடை",139,"maa","simile","mountain goat"],["ஞமலிக்","ஞமலி",140,"maa","scene","dog"],["தகரோ","தகர்",141,"maa","scene","ram"],["தொழில்மாக்கள்","மாக்கள்",121,"seythi","scene","customs men"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:142-158":{"book":"pattupattu","num":162,"t":["குறுந்தொடை நெடும்படிக்காற்","கொடுந்திண்ணைப் பஃறகைப்பிற்","புழைவாயிற் போகிடைகழி","மழைதோயும் உயர்மாடத்துச்","சேவடிச் செறிகுறங்கிற்","பாசிழைப் பகட்டல்குல்","தூசிடைத் துகிர்மேனி","மயிலியல் மானோக்கிற்","கிளிமழலை மென்சாயலோர்","வளிநுழையும் வாய்பொருந்தி","ஓங்குவரை மருங்கின் நுண்தா துறைக்கும்","காந்தளந் துடுப்பிற் கவிகுலை யன்ன","செறிதொடி முன்கை கூப்பிக் செவ்வேள்","வெறியாடு மகளிரொடு செறியத் தாஅய்க்","குழலகவ யாழ்முரல","முழவதிர முரசியம்ப","விழவறா வியலாவணத்து"],"p":"","r":"Past the yards stand houses with short-runged tall ladders, curved verandas, many-doored rooms, small gates and passages, and mansions so high they touch the clouds; there women with red feet, close thighs, green jewels, broad hips, silk at the waist, coral-red bodies, the gait of peacocks, the looks of deer, the prattle of parrots and gentle softness stand at the windows where the wind enters and join in worship of red Murugan their bangled forearms, which are like the bent clusters of kaanthal on a high hillside where fine pollen settles, and crowd close with the women who dance the veri, while the flute calls, the lute hums, the drum throbs and the great drum booms in the wide bazaar that never lacks a festival.","n":"The kaanthal, a kurinji flower, and the veri rite of the hills are brought into the port: the women of the mansions worship the hill god in the city's bazaar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","veri","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 142-158","lb":"PP9:142-158","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["துகிர்மேனி","துகிர்",148,"pira","simile","coral"],["மயிலியல்","மயில்",149,"pul","simile",""],["மானோக்கிற்","மான்",149,"maa","simile",""],["கிளிமழலை","கிளி",150,"pul","simile",""],["காந்தளந்","காந்தள்",153,"maram","simile",""],["செவ்வேள்","செவ்வேள்",154,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"],["வெறியாடு","வெறியாடு மகளிர்",155,"seythi","scene","veri dancers"],["குழலகவ","குழல்",156,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["யாழ்முரல","யாழ்",156,"yaazh","scene",""],["முழவதிர","முழவு",157,"parai","scene",""],["முரசியம்ப","முரசு",157,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:159-184":{"book":"pattupattu","num":163,"t":["மையறு சிறப்பின் தெய்வஞ் சேர்த்திய","மலரணி வாயிற் பலர்தொழ கொடியும்","வருபுனல் தந்த வெண்மணற் கான்யாற்று","உருகெழு கரும்பின் ஒண்பூப் போலக்","கூழுடைக் கொழுமஞ்சிகைத்","தாழுடைத் தண்பணியத்து","வாலரிசிப் பலிசிதறிப்","பாகுகுத்த பசுமெழுக்கிற்","காழூன்றிய கவிகிடுகின்","மேலூன்றிய துகிற்கொடியும்","பல்கேள்வித் துறைபோகிய","தொல்லாணை நல்லாசிரியர்","உறழ்குறித் தெடுத்த உருகெழு கொடியும்","வெளிலிளக்குங் களிறுபோலத்","தீம்புகார்த் திரைமுன்றுறைத்","தூங்குநாவாய் துவன்றிருக்கை","மிசைச்கூம்பி னசைச்கொடியும்","மீந்தடிந்து விடக்கறுத்து","ஊன்பொரிக்கும் ஒலிமுன்றில்","மணற்குவைஇ மலர்சிதறிப்","பலர்புகுமனைப் பலிப்புதவின்","நறவுநொடைக் கொடியோடு","பிறபிறவு நனிவிரைஇப்","பல்வே றுருவிற் பதாகை நீழல்","செல்கதிர் நுழையாச் செழுநகர் வரைப்பிற்","செல்லா நல்லிசை யுமரர் காப்பின்"],"p":"","r":"Over the city fly flags of many kinds: at the flower-decked doorway of the shrine where the flawless god is installed, worshipped by many; over the shops with granaries of grain and locked stores of cool goods, where white rice is strewn as offering on floors fresh-smeared with syrup, cloth flags on poles above bent screens, bright as the flower of the fearsome reed on a forest stream's white sand; the flags raised in challenge by good teachers of ancient authority who have mastered many branches of learning; the swaying flags on the masts of the ships crowded at the wave-washed ford of sweet Puhar, which rock like an elephant shaking its tethering post; the flags of the toddy sellers, in yards loud with frying fish and flesh, where sand is heaped and flowers strewn at the offering doors of houses that many enter; and so many other banners of every shape mingle that in their shade the sun's rays cannot enter the rich city, guarded by gods of undying fame.","n":"The flags sort the city by its institutions: shrine, trade, learning in debate (உறழ்குறித் தெடுத்த, flags raised as a challenge), the port and the toddy shop. Line 175 is corrupt in our text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["selvam","vazhipaadu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 159-184","lb":"PP9:159-184","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","ஆக்கம்"]],"k":[["தெய்வஞ்","தெய்வம்",159,"deyvam","scene",""],["மலரணி","மலர்",160,"maram","scene",""],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",162,"maram","simile","reed"],["கூழுடைக்","கூழ்",163,"unavu","scene","grain"],["வாலரிசிப்","அரிசி",165,"unavu","gift_food",""],["நல்லாசிரியர்","ஆசிரியர்",170,"seythi","scene","teachers"],["களிறுபோலத்","களிறு",172,"maa","simile",""],["மீந்தடிந்து","மீன்",176,"pira","gift_food","fish"],["விடக்கறுத்து","விடக்கு",176,"unavu","gift_food","flesh"],["நறவுநொடைக்","நறவு",180,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["யுமரர்","அமரர்",184,"deyvam","scene",""]],"en":[["town","புகார்",173]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:185-193":{"book":"pattupattu","num":164,"t":["நீரின் வந்த நிமிர்பரிப் புரவியும்","காலின் வந்த கருங்கறி மூடையும்","வடமலைப் பிறந்த மணியும் பொன்னும்","குடமலைப் பிறந்த ஆரமும் அகிலும்","தென்கடல் முத்துங் குணகடல் துகிரும்","கங்கை வாரியும் காவிரிப் பயுனும்","ஈழத் துணவும் காழகத் தாக்கமும்","அரியவும் பெரியவும் நெரிய ஈண்டி","வளந்தலை மயங்கிய நனந்தலை மறுகின்"],"p":"","r":"Horses of lofty gait brought by sea, sacks of black pepper brought by land, gems and gold born in the northern mountain, sandal and agil born in the western mountain, pearls of the southern sea and coral of the eastern sea, the yield of the Ganges and the produce of the Kaveri, food from Eelam and goods from Kalakam: rare things and great things crowd together in the wide streets where riches lie heaped and mingled.","n":"The list is built by the four directions (north, west, south, east), then two rivers, then two overseas lands, so the city is shown as the point where the whole known world's goods meet. காலின் வந்த (line 186) may mean 'by land' or 'by the wind', that is by sailing ship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 185-193","lb":"PP9:185-193","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புரவியும்","புரவி",185,"maa","scene","horse"],["கருங்கறி","கறி",186,"unavu","scene","pepper"],["ஆரமும்","ஆரம்",188,"maram","scene","sandal"],["அகிலும்","அகில்",188,"maram","scene",""],["முத்துங்","முத்து",189,"pira","scene","pearl"],["துகிரும்","துகிர்",189,"pira","scene","coral"],["துணவும்","உணவு",191,"unavu","scene",""]],"en":[["hill","வடமலைப்",187],["hill","குடமலைப்",188],["river","கங்கை",190],["river","காவிரி",190]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:194-217":{"book":"pattupattu","num":165,"t":["நீர்நாப் பண்ணு நிலத்தின் மேலும்","ஏமாப்ப இனிதுதுஞ்சிக்","கிளை கலித்துப் பகைபேணாது","வலைஞர்முன்றில் மீன்பிறழவும்","விலைஞர்குரம்பை மாவீண்டவும்","கொலைகடிந்தும் களவுநீக்கியும்","அமரர்ப் பேணியும் ஆவுதி அருத்தியும்","நல்லானொடு பகடோம்பியும்","நான்மறையோர் புகழ்பரப்பியும்","பண்ணியம் அட்டியும் பசும்பதங் கொடுத்தும்","புண்ணிய முட்டாத் தண்ணிழல் வாழ்க்கைக்","கொடுமேழி நசையுழவர்","நெடுநுகத்துப் பகல்போல","நடுவுநின்ற நன்னெஞ்சினோர்","வடுவஞ்சி வாய்மொழிந்து","தமவும் பிறவு மொப்ப நாடிக்","கொள்வதூஉ மிகைகொளாது கொடுப்பதூஉங் குறைகொடாது","பல்பண்டம் பகர்ந்துவீசும்","தொல்கொண்டித் துவன்றிருக்கைப்","பல்லாயமொடு பதிபழகி","வேறுவே றுயர்ந்த முதுவா யக்கற்","சாறயர் மூதூர் சென்றுதொக் காங்கு","மொழிபல பெருகிய பழிதீர் தேஎத்துப்","புலம்பெயர் மாக்கள் கலந்தினி துறையும்"],"p":"","r":"In water and on land creatures sleep safe and content, their kin thriving, heedless of enemies, so that fish leap in the fishermen's yards and beasts crowd at the meat-sellers' huts; for the ploughmen of the bent plough, whose cool-shaded life never lacks merit, have banned killing and put away theft, tend the gods and feed the sacrificial fire, keep good cows and oxen, spread the fame of the four-Veda brahmins, give cooked food and fresh provisions; and the merchants, fair-minded like the pin at the middle of those ploughmen's long yoke, fearing blame speak the truth, weigh their own goods and others' alike, take no more than is due and give no less, and sell many goods in the crowded mart of ancient riches; there, as kin of wise speech from many towns each raised in its own way gather at an old town holding its festival, people come from other lands, speaking many tongues, live mingled happily in this blameless country.","n":"The safety of fish at the fishermen's door and beasts at the meat-sellers' hut is the measure of the ban on killing, though the same city fries fish and flesh in lines 176 and 177. I take lines 199 to 204 as the ploughmen's virtues and lines 207 to 212 as the merchants', with the ploughman's yoke-pin as the simile between them; the sentence can also be read as one class throughout.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","vaaimai","kollaamai","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 194-217","lb":"PP9:194-217","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["வலைஞர்முன்றில்","வலைஞர்",197,"seythi","scene","fishermen"],["மீன்பிறழவும்","மீன்",197,"pira","scene","fish"],["விலைஞர்குரம்பை","விலைஞர்",198,"seythi","scene","meat-sellers"],["மாவீண்டவும்","மா",198,"maa","scene","beasts"],["நல்லானொடு","ஆன்",201,"maa","scene","cow"],["பகடோம்பியும்","பகடு",201,"maa","scene","ox"],["பசும்பதங்","பசும் பதம்",203,"unavu","gift_food","provisions"],["நசையுழவர்","உழவர்",205,"seythi","scene","ploughmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:218-228":{"book":"pattupattu","num":166,"t":["முட்டாச் சிறப்பிற் பட்டினம் பெறினும்","வாரிருங் கூந்தல் வயங்கிழை யொழிய","வாரேன் வாழிய நெஞ்சே கூருகிர்க்","கொடுவரிக் குருளை கூட்டுள் வளர்ந்தாங்கும்","பிறர் பிணியகத் திருந்து பீடுகாழ் முற்றி","அருங்கரை கவியக் குத்திக் குழிகொன்று","பெருங்கை யானை பிடிபுக் காங்கு","நுண்ணிதின் உணர நாடி நண்ணார்","செறிவுடைத் திண்காப் பேறிவாள் கழித்து","உருகெழு தாயம் ஊழினெய்திப்","பெற்றவை மகிழ்தல் செய்யான் செற்றோர்"],"p":"","r":"Even if I were given this city of unfailing glory, I will not come away leaving her of the long dark hair and bright jewels, my heart, long may you live; and the praise of the king begins: like a fierce-striped, sharp-clawed tiger cub growing up in a cage, he grew to full strength while held in others' bonds, and as a great-handed elephant whose tusks have grown breaks the hard bank of the pit, destroys the pit and goes to its cow, he planned with subtle care, climbed his foes' strong guarded wall, drew his sword and in due course gained his fearsome inheritance, yet did not rest content with what he had gained.","n":"This is the poem's only statement of the love situation, and it comes after 217 lines of the city: the weight of 'even if I got Puhar' is built before it is spoken. The caged tiger cub and the elephant breaking its pit picture the king's own captivity and escape, which the poem states only through images (பிறர் பிணியகத் திருந்து).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","nenju","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 218-228","lb":"PP9:218-228","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"mixed","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"செலவழுங்குதல்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["குருளை","குருளை",221,"maa","simile","tiger cub"],["யானை","யானை",224,"maa","simile",""],["பிடிபுக்","பிடி",224,"maa","simile","cow elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:229-251":{"book":"pattupattu","num":167,"t":["கடியரண் தொலைத்த கதவுகொல் மருப்பின்","முடியுடைக் கருந்தலை புரட்டு முன்றாள்","உகிருடை யடிய ஓங்கெழில் யானை","வடிமணிப் புரவியொடு வயவர் வீழப்","பெருநல் வானத்துப் பருந்துலாய் நடப்பத்","தூறிவர் துறுகற் போலப் போர்வேட்டு","வேறுபல் பூளைய டுழிஞை சூடிப்","பேய்க்கண் அன்ன பிளிறுகடி முரசம்","மாக்கண் அகலறை அதிர்வன முழங்க","முனைகெடச் சென்று முன்சம முருக்கித்","தலைதவச் சென்று தண்பணை எடுப்பி","வெண்பூக் கரும்பொடு செந்நெல் நீடி","மாவிதழ்க் குவளையொடு நெய்தலும் மயங்கிக்","கராஅங் கலித்த கண்ணகன் பொய்கைக்","கொழுங்காற் புதவமொடு செருந்தி நீடிச்","செறுவும் வாவிய மயங்கி நீரற்று","அறுகோட் டிரலையொடு மான்பிணை உகளவும்","கொண்டி மகளிர் உண்டுறை மூழ்கி","அந்தி மாட்டிய நந்தா விளக்கின்","மலரணி மெழுக்க மேறிப் பலர்தொழ","வம்பலர் சேக்கும் கந்துடைப் பொதியிற்","பருநிலை நெடுந்தூண் ஒல்கத் தீண்டிப்","பெருநல் யானையொடு பிடிபுணர்ந் துறையவும்"],"p":"","r":"His tall elephants, whose tusks broke the doors of his enemies' guarded forts, whose forefeet roll crowned dark heads and whose feet bear claws, fall with horses of polished bells and with warriors, and kites wheel in the great sky; eager for war, his men wear uzhinai mixed with many kinds of poolai, like a boulder overgrown with bush; with the drum of fearsome roar, its eyes like a demon's, booming on its wide face, he goes and wrecks the enemy front, crushes the vanguard, presses deep and lays waste the cool fields: white-flowered reeds grow tall with the paddy, kuvalai with dark petals tangles with neythal in the wide pond full of crocodiles, cherunthi grows tall at the sluices, fields and pools merge dry and the stag with fine horns frolics with its doe; and in the public hall with its sacred post, where captive women once bathed at the drinking ford and climbed to the flower-decked floor lit at dusk with unfailing lamps and worshipped by many, where travellers lodged, the great elephant lives with its cow, rubbing the thick tall pillar until it sways.","n":"The pairs that akam poems use for tender implication, the stag with its doe and the elephant with its cow, here only show that the enemy's fields and halls have gone wild. The hall's post (கந்து) and worship (பலர்தொழ) mark it as a shrine, now a stable for wild elephants.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","paazh","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 229-251","lb":"PP9:229-251","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",231,"maa","scene",""],["புரவியொடு","புரவி",232,"maa","scene","horse"],["பருந்துலாய்","பருந்து",233,"pul","scene","kite"],["பூளைய","பூளை",235,"maram","ornament",""],["டுழிஞை","உழிஞை",235,"maram","ornament",""],["முரசம்","முரசம்",236,"parai","scene",""],["கரும்பொடு","கரும்பு",240,"maram","scene","reed"],["செந்நெல்","செந்நெல்",240,"unavu","scene",""],["குவளையொடு","குவளை",241,"maram","scene",""],["நெய்தலும்","நெய்தல்",241,"maram","scene",""],["கராஅங்","கராம்",242,"pira","scene","crocodile"],["புதவமொடு","புதவம்",243,"maram","scene",""],["செருந்தி","செருந்தி",243,"maram","scene",""],["டிரலையொடு","இரலை",245,"maa","scene","stag"],["மான்பிணை","மான் பிணை",245,"maa","scene","doe"],["யானையொடு","யானை",251,"maa","scene",""],["பிடிபுணர்ந்","பிடி",251,"maa","scene","cow elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:252-270":{"book":"pattupattu","num":168,"t":["அருவிலை நறும்பூத் தூஉய்த் தெருவின்","முதுவாய்க் கோடியர் முழவொடு புணர்ந்த","திரிபுரி நரம்பின் தீந்தொடை யோர்க்கும்","பெருவிழாக் கழிந்த பேஎமுதிர் மன்றத்துக்","சிறுபூ நெருஞ்சியோ டறுகை பம்பி","அழல்வா யோரி அஞ்சுவரக் கதிர்ப்பவும்","அழுகுரற் கூகையோ டாண்டலை விளிப்பவும்","கணங்கொள் கூளியொடு கதுப்பிகுத் தசைஇப்","பிணந்தின் யாக்கைப் பேய்மகள் துவன்றவும்","கொடுங்கால் மாடத்து நெடுங்கடை துவன்றி","விருந்துண் டானாப் பெருஞ்சோற் றட்டில்","ஒண்சுவர் நல்லில் உயர்திணை யிருந்து","பைங்கிளி மிழற்றும் பாலார் செழுநகர்த்","தொடுதோ லடியரி துடிபடக் குழீஇக்","கொடுவி லெயினர் கொள்ளை யுண்ட","உணவில் வறுங்கூட் டுள்ளகத் திருந்து","வளைவாய்க் கூகை நன்பகற் குழறவும்","அருங்கடி வரைப்பின் ஊர்கவி னழியப்","பெரும்பாழ் செய்தும் அமையான் மருங்கற"],"p":"","r":"In the common where costly fragrant flowers were once strewn and old bards of wise speech listened with their drums to the sweet strings of the twisted lute, now that the great festivals are over and fear has grown, small-flowered nerunji and arukai grass spread, the fire-mouthed jackal howls fearfully, the owl of weeping cry and the aandalai call, and corpse-eating demon women crowd with hosts of goblins, loosing their hair; in the rich houses with bright walls and high seats where green parrots prattled, where guests crowded the long gates of curved-pillared mansions to eat from kitchens whose great rice never ran out, the hook-beaked owl hoots at broad noon from inside the empty granary that bow-bent hunters, gathered to the beat of the thudi, have looted; so he undoes the beauty of hard-guarded towns, and is not satisfied even with making a great waste.","n":"The ruined common is the reverse of the festival commons of Puhar earlier in the poem (lines 69 and 158), with the same lute, drum, flowers and feasting named as gone; the owl hooting at broad noon is the sign that the order of day and night has failed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paazh","nilaiyaamai","kedu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 252-270","lb":"PP9:252-270","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"vanji","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["நறும்பூத்","பூ",252,"maram","absent",""],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",253,"seythi","absent","bards"],["முழவொடு","முழவு",253,"parai","absent",""],["நரம்பின்","நரம்பு",254,"yaazh","absent","lute strings"],["நெருஞ்சியோ","நெருஞ்சி",256,"maram","scene",""],["டறுகை","அறுகை",256,"maram","scene",""],["யோரி","ஓரி",257,"maa","scene","jackal"],["கூகையோ","கூகை",258,"pul","scene","owl"],["டாண்டலை","ஆண்டலை",258,"pul","scene",""],["கூளியொடு","கூளி",259,"deyvam","scene","goblins"],["பேய்மகள்","பேய்மகள்",260,"deyvam","scene",""],["பெருஞ்சோற்","சோறு",262,"unavu","absent",""],["பைங்கிளி","கிளி",264,"pul","absent",""],["துடிபடக்","துடி",265,"parai","scene",""],["லெயினர்","எயினர்",266,"seythi","scene","hunters"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:271-291":{"book":"pattupattu","num":169,"t":["மலையகழ்க் குவனே கடல்தூர்க் குவனே","வான்வீழ்க் குவனே வளிமாற் றுவனெனத்","தன்முன்னிய துறைபோகலிற்","பல்லொளியர் பணிபொடுங்கத்","தொல்லரு வாளர் தொழில் கேட்ப","வடவர் வாடக் குடவர் கூம்பத்","தென்னவன் திறல்கெடச் சீறி மன்னர்","மன்னெயில் கதுவும் மதனுடை நோன்றாள்","மாத்தனை மறமொய்ம்பிற்","செங்கண்ணாற் செயிர்த்துநோக்கிப்","புன்பொதுவர் வழிபொன்ற","இருங்கோவேள் மருங்குசாயக்","காடுகொன்று நாடாக்கிக்","குளந்தொட்டு வளம்பெருக்கிக்","பிறங்குநிலை மாடத் துறந்தை போக்கிக்","கோயிலொடு குடிநிறீஇ","வாயிலொடு புழையமைத்து","ஞாயிறொறும் புதைநிறீஇ","பொருவேமெனப் பெயர்கொடுத்து","ஒருவேமெனப் புறக்கொடாது","திருநிலைஇய பெருமன் னெயில்"],"p":"","r":"He would dig out the hills, fill up the sea, bring down the sky and turn back the wind, they say, as he goes the way he has resolved: the Oliyar bow and shrink, the ancient Aruvalar heed his command, the northerners wilt, the westerners droop, the Pandya's strength is broken by his anger; with strong legs that seize the forts of kings, great might and martial power, glaring with red eyes he ends the line of the petty herdsmen and brings down Irungovel's house, clears forests and makes them country, digs tanks and increases wealth, extends Uraiyur of towering mansions, settles people with temples, builds gates and posterns, sets cover on every battlement, and holds the great fortress where fortune dwells, its men having given their names saying 'we will fight' and never turning back saying 'we are only one'.","n":"The king is praised as destroyer and founder in one sentence: the same man who wastes the enemy's land clears forest into country and digs tanks. போக்கி (line 285) may mean that he extended Uraiyur or that he left it; ஞாயிறொறும் (line 288) is read as ஞாயில் தொறும், 'at every battlement'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","aran","padai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 271-291","lb":"PP9:271-291","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"puram","th":"vaakai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[],"en":[["king","தென்னவன்",277],["king","இருங்கோவேள்",282],["town","துறந்தை",285]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:9:292-301":{"book":"pattupattu","num":170,"t":["மின்னொளியெறிப்பத் தம்மொளி மழுங்கி","விசிபிணி முழவின் வேந்தர் சூடிய","பசுமணி பொருத பரேரெறுழ்க் கழற்காற்","பொற்றொடிப் புதல்வர் ஒடி யாடவும்","முற்றிழை மகளிர் முகிழ்முலை திளைப்பவும்","செஞ்சாந்து சிதைந்த மார்பின் ஒண்பூண்","அரிமா அன்ன அணங்குடைத் துப்பின்","திருமா வளவன் தெவ்வர்க் கோக்கிய","வேலினும் வெய்ய கானமவன்","கோலினுந் தண்ணிய தடமென் தோளே"],"p":"","r":"Where kings, their own lustre dimmed as his flashes like lightning, bow until the fresh gems of their crowns rub the anklets on his strong thick legs, where sons with golden bracelets run and play and women in full jewels press their budding breasts against his chest, whose bright ornaments lie on red sandal paste smeared and spoiled, Thirumavalavan of fearsome strength like a lion: the forest is hotter than the spear he raised against his foes, and her broad soft shoulders are cooler than his sceptre.","n":"The whole eighty-line praise of Karikalan has been the first half of a double comparison: his spear (to enemies) and his sceptre (to his people) become the measures of the forest's heat and of her coolness, so the puram matter ends as the lover's reason for staying. The king is named only here, in line 299.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","sengol","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Pattinappalai 292-301","lb":"PP9:292-301","poet":"கடியலூர் உருத்திரங் கண்ணனார்","pat":"திருமாவளவன் (கரிகாற் பெருவளத்தான்)","ap":"mixed","th":"paalai","cth":"பாலை","ctu":"செலவழுங்குதல்","thd":0,"uri":"pirithal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","nenju",null,null,"செலவழுங்குதல்"]],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["முழவின்","முழவு",293,"parai","scene",""],["செஞ்சாந்து","சாந்து",297,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["அரிமா","அரிமா",298,"maa","simile","lion"]],"en":[["king","திருமா வளவன்",299]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:11"},"pp:10:1-20":{"book":"pattupattu","num":171,"t":["திருமழை தலைஇய இருணிற விசும்பின்","விண்ணதிர் இமிழிசை கடுப்பப் பண்ணமைத்துத்","திண்வார் விசித்த முழவொ டாகுளி","நுண்ணுருக் குற்ற விளங்கடர்ப் பாண்டில்","மின்னிரும் பீலி அணித்தழைக் கோட்டொடு","கண்ணிடை விடுத்த களிற்றுயிர்த் தூம்பின்","இளிப்பயிர் இமிரும் குறும்பரந் தூம்பொடு","விளிப்பது கவரும் தீங்குழல் துதைஇ","நடுவுநின் றிசைக்கும் அரிக்குரல் தட்டை","கடிகவர் பொலிக்கும் வல்வாய் எல்லரி","நொடிதரு பாணிய பதலையும் பிறவும்","கார்கோட் பலவின் காய்த்துணர் கடுப்ப","நேர்சீர் சுருக்கிக் காய கலப்பையிர்","கடுக்கலித் தெழுந்த கண்ணகன் சிலம்பில்","படுத்துவைத் தன்ன பாறை மருங்கின்","எடுத்துநிறுத் தன்ன இட்டருஞ் சிறுநெறி","தொடுத்த வாளியர் துணைபுணர் கானவர்","இடுக்கண் செய்யா தியங்குநர் இயக்கும்","அடுக்கல் மீமிசை அருப்பம் பேணாது","இடிச்சுர நிவப்பின் இயவுக்கொண் டொழுகித்"],"p":"","r":"The poem opens by addressing a bard through the gear his troupe carries, a drum tuned to the thunder of a dark rain-filled sky, a small drum, bright cymbals, a horn dressed with peacock feather, pipes that cry like an elephant, a sweet flute, a clapper and drums of many beats, packed in bags that hang like clusters of jackfruit, as the troupe climbs a rocky hill by a path as steep as a raised wall, where forest bowmen walking with their mates guide travellers without harm, and goes on over the high ridge heedless of its dangers.","n":"The bard is named by his equipment, not his person, so the troupe's craft is what qualifies it for the road and the gift; the bowmen of lines 17 and 18, who guide travellers without harm, are the first of the poem's hill folk, and they help rather than threaten.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","isai","neri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 1-20","lb":"PP10:1-20","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["திருமழை","மழை",1,"pira","simile","rain"],["முழவொ","முழவு",3,"parai","scene","drum"],["டாகுளி","ஆகுளி",3,"parai","scene","small drum"],["பாண்டில்","பாண்டில்",4,"yaazh","scene","cymbal"],["பீலி","பீலி",5,"pul","ornament","peacock feather"],["கோட்டொடு","கோடு",5,"yaazh","scene","horn"],["தூம்பின்","தூம்பு",6,"yaazh","scene","pipe"],["தீங்குழல்","குழல்",8,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["தட்டை","தட்டை",9,"yaazh","scene","clapper"],["எல்லரி","எல்லரி",10,"parai","scene","drum"],["பதலையும்","பதலை",11,"parai","scene","drum"],["பலவின்","பலா",12,"maram","simile","jackfruit"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",17,"seythi","scene","forest hunters"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:21-37":{"book":"pattupattu","num":172,"t":["தொடித்திரி வன்ன தொண்டுபடு திவவின்","கடிப்பகை யனைத்தும் கேள்வி போகாக்","குரலோர்த்துத் தொடுத்த சுகிர்புரி நரம்பின்","அரலை தீர உரீஇ வரகின்","குரல்வார்ந் தன்ன நுண்டுளை இரீஇச்","சிலம்பமை பத்தல் பசையொடு சேர்த்தி","இலங்குதுளை செறிய ஆணி முடுக்கிப்","புதுவது புனைந்த வெண்கை யாப்பமைத்துப்","புதுவது போர்த்த பொன்போற் பச்சை","வதுவை நாறும் வண்டுகமழ் ஐம்பால்","மடந்தை மாண்ட நுடங்கெழில் ஆகத்து","அடங்குமயிர் ஒழுகிய அவ்வாய் கடுப்ப","அகடுசேர்பு பொருந்தி அளவினில் திரியாது","கவடுபடக் கவைஇய சென்றுவாங் குந்தி","நுணங்கர நுவறிய நுண்ணீர் மாமைக்","களங்கனி யன்ன கதழ்ந்துகிளர் உருவின்","வணர்ந்தேந்து மருப்பின் வள்ளுயிர்ப் பேரியாழ்"],"p":"","r":"The troupe's great lute is described part by part: frets bound like twisted bangles, strings tuned by ear and rubbed clean, sound-holes as fine as an ear of millet, a bowl fitted with paste and pegs, a new white handle and a fresh cover that gleams like gold and lies like the line of soft hair on the lovely belly of a bride whose hair still smells of the wedding, its body dark as the kalam fruit and its neck curving up.","n":"The bride of lines 30 to 32 exists only to give the look of the lute's leather; Porunaratruppadai covers its lute with the same picture of a hair-line on a woman's belly, so the image reads as the bards' set way of describing their own instrument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","aatruppadai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 21-37","lb":"PP10:21-37","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["வரகின்","வரகு",24,"maram","simile","millet"],["வண்டுகமழ்","வண்டு",30,"pira","scene","bee"],["களங்கனி","களா",36,"maram","simile","kalam fruit"],["பேரியாழ்","பேரியாழ்",37,"yaazh","scene","great lute"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:38-50":{"book":"pattupattu","num":173,"t":["அமைவரப் பண்ணி அருள்நெறி திரியாது","இசைபெறு திருவின் வேத்தவை ஏற்பத்","துறைபல முற்றிய பைதீர் பாணரொடு","உயர்ந்தோங்கு பெருமலை ஊறின் றேறலின்","மதந்தபு ஞமலி நாவி நன்ன","துளங்கியல் மெலிந்த கல்பொரு சீறடிக்","கணங்கொள் தோகையிற் கதுப்பிகுத் தசைஇ","விலங்குமலைத் தமர்ந்த சேயரி நாட்டத்து","இலங்குவளை விறலியர் நிற்புறஞ் சுற்றக்","கயம்புக் கன்ன பயம்படு தண்ணிழல்","புனல்கால் கழீஇய மணல்வார் புறவில்","புலம்புவிட் டிருந்த புனிறில் காட்சிக்","கலம்பெறு கண்ணுளர் ஒக்கல் தலைவ"],"p":"","r":"Tuned in its proper mode and fit for the assembly of famous kings, the lute goes with bards who have mastered many forms, and around the leader stand the bright-bangled women dancers, their small feet worn on the hill's rocks till they are as red as the tongue of a spent hunting dog, resting with their hair let down like a flock of peacocks in a shade as cool as stepping into a pond, on a stretch of sand the stream has washed: so the speaker names the leader of the kin of performers who are given vessels as gifts.","n":"The vessels in line 50 (கலம்பெறு) are the troupe's title: they are named by what patrons give them, so the address already assumes the gift the poem will promise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","isai","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 38-50","lb":"PP10:38-50","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["ஞமலி","ஞமலி",42,"maa","simile","hunting dog"],["தோகையிற்","தோகை",44,"pul","simile","peacock"],["இலங்குவளை","வளை",46,"pira","ornament","conch bangle"],["கயம்புக்","கயம்",47,"pira","simile","pond"],["மணல்வார்","மணல்",48,"pira","scene","sand"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:51-66":{"book":"pattupattu","num":174,"t":["தூமலர் துவன்றிய கரைபொரு நிவப்பின்","மீமிசை நல்யாறு கடற்படர்ந் தாஅங்கு","யாமவ ணின்றும் வருதும் நீயிரும்","கனிபொழி கானம் கிளையொ டுணீஇய","துனைபறை நிவக்கும் புள்ளின மானப்","புனைதார்ப் பொலிந்த வண்டுபடு மார்பின்","வனைபுனை எழின்முலை வாங்கமைத் திரடோள்","மலர்போல் மழைக்கண் மங்கையர் கணவன்","முனைபாழ் படுக்கும் துன்னருந் துப்பின்","இசைநுவல் வித்தின் நசையே ருழவர்க்குப்","புதுநிறை வந்த புனலஞ் சாயல்","மதிமா றோரா நன்றுணர் சூழ்ச்சி","வின்னவில் தடக்கை மேவரும் பெரும்பூண்","நன்னன்சேய் நன்னற் படர்ந்த கொள்கையொடு","உள்ளினிர் சேறிர் ஆயிற் பொழுதெதிர்ந்த","புள்ளினிர் மன்ற எற்றாக் குறுதலின்"],"p":"","r":"As a good river with flower-heaped banks runs down to the sea, the speaker says, so we come from him, and you too, if you go with your mind on Nannan son of Nannan like a flock of birds flying fast to feed with their kin in a forest dropping fruit, go to a garlanded, bee-haunted chest, the husband of women with flower-like eyes, whose strength lays waste the enemy's front, who comes like a new flood to the farmers who sow the seed of praise, whose counsel never errs and whose great arm is skilled with the bow, and then you are birds that have met the right hour.","n":"Lines 60 and 61 make the poets farmers who sow praise and the patron the flood that fills their fields, so the gift is pictured as the harvest of the poet's own work rather than as charity; and in lines 65 and 66 the seekers' mere intention makes them birds of good omen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","eegai","nimittam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 51-66","lb":"PP10:51-66","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["நல்யாறு","யாறு",52,"pira","simile","river"],["புள்ளின","புள்",55,"pul","simile","birds"],["புனைதார்ப்","தார்",56,"maram","ornament","garland"],["மலர்போல்","மலர்",58,"maram","simile","flower"],["புனலஞ்","புனல்",61,"pira","simile","flood"],["புள்ளினிர்","புள்",66,"pul","simile","omen birds"]],"en":[["patron","நன்னன்சேய் நன்னற்",64]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:67-94":{"book":"pattupattu","num":175,"t":["ஆற்றின் அளவும் அசையுநற் புலமும்","வீற்றுவளஞ் சுரக்குஅவ நாடுபடு வல்சியும்","மலையும் சோலையும் மாபுகல் கானமும்","தொலையா னல்லிசை உலகமொடு நிற்பப்","பலர்புறங் கண்டவர் அருங்கலந் தரீஇப்","புலவோர்க்குச் சுரக்கும்அவ நீகை மாரியும்","இகழுநர்ப் பிணிக்கும் ஆற்றலும் புகழுநர்க்கு","அரசுமுழுது கொடுப்பினும் அமரா நோக்கமொடு","தூத்துளி பொழிந்த பொய்யா வானின்","வீயாது சுரக்குமவ நாள்மகி ழிருக்கையும்","நல்லோர் குழீஇய நாநவில் அவையத்து","வல்லா ராயினும் புறமறைத்துச் சென்றோரைச்","சொல்லிக் காட்டிச் சோர்வின்றி விளக்கி","நல்லிதின் இயக்குமவன் சுற்றத் தொழுக்கமும்","நீரகம் பனிக்கும் அஞ்சுவரு கடுந்திறல்","பேரிசை நவிர மேஎ யுறையும்","காரிஉண்டிக் கட வுள தியற்கையும்","பாயிருள் நீங்கப் பகல்செய்யா எழுதரு","ஞாயி றன்னவவன் வசையில் சிறப்பும்","இகந்தன ஆயினும் தெவ்வர் தேஎம்","நுகம்படக் கடந்து நூழி லாட்டிப்","புரைத்தோல் வரைப்பின் வேனிழற் புலவோர்க்குக்","கொடைக்கடன் இறுத்தவன் தொல்லோர் வரவும்","இரைதேர்ந் திவரும் கொடுந்தாள் முதலையொடு","திரைபடக் குழிந்த கல்லகழ் கிடங்கின்","வரைபுரை நிவப்பின் வான்றோய் இஞ்சி","உரைசெல வெறுத்தவன் மூதூர் மாலையும்","கேளினி வேளைநீ முன்னிய திசையே"],"p":"","r":"The speaker announces what the seeker is to hear: the length of the roads, the good places to rest, the foods of the land, its hills, groves and forests full of beasts; then the patron's fame that will stand as long as the world, the treasures he takes from those whose backs he has seen and pours like rain on poets, the strength that binds his scorners and the look that thinks nothing enough though he gave his whole kingdom to those who praise him, his daily court of joy that never fails, like a true sky pouring clean rain, the way his men in the assembly of the learned cover the failings of the unskilled and set them forth well, the nature of the god of the famous Naviram hill who ate the dark poison, his own faultless glory like the sun that rises to make the day, the line of his ancestors who conquered distant enemies and paid the debt of giving to poets, and the old town with a rock-cut moat where crocodiles hunt and walls as high as hills.","n":"Lines 77 to 80 are the poem's one view of the court from the side of the performer who fails: the patron's men cover for the unskilled and present them well, which tells the seeker the gift does not wait on a flawless performance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["eegai","pugazh","avai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 67-94","lb":"PP10:67-94","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["மாபுகல்","மா",69,"maa","scene","beasts"],["காரிஉண்டிக்","காரியுண்டிக் கடவுள்",83,"deyvam","scene","poison-eating god"],["முதலையொடு","முதலை",90,"pira","scene","crocodile"]],"en":[["hill","நவிர",82]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:95-113":{"book":"pattupattu","num":176,"t":["மிகுவளம் பழுநிய யாணர் வைப்பிற்","புதுவது வந்தன் றிதுவதன் பண்பே","வானமின்னு வசிவு பொழிய ஆனாது","இட்ட வெல்லாம் பெட்டாங்கு விளையப்","பெயலொடு வைகிய வியன்கண் இரும்புனத்து","அகலிரு விசும்பி னாஅல் போல","வாலிதின் விரிந்த புன்கொடி முசுண்டை","நீலத் தன்ன விதைப்புன மருங்கின்","மகுளி பாயாது மலிதுளி தழாலின்","அகளத் தன்ன நிறைசுனைப் புறவிற்","கெளவை போகிய கருங்காய் பிடியேழ்","நெய்கொள வொழுகின பல்கவ ரீரெண்","பொய்பொரு கயமுனி முயங்குகை கடுப்பக்","கொய்பத முற்றன குலவுக்குரல் ஏனல்","விளைதயிர்ப் பிதிர்வின் வீவுக் கிருவிதொறும்","குளிர்புரை கொடுங்காய் கொண்டன அவரை","மேதி யன்ன கல்பிறங்கு இயலின்","வாதிகை யன்ன கவைக்கதிர் இறைஞ்சி","இரும்புகவர் வுற்றன பெரும்புன வரகே"],"p":"","r":"Of the patron's rich land the speaker says a new season has come, and this is its nature: the sky flashed and rained without stint and all that was sown grew as hoped, so that on the wide wet hill fields the musundai creeper opened white like stars across the dark sky, the sown plots were blue as sapphire, the pools brimmed like pots, the sesame pods ran full of oil, the millet heads bent ready to pluck like the curling trunk of an elephant calf, the bean vines hung their cold pods, and the great fields of varagu, among rocks that lie like buffaloes, bowed their forked ears to the iron sickle.","n":"The harvest is the first proof of the patron's rule: plenty is shown before the gift itself; வாதிகை (line 112) is a rare word and the vehicle of that simile is not certain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","mazhai","uzhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 95-113","lb":"PP10:95-113","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["முசுண்டை","முசுண்டை",101,"maram","scene",""],["கயமுனி","கயமுனி",107,"maa","simile","elephant calf"],["ஏனல்","ஏனல்",108,"maram","scene","millet"],["விளைதயிர்ப்","தயிர்",109,"unavu","simile","curd"],["அவரை","அவரை",110,"maram","scene","bean"],["மேதி","மேதி",111,"maa","simile","buffalo"],["வரகே","வரகு",113,"maram","scene","millet"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:114-128":{"book":"pattupattu","num":177,"t":["பால்வார்பு கெழீஇப் பல்கவர் வளிபோழ்பு","வாலிதின் விளைந்தன ஐவன வெண்ணெல்","வேலீண்டு தொழுதி இரிவுற் றென்னக்","காலுறு துவைப்பிற் கவிழ்க்கனைத் திறைஞ்சிக்","குறையறை வாரா நிவப்பி னறையுற்று","ஆலைக் கலமருந் தீங்கழைக் கரும்பே","புயற்புனிறு போகிய பூமலி புறவின்","அவற்பதங் கொண்டன அம்பொதித் தோரை","தொய்யாது வித்திய துளர்படு துடவை","ஐயவி யமன்ற வெண்காற் செறுவின்","மையென விரிந்தன நீணறு நெய்தல்","செய்யாப் பாவை வளர்ந்துகவின் முற்றிக்","காயங் கொண்டன இஞ்சிமா விருந்து","வயவுப்பிடி முழந்தாள் கடுப்பக் குழிதொறும்","விழுமிதின் வீழ்ந்தன கொழுங்கொடிக் கவலை"],"p":"","r":"The list goes on: white hill paddy ripened in the wind, its stalks milky; sugarcane, tossed like a routed band of spearmen, bends and goes sweet to the press; thorai rice ripens in the flowered field after the rains; mustard thrives in the fields where blue lilies spread dark and fragrant; ginger and turmeric grow to their full beauty; and the rich yam vines have sunk into every pit like the knees of a pregnant she-elephant.","n":"Lines 116 to 118 (the routed spearmen) and 125 to 126 (செய்யாப் பாவை, இஞ்சிமா) can be divided more than one way, so which crop each simile describes is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadu","uzhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 114-128","lb":"PP10:114-128","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","ஆக்கம்"]],"k":[["வெண்ணெல்","நெல்",115,"maram","scene","hill paddy"],["கரும்பே","கரும்பு",119,"maram","scene","sugarcane"],["தோரை","தோரை",121,"maram","scene","rice"],["ஐயவி","ஐயவி",123,"maram","scene","mustard"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",124,"maram","scene","water lily"],["இஞ்சிமா","இஞ்சி",126,"maram","scene","ginger"],["வயவுப்பிடி","பிடி",127,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["கவலை","கவலை",128,"maram","scene","yam"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:129-144":{"book":"pattupattu","num":178,"t":["காழ்மண் டெஃகம் களிற்றுமுகம் பாய்ந்தென","ஊழ்மல ரொழிமுகை உயர்முகந் தோயத்","துறுகல் சுற்றிய சோலை வாழை","இறுகுகுலை முறுகப் பழுத்த பயம்புக்கு","ஊழுற் றலமரும் உந்தூழ் அகலறைக்","கால மன்றியும் மரம்பயன் கொடுத்தலிற்","காலின் உதிர்ந்தன கருங்கனி நாவல்","மாறுகொள வொழுகின ஊறுநீ ருயவை","நூறொடு குழீஇயின கூவை சேறுசிறந்து","உண்ணுநர்த் தடுத்தன தேமாப் புண்ணரிந்து","அரலை உக்கன நெடுந்தாள் ஆசினி","விரலூன்று படுகண் ஆகுளி கடுப்பக்","குடிஞை இரட்டு நெடுமலை அடுக்கத்துக்","கீழு மேலுங் கார்வாய்த் தெதிரிக்","கரஞ்செல் கோடியர் முழவிற் றூங்கி","முரஞ்சுகொண் டிறைஞ்சின அலங்குசினைப் பலவே"],"p":"","r":"The fruit of the hills follows: the plantain round the boulders, its bud thrust out like a spear driven into an elephant's face, ripens in tight bunches; the great bamboo sways on the wide rocks; because the trees give fruit even out of season, black jambu fruit falls in the wind, the uyavai creeper runs with water, arrowroot clusters, sweet mangoes are too many for those who eat, breadfruit sheds its seeds, and on the high slope where the owl calls like a small drum struck with a finger, the jackfruit, rained on above and below, hangs heavy from swaying boughs like the drums of wandering players.","n":"Line 134 gives the reason for the whole catalogue: the trees here give fruit out of their season, so the land's plenty is more than nature's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","undi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 129-144","lb":"PP10:129-144","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","ஆக்கம்"]],"k":[["களிற்றுமுகம்","களிறு",129,"maa","simile","elephant"],["வாழை","வாழை",131,"maram","scene","plantain"],["உந்தூழ்","உந்தூழ்",133,"maram","scene","great bamboo"],["நாவல்","நாவல்",135,"maram","scene","jambu"],["ருயவை","உயவை",136,"maram","scene",""],["கூவை","கூவை",137,"maram","scene","arrowroot"],["தேமாப்","தேமா",138,"maram","scene","sweet mango"],["ஆசினி","ஆசினி",139,"maram","scene","breadfruit"],["ஆகுளி","ஆகுளி",140,"parai","simile","small drum"],["குடிஞை","குடிஞை",141,"pul","scene","owl"],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",143,"seythi","simile","wandering players"],["பலவே","பலா",144,"maram","scene","jackfruit"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:145-157":{"book":"pattupattu","num":179,"t":["தீயி னன்ன ஒண்செங் காந்தள்","தூவற் கலித்த புதுமுகை ஊன்செத்து","அறியா தெடுத்த புன்புறச் சேவல்","ஊஉ னன்மையி னுண்ணா துகுத்தென","நெருப்பி னன்ன பல்லிதழ் தாஅய்","வெறிக்களம் கடுக்கும் வியலறை தோறும்","மணஇல் கமழு மாமலைச் சாரல்","தேனினர் கிழங்கினர் ஊனார் வட்டியர்","சிறுகட் பன்றிப் பழுதுளிப் போக்கிப்","பொருதுதொலை யானைக் கோடுசீ ராகத்","தூவொடு மலிந்த காய கானவர்","செழும்பல் யாணர்ச் சிறுகுடிப் படினே","இரும்பே ரொக்கலொடு பதமிகப் பெறுகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"On the great hill slope, where a dull-backed hawk snatched up the new bud of a fire-bright kanthal, taking it for meat, and dropped it when it proved no flesh, where the petals lie strewn like fire on every rock as if it were the ground of the veri rite and the slope smells like a wedding house, the speaker promises that if the troupe reaches the rich small village of the hunters, who carry honey, roots and baskets of meat, drive off the small-eyed boar and carry their loads on poles of tusks from elephants killed in fight, they will get food in plenty with all their kin.","n":"The hawk that drops the flower once it finds it is not flesh is a comic picture of the hill's plenty, where even the birds are fooled; the veri ground of line 150 brings Murugan's rite into the guide only as an image of colour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","undi","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 145-157","lb":"PP10:145-157","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",145,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",147,"pul","scene","hawk"],["தேனினர்","தேன்",152,"unavu","gift_food","honey"],["கிழங்கினர்","கிழங்கு",152,"unavu","gift_food","roots"],["ஊனார்","ஊன்",152,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["பன்றிப்","பன்றி",153,"maa","scene","boar"],["யானைக்","யானை",154,"maa","scene",""],["கானவர்","கானவர்",155,"seythi","scene","hunters"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:158-169":{"book":"pattupattu","num":180,"t":["அன்றவ ணசைஇ அற்சேர்ந் தல்கிக்","கன்றெரி யொள்ளிணர் கடும்பொடு மலைந்து","சேந்த செயலைச் செப்பம் போகி","அலங்குகழை நரலும் ஆரிப் படுகர்ச்","சிலம்படைந் திருந்த பாக்க மெய்தி","நோனாச் செருவின் வலம்படு நோன்றாள்","மான விறல்வேள் வயிரிய மெனினே","நும்மில் போல நில்லாது புக்குக்","கிழவிர் போலக் கேளாது கெழீஇச்","சேட்புலம் பகல இனிய கூறிப்","பரூஉக்குறை பொழிந்த நெய்க்கண் வேவையொடு","குரூஉக்க ணிறடிப் பொம்மல் பெறுகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"Staying there that day and resting the night, the troupe, wearing bright clusters of flowers with their kin, is to go on by the path of the red asoka trees to the hamlet on the slope where swaying bamboo creaks, and if they say they are musicians of the victorious Vel, the people will take them in as if it were their own house, embrace them as kin without asking who they are, speak sweet words to end the loneliness of a far country, and give them thick cuts of meat cooked in ghee and heaps of rice.","n":"The welcome is opened by a name: the strangers are taken in as kin, unasked, once they say they are the Vel's musicians (lines 163 and 164), so the hill people's hospitality is part of the patron's reach and not only their own custom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","undi","sutram","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 158-169","lb":"PP10:158-169","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["யொள்ளிணர்","இணர்",159,"maram","ornament","flower cluster"],["செயலைச்","செயலை",160,"maram","scene","asoka"],["அலங்குகழை","கழை",161,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["நெய்க்கண்","நெய்",168,"unavu","gift_food","ghee"],["வேவையொடு","வேவை",168,"unavu","gift_food","cooked meat"],["பொம்மல்","பொம்மல்",169,"unavu","gift_food","heaped rice"]],"en":[["patron","விறல்வேள்",164]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:170-188":{"book":"pattupattu","num":181,"t":["ஏறித் தரூஉம் இலங்குமலைத் தாரமொடு","வேய்ப்பெயல் விளையுள் தேக்கட் தேறல்","குறைவின்று பருகி நறவுமகிழ்ந்து வைகறைப்","பழஞ்செருக் குற்றநும் அனந்தல் தீர","அருவி தந்த பழஞ்சிதை வெண்காழ்","வருவிசை தவிர்த்த கடமான் கொழுங்குறை","முளவுமாத் தொலைச்சிய பைந்நிணப் பிளவை","பிணவுநாய் முடுக்கிய தடியொடு விரைஇ","வெண்புடைக் கொண்ட துய்த்தலைப் பழனின்","இன்புளிக் கலந்து மாமோ ராகக்","கழைவளர் நெல்லின் அரியுலை ஊழ்த்து","வழையமை சாரல் கமழத் துழைஇ","நறுமலர் அணிந்த நாறிரு முச்சிக்","குறமகள் ஆக்கிய வாலவிழ் வல்சி","அகமலி உவகை ஆர்வமொ டளைஇ","மகமுறை தடுப்ப மனைதொறும் பெறுகுவிர்","செருச்செய் முன்பிற் குருசில் முன்னிய","பரிசில் மறப்ப நீடலும் உரியிர்","அனைய தன்றவன் மலைமிசை நாடே"],"p":"","r":"With the hill's goods that the hunters climb up to fetch, the people will give the troupe honey-wine ripened in bamboo to drink without stint, and at dawn, to clear last night's heaviness, a meal of white grains the waterfall brought, fat cuts of sambar deer, slabs of porcupine fat and meat the hound ran down, cooked with sweet tamarind and buttermilk, bamboo rice stirred until the slope smells of it, and the white rice cooked by the hill woman with flowers in her hair; house after house will press it on them with full-hearted joy, holding them back as kin, so that they may stay long and even forget the gift they set out to ask of the warrior lord, for his hill country is more than this.","n":"Lines 186 to 188 turn the promise round: the danger here is not hardship but too much welcome, for the seeker may forget the gift; the next unit duly tells him not to stay many days. The closing அனைய தன்று may also be read as 'his hill country is not like that', a warning rather than a boast.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","undi","parisil","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 170-188","lb":"PP10:170-188","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["தேறல்","தேறல்",171,"unavu","gift_food","honey-wine"],["நறவுமகிழ்ந்து","நறவு",172,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["கடமான்","கடமான்",175,"maa","gift_food","sambar deer"],["முளவுமாத்","முளவுமா",176,"maa","gift_food","porcupine"],["பிணவுநாய்","நாய்",177,"maa","scene","hound"],["இன்புளிக்","புளி",179,"maram","gift_food","tamarind"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",180,"maram","gift_food","bamboo rice"],["நறுமலர்","மலர்",182,"maram","ornament","flowers"],["குறமகள்","குறமகள்",183,"seythi","scene","hill woman"],["வல்சி","வல்சி",183,"unavu","gift_food","rice"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:189-199":{"book":"pattupattu","num":182,"t":["நிரையிதழ்க் குவளைக் கடிவீ தொடினும்","வரையர மகளிர் இருக்கை காணினும்","உயிர்செல வெம்பிப் பனித்தலும் உரியிர்","பலநா ணில்லாது நிலநாடு படர்மின்","விளைபுன நிழத்தலிற் கேழல் அஞ்சிப்","புழைதொறு மாட்டிய இருங்கல் அடாடர்","அரும்பொறி உடைய வாறே நள்ளிருள்","அலரிவிரிந்த விடியல் வைகினிர் கழிமின்","நளிந்துபலர் வழங்காச் செப்பந் துணியின்","முரம்புகண் உடைந்த பரலவற் போழ்வில்","கரந்துபாம் பொடுங்கும் பயம்புமா ருளவே"],"p":"","r":"If the troupe so much as touches the fragrant blossom of the tiered kuvalai or sees the haunt of the hill goddesses, they may burn and shiver as if life were leaving, so they are not to stay many days but go on toward the plains; because the ripe fields are being raided, heavy stone traps are set for boars at every gap, so they should stop at midnight and go on at dawn, and if they choose a path few people use, there are pits in the broken gravel where snakes hide.","n":"The first warnings are about sacred ground, not beasts: the flower and the dwelling belong to the hill's goddesses, and to touch or look brings the fever of possession (பனித்தல், line 191).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["neri","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 189-199","lb":"PP10:189-199","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["குவளைக்","குவளை",189,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["வரையர","வரையர மகளிர்",190,"deyvam","scene","hill goddesses"],["கேழல்","கேழல்",193,"maa","scene","boar"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:200-210":{"book":"pattupattu","num":183,"t":["குறிக்கொண்டு மரங் கொட்டி நோக்கிச்","செறிதொடி விறலியர் கைதொழூஉப் பழிச்ச","வறிதுநெறி ஓரீஇ வலஞ்செயாக் கழிமின்","புலந்துபுனிறு போகிய புனஞ்சூழ் குறவர்","உயர்நிலை இதணம் ஏறிக் கைபுடையூஉ","அகன்மலை யிறும்பில் துவன்றிய யானைப்","பகனிலை தவிர்க்கும் கவணுமிழ் கடுங்கல்","இருவெதிர் ஈர்ங்கழை தத்திக் கல்லெனக்","கருவிர லூகம் பார்ப்போ டிரிய","உயிர்செகு மரபிற் கூற்றத் தன்ன","வரும்விசை தவிராது மரமறையாக் கழிமின்"],"p":"","r":"At the tree marked as sacred the troupe is to look, knock and, while the dancers fold their hands and praise, circle it on the right without idly leaving the path; and where hill farmers on high platforms clap their hands and sling stones to stop the elephants from lingering by day in the thicket, stones that skip off the great wet bamboo so that the black-fingered monkey flees with its young and that come with the force of Death, they must pass under the cover of the trees.","n":"The rite of lines 200 to 202 turns on rare forms (மரங் கொட்டி, வலஞ்செயா); வலஞ்செயா is read here as the old participle 'having circled to the right', parallel to மரமறையா in line 210, but it could be a negative, 'without circling'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["neri","vazhipaadu","kootru"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 200-210","lb":"PP10:200-210","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["மரங்","மரம்",200,"maram","scene","sacred tree"],["குறவர்","குறவர்",203,"seythi","scene","hill farmers"],["யானைப்","யானை",205,"maa","scene",""],["இருவெதிர்","வெதிர்",207,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["லூகம்","ஊகம்",208,"maa","scene","black monkey"],["கூற்றத்","கூற்று",209,"deyvam","simile","Death"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:211-224":{"book":"pattupattu","num":184,"t":["உரவுக்களிறு கரக்கும் இடங்கர் ஒடுங்கி","இரவின் அன்ன இருள்தூங்கு வரைப்பின்","குமிழி சுழலும் குண்டுகய முடுக்கர்","அகழ்இழிந் தன்ன கான்யாற்று நடவை","வழூஉமருங் குடைய வழாஅல் ஓம்பிப்","பரூஉக்கொடி வலந்த மதலை பற்றித்","துருவி னன்ன புன்றலை மகாரோடு","ஒருவிர் ஒருவிர் ஓம்பினிர் கழிமின்","அழுந்துபட் டலமரும் புழகமல் சாரல்","விழுந்தோர் மாய்க்குங் குண்டுகயத் தருகா","வழும்புகண் புதைத்த நுண்ணீர்ப் பாசி","அடிநிலை தளர்க்கும் அருப்பமும் உடைய","முழுநெறி பிணங்கிய நுண்கோல் வேரலோடு","எருவை மென்கோல் கொண்டனிர் கழிமின்"],"p":"","r":"Where the forest river runs like a moat through a gorge dark as night, with whirling pools where crocodiles lurk that can hide even a strong elephant, the troupe is to keep from slipping, hold on to the thick creepers that twine the tree trunks and cross one by one, each guarding the other, with their small children whose heads are tufted like a sheep's; and by the deep pools of the slope, where those who fall sink and die and fine moss over the stones makes the foot slide, they must take staves of thin reed and soft cane and go on.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["neri","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 211-224","lb":"PP10:211-224","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["உரவுக்களிறு","களிறு",211,"maa","scene","elephant"],["இடங்கர்","இடங்கர்",211,"pira","scene","crocodile"],["பரூஉக்கொடி","கொடி",216,"maram","scene","creeper"],["பாசி","பாசி",221,"maram","scene","moss"],["வேரலோடு","வேரல்",223,"maram","scene","small bamboo"],["எருவை","எருவை",224,"maram","scene","reed"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:225-233":{"book":"pattupattu","num":185,"t":["உயர்நிலை மாக்கற் புகர்முகம் புதைய","மாரியி னிகுதரு வில்லுமிழ் கடுங்கணைத்","தாரொடு பொலிந்த வினைநவில் யானைச்","சூழியிற் பொலிந்த சுடர்ப்பூ விலஞ்சி","ஓரியாற் றியவின் மூத்த புரிசைப்","பராவரு மரபிற் கடவுட் காணிற்","தொழாஅநிர் கழியின் அல்லது வறிது","நும்மியந் தொடுதல் ஓம்புமின் மயங்குதுளி","மாரி தலையுமவன் மல்லல் வெற்பே"],"p":"","r":"Where arrows from bows pour like rain over the spotted face of the high rock, where garlanded elephants trained for work stand and the pool of bright flowers is adorned like an elephant's brow, if the troupe sees the god of ancient worship at the old walls by the lone river's course, they are not to pass without bowing to him, and they must not strike their instruments idly, for such is the patron's rich hill, where the rain falls thick.","n":"The rule of lines 231 and 232 concerns the bards' own craft: before the god they must bow, and they must not sound their instruments idly, so the music that wins the gift is not to be spent carelessly on the road. Lines 225 to 228 are hard to construe; ஓரியாற்று (229) may be 'the lone river' or a river named for Ori.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","isai","neri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 225-233","lb":"PP10:225-233","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["மாரியி","மாரி",226,"pira","scene","rain"],["யானைச்","யானை",227,"maa","scene",""],["சுடர்ப்பூ","பூ",228,"maram","scene","flowers"],["கடவுட்","கடவுள்",230,"deyvam","scene","god"],["மாரி","மாரி",233,"pira","scene","rain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:234-255":{"book":"pattupattu","num":186,"t":["அலகை யன்ன வெள்வேர்ப் பீலிக்","கலவ மஞ்ஞை கட்சியில் தளரினும்","கடும்பறைக் கோடியர் மகாஅ ரன்ன","நெடுங்கழைக் கொம்பர்க் கடுவன் உகளினும்","நேர்கொள் நெடுவரை நேமியின் தொடுத்த","சூர்புகல் அடுக்கத்துப் பிரசங் காணினும்","ஞெரேரென நோக்கல் ஓம்புமின் உரித்தன்று","நிரைசெலல் மெல்லடி நெறிமாறு படுகுவிர்","வரைசேர் வகுந்திற் கானத்துப் படினே","கழுதிற் சேணோன் ஏவொடு போகி","இழுதி னன்ன வானிணஞ் செருக்கி","நிறப்புண் கூர்ந்த நிலந்தின் மருப்பின்","நெறிக்கெடக் கிடந்த இரும்பிணர் எருத்தின்","இருள்துணிந் தன்ன ஏனங் காணின்","முளிகழை இழைந்த காடுபடு தீயின்","நளிபுகை கமழா திறாயினிர் மிசைந்து","துகளறத் துணிந்த மணிமருள் தெண்ணீர்","குவளையம் பைஞ்சுனை அசைவிடப் பருகி","மிகுத்துப் பதங்கொண்ட பரூஉக்கட் பொதியினிர்","புட்கை போகிய புன்றலை மகாரோடு","அற்கிடை கழிதல் ஓம்பி ஆற்றநும்","இல்புக் கன்ன கல்லளை வதிமின்"],"p":"","r":"Even if a peacock with its eyed train falters at its nest, a male monkey leaps on the long bamboo like the children of drum-beating players, or beehives hang like a wheel on the cliff the goddesses love, the troupe is not to gape at them, for walking in a line they will lose the path; and if in the forest they find a boar that a watchman's arrow has killed, black as a cut of darkness, its fat white as ghee, lying across the path with its tusks in the earth, they may roast it in a fire of rubbed dry bamboo and eat, drink at the clear kuvalai pool, pack what is left, and, rather than travel by night with their small children, shelter in a rock cave as if going into a house.","n":"The things not to gape at are each a wonder of the hill, and the cliff of the beehives is the goddesses' (சூர்புகல்), so the warning is both practical and sacred. அலகை (234) is unclear as the vehicle for the feathers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["neri","undi","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 234-255","lb":"PP10:234-255","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"]],"k":[["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",235,"pul","scene","peacock"],["கோடியர்","கோடியர்",236,"seythi","simile","players"],["கடுவன்","கடுவன்",237,"maa","scene","male monkey"],["சூர்புகல்","சூர்",239,"deyvam","scene","goddesses"],["பிரசங்","பிரசம்",239,"pira","scene","beehive"],["சேணோன்","சேணோன்",243,"seythi","scene","watchman"],["ஏனங்","ஏனம்",247,"maa","gift_food","boar"],["முளிகழை","கழை",248,"maram","scene","dry bamboo"],["குவளையம்","குவளை",251,"maram","scene","blue lily"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:256-270":{"book":"pattupattu","num":187,"t":["அல்சேர்ந் தல்கி அசைதல் ஓம்பி","வான்கண் விரிந்த விடிய லேற்றெழுந்து","கானகப் பட்ட செந்நெறிக் கொண்மின்","கயம்கண் டன்ன அகன்பை யங்கண்","மைந்துமலி சினத்த களிறுமதன் அழிக்கும்","துஞ்சுமரங் கடுக்கும் மாசுணம் விலங்கி","இகந்துசேட் கமழும் பூவும் உண்டோர்","மறந்தமை கல்லாப் பழனும் ஊழிறந்து","பெரும்பயங் கழியினும் மாந்தர் துன்னார்","இருங்கால் வீயும் பெருமரக் குழாமும்","இடனும் வலனும் நினையினர் நோக்கிக்","குறியறிக் தவையவை குறுகாது கழிமின்","கோடுபல முரஞ்சிய கோளி யாலத்துக்","கூடியத் தன்ன குரல்புணர் புள்ளின்","நாடுகா ணனந்தலை மென்மெல அகன்மின்"],"p":"","r":"Not wearing themselves out by night, the troupe is to rise at dawn and take the straight forest path, avoiding the python that lies like a sleeping tree with a pond-wide mouth that crushes a raging elephant's strength, and the flowers whose scent carries far, the fruit too sweet for those who eat it to forget, and the clumps of great dark-stemmed trees, which the hill people never approach even when their season is past and their yield falls unused; looking left and right and knowing their signs, they are to pass these by, and move away slowly from the wide land where the birds' voices join like a crowd in a many-branched banyan.","n":"Lines 262 to 267 warn against things good in themselves, fragrant flowers and unforgettable fruit, that the people of the hill will not touch even when they fall unused: the guide's knowledge is of forbidden plenty, and the rule is to read the signs (குறி) rather than trust appetite. Line 263 (மறந்தமை கல்லா) can also be read of fruit that makes the eater forget.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["neri","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 256-270","lb":"PP10:256-270","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["களிறுமதன்","களிறு",260,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மாசுணம்","மாசுணம்",261,"pira","scene","python"],["பூவும்","பூ",262,"maram","scene","flowers"],["பழனும்","பழம்",263,"maram","scene","fruit"],["யாலத்துக்","ஆலம்",268,"maram","simile","banyan"],["புள்ளின்","புள்",269,"pul","scene","birds"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:271-286":{"book":"pattupattu","num":188,"t":["மாநிழற் பட்ட மரம்பயில் இறும்பின்","ஞாயிறு தெறாஅ மாசு நனந்தலைத்","தேஎ மருளும் அமைய மாயினும்","இறாஅவன் சிலையர் மாதேர்பு கொட்கும்","குறவரு மருளுங் குன்றத்துப் படினே","அகன்கட் பாறை துவன்றிக் கல்லென","இயங்கல் ஓம்பிநும் மியங்கள் தொடுமின்","பாடின் அருவிப் பயங்கெழு மீமிசைக்","காடுகாத் துறையுங் கானவர் உளரே","நிலைத்துறை வழீஇய மதனழி மாக்கள்","புனற்படு பூசலின் விரைந்துவல் லெய்தி","உண்டற் கினிய பழனுங் கண்டோர்","மலைதற் கினிய பூவுங் காட்டி","ஊறு நிரம்பிய ஆறவர் முந்துற","நும்மி னெஞ்சத் தவலம் வீட","இம்மென் கடும்போ டினியிர் ஆகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"If the troupe comes into the hill where even the hill farmers lose their way, a dark thicket of trees in deep shade that the sun does not burn, where bowmen circle searching for game, they are not to scatter noisily over the wide rocks but gather and sound their instruments, for on the high slopes of the singing waterfalls live hunters who guard the forest, and they, like men running at the cry of people swept from a ford by the flood, will come quickly, show them fruit sweet to eat and flowers good to wear, and lead them ahead along the dangerous way, so that the grief in their hearts ends and they and their kin are glad.","n":"The instruments the troupe was told not to strike idly at the shrine (line 232) are here its signal for help: on this road the bards' music is how they are found.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["neri","sutram","isai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 271-286","lb":"PP10:271-286","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["குறவரு","குறவர்",275,"seythi","scene","hill farmers"],["மியங்கள்","இயம்",277,"yaazh","scene","instruments"],["அருவிப்","அருவி",278,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",279,"seythi","scene","forest guards"],["பழனுங்","பழம்",282,"unavu","gift_food","fruit"],["பூவுங்","பூ",283,"maram","ornament","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:287-307":{"book":"pattupattu","num":189,"t":["அறிஞர் கூறிய மாதிரங் கைக்கொள்பு","குறியவும் நெடியவும் ஊழிழிபு புதுவோர்","நோக்கினும் பனிக்கும் நோய்கூர் அடுக்கத்து","அலர்தாய வரிநிழல் அசையினிர் இருப்பிற்","பலதிறம் பெயர்பவை கேட்குவிர் மாதோ","கலைதொடு பெரும்பழம் புண்கூர்ந் தூறலின்","மலைமுழுதுங் கமழு மாதிரந் தோறும்","அருவிய நுகரும் வானர மகளிர்","வருவிசை தவிராது வாங்குபு குடைதொறும்","தெரியிமிழ் கொண்டநும் இயம்போ லின்னிசை","இலங்கேந்து மருப்பின் இனம்பிரி ஒருத்தல்","விலங்கல் மீமிசைப் பணவைக் கானவர்","புலம்புக் குண்ணும் புரிவளைப் பூசல்","சேயளைப் பள்ளி எஃகுறு முள்ளின்","எய்தெற இழுக்கிய கானவர் அழுகை","கொடுவரி பாய்ந்தெனக் கொழுநர் மார்பின்","நெடுவசி விழுப்புண் தணிமார் காப்பென","அறல்வாழ் கூந்தற் கொடிச்சியர் பாடல்","தலைநாட் பூத்த பொன்னிணர் வேங்கை","மலைமா ரிடூஉம் ஏமப் பூசல்","கன்றரைப் பட்ட கயந்தலை மடப்பிடி"],"p":"","r":"Following the directions the knowing have given and going down the short and tall slopes in turn, on a cliff so steep that strangers shiver to look at it, if the troupe rests in the striped shade where flowers have fallen, it will hear many kinds of sound: in every quarter, where the whole hill smells of great fruit that the stag has touched oozing from their wounds, the sweet sound, like their own instruments, of the celestial women bathing in the falls; the uproar of conch-bangled women when a lone tusker strays from its herd and eats the hunters' platform field; the weeping of hunters who slipped while shooting a porcupine in its far cave; the song that the hill women with sand-dark hair sing as a guard to soothe the long deep wound a tiger tore on their husbands' chests; the protective shout raised at the golden vengai that flowered on the first day; and, as the chapter ends, the young soft-headed she-elephant caught in a pit.","n":"The catalogue sets the goddesses' bathing beside the hunters' weeping and the wives' song over tiger wounds, all as sound offered to musicians; the wives' song (lines 302 to 304) is called காப்பு, a guarding rite, the same rite of song over a wound that Purananuru 281 performs for a warrior.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["isai","naadu","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 287-307","lb":"PP10:287-307","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["கலைதொடு","கலை",292,"maa","scene","stag"],["பெரும்பழம்","பழம்",292,"maram","scene","fruit"],["வானர","வானர மகளிர்",294,"deyvam","scene","celestial women"],["ஒருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",297,"maa","scene","lone tusker"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",298,"seythi","scene","hunters"],["முள்ளின்","முள்",300,"maa","scene","porcupine quill"],["கொடுவரி","கொடுவரி",302,"maa","scene","tiger"],["கொடிச்சியர்","கொடிச்சியர்",304,"seythi","scene","hill women"],["பாடல்","பாடல்",304,"yaazh","scene","song"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",305,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["மடப்பிடி","பிடி",307,"maa","scene","young she-elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:12"},"pp:10:308-322":{"book":"pattupattu","num":190,"t":["வலிக்குவரம் பாகிய கணவன் ஓம்பலின்","ஒண்கேழ் வயப்புலி பாய்ந்தெனக் கிளையொடு","நெடுவரை இயம்பும் இடியுமிழ் தழங்குகுரல்","கைக்கோண் மறந்த கருவிரன் மந்தி","அருவிடர் வீழ்ந்ததன் கல்லாப் பார்ப்பிற்கு","முறிமே யாக்கைக் கிளையொடு துவன்றிச்","சிறுமை யுற்ற களையாப் பூசல்","கலைகை யற்ற காண்பின் நெடுவரை","நிலைபெய் திட்ட மால்புநெறி யாகப்","பெரும்பயன் தொகுத்த தேங்கொள் கொள்ளை","அருங்குறும் பெறிந்த கானவர் உவகை","திருந்துவேல் அண்ணற்கு விருந்திறை சான்மென","நறவுநாட் செய்த குறவர்தம் பெண்டிரொடு","மான்றோற் சிறுபறை கறங்கக் கல்லென","வான்றோய் மீமிசை அயருங் குரவை"],"p":"","r":"The catalogue of the hill's sounds goes on: the thunderous roar that the herd sends echoing along the high slope when a bright, strong tiger springs; the endless wailing of a troop of leaf-eating monkeys crowding round when a black-fingered mother loses her grip and her untaught young falls into a deep cleft; the glee of the forest men who, by a ladder fixed up a cliff no stag can climb, have taken a great haul of honey and, having broken a hard stronghold, say it will serve as guest-tribute for their lord of the shapely spear; and the kuravai the hill men dance with their women on the sky-touching heights after a day of brewing toddy, to the beat of the small deer-skin drum.","n":"Line 308 closes a clause begun in the previous chapter (a mate who guards, the limit of strength). Every item here is a noun of sound (தழங்குகுரல், பூசல், உவகை's shout, குரவை), all of them subjects waiting for the verb இயம்ப in line 348: the idyll's title, the 'resonance born of the mountain', is this whole inventory. The forest men's honey and fort-spoil are reserved as tribute for the lord before they are enjoyed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","naadu","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 308-322","lb":"PP10:308-322","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["வயப்புலி","வயப் புலி",309,"maa","scene","tiger"],["மந்தி","மந்தி",311,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["பார்ப்பிற்கு","பார்ப்பு",312,"maa","scene","young monkey"],["கலைகை","கலை",315,"maa","scene","stag"],["மால்புநெறி","மால்பு",316,"seythi","scene","ladder"],["தேங்கொள்","தேன்",317,"unavu","gift_food","honey"],["கானவர்","கானவர்",318,"seythi","scene","forest men"],["நறவுநாட்","நறவு",320,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["குறவர்தம்","குறவர்",320,"seythi","scene","hill men"],["சிறுபறை","சிறு பறை",321,"parai","scene","small drum"],["குரவை","குரவை",322,"yaazh","scene","round dance"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:323-339":{"book":"pattupattu","num":191,"t":["நல்லெழி னெடுந்தேர் இயவுவந் தன்ன","கல்யா றொலிக்கும் விடர்முழங் கிரங்கிசை","நெடுஞ்சுழிப் பட்ட கடுங்கண் வேழத்து","உரவுச்சினந் தணித்துப் பெருவெளிற் பிணிமார்","விரவுமொழி பயிற்றும் பாக ரோதை","ஒலிகழைத் தட்டை புடையுநர் புனந்தொறும்","கிளிகடி மகளிர் விளிபடு பூசல்","இனத்திற் றீர்ந்த துளங்கிமி னல்லேறு","மலைத்தலை வந்த மரையான் கதழ்விடை","மாறா மைந்தின் ஊறுபடத் தாக்கிக்","கோவலர் குறவரோ டொருங்கியைந் தார்ப்ப","வள்ளிதழ்க் குளவியுங் குறிஞ்சியுங் குழைய","நல்லேறு பொரூஉம் கல்லென் கம்பலை","காந்தள் துடும்பிற் கமழ்மட லோச்சி","வன்கோட் பலவின் சுளைவிளை தீம்பழம்","உண்டுபடு மிச்சிற் காழ்பயன் கொண்மார்","கன்று கடாஅ வுறுக்கு மகாஅ ரோதை"],"p":"","r":"More sounds: the deep echo in the clefts where the rocky stream roars as if a tall fine chariot were coming along the track; the cries of mahouts speaking a mixed tongue to cool the rage of a fierce elephant caught in a deep pool, so as to tie it to a great post; the shouts of girls scaring parrots from every millet plot, beating split-bamboo clappers; the clamour as a fine bull with shaking hump, strayed from its herd, clashes with unfailing strength against the eager bull of the wild cattle come down the hill, while cowherds and hill men cheer together and the kulavi and kurinji are crushed; and the noise of the boys who, brandishing fragrant kaanthal stalks, drive off the calves to get the seeds from the leftovers of the sweet jackfruit segments they have eaten.","n":"The mahouts' விரவுமொழி, a speech of mixed words, is one of the few signs in the idyll of the many tongues of the hill country. The bull fight brings together the herders of the slopes (கோவலர்) and the hill men (குறவர்) in one cheer: the patron's land is shown as a meeting of peoples.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 323-339","lb":"PP10:323-339","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["கல்யா","கல் யாறு",324,"pira","scene","rocky stream"],["வேழத்து","வேழம்",325,"maa","scene","elephant"],["பாக","பாகர்",327,"seythi","scene","mahouts"],["ஒலிகழைத்","கழை",328,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["தட்டை","தட்டை",328,"seythi","scene","bird-clapper"],["கிளிகடி","கிளி",329,"pul","scene","parrot"],["னல்லேறு","நல் ஏறு",330,"maa","scene","bull"],["மரையான்","மரை ஆன்",331,"maa","scene","wild cow"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",333,"seythi","scene","cowherds"],["குறவரோ","குறவர்",333,"seythi","scene","hill men"],["குளவியுங்","குளவி",334,"maram","scene","wild jasmine"],["குறிஞ்சியுங்","குறிஞ்சி",334,"maram","scene","kurinji plant"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",336,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["பலவின்","பலா",337,"maram","gift_food","jackfruit"],["கன்று","கன்று",339,"maa","scene","calves"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:340-351":{"book":"pattupattu","num":192,"t":["மழைகண் டன்ன ஆலைதொறு ஞெரேரெனக்","கழைகண் ணுடைக்குங் கரும்பி னேத்தமும்","தினைகுறு மகளிர் இசைபடு வள்ளையும்","சேம்பு மஞ்சளும் ஓம்பினர் காப்போர்","பன்றிப் பறையுங் குன்றகச் சிலம்பும்","என்றிவ் வனைத்தும் இயைந்தொருங் கீண்டி","அவலவு மிசையவுந் துவன்றிப் பலவுடன்","அலகைத் தவிர்த்த எண்ணருந் திறத்த","மலைபடு கடாஅ மாதிரத் தியம்பக்","குரூஉக்கட் பிணையல் கோதை மகளிர்","முழவுத்துயில் அறியா வியலு ளாங்கண்","விழவின் அற்றவன் வியன்கண் வெற்பே"],"p":"","r":"And the work-song of the sugar cane that suddenly bursts its joints in every mill, the vallai song of the girls pounding millet, the drums and the hill echoes of those who guard the taro and turmeric from boars: all these together, gathered in the hollows and on the heights, past counting, make the resonance of the mountain sound in every direction; his wide hill, where the women wear garlands of coloured flowers and the drum never sleeps in the broad town, is like a festival.","n":"Line 348 is the line that names the poem: மலைபடு கடாஅம், the resonance born of the mountain, compared by its very word (கடாம், the musth of an elephant) to a living beast. The whole list from the previous chapter to here is the subject of இயம்ப. The vehicle of line 340 is uncertain: the mills may be likened to rain clouds for their noise or for the steam over them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","naadu","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 340-351","lb":"PP10:340-351","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["ஆலைதொறு","ஆலை",340,"seythi","scene","cane mill"],["கரும்பி","கரும்பு",341,"maram","scene","sugar cane"],["தினைகுறு","தினை",342,"unavu","scene","millet"],["வள்ளையும்","வள்ளை",342,"yaazh","scene","pounding song"],["சேம்பு","சேம்பு",343,"maram","scene","taro"],["மஞ்சளும்","மஞ்சள்",343,"maram","scene","turmeric"],["பன்றிப்","பன்றி",344,"maa","scene","boar"],["பறையுங்","பறை",344,"parai","scene","drum"],["பிணையல்","பிணையல்",349,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கோதை","கோதை",349,"maram","ornament","garland"],["முழவுத்துயில்","முழவு",350,"parai","scene","drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:352-372":{"book":"pattupattu","num":193,"t":["கண்ண் டண்ண்னெனக் கண்டுங் கேட்டுங்","உண்டற் கினிய பலபா ராட்டியும்","இன்னும் வருவ தாக நமக்கெனத்","தொன்முறை மரபினி ராகிப் பன்மாண்","செருமிக்குப் புகலுந் திருவார் மார்பன்","உருமுரறு கருவிய பெருமலை பிற்பட","இறும்பூது கஞலிய இன்குரல் விறலியர்","நறுங்கார் அடுக்கத்துக் குறிஞ்சி பாடிக்","கைதொழூஉப் பரவிப் பழிச்சினிர் கழிமின்","மைபடு மாமலை பனுவலிற் பொங்கிக்","கைதோய் வன்ன கார்மழைத் தொழுதி","தூஉ யன்ன துவலை தூற்றலின்","தேஎந் தேறாக் கடும்பரிக் கடும்பொடு","காஅய்க் கொண்டநும் இயந்தொய் படாமற்","கூவல் அன்ன விடரகம் புகுமின்","இருங்கல் இகுப்பத் திறுவரை சேராது","குன்றிடம் பட்ட ஆரிடர் அழுவத்து","நின்று நோக்கினும் கண்வாள் வெளவும்","மண்கனை முழவின் தலைக்கோல் கொண்டு","தண்டுகா லாகத் தளர்தல் ஓம்பி","ஊன்றினிர் கழிமின் ஊறுதவப் பலவே"],"p":"","r":"Having looked and listened till your eyes are cooled, and praised the many things sweet to eat, saying 'there is more to come for us', you who keep the old custom should leave behind the great thundering mountain of the lord whose fortunate chest loves battle, let your viraliyar sing kurinji on the fragrant rainy slope, worship it with joined hands and go on; when the flock of rain clouds, so low on the dark hill that a hand could touch it, sprays its fine drizzle, enter clefts like wells so that your kin, who cannot tell the way, keep the instruments they carry from harm; and in the gorge where boulders roll and even to stand and look makes the eyes swim, keep away from the broken cliff, use the head-stick of your earth-smeared drum as a staff, guard against slipping and lean on it as you go, for the dangers are very many.","n":"Leaving the mountain is itself a rite: the troupe sings the mountain's own mode, kurinji, and bows to it before going. The care in the instructions is for the instruments as much as for the people: the drum's stick becomes a walking staff, so the bard's craft is what carries him through the danger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","vazhipaadu","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 352-372","lb":"PP10:352-372","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["விறலியர்","விறலி",358,"seythi","scene","dancing women"],["குறிஞ்சி","குறிஞ்சி",359,"yaazh","scene","kurinji mode"],["கார்மழைத்","கார் மழை",362,"pira","scene","rain clouds"],["துவலை","துவலை",363,"pira","scene","drizzle"],["இயந்தொய்","இயம்",365,"yaazh","scene","instruments"],["கூவல்","கூவல்",366,"pira","simile","well"],["முழவின்","முழவு",370,"parai","scene","drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:373-389":{"book":"pattupattu","num":194,"t":["அயில்காய்ந் தன்ன கூர்ங்கற் பாறை","வெயில்புறந் தரூஉம் இன்னல் இயக்கத்துக்","கதிர்சினந் தணிந்த அமயத்துக் கழிமின்","உரைசெல வெறுத்தவவ னீங்காச் சுற்றமொடு","புரைதவ உயரிய மழைமருள் பஃறோல்","அரசுநிலை தளர்க்கும் அருப்பமும் உடைய","பின்னி யன்ன பிணங்கரி னுழைதொறும்","முன்னோன் வாங்கிய கடுவிசைக் கணைக்கோல்","இன்னிசை நல்யாழ்ப் பத்தரும் விசிபிணி","மண்ணார் முழவின் கண்ணு மோம்பிக்","கைபிணி விடாஅது பைபயக் கழிமின்","களிறுமலைந் தன்ன கண்கூடு துறுகல்","தளிபொழி கானந் தலைதவப் பலவே","ஒன்னாத் தெவ்வர் உலைவிடத் தார்த்தென","நல்வழிக் கொடுத்த நாணுடை மறவர்","செல்லா நல்லிசைப் பெயரொடு நட்ட","கல்லேசு கவலை எண்ணுமிகப் பலவே"],"p":"","r":"Over the path where rocks sharp as heated spearheads are guarded by the sun, travel only when the rays' heat has eased; his land has strongholds that shake kings from their footing, with his retinue that never leaves him, whose fame has spread, and many shields raised high like clouds; at every opening of the thickets tangled like braids, keep from the swift-springing shaft the man ahead has drawn, guard the bowl of your sweet yaazh and the stretched head of your drum, keep your grip, and go slowly; boulders packed together like fighting elephants stand in the dripping forest beyond counting, and at the forks of the path there are countless stones planted with the names of undying fame of the honourable warriors who gave themselves on the good path when the hostile foe was routed with a shout.","n":"The shaft of line 380 is most naturally the branch the walker in front has bent aside, which springs back like an arrow; the reading is not certain. The hero-stones do not mark danger, as they do on the akam roads: they carry names (பெயரொடு நட்ட), and the guide counts them as part of the land's glory. Hour is a precaution: cross the hot rock only when the sun has weakened.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","nadukal","aran","idukkan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 373-389","lb":"PP10:373-389","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["யாழ்ப்","யாழ்",381,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["முழவின்","முழவு",382,"parai","scene","drum"],["களிறுமலைந்","களிறு",384,"maa","simile","elephant"],["மறவர்","மறவர்",387,"seythi","scene","warriors"]],"en":[["herostone","கல்லேசு",389]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:390-402":{"book":"pattupattu","num":195,"t":["இன்புறு முரற்கைநும் பாட்டுவிருப் பாகத்","தொன்றொழுகு மரபினும் மருப்பிகுத்துத் துனைமின்","பண்டுநற் கறியாப் புலம்பெயர் புதுவிர்","சந்து நீவிப் புன்முடிந் திடுமின்","செல்லுந் தேஎத்துப் பெயர்மருங் கறிமார்","கல்லெறிந் தெழுதிய நல்லரை மராஅத்து","கடவு ளோங்கிய காடேசு கவலை","ஒட்டா தகன்ற ஒன்னாத் தெவ்வர்","சுட்டினும் பனிக்குஞ் சுரந்தவப் பலவே","தேம்பாய் கண்ணித் தேர்வீசு கவிகை","ஓம்பா வள்ளற் படர்ந்திகும் எனினே","மேம்பட வெறுத்தவன் தொஃறிணை மூதூர்","ஆங்கன மற்றே நம்ம னோர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"As your songs please, keep the ancient custom, lower the neck of your instrument and hurry on; you newcomers who have never known this land should tie knots of grass where the ways cross, so as to know the names of the places you are bound for; there are many forks of the forest path where the god stands high in the kadamba with a fine trunk on which letters have been scratched with stones, and many wastes that the foes who fled without joining battle tremble even to mention; if you say you are going to the generous lord with the honey-dripping garland, whose hand flings away chariots and who does not hoard, his ancient town of old lineage is that way, so it is for people like us.","n":"The tree with letters scratched on it (கல்லெறிந் தெழுதிய, 395) is a signpost: writing on the road, an early witness to letters used for travellers. Lines 391 (மருப்பிகுத்து) and 401 to 402 are hard: what is lowered may be the horn-neck of the yaazh, and தொஃறிணை and ஆங்கனம் அற்றே are read here as 'of old lineage' and 'it is that way', which I cannot confirm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","vazhipaadu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 390-402","lb":"PP10:390-402","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["புன்முடிந்","புல்",393,"maram","scene","grass"],["மராஅத்து","மராஅம்",395,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["கடவு","கடவுள்",396,"deyvam","scene","god"],["கண்ணித்","கண்ணி",399,"maram","ornament","garland"],["தேர்வீசு","தேர்",399,"seythi","gift_food","chariot"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:403-417":{"book":"pattupattu","num":196,"t":["அசைவுழி யசைஇ அஞ்சாது கழிமின்","புலியுற வெறுத்ததன் வீழ்பிணை யுள்ளிக்","கலைநின்று விளிக்குங் கானம் ஊழிறந்து","சிலையொலி வெரீஇய செங்கண் மரைவிடை","தலையிரும்பு கதழும் நாறுகொடிப் புறவின்","வேறுபுலம் படர்ந்த ஏறுடை இனத்த","வளையான் தீம்பால் மிளைசூழ் கோவலர்","வளையோர் உவப்பத் தருவனர் சொரிதலின்","பலம்பெறு நசையொடு பதிவயிற் றீர்ந்தநும்","புலம்புசேண் அகலப் புதுவிர் ஆகுவிர்","பகர்விரவு நெல்லின் பலவரி யன்ன","தகர்விரவு துருவை வெள்ளையொடு விரைஇக்","கல்லென் கடத்திடைக் கடலின் இரைக்கும்","பல்யாட் டினநிரை எல்லினிர் புகினே","பாலும் மிதவையும் பண்ணாது பெறுகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"Rest where you tire and go on without fear: beyond the forest where the stag stands calling, remembering the beloved doe the tiger came for, and past the mullai country of fragrant creepers where the red-eyed bull of the wild cattle, frightened by the sound of bows, rushes into the thicket, the cowherds of the hedged land, whose herds with their bulls have gone to other pastures, will pour out the sweet milk of their white cows to the joy of the bangled women, and the loneliness of leaving home in hope of gain will go far from you, and you will be like new; if you come in at nightfall, where the many flocks of rams, ewes and white goats, mixed like the rows of grain at a barter, bleat like the sea in the noisy waste, you will get milk and porridge without asking.","n":"The stag calling for the doe the tiger took is an image akam poets use for loss, but here it is only the edge of a forest the troupe passes on its way to milk and porridge; in a puram guide poem it carries no hidden sense. The bards' homesickness (புலம்பு) is cured by food, not by return.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aatruppadai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 403-417","lb":"PP10:403-417","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["புலியுற","புலி",404,"maa","scene","tiger"],["வீழ்பிணை","பிணை",404,"maa","scene","doe"],["கலைநின்று","கலை",405,"maa","scene","stag"],["மரைவிடை","மரை விடை",406,"maa","scene","wild bull"],["நாறுகொடிப்","கொடி",407,"maram","scene","creepers"],["ஏறுடை","ஏறு",408,"maa","scene","bull"],["வளையான்","வளை ஆன்",409,"maa","gift_food","white cow"],["தீம்பால்","பால்",409,"unavu","gift_food","milk"],["கோவலர்","கோவலர்",409,"seythi","scene","cowherds"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",413,"unavu","simile","paddy"],["தகர்விரவு","தகர்",414,"maa","scene","ram"],["துருவை","துருவை",414,"maa","scene","ewe"],["வெள்ளையொடு","வெள்ளை",414,"maa","scene","white goat"],["கடலின்","கடல்",415,"pira","simile","sea"],["பல்யாட்","யாடு",416,"maa","scene","sheep"],["பாலும்","பால்",417,"unavu","gift_food","milk"],["மிதவையும்","மிதவை",417,"unavu","gift_food","porridge"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:418-427":{"book":"pattupattu","num":197,"t":["துய்ம்மயிர் அடக்கிய சேக்கை யன்ன","மெய்யுரித் தியற்றிய மிதியதட் பள்ளித்","தீத்துணை யாகச் சேந்தனிர் கழிமின்","கூப்பிடு கடக்குங் கூர்நல் லம்பிற்","கொடுவிற் கூளியர் கூவை காணிற்","படியோர்த் தேய்த்த பணிவில் ஆண்மைக்","கொடியோள் கணவற் படர்ந்திகு மெனினே","தடியுங் கிழங்குந் தண்டினர் தரீஇ","ஓம்புநர் அல்ல துடற்றுநர் இல்லை","ஆங்குவியங் கொண்மின் அதுவதன் பண்பே"],"p":"","r":"Sleep on beds of trampled hide flayed from beasts, soft as a mattress packed with fine hair, with the fire for company, and go on; if you meet a band of curved-bow Kooliyar, whose sharp arrows outrun a shout, and say you are going to the husband of the creeper-slender lady, whose unbowing manhood wore down those who stood against him, they will gather meat and roots and bring them to you: there they are guardians, not attackers; go your way, for that is their nature.","n":"The patron is named only through his wife (கொடியோள் கணவன்) and his manhood, yet saying it is a safe-conduct: men whose arrows are faster than a call for help become providers. The fear the bowmen would raise is called up only to be cancelled (உடற்றுநர் இல்லை), so no meyppadu is shown.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","virundhu","adaikkalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 418-427","lb":"PP10:418-427","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["தீத்துணை","தீ",420,"pira","scene","fire"],["லம்பிற்","அம்பு",421,"seythi","scene","arrow"],["கொடுவிற்","வில்",422,"seythi","scene","bow"],["கூளியர்","கூளியர்",422,"seythi","scene","bowmen"],["தடியுங்","தடி",425,"unavu","gift_food","meat"],["கிழங்குந்","கிழங்கு",425,"unavu","gift_food","roots"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:428-443":{"book":"pattupattu","num":198,"t":["தேம்பட மலர்ந்த மராஅமெல் லிணரும்","உம்பல் அகைத்த ஒண்முறி யாவும்","தளிரொடு மிடைந்த காமரு கண்ணி","திரங்குமர னாரிற் பொலியச் சூடி","முரம்புகண் ணுடைந்த நடவை தண்ணென","உண்டனிர் ஆடிக் கொண்டனிர் கழிமின்","செவ்வீ வேங்கைப் பூவி னன்ன","வேய்கொள் அரிசி மிதவை சொரிந்த","சுவல்விளை நெல்லின் அவரையம் பைங்கூழ்","அற்கிடை உழந்தநும் வருத்தம் வீட","அகலு ளாங்கட் கழிமிடைந் தியற்றிய","புல்வேய் குரம்பைக் குடிதொறும் பெறுகுவிர்","பொன்னறைந் தன்ன நுண்ணேர் அரிசி","வெண்ணெறிந் தியற்றிய மாக்கண் அமலை","தண்ணெ ணுண்ணிழு துள்ளீ டாக","அசையினிர் சேப்பின் அல்கலும் பெறுகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"Wearing lovely garlands of the soft honey-blooming kadamba clusters and all the bright sprouts the elephant has broken, woven with young leaves and tied with dried fibre, eat, bathe and take what you want on the path through the broken gravel that is cool there, and go on; in every grass-roofed hut built with crowded poles in the wide hollow, so that the toil of your night's labour ends, you will get porridge of bamboo rice red as vengai flowers, and green pulse of avarai with paddy grown on the ridge; and if you rest and stay you will get every day rice fine as beaten gold, cooked in white dense balls with cool fine ghee inside.","n":"The garland of line 430 is made from what an elephant has left (உம்பல் அகைத்த): the troupe adorns itself with the forest's leftovers. The meals rise in richness as the stay lengthens, from roadside eating to porridge in every hut to ghee-filled rice every day.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 428-443","lb":"PP10:428-443","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["மராஅமெல்","மராஅம்",428,"maram","ornament","kadamba"],["உம்பல்","உம்பல்",429,"maa","scene","elephant"],["ஒண்முறி","முறி",429,"maram","ornament","sprouts"],["தளிரொடு","தளிர்",430,"maram","ornament","young leaves"],["வேங்கைப்","வேங்கை",434,"maram","simile","kino tree"],["வேய்கொள்","வேய் அரிசி",435,"unavu","gift_food","bamboo rice"],["மிதவை","மிதவை",435,"unavu","gift_food","porridge"],["நெல்லின்","நெல்",436,"unavu","gift_food","paddy"],["அவரையம்","அவரை",436,"unavu","gift_food","beans"],["புல்வேய்","புல்",439,"maram","scene","thatch grass"],["அரிசி","அரிசி",440,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["அமலை","அமலை",441,"unavu","gift_food","rice balls"],["ணுண்ணிழு","இழுது",442,"unavu","gift_food","ghee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:444-453":{"book":"pattupattu","num":199,"t":["விசையங் கொழித்த பூழி யன்ன","உண்ணுநர்த் தடுத்த நுண்ணிடி நுவணை","நொய்ம்மர விறகின் ஞெகிழி மாட்டிப்","பனிசேண் நீங்க இனிதுடன் துஞ்சிப்","புலரி விடியற் புள்ளோர்த்துக் கழிமின்","புல்லரைக் காஞ்சிப் புனல்பொரு புதவின்","மெல்லவ லிருந்த ஊர்தொறு நல்லியாழ்ப்","பண்ணுப்பெயர்த் தன்ன காவும் பள்ளியும்","பன்னா ணிற்பினும் சேந்தனிர் செலினும்","நன்பல வுடைத்தவன் தண்பணை நாடே"],"p":"","r":"Lighting a blaze of light wood, eat the fine millet flour, like dust sifted from sugar, that holds back other eaters, sleep sweetly together far from the cold, and at dawn take the birds' omens and go on; whether you stay many days or rest and go, his cool fertile country has much good, with groves and lodges, varied as the modes changed on a fine yaazh, in every town on the soft low ground beside sluices struck by water where the thin-trunked kaanji grows.","n":"The simile of line 451 is a musician's: passing from town to town is like a bard changing the mode on his yaazh, so the landscape is heard as a performance. Line 445's உண்ணுநர்த் தடுத்த is read as a flour so filling or so sweet that it stops the eaters; the sense is not certain. The country now leaves the hills for the watered low ground.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","naadu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 444-453","lb":"PP10:444-453","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[],"k":[["நுவணை","நுவணை",445,"unavu","gift_food","millet flour"],["விறகின்","விறகு",446,"maram","scene","firewood"],["ஞெகிழி","ஞெகிழி",446,"pira","scene","blaze"],["புள்ளோர்த்துக்","புள்",448,"pul","scene","omen birds"],["காஞ்சிப்","காஞ்சி",449,"maram","scene","kaanji tree"],["புனல்பொரு","புனல்",449,"pira","scene","water"],["நல்லியாழ்ப்","யாழ்",450,"yaazh","simile","lute"],["காவும்","கா",451,"maram","scene","groves"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:454-464":{"book":"pattupattu","num":200,"t":["கண்புமலி பழனங் கமழத் துழைஇ","வலையோர் தந்த இருஞ்சுவல் வாளை","நிலையோர் இட்ட நெடுநாண் தூண்டிற்","பிடிக்கை யன்ன செங்கண் வராஅல்","துடிக்கண் அன்ன குறையொடு விரைஇப்","பகன்றைக் கண்ணிப் பழையர் மகளிர்","ஞெண்டாடு செறுவிற் றராய்க்கண் வைத்த","விலங்கல் அன்ன போர்முதற் றொலைஇ","வளஞ்செய் வினைஞர் வல்சி நல்கத்","துளங்குதசும்பு வாக்கிய பசும்பொதித் தேறல்","இளங்கதிர் ஞாயிற்றுக் களங்கடொறும் பெறுகுவிர்"],"p":"","r":"In the fields fragrant with kanpu reeds, the women of the Pazhaiyar, wearing pakandrai garlands, set on the raised bank of a crab-haunted plot the dark-backed vaalai the net-men bring and the red-eyed varaal, like an elephant cow's trunk, taken on the long lines of men who stand to fish, mixed with cut pieces round as the heads of hand drums; and when the workers who make the land rich have finished threshing the hill-like stacks and give out their food, you will get at every threshing floor, in the young sun of morning, toddy from fresh packets poured out of swaying jars.","n":"Line 460 is a repaired reading (தராய், a raised bank). The workers' gift is made at the moment of harvest: the bard arrives when the land is richest and is fed from the threshing floor itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","uzhavu","kal","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 454-464","lb":"PP10:454-464","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["கண்புமலி","கண்பு",454,"maram","scene","reed"],["வலையோர்","வலையோர்",455,"seythi","scene","net-men"],["வாளை","வாளை",455,"pira","gift_food","vaalai fish"],["தூண்டிற்","தூண்டில்",456,"seythi","scene","fishhook"],["பிடிக்கை","பிடி",457,"maa","simile","elephant cow"],["வராஅல்","வரால்",457,"pira","gift_food","varaal fish"],["துடிக்கண்","துடி",458,"parai","simile","hand drum"],["பகன்றைக்","பகன்றை",459,"maram","ornament","pakandrai flower"],["பழையர்","பழையர்",459,"seythi","scene","toddy-folk"],["ஞெண்டாடு","ஞெண்டு",460,"pira","scene","crab"],["வினைஞர்","வினைஞர்",462,"seythi","scene","farm workers"],["வல்சி","வல்சி",462,"unavu","gift_food","food"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",463,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:465-477":{"book":"pattupattu","num":201,"t":["முள்ளரித் தியற்றிய வெள்ளரி வெண்சோறு","வண்டுபடக் கமழுந் தேம்பாய் கண்ணித்","திண்டேர் நன்னற்கும் அயினி சான்மெனக்","கண்டோர் மருளக் கடும்புடன் அருந்தி","எருதெறி களமர் ஓதையொடு நல்யாழ்","மருதம் பண்ணி அசையினிர் கழிமின்","வெண்ணெல் அரிநர் தண்ணுமை வெரீஇச்","செங்கண் எருமை இனம்பிரி ஒருத்தல்","கனைசெலல் முன்பொடு கதழ்ந்துவரல் போற்றி","வனைகலத் திகிரியிற் குமிழி சுழலும்","துனைசெலற் றலைவாய் ஓவிறந் தொலிக்கும்","காணுநர் வயாஅங் கட்கின் சேயாற்றின்","யாணர் ஒருகரைக் கொண்டனிர் கழிமின்"],"p":"","r":"Eat with your troupe, to the wonder of all who see, white rice cooked with cucumber whose spines are scraped off, food you will say is fit even for Nannan of the strong chariot whose honey-dripping garland is fragrant with bees; then rest, playing marutham on your fine yaazh to the shouts of the field-hands driving their oxen, and go on; watching for the charge of the lone bull buffalo, strayed from its red-eyed herd in fright at the drums of the reapers of white paddy, take the one rich bank of the Seyaru, pleasant to the eye and longed for by those who see it, whose swift head-current roars without pause where bubbles whirl like a potter's wheel, and go on.","n":"As the troupe played kurinji when leaving the hill (359), here it plays marutham among the fields: the bards change mode with the land. வெள்ளரி may be white grain rather than cucumber; the scraped spines (முள்ளரித்து) favour the cucumber.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","virundhu","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 465-477","lb":"PP10:465-477","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"],["accham","விலங்கு"]],"k":[["வெள்ளரி","வெள்ளரி",465,"unavu","gift_food","cucumber"],["வெண்சோறு","சோறு",465,"unavu","gift_food","rice"],["கண்ணித்","கண்ணி",466,"maram","ornament","garland"],["எருதெறி","எருது",469,"maa","scene","oxen"],["களமர்","களமர்",469,"seythi","scene","field-hands"],["நல்யாழ்","யாழ்",469,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["மருதம்","மருதம்",470,"yaazh","scene","marutham mode"],["வெண்ணெல்","வெண்ணெல்",471,"unavu","scene","white paddy"],["அரிநர்","அரிநர்",471,"seythi","scene","reapers"],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",471,"parai","scene","drum"],["எருமை","எருமை",472,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["ஒருத்தல்","ஒருத்தல்",472,"maa","scene","lone bull"],["திகிரியிற்","திகிரி",474,"seythi","simile","potter's wheel"],["குமிழி","குமிழி",474,"pira","scene","bubbles"]],"en":[["patron","நன்னற்கும்",467],["river","சேயாற்றின்",476]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:478-491":{"book":"pattupattu","num":202,"t":["நிதியந் துஞ்சு நிவந்தோங்கு வரைப்பிற்","பதியெழ லறியாப் பழங்குடி கெழீஇ","வியலிடம் பெறாஅ விழுப்பெரு நியமத்து","யாறெனக் கிடந்த செருவிற் சாறென","இகழுநர் வெரூஉங் கவலை மறுகிற்","கடலெனக் காரென ஒலிக்குஞ் சும்மையொடு","மலையென மழையென மாட மோங்கித்","துனிதீர் காதலின் இனிதமர்ந் துறையும்","பனிவார் காவிற் பல்வண் டிமிரும்","நனிசேய்த் தன்றவன் பழவிறன் மூதூர்","பொருந்தாத் தெவ்வர் இருந்தலை துமியப்","பருந்துபடக் கடக்கும் ஒள்வாண் மறவர்","கருங்கடை எஃகஞ் சாத்திய புதவின்","அருங்கடி வாயில் அயிராது புகுமின்"],"p":"","r":"His ancient town of old valour is not far: within its high rising enclosure where wealth lies asleep, old families who never leave their place live together; its great market is too crowded for its wide space, its ways lie spread like a river, its festival is like a battle, its lanes forking so that scorners fear them; with a din like the sea and like thunder, mansions rising like mountains and like clouds, people dwelling sweetly in a love that knows no quarrel, and cool groves loud with bees; enter without doubting the closely guarded gate whose doors bear the black-butted spears leaned there by bright-sworded warriors who cut off the heads of hostile foes so that kites gather.","n":"The town is built of two opposites held together: a market crowded like a battle and people living in love without quarrel; the gate is armed against foes and open to bards (அயிராது புகுமின்). Line 481's செருவிற் சாறென ('a festival like a battle') is odd after யாறெனக் கிடந்த, which would suit தெருவின் (streets).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aatruppadai","aran","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 478-491","lb":"PP10:478-491","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["பல்வண்","வண்டு",486,"pira","scene","bees"],["காவிற்","கா",486,"maram","scene","groves"],["மறவர்","மறவர்",489,"seythi","scene","warriors"],["பருந்துபடக்","பருந்து",489,"pul","scene","kites"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:492-513":{"book":"pattupattu","num":203,"t":["மன்றில் வதியுநர் சேட்புலப் பரிசிலர்","வெல்போர்ச் சேஎய்ப் பெருவிற லுள்ளி","வந்தோர் மன்ற அளியர் தாமெனக்","கண்டோ ரெல்லாம் அமர்ந்தினிது நோக்கி","விருந்திறை அவரவர் எதிர்கொளக் குறுகிப்","பரிபுலம் பலைத்தநும் வருத்தம் வீட","எரிகான் றன்ன பூஞ்சினை மராஅத்துத்","தொழுதி போக வலிந்தகப் பட்ட","மடநடை ஆமான் கயமுனிக் குழவி","ஊமை எண்கின் குடாவடிக் குருளை","மீமிசைக் கொண்ட கவர்பரிக் கொடுந்தாள்","வரைவாழ் வருடை வன்றலை மாத்தகர்","அரவுக்குறும் பெறிந்த சிறுகண் தீர்வை","அளைச்செறி உழுவை கோளுற வெறுத்த","மடக்கண் மரையான் பெருஞ்செவிக் குழவி","அரக்குவிரித் தன்ன செந்நில மருங்கிற்","பரற்றவழ் உடும்பின் கொடுந்தாள் ஏற்றை","வரைப்பொலிந் தியலும் மடக்கண் மஞ்ஞை","கானக் கோழிக் கவர்குரற் சேவல்","கானப் பலவின் முழவுமருள் பெரும்பழம்","இடிக்கலப் பன்ன நறுவடி மாவின்","வடிச்சேறு விளைந்த தீம்பழத் தாரம்"],"p":"","r":"In the common ground the suppliants from far lands staying there, remembering the great valour of the red lord who wins his wars, will say 'surely those who have come deserve pity'; all who see you look on you sweetly and gladly, and each comes to meet you with a guest's welcome, so that the toil the long road has beaten into you may end; and there crowd the gifts of the hill: the calf of the gentle-gaited forest cow caught when its herd fled the kadamba whose flowered boughs seem to spit fire, the curve-pawed cub of the silent bear, the great strong-headed ram of the cliff-dwelling varudai with crooked legs and leaping gait, the small-eyed creature that kills the snakes in their holes, the big-eared calf of the soft-eyed wild cow seized by the tiger that lurks in its den, the crooked-legged male monitor that crawls on gravel by ground red as spread lac, the soft-eyed peacock that goes gracing the hill, the forest cock with its forked call, the great drum-like fruits of the forest jack, and the sweet ripe fruit of the fragrant mango, full of juice.","n":"The welcome is a look before it is a gift: the whole town 'looks sweetly' (அமர்ந்தினிது நோக்கி) on the newcomers. The gifts are the hill's own wild young, caught alive, and its fruits: the patron gives his land, not only his treasury. சேஎய் (493) calls the patron by Murugan's epithet, the red one. தீர்வை (504) is a creature I cannot identify, and the simile of 512 (இடிக்கலப்பு) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","parisil","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 492-513","lb":"PP10:492-513","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["மராஅத்துத்","மராஅம்",498,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["ஆமான்","ஆமான்",500,"maa","gift_food","forest cow"],["எண்கின்","எண்கு",501,"maa","gift_food","bear"],["வருடை","வருடை",503,"maa","gift_food","mountain goat"],["மாத்தகர்","தகர்",503,"maa","gift_food","great ram"],["அரவுக்","அரவு",504,"pira","scene","snake"],["தீர்வை","தீர்வை",504,"maa","gift_food","unidentified beast"],["உழுவை","உழுவை",505,"maa","scene","tiger"],["மரையான்","மரை ஆன்",506,"maa","gift_food","wild cow"],["உடும்பின்","உடும்பு",508,"maa","gift_food","monitor lizard"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",509,"pul","gift_food","peacock"],["கோழிக்","கோழி",510,"pul","gift_food","forest fowl"],["பலவின்","பலா",511,"maram","gift_food","jackfruit"],["மாவின்","மா",512,"maram","gift_food","mango"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:514-531":{"book":"pattupattu","num":204,"t":["தூவற் கலித்த இவர்நனை வளர்கொடி","காஅய்க் கொண்ட நுகமரு ணூறை","பரூஉப்பளிங் குதிர்த்த பலவுறு திருமணி","குரூஉப்புலி பொருத புண்கூர் யானை","முத்துடை மருப்பின் முழுவலி மிகுதிரள்","வளையுடைந் தன்ன வள்ளிதழ்க் காந்தள்","நாகந் திலக நறுங்காழ் ஆரம்","கருங்கொடி மிளகின் காய்த்துணர்ப் பசுங்கறி","திருந்தமை விளைந்த தேக்கள் தேறல்","கானிலை எருமைக் கழைபெய் தீந்தயிர்","நீனிற வோரி பாய்ந்தென நெடுவரை","நேமியிற் செல்லும் நெய்க்கண் இறாஅல்","உடம்புணர்வு தழீஇய ஆசினி யனைத்தும்","குடமலைப் பிறந்த தண்பெருங் காவிரி","கடன்மண் டழுவத்துக் கயவாய் கடுப்ப","நோனாச் செருவி னெடுங்கடைத் துவன்றி","வானத் தன்ன வளமலி யானைத்","தாதெருத் ததைந்த முற்ற முன்னி"],"p":"","r":"With them the growing creeper whose buds have shot up in the spray, the yam like a yoke carried slung from a pole, the many-faceted fine gems shed from great crystal, the pearl-holding tusks, massive and strong, of an elephant wounded in a fight with a striped tiger, kaanthal with large petals like broken conch bangles, naagam, tilakam and the fragrant heartwood of sandal, green pepper in clusters from the dark pepper vine, well-matured toddy of honey, sweet curd of the forest buffalo poured into bamboo, the ghee-eyed honeycomb that rolls like a wheel down the high slope when the dark-blue ori leaps on it, and wild jack clasped together: all of these, crowding at the long gate of his unbearable war like the broad Kaveri, born on Kudamalai, at the mouth where it plunges into the sea; approach the courtyard thick with the pollen-strewn dung of his elephants, as many as the sky.","n":"The great simile turns the list into a river: the wealth of the hills flows down to the patron's gate as the Kaveri flows down from Kudamalai to the sea, so the gate is the estuary of the land. What ஓரி (524) names is uncertain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parisil","selvam","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 514-531","lb":"PP10:514-531","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["வளர்கொடி","கொடி",514,"maram","gift_food","creeper"],["ணூறை","நூறை",515,"maram","gift_food","yam"],["திருமணி","மணி",516,"pira","gift_food","gems"],["குரூஉப்புலி","புலி",517,"maa","scene","tiger"],["யானை","யானை",517,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மருப்பின்","மருப்பு",518,"maa","gift_food","tusk"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",519,"maram","gift_food","glory lily"],["நாகந்","நாகம்",520,"maram","gift_food","naagam tree"],["திலக","திலகம்",520,"maram","gift_food","tilakam tree"],["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",520,"maram","gift_food","sandalwood"],["மிளகின்","மிளகு",521,"maram","gift_food","pepper"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",522,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["எருமைக்","எருமை",523,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["தீந்தயிர்","தயிர்",523,"unavu","gift_food","curd"],["வோரி","ஓரி",524,"maa","scene","ori (uncertain beast)"],["இறாஅல்","இறால்",525,"pira","gift_food","honeycomb"],["ஆசினி","ஆசினி",526,"maram","gift_food","wild jack"],["யானைத்","யானை",530,"maa","scene","elephants"]],"en":[["hill","குடமலைப்",527],["river","காவிரி",527]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:532-546":{"book":"pattupattu","num":205,"t":["மழையெதிர் படுகண் முழுவுகண் இகுப்பக்","கழைவளர் தூம்பின் கண்ணிடம் இமிர","மருதம் பண்ணிய கருங்கோட்டுச் சீறியாழ்","நரம்புமீ திறவா துடன்புணர்ந் தொன்றிக்","கடவ தறிந்த இன்குரல் விறலியர்","தொன்றொழுகு மரபிற் றம்மியல்பு வழாஅது","அருந்திறற் கடவுட் பழிச்சிய பின்றை","விருந்திற் பாணி கழிப்பி நீண்மொழிக்","குன்றா நல்லிசைக் சென்றோர் உம்பல்","இன்றிவட் செல்லா துலகமொடு நிற்ப","இடைத்தெரிந் துணரும் பெரியோர் மாய்ந்தெனக்","கொடைக்கட னிறுத்த செம்ம லோயென","வென்றிப் பல்புகழ் விறலோ டேத்திச்","சென்றது நொடியவும் விடாஅன் நசைதர","வந்தது சாலும் வருத்தமும் பெரிதெனப்"],"p":"","r":"As the drumheads boom as if answering the rain clouds and the holes of the bamboo pipe hum, the sweet-voiced viraliyar, playing marutham on the black-necked small yaazh with strings in unison that never overstep, knowing what is due and keeping their nature by the old custom, first praise the god of great power, then finish the song of welcome, and with long words hail him: 'Lord, scion of those whose good fame does not fail, you who, now that the great ones who could tell merit have died, have paid the debt of giving so that fame does not leave the world today but stays with it'; praising his victories, his many fames and his strength; but he does not let them even tell of their journey: 'Desire brought you; your coming is enough; your toil was great.'","n":"Giving is called a debt (கொடைக்கடன்), owed to the world now that the old discerning patrons are gone: the patron inherits the obligation along with the line. His interruption is the best praise in the poem: the suppliants are spared the telling of their hardship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","parisil"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 532-546","lb":"PP10:532-546","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["முழுவுகண்","முழவு",532,"parai","scene","drum"],["தூம்பின்","தூம்பு",533,"yaazh","scene","pipe"],["மருதம்","மருதம்",534,"yaazh","scene","marutham mode"],["சீறியாழ்","சீறியாழ்",534,"yaazh","scene","small lute"],["நரம்புமீ","நரம்பு",535,"yaazh","scene","strings"],["விறலியர்","விறலி",536,"seythi","scene","dancing women"],["கடவுட்","கடவுள்",538,"deyvam","scene","god"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:547-566":{"book":"pattupattu","num":206,"t":["பொருமுரண் எதிரிய வயவரொடு பொலிந்து","திருநகர் முற்றம் அணுகல் வேண்டி","கல்லென் ஒக்கல் நல்வலத் திரீஇ","உயர்ந்த கட்டில் உரும்பில் சுற்றத்து","அகன்ற தாயத் தஃகிய நுட்பத்து","இலமென மலர்ந்த கைய ராகித்","தம்பெயர் தம்மொடு கொண்டனர் மாய்ந்தோர்","நெடுவரை இழிதரு நீத்தஞ்சால் அருவிக்","கடுவரற் கலுழிக் கட்கின் சேயாற்று","வடுவாழ் எக்கர் மணலினும் பலரே","அதனால் புகழொடுங் கழிகநம் வரைந்த நாளெனப்","பரந்திடங் கொடுக்கும் விசும்புதோய் உள்ளமொடு","நயந்தனிர் சென்ற நும்மினுந் தான்பெரிது","உவந்த உள்ளமோ டமர்ந்தினிது நோக்கி","இழைமருங் கறியா நுழைநூற் கலிங்கம்","எள்ளறு சிறப்பின் வெள்ளரைக் கொளீஇ","முடுவல் தந்த பைந்நிணத் தடியொடு","நெடுவெ ணெல்லின் அரிசிமுட் டாது","தலைநாள் அன்ன புகலொடு வழிச்சிறந்து","பலநாள் நிற்பினும் பெறுகுவிர் நில்லாது"],"p":"","r":"He reflects that those who shone among warriors ready for battle, who, wishing to approach the courtyard of a fortunate city, kept their noisy kin about them on the right, who sat on high thrones among fearless kin with wide inheritance and keen wit, and yet spread their hands saying 'we have nothing', died taking their names with them, and they outnumber the sands of the scarred banks of the Seyaru, whose fierce muddy flood pours down from the high hill's waterfall; 'therefore let our allotted days pass with fame': with that sky-touching heart that gives room to all, gladder than you who came in longing, he looks on you sweetly, wraps your waist with fine cloth whose threads cannot be seen, and feeds you with the fresh fat meat the hounds bring and unfailing rice of long white paddy, welcoming you as on the first day however many days you stay.","n":"On the reading taken here, இலமென மலர்ந்த கையர் (552) are men who spread empty hands saying 'we have nothing', so their names died with them; read instead as hands that opened when suppliants said 'we have nothing', the lines would say that even great givers died, and fame alone remains. Either way the patron's giving is argued from death. Who speaks lines 547 to 557 is not marked: the patron's thought is given as a quotation closed by என (557).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","nilaiyaamai","eegai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 547-566","lb":"PP10:547-566","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"kaanchi","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":1,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["அருவிக்","அருவி",554,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["மணலினும்","மணல்",556,"pira","simile","sand"],["கலிங்கம்","கலிங்கம்",561,"seythi","gift_food","cloth"],["முடுவல்","முடுவல்",563,"maa","scene","hound"],["பைந்நிணத்","நிணம்",563,"unavu","gift_food","fat meat"],["ணெல்லின்","நெல்",564,"unavu","gift_food","paddy"],["அரிசிமுட்","அரிசி",564,"unavu","gift_food","rice"]],"en":[["river","சேயாற்று",555]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"},"pp:10:567-583":{"book":"pattupattu","num":207,"t":["செல்வேந் தில்லவெந் தொல்பதிப் பெயர்ந்தென","மெல்லெனக் கூறி விடுப்பின் நும்முள்","தலைவன் தாமரை மலைய விறலியர்","சீர்கெழு சிறப்பின் விளங்கிழை அணிய","நீரியக் கன்ன நிரைசெலல் நெடுந்தேர்","வாரிக் கொள்ளா வரைமருள் வேழம்","கறங்குமணி துவைக்கும் ஏறுடைப் பெருநிரை","பொலம்படைப் பொலிந்த கொய்சுவற் புரவி","நிலந்தினக் கிடந்த நிதியமொ டனைத்தும்","இலம்படு புலவர் ஏற்றகைந் நிறையக்","கலம்பெயக் கவிழ்ந்த கழறொடித் தடக்கையின்","வளம்பிழைப் பறியாது வாய்வளம் பழுநிக்","கழைவளர் நவிரத்து மீமிசை ஞெரேரென","மழைசுரந் தன்ன ஈகை நல்கித்","தலைநாள் விடுக்கும் பரிசின் மலைநீர்","வென்றெழு கொடியிற் றோன்றும்","குன்றுசூழ் இருக்கை நாடுகிழ வோனே"],"p":"","r":"If you tell him gently, 'we will not stay; we go back to our old village', he will crown the leader among you with a lotus and have the viraliyar wear bright jewels of great worth, and with tall chariots that run in rows like flowing water, hill-like elephants too great to be held, great herds with bulls and ringing bells, cut-maned horses bright in golden trappings and treasure heaped till it wears down the ground, all of it filling the outstretched hands of needy poets, from his great hand with its sliding bracelet that tips as it pours out vessels, never knowing a failure of wealth and rich in kind speech, giving like the rain that suddenly pours on the high peak of Naviram where the bamboo grows, he sends you off with gifts on the first day you ask: he, the lord of the land of hill-ringed dwellings where the mountain waters show like victory flags.","n":"The whole idyll is one sentence whose subject arrives only in its last word, நாடுகிழவோனே: everything the guide has said waits on the patron's title. The leaving gift is given on the first day it is asked (தலைநாள்), matching the welcome 'as on the first day' (565): neither the welcome nor the farewell wears out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","parisil","aatruppadai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Malaipadukadam 567-583","lb":"PP10:567-583","poet":"இரணிய முட்டத்துப் பெருங்குன்றூர்ப் பெருங்கெளசிகனார்","pat":"நன்னன் வேண்மான்","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"பாடாண்திணை","ctu":"ஆற்றுப்படை","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":["ஆற்றுப்படை"],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",569,"maram","ornament","lotus"],["விறலியர்","விறலி",569,"seythi","scene","dancing women"],["நெடுந்தேர்","தேர்",571,"seythi","gift_food","chariots"],["வேழம்","வேழம்",572,"maa","gift_food","elephants"],["ஏறுடைப்","ஏறு",573,"maa","gift_food","bulls"],["புரவி","புரவி",574,"maa","gift_food","horses"],["கழைவளர்","கழை",579,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["மழைசுரந்","மழை",580,"pira","simile","rain"],["மலைநீர்","மலை நீர்",581,"pira","simile","mountain streams"]],"en":[["hill","நவிரத்து",579]],"dv":[],"ch":"pp:13"}},"links":[{"a":"pp:1:78-102","b":"p:568","type":"develops","note":"Nakkirar gives each of the six faces and the twelve hands a work in the world; Arunagirinathar asks to hold the same faces, arms, feet, vel and peacock in thought every day.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The list of the god's faces and arms began as a description of what he does for the world and became, in the later hymn, a sequence to meditate on, so the same inventory moved from cosmology to devotional practice.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:1:126-147","b":"s:845","type":"develops","note":"The sages who have cast out lust and fierce anger walk first into Avinankudi; the Siddhar poet clears lust, anger and the great lie out of his heart so that it can become the Lord's bedchamber.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Driving out lust and anger is in the idyll the qualification of a class of sages to approach the god's shrine, and in the later poem the housework every devotee must do to make the heart itself the shrine.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:1:160-176","b":"p:1326","type":"develops","note":"The idyll's assembly of gods comes with Brahma 'in mind' and a need it does not name; the Thiruppugazh number-poem tells that Murugan imprisoned Brahma and freed him when the three gods came begging at his feet.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"If the Thiruppugazh's story is the one behind lines 160 to 165, the oldest Murugan poem already assumes the episode of Brahma's imprisonment, and the later hymn only states outright what the idyll leaves as an allusion.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:1:177-189","b":"p:1326","type":"develops","note":"Nakkirar's Eragam is the shrine where brahmins recite the rare six-letter mantra; Arunagirinathar's Eragam is where 'the brahmins of the six letters' (ஆறெழுத்து அந்தணர்) praise his feet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The six-letter brahmins of Eragam pass from Nakkirar's description into Arunagirinathar's epithet almost word for word, showing that the later poet read the shrine through this idyll's line 186.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:1:177-189","b":"t:226","type":"qualifies","note":"Nakkirar defines the brahmins of Eragam by their outward order (six duties, three fires, sacred thread, wet cloth, chanting at set hours); Tirumular defines the true brahmins by meditating on the mantra and riding the chariot of love so as not to sink in maya.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same figure of the mantra-reciting brahmin is praised by the idyll for keeping the forms and by Tirumular only for the inner love the forms should lead to, which shows the Saiva yogic tradition re-grounding a figure it inherited.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:1:206-217","b":"p:306","type":"develops","note":"In the idyll, dancing on every hill with the hill women is the god's settled nature (குன்றுதொறாடலும் நின்றதன் பண்பே); in the Thiruppugazh, Kunruthoradal is the name of a shrine where the hill tribes worship him with frenzied dance (வெறியாடி).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"A phrase that in the Sangam idyll describes the god's habit among the hill people became by Arunagirinathar's time the proper name of a shrine, so the idyll's description is the origin of a later place of pilgrimage.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:1:218-226","b":"s:1444","type":"tension","note":"The idyll finds the god in every outer place of worship, festival ground, veri ground, grove, river, crossroads and kadamba tree; Sivavakkiyar asks what use any puja is while the Lord being sought is within us. On the reading that the Siddhar rejects outer places and rites altogether, the two are incompatible.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The idyll widens the god's presence outward to every spot where he is called, and the Siddhar narrows it to the one place inside the worshipper, so the pair marks the two ends of the Tamil argument over where the divine is met.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:1:227-249","b":"t:197","type":"tension","note":"The idyll's rite offers rice mixed with a ram's blood and spreads millet with blood, and calls it worship that brings each seeker what he sought; Tirumular says that in worship the bright flower is not killing other small lives. Read plainly, one counts a killing as part of right worship and the other counts not killing as the best offering.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Blood offering stands in the oldest Murugan poem as an ordinary part of effective worship, so Tirumular's rule that not killing is the true flower is a reform of an existing Tamil practice, not a restatement of it.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:1:227-249","b":"s:1473","type":"tension","note":"The idyll says those who worship with offerings of goat's blood and red millet obtain what they ask; Sivavakkiyar says that offering goat and cock at the shrine when you are sick does not stop you wasting away. The claims about whether such offerings work are incompatible.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Siddhar's mockery of goat and cock sacrifice is aimed at a rite the Sangam idyll describes in detail and promises results from, so his scepticism attacks a claim with a long and respected Tamil history.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:1:266-276","b":"p:209","type":"develops","note":"Nakkirar calls the god 'bull among poets of praised good speech' (புலவர் ஏறே); the Thiruppugazh says Murugan delighted in the poems of Keeran (Nakkirar) and taught him grammar.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The idyll's epithet makes the god a poet, and the later tradition turns that into a story in which the idyll's own author becomes the god's pupil, so the poem's praise of the god's speech came back as the poet's legend.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:1:277-295","b":"t:13","type":"agrees","note":"The seeker says it is hard for living beings to know the god to his measure (நின்னளந்து அறிதல் மன்னுயிர்க்கு அருமையின்); Tirumular says Vishnu, Brahma and the gods, measuring with thought, cannot conceive the Lord, who stands beyond all measuring.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The theme that the god cannot be measured, which Tirumular argues through the gods' failure, is already in the Sangam idyll as the suppliant's reason for coming to the feet, so unknowability led to approach rather than to despair from the start.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:1:277-295","b":"p:8","type":"develops","note":"In the idyll the god appears in his own form to a living suppliant and says 'Fear not; I knew of your coming'; in the Thiruppugazh the poet asks that at the moment Death's servants come he appear on the peacock and end his fear.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The god's appearance and his word against fear, which the idyll promises to any suppliant who makes the journey, the later poet saves for the single hour of death, showing how the guide-poem's promise of a meeting became a plea for rescue.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:2:60-72","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural likens the wealth of a man of நயன் to a fruit tree ripening in the middle of the town; the guide says he went to Karikalan like a bird that thinks of a fruiting tree, through a gate that bars no one in need.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the tree from the giver's side and makes its public place the point, while the idyll shows the same tree from the hungry bird's side and supplies the condition the couplet compresses into உள்ளூர், an open gate, so the maxim's promise turns out to depend on access as much as on fruit."},{"a":"pp:2:60-72","b":"pn:209","type":"qualifies","note":"Puram 209 compares the bard to birds that fly to a fruit tree in a mountain cleft and turn back with nothing when it is bare; the guide uses the bird and fruit tree as the image of certain success.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The bird-and-fruit-tree simile carried the bard's risk as well as his hope, since in Puram 209 the tree can be bare, so the guide poem's use of it is a claim that this patron is never the empty tree, and its first-person testimony is offered to meet exactly that doubt."},{"a":"pp:2:119-129","b":"pn:140","type":"qualifies","note":"Puram 140 mocks the lord of Nanjil as a simpleton who gave an elephant when asked for a little rice, giving that knows no measure; here Karikalan gives elephants with mates and calves by the measure he knows, and the bard takes by the measure he knows.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The elephant given to a bard was a stock sign of excessive giving that one poet could tease as folly ignoring measure, while the idyll answers that charge by naming two measures, the giver's and the taker's, so the excess is kept in bounds by the receiver's restraint."},{"a":"pp:2:130-148","b":"pn:66","type":"tension","note":"The idyll makes the battle of Venni, where two great kings fell on one field, the crown of Karikalan's praise; Puram 66, addressed to Karikalan himself, says the king who was wounded in the back at Venni and starved himself facing north is the better man.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same victory at Venni is in one poem the proof of Karikalan's greatness and in the other the occasion for saying the loser surpassed him, which shows that praise addressed to a victor could, in this poetry, rank the defeated king's honour above the win itself."},{"a":"pp:2:149-162","b":"pn:385","type":"same_image","note":"In Puram 385 the lord of Ambar, hearing a bard's drum at another man's door, strips off his earth-eaten rags, dresses him in white and ends his hunger; the guide promises that Karikalan will strip off the bard's stitched rags and give him silk before he asks.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Puram 385 tells the removal of rags and the gift of cloth as one patron's spontaneous kindness, while the guide poem can predict it as the first step of a known sequence, which suggests the re-dressing of the bard was a recognised rite of reception rather than a single generous act.","gr":3},{"a":"pp:2:163-177","b":"n:2","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 2 tells those who gain wealth to share its food with many at once, since wealth turns like a cart wheel; the idyll's bard passes on to others what he received on the road, and the king, weighing the instability of the world, will not dismiss his guest.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The impermanence that the Naladiyar uses as an argument for giving is already in the idyll a motive at both ends of the gift, the bard redistributing as he goes and the king holding on to the guest, so instability argued for keeping company as well as for releasing wealth before it became a quatrain about wealth alone."},{"a":"pp:2:218-231","b":"pn:7","type":"qualifies","note":"Puram 7 praises Karikalan for delighting in plunder by the light of burning enemy towns, so that the good things are gone from others' rich lands of fields and fish; the idyll shows his own land as one umbrella under which all five landscapes mingle in plenty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two poems show the same Chola fertility, fields where water and fish abound, as what Karikalan destroys in other kings' lands and what he guarantees in his own, so the guide poem's harmony of the five lands is the domestic face of a conquest the puram poet praises openly."},{"a":"pp:2:232-248","b":"k:545","type":"tension","note":"The Kural says rain and harvest gather together in the land of the king who wields the sceptre rightly; the idyll grants that in Karikalan's land, under a sceptre it has just called just, the rain can fail in great drought, and gives the harvest to the Kaveri instead.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On the idyll's reading the just king's land is not spared drought, which the Kural's couplet implies it should be, so the poet keeps the king's righteousness and the land's plenty together by moving the source of harvest from the sky, which answers to the sceptre, to the river, which does not need to."},{"a":"pp:2:232-248","b":"pn:117","type":"qualifies","note":"Puram 117 says that even when Saturn smokes, comets appear and Venus runs south, the rain never failed in the dry land of the just king; the idyll describes a sun that makes kings tremble, falls that dwindle and clouds that forget the sea, and sets the Kaveri against them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both poems meet the same fear, that signs in the sky foretell drought and blame the king, but Puram 117 denies that the rain fails in a good king's land while the idyll admits the failure and lets the river make it harmless, two different ways of protecting a ruler's name from a bad season."},{"a":"pp:3:32-40","b":"pp:1:277-295","type":"develops","note":"The guide here calls the bard he directs முதுவாய் இரவல (line 40); in Thirumurugatruppadai line 284 the god's attendants announce the devotee to Murugan in the same words, முதுவாய் இரவலன்.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same title for a seasoned suppliant names a bard seeking a chief in one guide poem and a devotee seeking the god in another, so the devotional guide poem approached Murugan through the bard's petition at a patron's gate and kept even its form of address."},{"a":"pp:3:41-50","b":"a:91","type":"same_image","note":"Both describe Kuttuvan's western land by a buffalo that leaves the lotus pond to rest in jackfruit shade; Akananuru 91 says the hero will not stay for it even if he gains it, and the idyll calls even Kuttuvan's Vanji empty.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The buffalo resting in the jackfruit shade was a set emblem of the Chera land's ease, and both genres used it as the standard of plenty that is outweighed: by the heroine's beauty in the love poem and by a minor chief's giving in the guide poem."},{"a":"pp:3:84-113","b":"pn:158","type":"develops","note":"Both name the seven givers (Pari, Ori, Kaari or Malaiyan, Athikan or Ezhini, Pekan, Aay, Nalli), with the shared formula for Pekan's hill guarded by a fearsome god (அருந்திறல் அணங்கின் / அருந்திறல் கடவுள்) and the same closing என ஆங்கு எழுவர.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The seven were a fixed list with stock epithets that a poet could set before a living patron, and the two poems use it differently: Perunchithiranar says the seven are dead so Kumanan must take their place, while the idyll says Nalliyakkodan already bears alone the yoke the seven bore together."},{"a":"pp:3:84-113","b":"pn:91","type":"develops","note":"Avvai praises Athiyaman for giving her the nelli fruit without saying what it would do for him; the idyll lists Athikan as the giver of the nectar-yielding fruit to Avvai.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Avvai's poem makes the hiddenness of the fruit's virtue the point of the gift, whereas the idyll names that virtue openly (அமிழ்துவிளை), so by the time the story joined the list of seven the secret had become its public meaning."},{"a":"pp:3:84-113","b":"pn:141","type":"qualifies","note":"Paranar cites Pekan's gift of a cloak to a peacock as proof that his giving looks to others' need and not to merit in the next world; the idyll lists the same gift as Pekan's emblem among the seven.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In Purananuru 141 the peacock's cloak is evidence in an argument about why a chief gives, while in the idyll it is only the sign by which Pekan is recognised, so a deed that once carried a claim about motive could circulate as a bare token of liberality."},{"a":"pp:3:130-145","b":"pn:164","type":"develops","note":"Both show a bard's hunger by a mushroom growing in a cold kitchen (ஆம்பி) and a starving woman and her children; Perunthalai Saathanar shows it to Kumanan as his present state, the idyll's guide as a past state the patron ended.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same kitchen of poverty serves as a petition in the Purananuru poem and as testimony in the guide poem, which shows how the guide form works: the bard's own former misery, once relieved, becomes the credential that sends another bard to the same patron."},{"a":"pp:3:203-220","b":"k:386","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says a ruler easy to see and not harsh of speech has his land praised above others; the idyll praises a gate never shut to bards, poets and brahmins, a glad face and freedom from fierce anger, each praised by a named class of people.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's abstract reward of praise has a concrete source in the idyll, where the people at the open gate (wise men, warriors, women and suppliants) are the ones who do the praising, so accessibility is rewarded by the very classes it admits."},{"a":"pp:3:221-234","b":"k:546","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 546 says victory comes not from the spear but from the unbending sceptre; the bard's song praises both, the sceptre that shades the ploughmen and the spear that burns the chariot warriors.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The bard gives sceptre and spear equal places by assigning them to different people, subjects and enemies, while the Kural ranks the sceptre above the spear even in war, so the ethical book turns the praise poem's balanced pair into a hierarchy."},{"a":"pp:4:1-22","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"The idyll's bards wander like birds that search for fruiting trees on a hill the rain has left; the Kural likens the wealth of a man with nayan to a fruit tree ripening in the middle of the town.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The patron-as-fruit-tree image is seen from the tree's side in the Kural and from the birds' side in the idyll, and the idyll adds the condition the Kural's town tree removes: the guide poem exists because, for the wandering bard, the fruiting tree is far off and must be pointed out.","gr":3},{"a":"pp:4:1-22","b":"pn:138","type":"develops","note":"Both address a ragged bard with a small lute; Purananuru 138 sums up the road he has already crossed in three lines (cattle paths, deer hills, fish fords), and the idyll lays out the road still ahead land by land.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The guide poem's landscape sequence appears in the short poem as a compressed formula for the journey behind the bard; the idyll turns it round into the journey before him and expands each land into a settlement with its own meal."},{"a":"pp:4:23-45","b":"pn:141","type":"develops","note":"In Purananuru 141 a bard resting on the road with his gifts tells a hungry bard that before seeing Pekan they were poorer than he; the idyll's speaker, with horses and elephants, says he and his hungry kin come from Thiraiyan.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The meeting of a rewarded bard and a hungry one is the whole drama of the short poem but only the opening frame of the idyll, which shows that the long guide poem grew by keeping that encounter and filling the space between it and the patron with the road."},{"a":"pp:4:23-45","b":"a:340","type":"develops","note":"The idyll says Thiraiyan is of the line the waves brought (திரைதரு மரபு); Akananuru 340, a shore poem, likens the girl to his Pavaththiri of many flowering seaside groves.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two place Thiraiyan as a ruler of the coast: the praise poem's sea-born lineage and the love poem's choice of his seaside town as the measure of a neythal girl's beauty rest on the same identity, so the akam simile draws on the fame the guide poem states."},{"a":"pp:4:23-45","b":"k:545","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says rain and harvest gather in the land of the king who rules by right; the idyll says that in the land of Thiraiyan's just sceptre robbers are absent and thunder, snakes and beasts do no harm.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The idyll extends the Kural's claim that a just ruler's land is blessed by nature from rain and crops to the dangers of the road, so for a travelling bard the just sceptre is proved by a safe journey rather than a good harvest."},{"a":"pp:4:46-65","b":"a:159","type":"qualifies","note":"Both use the formula of the salt carts that proclaim their rate (கொள்ளை சாற்றி, கொடுநுக ஒழுகை); in Akananuru 159 raiding bowmen cook meat at the cold hearths the salt carters left, on the road the lover crossed.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same stock road scene serves opposite ends: in the love poem the salt-carters' track is where raiders lurk and the woman fears for her man, while in the guide poem it is a road made safe for travellers by day under the patron, so the formula's meaning is set by whose land the road runs through."},{"a":"pp:4:134-146","b":"k:922","type":"qualifies","note":"The idyll's warriors trade raided cattle for toddy and drink home brew all day as part of a life of valour; the Kural forbids drink, allowing it only to those who do not wish to be counted by the worthy.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's ironic concession names a class of people who do not seek the esteem of the worthy, and the idyll shows that such a class was a whole warrior community whose honour lay in the raid, not in sobriety, so the Kural's ban addresses a scale of values the older poem did not share."},{"a":"pp:4:147-168","b":"kl:110","type":"develops","note":"The idyll's herd girl buys food with buttermilk (அளை) but will not take gold for ghee; in Kalittokai 110 the herd girl rebukes a man who thinks one easy to buy buttermilk from is easy for butter too.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kalittokai girl's retort turns on a real price difference that the idyll records, buttermilk as cheap daily barter and ghee as a good kept for livestock, so her wordplay about being 'easy' depends on the herding economy the guide poem describes."},{"a":"pp:4:184-196","b":"k:1033","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says only those who live by their own ploughing truly live and all others bow to eat and follow behind; the idyll's bard, who lives by praise, is fed millet and beans at the farmers' village.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The idyll shows the arrangement the Kural ranks: the bard is exactly one who eats from another's plough, yet the guide poem treats being fed by farmers as the patron's honour and the bard's due, not as a lesser life, so the Kural's verdict is a moral claim against the old patronage order."},{"a":"pp:4:242-256","b":"k:734","type":"develops","note":"The Kural defines a land as one free of severe hunger, endless disease and destructive enemies; the idyll describes a town that has never known hunger, whose granaries grow old unopened and whose children sleep fed on milk.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the Kural defines by absences the idyll shows by presences (unopened granaries, toy chariots, nurses, white rice and fowl for a guest), and its phrase தொல்பசி அறியா makes freedom from hunger a property of the town's history, not only of its present."},{"a":"pp:4:257-262","b":"k:1078","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural says the base, like sugarcane, yield only when crushed; the idyll's cane presses roar day and night and the bard is told to drink the juice as he likes.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's image of giving extracted by force uses the same press that the idyll presents as the very sound of open plenty, so the simile's sting depends on the contrast: what the base give only under crushing, the cane in a good land gives to any passing guest."},{"a":"pp:4:271-282","b":"k:922","type":"tension","note":"The guide promises the bards rice toddy brewed by fisherfolk and roasted fish until they stagger; the couplet says do not drink, or let only those who do not care for the esteem of the worthy drink.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The idyll counts drinking to the point of staggering among the best hospitality of the road, given freely by the poorest folk, while the couplet makes drink the mark of one who forfeits esteem, so the ethics book is rejecting a pleasure the oldest praise poetry offered as part of the gift.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:4:297-310","b":"pn:122","type":"same_image","note":"Both praise a wife as chaste like the northern star: here the Brahmin wife who cooks for the bards, in Purananuru 122 Kari's wife, the one thing he keeps while all else goes to Brahmins and bards.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In both poems the star-chaste wife stands at the centre of a house that gives to Brahmins and bards, but in the chief's poem she is what is kept back from giving and in the idyll she is the one who gives, so the simile marked the giving household from either side."},{"a":"pp:4:311-335","b":"n:395","type":"develops","note":"In the idyll a kingfisher strikes a bathing girl's golden earring for a fish and carries it off to a sacrificial pillar; in the Naladiyar quatrain a kingfisher takes the beloved's eyes for carp but does not dive, seeing her brow as a drawn bow.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The kingfisher's mistake about a woman's ornament or feature was a set conceit: the idyll lets the bird act on its mistake as part of a port scene, while the later love quatrain stops the bird short, turning the same error into praise of the woman's brow.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:4:336-345","b":"k:256","type":"tension","note":"The toddy-sellers keep a boar in a pit for many days, fed on rice flour and kept from its sow, to serve its fat to the bards; the couplet says that if no one killed to eat, no one would bring flesh for sale.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The idyll describes exactly the chain the couplet condemns, an animal fattened in confinement to be served and sold, and offers it as a reward of the road, so the Kural's argument against buying meat cuts against a hospitality the guide poems praise."},{"a":"pp:4:363-392","b":"pr:13:22-36","type":"qualifies","note":"The Paripadal hymn makes the god lying in waking sleep on the many-hooded serpent in the milk sea one of his three cosmic forms; the idyll places the one on the serpent bed at a grove shrine on the road where bards stop to praise him and play a little.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Together they show the serpent-bed form both as cosmic theology and as the god of a particular shrine on the road to Kanchi, so in the Sangam period the same image was already tied to local worship that a passing bard could visit."},{"a":"pp:4:393-411","b":"pr:thirattu:8","type":"same_image","note":"The Paripadal fragment likens Madurai to the lotus from Mayon's navel, its streets the petals and the lord's palace the pericarp; the idyll has Kanchi's tall-housed town rise like the pericarp of the lotus from the blue god's navel that bore the four-faced one.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The navel-lotus with the central great house as its pericarp was a shared figure of city praise used for Kanchi as well as Madurai, so it was a convention for a Tamil capital rather than a legend of one city."},{"a":"pp:4:412-435","b":"pn:2","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 2 praises a Chera for feeding the armies without limit while the hundred fought the Five until they fell; the idyll uses the same war to say its king roared at his victory like the Five when all the hundred fell.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows two ways Sangam praise borrowed the Bharata war: the Chera poem keeps its king outside the war as the feeder of both sides, while the Tondai idyll puts its king in the victors' place, so the epic could measure a king's giving or his conquest."},{"a":"pp:4:436-447","b":"k:548","type":"qualifies","note":"The idyll's king answers both those who seek justice and those who seek their needs, with a clear sight that discerns between them; the couplet says a ruler not easy of access who does not examine and do justice perishes by himself.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The couplet names access and examined judgment as two duties whose lack ruins a king, while the idyll shows them as one daily audience in which judgment and gifts are granted together and discernment joins them, so justice and giving were seen as a single court function.","gr":4},{"a":"pp:4:436-447","b":"pn:185","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 185, by Thondaiman Ilanthiraiyan himself, says the realm is a cart that runs well only if its driver is skilled and otherwise sinks in the mire of enmity; the idyll shows the same king's court running smoothly, justice and gifts granted to all.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read beside the patron's own conditional maxim, the bard's portrait answers the king's worry with a finished picture: what Ilanthiraiyan states as a risk depending on the driver, the guide poem states as already achieved, which shows the work praise did for a king who wrote about failure.","gr":3},{"a":"pp:4:448-461","b":"p:15","type":"tension","note":"The guide scripts the bard's praise of the patron (warrior of warriors, lord of the wealthy, of unceasing giving) as the way to his gifts; Arunagirinathar confesses that praising people as lotus-handed and clouds in giving, to beg from them, wounded his heart, and asks to serve Murugan instead.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The patron praise that the guide poem teaches as a craft with a sure reward is in the later hymn the very habit the poet repents, so the bard's profession that founded Tamil praise poetry became for the devotional poet the model of misplaced service."},{"a":"pp:4:448-461","b":"pp:1:38-66","type":"develops","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai has a flesh-eating demoness dance the thunangai after the god kills Sur; here the bard's praise is likened to the finger-snapping before the goddess of the thunangai, mother of the one who killed Sur in the sea.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both guide poems use the Sur victory and its thunangai dance, but the god's guide poem uses it to praise the god while the king's guide poem makes it the model for praising a man, so the form of Murugan's praise was lent to the praise of a patron."},{"a":"pp:4:462-479","b":"k:333","type":"develops","note":"Weighing the condition of the unlasting world, the king gives before the bard has finished asking; the couplet says wealth does not stay, so when you have it do at once what lasts.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The idyll turns the couplet's 'at once' into a patron's habit with its motive stated, wealth that will not stay given for fame that will, so the maxim had a heroic model in the bard's patron before it became a rule of the ethics book.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:4:462-479","b":"pn:390","type":"qualifies","note":"Both strip the suppliant's rag like old pond moss, dress him in new cloth and feed him meat and rice; Purananuru 390 ends by saying that kings with elephants do not even know such feeding.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The rag, cloth and meal follow a fixed order of the gift rite in both, and holding them together shows the idyll claiming for a crowned king the very hospitality that Purananuru 390 boasts only the lesser chief gives, so the boast was rhetoric and not a rule of rank."},{"a":"pp:4:480-500","b":"pn:361","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 361's patron gives golden lotuses to bards and garlands to the singing women and needs no one to tell him the world does not last; the idyll's king crowns the bard with a golden lotus and garlands the viraliyar after weighing the unlasting world (line 466).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Placed together, the golden lotus for the bard and the garland for the singing woman appear as a fixed pair of gifts tied to one stated motive, the patron's knowledge that the world does not last, which lets the Purananuru king face Death and makes the idyll's king hurry the gift.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:5:1-23","b":"pp:7:157-166","type":"same_image","note":"Both idylls have women reassure the waiting wife with the words இன்னே வருகுவர் (they will come soon), and both have her refuse it in tears: காட்டவுங் காட்டவுங் காணாள் கலுழ்சிறந்து here, உகத்தவை மொழியவும் ஒல்லாள் மிகக்கலுழ்ந்து in Nedunalvadai.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The promise of a quick return and the wife's weeping refusal form one fixed sequence in both idylls, but Mullaippattu takes the promise from a herd-girl's words to a calf, read as an omen, while Nedunalvadai puts it in the grey-haired nurses' mouths as plain comfort, so the convention let the same words come from chance speech or from household authority, and in both the comfort must fail."},{"a":"pp:5:1-23","b":"kn:12","type":"develops","note":"Kar Narpathu 12 says the dark clouds rising every day on the right (வைகலு மேரும் வலம்) are a sign beyond doubt (ஐயந்தீர் காட்சி) that he will come; Mullaippattu opens with a cloud that drinks the sea and rises on the right (வலனேர்பு).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read with Kar Narpathu's explicit rule that a cloud rising on the right foretells the lover's return, Mullaippattu's opening simile is already an omen in the sky set before the old women's omen in the street, and the poem closes with the returning army also rising on the right (line 91), so the idyll is framed by the sign and its fulfilment."},{"a":"pp:5:1-23","b":"a:9","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 9 the returning husband pictures his wife at the evening when calves come home, standing at a corner of the house and praying at every chirp of the lizard; in Mullaippattu the old women seek the omen at evening, a calf supplies it, and the wife will not accept it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The husband's picture of a wife eagerly reading every omen is his own fancy on the road; Mullaippattu, told from the house, gives the omen-seeking to the old women and shows the wife refusing its comfort, which qualifies the hopeful, praying wife of the man's imagination."},{"a":"pp:5:45-66","b":"pn:22","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 22 praises the king under whose umbrella the unarmed sleep safe (வாள் மருங்கு இலோர் காப்பு உறங்க) because he himself does not sleep (நீ துஞ்சாய் மாறே); Mullaippattu sets armed women, old guards, hour-keepers, Yavanas and Mlecchas round the king's own bed at midnight.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The praise of a wakeful king who lets others sleep safely has a reverse side that only the idyll shows: the king's own sleep needs a whole household of armed and foreign watchers, so royal protection of the realm's sleep rests on others guarding his.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:5:67-79","b":"pp:7:167-188","type":"develops","note":"Mullaippattu's king lies awake thinking of the maimed tusker, the fallen men and the arrow-struck horses; Nedunalvadai's king goes out at midnight with a lamp-bearer to see his soldiers' wounds (விழுப்புண் காணிய புறம்போந்து) and does not sleep (பள்ளி கொள்ளான்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two idylls give the same scene of a king kept awake in camp by his wounded, as inward thought lying with a hand on the bed in Mullaippattu and as an outward round of inspection in Nedunalvadai, and only Mullaippattu lets the night end in the sweet sleep (இன்துயில்) that the wife at home is denied."},{"a":"pp:5:67-79","b":"k:780","type":"develops","note":"Kural 780: a death that fills the eyes of those who maintained one with tears is worth begging for; Mullaippattu's king remembers those who fell having made good the rice they ate (சோறுவாய்த் தொழிந்தோர் உள்ளியும்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural measures the warrior's best death by the tears of the one who fed him, and the idyll shows that patron from inside, lying awake over the men who repaid his rice with their lives, so the maxim gains a scene where the debt of food and the king's grief are both seen."},{"a":"pp:5:67-79","b":"a:214","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 214: in the rain-drowned camp the king, set on glory, has no sleep (கண்படை இலனே), and the soldier wonders how his wife fares alone in the rainy evening; Mullaippattu 67: the king, eager for battle, gets no sleep (கண்படை பெறாஅ), and the next lines turn to the wife.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Akananuru 214 compresses the idyll's two scenes, the sleepless king in the rainy camp and the wife alone at evening, but splits king and husband into two men, which shows that Mullaippattu's joining of them, the wife's husband being the sleepless king himself, is the idyll's choice and not the convention's rule."},{"a":"pp:5:80-103","b":"kn:8","type":"same_image","note":"In Kar Narpathu 8 the kaayaa opens buds as if dipped in the eye's collyrium (கண்ணிய லஞ்சனங் தோய்ந்தபோற் காயாவும்) and so announces his return; in Mullaippattu 93 the kaayaa blooms collyrium (காயா வஞ்சன மலர) on the road his chariot is driving.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The collyrium-dark kaayaa is a promise in Kar Narpathu, pointed to by the friend while the man is away, and in the idyll it is scenery the returning chariot passes, so the same flower serves as proof of the return in the short poem and as the return itself in the long one."},{"a":"pp:5:80-103","b":"pp:7:167-188","type":"same_image","note":"Mullaippattu 85 lights the wife's room with பாவை விளக்கிற் பரூஉச்சுட ரழல; Nedunalvadai 175 lights the king's midnight round of the wounded with பாண்டில் விளக்கிற் பரூஉச்சுட ரழல.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same half-line of a lamp's thick flame burning lights the sleepless wife at home in Mullaippattu and the sleepless king in camp in Nedunalvadai, so the formula belongs to the night vigil itself and the two idylls assign that vigil in opposite directions."},{"a":"pp:6:1-23","b":"k:545","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says rain and harvest gather in the land of the king who rules by right; the idyll opens on a world where the rain does its work, one seed yields a thousand and men are free of disease, and credits it to the rule of the patron's ancestors among truthful men.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The idyll gives the Kural's cause-and-effect rule a cosmic setting and a past: the plenty is the order of the world itself and belongs to the ancestors' rule, so a king inherits a moral weather he must keep up rather than earning it afresh, which is what the counsel later in the poem asks of him."},{"a":"pp:6:24-42","b":"pn:26","type":"develops","note":"The same poet praises the same king for the battlefield sacrifice: in Purananuru 26 with crowned heads for a hearth, blood for water and bangled arms for ladles; in the idyll with the arm as ladle and the flesh-rice served to the soldiers who did not step back.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Mangudi Maruthanar's two accounts of the rite show what it was for: the short poem sets it beside the king's Vedic sacrifices as a second, martial sacrifice, while the idyll names who eats it, the soldiers who held their ground, so the horror is also the army's feast and reward."},{"a":"pp:6:106-130","b":"a:209","type":"same_image","note":"The idyll gives the victory at Alankanam as its patron's crowning deed (camping fearsomely, killing kings, taking their drums); in Akananuru 209 the friend says the gossip about the heroine is louder than the roar at Alankanam when Chezhiyan beat the seven.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two show the battle the court poet offers as the king's defining glory already serving an akam poet as a proverbial measure of noise for village gossip, so a royal victory entered the common stock of comparison and was expected to be known to every hearer."},{"a":"pp:6:152-176","b":"pn:23","type":"develops","note":"Kalladanar tells the same king that he has seen the ruin of the enemy's land, ending on a doe with her fawn, her stag killed by a tiger, nibbling velai flowers in a manless waste; the idyll lists, line by line, each human use of the enemy's land replaced by a wild one, with the owl and its mate in the fallen mansions and the boar and its sow in the ploughed fields.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both poets of this king measure conquest by animal couples moving into places emptied of human families, so the ravaged-land description had a fixed device; the idyll turns Kalladanar's single pathetic image into a systematic inversion of the land's uses, which is the material its kaanchi counsel will later weigh."},{"a":"pp:6:177-198","b":"pn:182","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 182 says the world endures because some would not eat even Indra's nectar alone; the idyll says the king would not leave true friendship for a lie even if he gained the high world with its nectar.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The 'even for the gods' nectar' test appears in both, but Purananuru 182 uses it for sharing among anonymous worthies while the idyll uses it for a king's truthfulness to his friends, so the virtues that hold the world together were also demanded of the ruler, and the measure for them was the same."},{"a":"pp:6:199-209","b":"k:657","type":"develops","note":"The Kural rates the worst poverty of the worthy above wealth won under blame; the idyll says the king, offered the treasure Vanan buried in the south, would not say 'let blame come to us', but would rather give treasure away for fame.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The idyll shows the Kural's maxim as a king's test with a named treasure and adds the alternative the Kural leaves out, giving wealth away for fame, so refusing blamed gain is tied to giving: the ruler's honour lies in both refusing and spending."},{"a":"pp:6:210-237","b":"pn:24","type":"qualifies","note":"Mangudi Kizhar ends Purananuru 24 by saying that the only ones who truly lived are those who, born in the wide world, did not pass with the many but stayed in fame; in the idyll he says the kings who ruled the wide world with their fame spreading are more than the sand on the shore, and are gone.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same poet who told this king that fame alone outlasts death here counts the famous kings by the grains of sand, so fame does not exempt anyone from passing and is itself common; the counsel of the idyll is harder than the short poem's consolation, and the pair shows the kaanchi argument being sharpened by its own author.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:6:261-270","b":"pr:17:22-39","type":"develops","note":"The idyll counts the noise of the garlanded crowd playing together on cool Parankunram among the sounds of the marutham countryside; Paripadal 17 describes the crowds revelling between song-rich Koodal and Parankunram, garlanded men and women dancing so that the bees cannot reach the flowers.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the Maduraikkanchi the Parankunram festival is one sound among the reaping drum and the herons, part of the king's agrarian land, while the Paripadal makes the same crowd its subject and the hill's beauty, so the festival a court poet counted as rural plenty became, in the devotional songs, the sign of the god's hill."},{"a":"pp:6:410-430","b":"k:17","type":"qualifies","note":"The idyll's sea keeps its level because what the clouds take and what the rivers bring balance; the Kural says the vast sea itself would lose its nature if the cloud took and did not give back.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sea's unchanging fullness was a stock figure of plenty in the city poem, and the Kural takes the same sea to show that the fullness depends on the return, which, read back into the market, makes Madurai's inexhaustible plenty the effect of continual exchange rather than a fixed store."},{"a":"pp:6:468-488","b":"k:30","type":"qualifies","note":"The idyll's அந்தணர் are marked by Veda, rite and high conduct, and only in passing by a loving heart; the Kural says those called அந்தணர் are the virtuous because they are kind to every creature.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Holding the two together shows the Kural keeping the idyll's name but moving its definition from the Veda and the rite to the one trait the idyll mentions last, kindness, so the couplet reads as a deliberate redefinition of a status the court poem took for granted.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:6:489-506","b":"pn:6","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 6 wishes the Pandya Mudukudumi to lean to no side like the beam of a balance (ஞமன்); the idyll gives the same balance (ஞெமன்கோல்) to Madurai's court of justice, which puts away fear, grief, desire, hatred and delight.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The impartiality that the earlier poem asks of the king in person has, in Nedunjeliyan's Madurai, become the work of an institution, and the idyll spells out the five passions the court must set aside, which the wish to the king left unnamed."},{"a":"pp:6:489-506","b":"k:118","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's worthy man is like the balance that first levels itself and then weighs; the idyll's court is straight like the balance once it has put away fear, grief, desire, hatred and delight.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The idyll's list says what the Kural's 'first making itself level' consists of, the clearing of five named passions before judgment, so the couplet's compressed image can be unpacked from a description of a working court."},{"a":"pp:6:562-589","b":"k:913","type":"qualifies","note":"The idyll names the courtesans' many deceiving lies and hidden embraces and their draining of rich young men, but lists them among the splendours of the city; the Kural likens the false embrace of women who sell themselves to embracing a stranger's corpse in a dark room.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"The deceit the Kural turns into horror was already admitted, in the same terms of lying and false embrace, by the court poet, who still counted it as part of the city's wealth, so the moralist's chapter answers a practice that praise poetry had treated as a sign of prosperity.","gr":3},{"a":"pp:6:604-619","b":"pp:1:218-226","type":"develops","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai lists the velan's veri ground and the festivals of every town among the places where Murugan is found; the idyll shows the velan and a priestess at work in Madurai's squares, serving pregnant women, with kurinji and kadamba worn in the city.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The guide poem gives the rite's places and the city poem gives its users, so together they show the hill god's veri carried into the capital and used for women's life crises such as pregnancy, which neither poem states alone."},{"a":"pp:6:687-699","b":"pn:23","type":"develops","note":"Kalladanar shows Nedunjeliyan's work in enemy country: fouled fords, plundered fields, felled guarded trees, burned houses, an empty waste; the idyll shows tuskers, horses and herds brought by the light of burning towns flowing into Madurai like the Ganges into the sea.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two poems are the two ends of one process under the same king: the ruin praised in the Purananuru is the source of the abundance praised in Maduraikkanchi, so the city's splendour is the enemy land's emptiness moved to the capital."},{"a":"pp:6:725-746","b":"k:528","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says many will live by a king who looks at people not alike but by rank (வரிசை); the idyll's king crowns warriors with the gold thumbai 'not asking who they are' but weighing their order (முறைகருதுபு), after listing the deeds that rank them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The idyll shows a ceremony in which the king's ranking goes by deeds rather than by birth, which supports reading the Kural's வரிசை as an order of merit and shows what the couplet's 'many will live looking to it' meant in practice for a warrior following."},{"a":"pp:6:747-763","b":"pn:72","type":"develops","note":"In Purananuru 72 Nedunjeliyan stakes his vow on the poverty of not giving to suppliants; the idyll shows his gate never barred, bards, singers and poets called in, and chariots and elephants given.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the open-gate scene is the vow's condition kept: what the young king named as the shame he would accept if he failed (not being able to give) is what the poet, in the king's own lifetime, presents as his settled daily practice."},{"a":"pp:6:747-763","b":"pn:15","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 15 addresses Mudukudumi himself, asking whether his enemies or his completed Vedic sacrifices are more; the idyll measures Nedunjeliyan's feast against one shared with the teachers trained in the sacrifices of Mudukudumi of many halls.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The sacrifices that Purananuru 15 praises as one king's deeds had become, by Nedunjeliyan's time, an inherited institution with its own line of teachers, and the standard against which a later Pandya's liberality was measured."},{"a":"pp:6:764-782","b":"pn:72","type":"develops","note":"Nedunjeliyan vows that if he fails, poets with Mangudi Maruthan at their head may cease to sing his land; the idyll, by Mangudi Maruthanar, ends with chiefs with Maran at their head praising him in assembly.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The vow names the poet whose song was at stake, and the idyll is that poet's song, ending in a praise-assembly built on the same 'X at the head' formula, so the pair shows the poet answering the king's vow in kind while closing, as kaanchi, on the bound of the king's good time."},{"a":"pp:6:764-782","b":"pn:6","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 6 ends by wishing Mudukudumi to last on earth like the cool moon and the burning sun; the idyll uses the same pair to show Nedunjeliyan shining among his kin, then closes on the good time he has been given within a bound.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The kaanchi poet takes the dynasty's formula of sun and moon but uses it for brightness among kin, not for duration, and pointedly ends on the limit of the span, so the closing withholds exactly the wish for lastingness that the praise poem gave."},{"a":"pp:6:764-782","b":"n:6","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 6 starts from the appointed span of days and concludes: give your wealth away, for tomorrow the funeral drum may sound; the idyll starts from the good time given within a bound and concludes: rejoice and live sweetly every day.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both counsels rest on the same premise of a fixed span, but the idyll, after showing the king's giving, lets pleasure stand beside it as a right answer, while the ethics book allows only giving, so the later text narrows a kaanchi argument that once had room for enjoyment."},{"a":"pp:7:1-20","b":"a:264","type":"same_image","note":"Both open the cold season with white musundai, kodal and cowherds coming in from the rain with wet garlands, and both close the description with the same words, கூதிர்நின் றன்றால் followed by பொழுதே (here போதே, line 72).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In Akananuru 264 the formula hands the season to a waiting woman who asks whether the men in the king's camp know their own state, so the idyll's cold-season opening, which ends on the same words, reads as that akam season-poem expanded, with its closing camp scene as the answer to her question."},{"a":"pp:7:136-156","b":"pp:5:1-23","type":"qualifies","note":"In both idylls the waiting wife is told இன்னே வருகுவர் and still weeps; in Mullaippattu the words are an omen overheard from a herd girl to her calves, in Nedunalvadai they are the foster mothers' practised comfort.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Mullaippattu's promise is a sign that its poem fulfils with the returning chariot, while Nedunalvadai's is only kind speech that its poem never fulfils, so the same words separate a poem of waiting rewarded from one that can end only in the poet's wish.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:7:157-166","b":"a:136","type":"same_image","note":"The queen gazes at a painted moon with Rohini and sighs; Akananuru 136 sets a wedding at the hour when the moon had joined Sakatam, the Rohini asterism, in a spotless conjunction.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Since weddings were timed to the moon's union with Rohini, the painting over the queen's bed is also the sign under which she was married, so her long sigh can be read as memory of her own wedding as well as envy of a pair never parted."},{"a":"pp:7:167-188","b":"k:776","type":"develops","note":"The Kural counts every day without a glorious wound (விழுப்புண்) as wasted; the idyll's king walks the camp at midnight in the rain to see the glorious wounds (விழுப்புண்) of the warriors who felled elephants.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the wound a soldier's private reckoning of his days, and the idyll shows its public side, the king coming out at night to look at each wound, so the honour the soldier counts by was conferred in person by his king's seeing it."},{"a":"pp:7:167-188","b":"a:214","type":"qualifies","note":"In both a king in a rain-soaked camp will not sleep (கண்படை இலனே; பள்ளி கொள்ளான்); in Akananuru the soldier cites it to tell his heart the war is his duty while she hears the evening alone, in Nedunalvadai the poet sets it beside the queen's grief and prays for the war's end.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The king's sleepless diligence is the soldier's reason for staying away in the Akananuru and the poet's ground for hoping the war ends soon in the idyll, so one camp image can either lengthen the separation or promise its end, depending on whose grief frames it."},{"a":"pp:7:167-188","b":"pp:5:67-79","type":"develops","note":"Mullaippattu's king lies sleepless in his tent thinking of elephants wounded by spears and of men who fell repaying their food; Nedunalvadai's king rises and goes out at midnight to see his wounded.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The two camp kings show one care in two forms, remembered in bed in Mullaippattu and acted out by walking the lines in Nedunalvadai, so the night-time thought for the wounded is a fixed mark of the good war king in these idylls, the more vivid of them putting it into motion.","gr":3},{"a":"pp:7:101-114","b":"pp:5:45-66","type":"same_image","note":"Yavanas make the lamp-figures that light the queen's inner chambers in Nedunalvadai; in Mullaippattu fierce armed Yavanas guard the king's tent.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the idylls place Yavanas at both ends of a royal household, as craftsmen inside the women's palace and as bodyguards in the war camp, so the king trusted these foreigners with the light of his queen's rooms and with his own person."},{"a":"pp:8:1-22","b":"a:98","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 98 the girl fears that the mother's velan rite, held for a sickness read as Murugan's seizure, will expose the secret; in the idyll the mother's diviners and offerings to many gods have already failed, and the friend makes that failure the ground for telling her.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two show that the misread sickness and its useless rite are the hinge of the kurinji plot: the short poem stops at the girl's dread of exposure, and Kapilar's idyll starts from the same point and turns the failed cure into the friend's reason to disclose the love and press for marriage."},{"a":"pp:8:1-22","b":"k:964","type":"develops","note":"The Kural likens those fallen from their station to hair fallen from the head; the friend says broken jewels can be rejoined but lost worth and fame cannot be washed clean even by sages.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The friend uses the Kural's idea that a fall in standing is beyond repair as an argument for speaking at once, not for keeping quiet: because a girl's good name cannot be restored once it is lost, the secret has to come out before gossip costs her it, so the maxim on honour drives the plot of disclosure."},{"a":"pp:8:23-34","b":"pn:45","type":"develops","note":"Kovur Kizhar stands between two Chola kings at war and tells them that whichever loses, their own house loses; the friend says she holds two great fears like the worthy who stand between two great kings bent on war.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Purananuru 45 shows from inside the position the idyll's simile names, the mediator who can win nothing for either side: so the friend's double fear, for the father's honour and for the girl's life, is modelled on the puram envoy's predicament, and the akam go-between borrows the role of the poet who speaks between kings."},{"a":"pp:8:128-145","b":"a:48","type":"develops","note":"Both are told by the friend to the mother in disclosure: in Akananuru 48 a garlanded hunter with a bow asks the girls which way the beast went; in the idyll the hunter says he has lost a beast that came this way and, when they keep silent, asks whether even speaking with him is a blame.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the hunter's question about a lost beast stands out as the conventional opening of the hill meeting reported in அறத்தொடு நிற்றல், and the idyll supplies what the short poem leaves to the mother's wit: the girls' silence out of fear of blame and the man's answer to that fear."},{"a":"pp:8:166-186","b":"pp:1:218-226","type":"same_image","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai places Murugan at the veri ground and under the fresh-flowered kadamba; the idyll compares the terrified girls holding hands to the ring of women possessed by Neduvel dancing round a kadamba trunk.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The possession dance that Thirumurugatruppadai describes as worship is used by Kapilar as the image for the girls at the very moment the love begins, so the god-seizure the mother wrongly suspects in the opening lines comes back as a simile for what really happened."},{"a":"pp:8:187-199","b":"a:2","type":"same_image","note":"Both poems by Kapilar have toddy made by ripe jackfruit in a long rock pool (பாறை நெடுஞ்சுனை, விளைந்த தேறல்) drunk unknowingly by a creature: a monkey that cannot climb and sleeps, a peacock that staggers like a rope-dancer.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same poet uses one formula for two jobs: in Akananuru 2 the drunk monkey is an argument that the lover can easily find the joy he plans, while in the idyll the drunk peacock only praises the plenty of the lover's land, which shows that in Kapilar the image's force depends on where it stands in the speech, not on the image itself."},{"a":"pp:8:200-214","b":"k:81","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says household life and its guarding of goods exist to care for guests; the man's oath promises a house of open doors where he and the girl will eat what is left after feeding guests.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's rule that the household exists for guests is already in the idyll the language in which a hill man pledges marriage: what he swears to give the girl is not a feeling but a guest-feeding house, so the ethic of virundhu was the content of the promise before it was a maxim."},{"a":"pp:8:234-246","b":"a:122","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 122 lists the hindrances that keep the lover away (the sleepless town, the mother, guards who hurry, the dog, the moon); the idyll lists nearly the same (guards hurrying, the fierce dog, the mother waking, the moon) as what turns him back every night.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In Akananuru 122 the girl uses the catalogue of hindrances to complain about their secret love, whereas the friend in the idyll gives it to the mother as proof of the man's constancy, since he goes back each time without anger, so the same list can work as a lament or as a testimonial depending on who is listening."},{"a":"pp:8:247-261","b":"a:112","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 112 the friend tells the man who has come at night of the bear and the tiger on his path and asks why he does not marry; the idyll ends with the girl weeping over the tiger, bear, elephant, crocodiles and spirits of his night path, told to the mother.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"The dangers of the night path press for marriage from both sides: in Akananuru 112 they are told to the man to make him propose, and in the idyll to the mother to make the family consent, so the same catalogue is used as leverage on whichever party is holding back."},{"a":"pp:9:1-28","b":"k:13","type":"tension","note":"The Kural says that if the sky fails, hunger torments the whole wide world; the idyll says that though the sky fails, the Kaveri does not, and the Chola fields never stop yielding. On the literal reading the claims conflict: the Kural admits no land exempt from failed rain, the idyll names one.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together they show that the Kural's universal law of rain met a regional boast in the poets' own geography, a river delta praised precisely as the place the warning does not reach, although the idyll's own line 6 (மலைத்தலைய) concedes that the river too begins as rain on the hills."},{"a":"pp:9:118-141","b":"k:756","type":"develops","note":"The Kural names customs (உல்கு) as one of the three kinds of the king's wealth; the idyll shows Puhar's customs men levying duty every day without shortfall and stamping the tiger seal on goods for the good king.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural's single word for customs revenue is in the idyll a working institution with officers, a royal seal and a daily round at the port, so the couplet's category can be seen to name an administration the earlier poets had watched in operation.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:9:194-217","b":"k:120","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says a trader's true trade is to care for others' goods as his own; the idyll's merchants weigh their own goods and others' alike (தமவும் பிறவு மொப்ப நாடி) and take no more and give no less.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The idyll's phrase is nearly the Kural's பிறவும் தமபோல், and it adds the working test of fair dealing at the scale, so the maxim appears first as the praised reputation of a real merchant class in a named port before it is stated as a rule for all traders."},{"a":"pp:9:194-217","b":"k:256","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural argues that if no one killed to eat, no one would sell meat; the idyll praises a city where killing is banned and beasts crowd safely at the meat-sellers' huts, yet the same city fries fish and flesh in its toddy-shop yards (lines 176-177) and its fishermen eat prawns and tortoise (lines 63-64).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together they show the ban on killing in the older poem as the virtue of particular classes in a city that still ate meat, whereas the Kural's demand-side argument makes every buyer answerable, so the later ethic is stricter than the ideal the idyll praised."},{"a":"pp:9:218-228","b":"a:213","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 213 the friend assures the heroine that the man will not linger abroad even if he were given the world of the gods with its nectar (அமிழ்தொடு பெறினும்); in the idyll the man himself says he will not leave her even if he were given the city (பட்டினம் பெறினும்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The 'even if he were given' formula is in the Akananuru a friend's comfort about a man already gone; Pattinappalai puts it in the man's own mouth before he leaves and spends 217 lines making the offered prize a real city, one it has itself likened to heaven (line 104), so a convention of comfort becomes the frame of a whole idyll."},{"a":"pp:9:218-228","b":"a:123","type":"tension","note":"In Akananuru 123 the man in the desert mocks his heart, which can neither cross the hills for wealth nor stay with her without fearing poverty; the Pattinappalai hero tells his heart he will stay with her though the richest city is offered. On the reading that both answer the same choice between wealth and love, the first treats staying as fear of poverty and the second as the right decision.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Paalai poets argued both sides of the same debate with the heart, and the pair shows that giving up the journey (செலவழுங்குதல்) was a recognised, honourable answer to the very reproach Akananuru 123 makes, not only a failure of nerve.","gr":2},{"a":"pp:9:229-251","b":"pn:7","type":"qualifies","note":"Karunkuzhal Athanar tells Karikalan to his face that his delight in plunder by the light of burning towns makes the good lands of others cease to be; the idyll describes the same king's wasting of enemy fields and halls as glory and then uses it to measure the heat of the desert.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same king's ravaging of fertile land could be addressed to him as a praise with a sting (நல்ல இல்ல ஆகுபவால்) or listed without reserve in a long court praise, so the pair shows how much the frame, a short puram address or an akam idyll, decided whether a poet could let the cost of conquest be heard."},{"a":"pp:9:229-251","b":"pp:1:218-226","type":"develops","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai names the common, the public hall and the place with the sacred post (மன்றமும் பொதியிலும் கந்துடை நிலையினும்) among the places where the god is worshipped; the idyll shows such a hall with its post (கந்துடைப் பொதியில்), once lit with lamps and worshipped by many, now a lodging for a wild elephant and its cow.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pair shows that the hall with the sacred post was a standing institution of town worship in both poems, and that wasting an enemy country meant emptying exactly these places of worship, so the idyll's picture of conquest is measured against the ordinary religious life the Murugan poem describes."},{"a":"pp:9:271-291","b":"pn:66","type":"qualifies","note":"Venni Kuyathiyar praises Karikal's victory but says the king he defeated at Venni, who sat facing north to die ashamed of a wound on his back, is better than he; the idyll's catalogue of the peoples Karikalan crushed has no such voice, and praises his men for never turning their backs (ஒருவேமெனப் புறக்கொடாது).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both poems turn on the shame of the turned back, but the short puram poem lets a woman poet rank the defeated king's honour above the victor's, while the long praise gives the ethic only to the victor's side, so the pair shows what the conquest catalogue had no room to say."},{"a":"pp:9:292-301","b":"k:546","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says victory comes not from the spear but from the sceptre that does not bend; the idyll gives Karikalan both, the spear hot to his enemies and the sceptre cool to his people, and uses them as the measures of the forest's heat and the heroine's coolness.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural ranks the sceptre above the spear as the source of victory, while the idyll keeps the two as equal faces of one king, each true in its own sphere, so the pair shows the rhyme வேல் and கோல் as an older fixed figure of kingship that the Kural argued with and the akam poet borrowed for love."},{"a":"pp:9:142-158","b":"pp:1:218-226","type":"develops","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai lists the veri ground the velan prepares (வேலன் தைஇய வெறியயர் களனும்) among the god's dwellings, with village festivals and hill places; the idyll sets mansion women at their windows joining hands to red Murugan and crowding with the veri dancers in Puhar's festival bazaar.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The veri rite, which the Murugan poem places in the hills and the village grounds, appears in Pattinappalai in a port city's bazaar among the mansions, so the pair shows the possession rite of the hills carried into urban festival life by the time of the idyll."},{"a":"pp:10:21-37","b":"pp:2:1-22","type":"same_image","note":"Both guide poems describe the bard's lute part by part, and both lay its leather cover like the line of soft hair on a woman's belly (மயிர் ஒழுகிய); Porunaratruppadai's woman is newly pregnant, Malaipadukadam's a bride whose hair still smells of the wedding.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The hair-line simile for the lute's cover recurs, with the same verb, in two idylls by different poets, so it was a fixed part of the bards' description of their own instrument, and each poet varied only the stage of the woman's life."},{"a":"pp:10:67-94","b":"a:349","type":"develops","note":"The idyll says Nannan brings back treasures from those whose backs he has seen and pours his gifts on poets (சுரக்கும், 71-72) and lays waste the enemy front (முனைபாழ், 59); Akananuru 349, in a heroine's lament, names a Nannan who lays waste the front, breaks forts and pours their wealth unasked (சூழாது சுரக்கும்) on bards who come with their kin.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"An akam heroine can name Nannan's land by a two-line formula that the guide poem unfolds into a whole promise to a troupe, which shows the patron's praise circulated as a fixed epithet that love poets could assume their hearers knew, though the identity of the two Nannans is not certain."},{"a":"pp:10:158-169","b":"k:90","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says a guest wilts at a host's changed face; in the idyll the hill people take the troupe in as kin without asking who they are and speak sweet words to end the loneliness of a far country, but only after the troupe says it is the Vel's musicians.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The idyll confirms the Kural's point that a welcome lies in the manner more than the meal, but it shows the welcome opened by the patron's name rather than owed to any guest, so in the guide poem hospitality is a sign of a king's reach, not the householder's duty the Kural makes it."},{"a":"pp:10:287-307","b":"pn:281","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 281 calls a friend to guard a warrior's wound with lute, mustard, incense and the singing of kaanchi; Malaipadukadam hears among the hill's sounds the song hill women sing as a guard (காப்பு) to soothe the tiger-torn wounds on their husbands' chests.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The rite of guarding a wound with song, which the Purananuru gives to a hero hurt in the king's war, appears in the idyll as an ordinary household sound of the hill after a hunting injury, so the puram poem's rite drew on a common practice of protection rather than one reserved for battle."},{"a":"pp:10:547-566","b":"pn:165","type":"develops","note":"Perunthalai Sathanar says the wealthy who gave nothing to the needy are not remembered, while those who set up fame are; in the idyll the patron counts the enthroned rich whose names died with them as more than river sand and decides that his days shall pass with fame.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The argument a bard uses in Purananuru 165 to shame and urge patrons appears in the idyll as the patron's own reasoning, so the guide poem shows the bards' doctrine of fame taken in by the giver as his motive for giving."},{"a":"pp:10:547-566","b":"k:233","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says nothing stands undying in the world but towering fame; the idyll sets beside the patron's choice of fame a crowd of dead rich men past counting whose names went with them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states that fame alone survives without saying what the alternative looks like; the idyll supplies it as a number, the sands of a riverbank, so the couplet's single survivor is measured against a vast forgetting."},{"a":"pp:10:532-546","b":"k:223","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says giving without the misery of saying 'I have nothing' is found only in the well-born; the patron, hailed as the scion of an unfailing line, will not let the bards even finish telling of their journey before he welcomes them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"One of the senses of the couplet, giving before the asker must speak of his need, is in the idyll a court scene in which the patron cuts short the suppliants' account, and the idyll ties it, as the Kural does, to the giver's lineage."},{"a":"pp:10:492-513","b":"k:90","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says a guest wilts when the host's face changes; in the idyll everyone in the patron's town looks on the arriving bards sweetly and gladly before any gift is given.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the host's face the guest's first need inside one household; the idyll extends that glad look to a whole town, so the welcoming face becomes a public mark of the patron's realm rather than a private courtesy."},{"a":"pp:10:478-491","b":"pn:151","type":"qualifies","note":"The guide tells the bards to enter Nannan's heavily guarded gate without hesitation; Purananuru 151 tells a kinsman of Nannan of the golden chariot that the poet's people have shunned his hill because of a door shut against singers.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"If both poems speak of one house, the open guarded gate the idyll promises is the standard by which Perunthalai Sathanar shames the kinsman: a lineage's fame for an open door becomes a claim its heirs can fail."},{"a":"pp:10:308-322","b":"a:349","type":"same_image","note":"In the idyll the forest men who have broken a hard stronghold (அருங்குறும் பெறிந்த) set aside their spoil as guest-tribute for their lord; in Akananuru 349 Nannan pours out unasked to weary bards the great wealth taken from broken strongholds (அருங்குறும்பு எறிந்த).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together the two poems show a chain of wealth: the spoil of forts taken by the hill people goes to Nannan as tribute and goes out again from him to the bards, so the patron's generosity praised in love poetry rests on his people's raids."},{"a":"pp:10:373-389","b":"pp:10:547-566","type":"develops","note":"On the road, stones stand at the forks bearing the names of undying fame (செல்லா நல்லிசைப் பெயரொடு) of warriors who gave their lives; at court the patron reflects that rich men who did not give died taking their names with them.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The idyll makes one argument across the road and the court: a name survives on the stone of the man who gave his life or in the fame of the man who gives his wealth, and is lost with the man who gives nothing."}]});
